(window.ATLAS_LATER = window.ATLAS_LATER || []).push({"fields":{"FIGURES":{"thakurprasad":{"blurb":"Dance-master at the Lucknow court in the last years before the Mutiny, remembered in his family's own histories as the man who taught kathak's intricacies to the Nawab himself. His documented pupils were Durga Prasad's sons, Bindadin and Kalka Prasad, and everything later called the Lucknow gharana descends from that schoolroom. Of his own dancing nothing survives; he is a founder known entirely through the people he taught.","src":"Teri Skillman, 'Kathak: The Churning of Tradition and Creative Resurgence' — Asia Pacific Dance Festival, University of Hawai'i at Manoa (dancer-teacher at the Nawab's court in Lucknow; taught Bindadin Maharaj and Kalka Prasad)"},"wajidalishah":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Wajid Ali Shah: Mirza Wajid Ali Shah, 30 July 1822, eleventh and last King of Awadh","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Wajid Ali Shah: 1 September 1887","blurb":"Awadh's last king and kathak's working patron: he composed as 'Akhtarpiya', staged rahas dance-dramas at his court, and wrote treatises on its music and dance — the 400-page 'Bani' among them — describing gats modelled on the fanning and water-carrying gestures of the Kahar community. The British annexed his kingdom in 1856 on charges of misrule that historians doubt; in Calcutta exile at Metiabruz he rebuilt a small Lucknow and composed until his death in 1887.","src":"Teri Skillman, 'Kathak: The Churning of Tradition and Creative Resurgence' — Asia Pacific Dance Festival, University of Hawai'i at Manoa (last and most famous Nawab of Awadh; patron of kathaks at his court; poet-composer)"},"bindadinmaharaj":{"srcb":"Mahindra Sanatkada Lucknow Festival","srcd":"Mahindra Sanatkada Lucknow Festival, 'Kalka-Bindadin Gharana': 'it was an utter loss to the nation when he passed away in 1918'; Lorna Sanders, Akram Khan's Rush (2004), p. 102, gives 'Bindadin Maharaj (1830-1918)'","blurb":"Set to practise tatkaar twelve hours a day for four years from the age of twelve, he became the Lucknow gharana's poet: thumris signed 'Binda' — about three thousand by the family's count, more than five thousand by other tallies — through which kathak's expressive abhinaya took its modern shape. His students included the star tawaifs of the age, Gauhar Jan among them; childless himself, he poured the tradition into his brother Kalka Prasad's three sons.","src":"Mahindra Sanatkada Lucknow Festival — 'Kalka-Bindadin Gharana' page (born 1830; composed about 3,000 thumris; pioneer of the gharana); Sahapedia, Siddhi Goel — 'Kathak: Aesthetics, History and Influences' (composer of abhinaya pieces: thumri, bhajan, dadra, hori)"},"kalkaprasad":{"srcd":"James Kippen and Andrew Bel, 'Lucknow Kathak Dance', Bansuri vol. 13 (1996): 'Bindadin Maharaj (d.1918) and his younger brother Kalka Prasad (d.c.1910) became without a doubt the most celebrated dancers of their time'; nrityashiksha.com agrees, 'He died in Lucknow around 1910 AD'. Both hedge with 'around', which the map's bare 1910 does not","blurb":"The rhythm half of the celebrated 'Kalka-Bindadin ki jodi': his elder brother wrote the poetry, Kalka Prasad commanded the percussive science, and the pair performed as one famous act. His larger bequest was dynastic — his sons Achhan, Lachhu and Shambhu Maharaj carried the gharana across the twentieth century, and their pupils carried it around the world.","src":"Sahapedia — Shambhu Maharaj profile ('Kalka Prasad, his father, was a tabla wizard'); Mahindra Sanatkada Lucknow Festival — 'Kalka-Bindadin Gharana' page (noted for rhythmic prowess; father of Achhan, Lachhu and Shambhu Maharaj)"},"enricocecchetti":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Enrico Cecchetti: 21 June 1850, born in the costuming room of the Teatro Tordinona in Rome","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Enrico Cecchetti: 13 November 1928","blurb":"Born in a costume room of Rome's Teatro Tordinona, he danced both the Bluebird and the wicked fairy Carabosse at the 1890 premiere of The Sleeping Beauty, and past sixty was still onstage as the Magician in Petrushka. What he left behind was a syllabus: fixed exercises, drilled in daily order until the body owned them. The pupil list is most of a century — Pavlova, Nijinsky, Karsavina, Massine, Rambert, de Valois, Markova, Balanchine.","src":"Beaumont 1922"},"emilejaquesdalcroze":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Emile Jaques-Dalcroze: 6 July 1865","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Emile Jaques-Dalcroze: 1 July 1950","blurb":"A harmony professor in Geneva who built a method for hearing music with the whole body, then took it to Hellerau, a garden city outside Dresden where his institute admitted workers and their children alongside professionals. His Orpheus with Adolphe Appia dissolved the proscenium — performers and five thousand spectators in one continuously lit hall — and drew Shaw, Stanislavsky and Diaghilev. Mary Wigman danced a fury in it, then left him because he would not let movement go without music.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 2 (Gestalt im Raum)"},"pandanallurmeenakshisundarampillai":{"srcb":"Anne-Marie Gaston, Bharata Natyam: From Temple to Theatre (1996), section heading","srcd":"Gaston, Bharata Natyam: From Temple to Theatre (1996): '(1869-1954)'. en.wikipedia gives 1964; this map follows the monograph","blurb":"The nattuvanar of Pandanallur village: schooled in his family's line from the Thanjavur Quartet and married into Sivanandam's branch, he taught the inherited repertoire with a geometer's exactness — linear adavus, understated abhinaya, ankle bells sounded rather than stamped. When the revival generation went looking for authority it walked to his village: Rukmini Devi, Ram Gopal, Mrinalini Sarabhai and Tara Chaudhri all carried versions of his classroom onto the world's stages.","src":"Kalakshetra Foundation — 'The Founder' page ('Pandanallur Meenakshisundaram Pillai was her guru', of Rukmini Devi Arundale)"},"sergeidiaghilev":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Sergei Diaghilev: 31 March [O.S. 19 March] 1872","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Sergei Diaghilev: 19 August 1929","blurb":"He never choreographed a step; he commissioned. Stravinsky's Firebird, Petrushka and Rite of Spring, Debussy's Jeux, Ravel's Daphnis et Chloé, Satie's Parade, Picasso on Pulcinella — and four choreographers in succession, Fokine, Nijinsky, Massine, Balanchine, each pushed further than they had meant to go. He ran the whole thing paycheck to paycheck, his well-cut suits fraying at the cuffs, and died of diabetes in Venice in 1929.","src":"Garafola 1989"},"isadoraduncan":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Isadora Duncan: 'May 26, 1877, or May 27, 1878' — the article carries both, unresolved; this map follows 1877","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Isadora Duncan: September 14, 1927","blurb":"She threw out ballet for what she called natural movement: barefoot, in a white Greek tunic, dancing to concert music never meant for dance. The schools she founded in Berlin and Moscow were meant to raise the new body from childhood, and six pupils — the Isadorables — carried her name and manner onward. Her life kept breaking in public: her two children drowned in the Seine in 1913, and in Nice in 1927 her scarf tangled in a car's open wheel and killed her.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006)"},"rudolflaban":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Rudolf von Laban (Laban Rudolf): 15 December 1879","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Rudolf von Laban: 1 July 1958","blurb":"He ran his school on the rubric dance-sound-word and let dancers work to speech, drums or silence — the permission Wigman took and never gave back. As Berlin State Opera ballet director he had the school Aryanised before the law required it, and from 1934 ran Goebbels's Deutsche Tanzbühne and the Rangsdorf camp the minister inspected and liked. Goebbels banned his Olympic piece after the dress rehearsal and he was finished — though he drew ministry money into November 1937, and the Gestapo saw him out with a paper saying there was nothing against him.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006)"},"ruthstdenis":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ruth St. Denis: born Ruth Dennis, January 20, 1879","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Ruth St. Denis: died July 21, 1968","blurb":"A skirt-dancer and high-kicker in Belasco's touring companies until 1904, when a cigarette poster of the goddess Isis turned her toward dance as a serious art. Radha, her Hindu ballet, triumphed at New York's Hudson Theater in 1906; Denishawn, the school and company she founded with Ted Shawn — hired as her partner in April 1914, married that August — trained Martha Graham. She ended as she intended, staging religious dances for churches and her Society of Spiritual Arts.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 7 (Mary Wigman and American Dance)"},"jacquescopeau":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jacques Copeau: 4 February 1879","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Jacques Copeau: 20 October 1949","blurb":"He introduced, almost single-handedly, the idea that French actors needed years of continuous training, at a moment when he judged the theatre around him artistically bankrupt. His Vieux-Colombier school put movement, mask and play at the centre — Hébert's natural gymnastics over Dalcroze's eurhythmics — and taught by stripping away, to recover the child's instinct for play. Decroux, Barrault, Marceau and Lecoq all trace to his 1920s teaching; he himself wanted theatre returned to its classics, not made new.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"papaaugustin":{"blurb":"A Haitian master drummer who taught percussion at Dunham's New York school and kept it moored to his home country. When an exhausted Dunham came to New York to perform rituals, he insisted she go to Haiti instead and be re-baptized by one of Port-au-Prince's most important mambos. And in 1951 it was Augustin who brought Haitian officials to watch Lavinia Williams teach in Brooklyn — the visit that led Haiti's government to hire her to found its national school of folkloric dance.","src":"Das, Katherine Dunham: Dance and the African Diaspora (2017), ch. 5; also Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 6 — in his description of the Dunham School at its height"},"sunderprasad":{"srcd":"en.wikipedia, Sunder Prasad: 'Pandit Sunder Prasad (died 29 May 1970) was a guru of the Jaipur Gharana of Kathak' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A Jaipur-gharana son — brother of Jai Lal, trained by their father Chunnilal and uncle Durga Prasad — who went to Bindadin Maharaj of Lucknow and spent his life joining the two schools' virtues. He was above all a maker of dancers: some thirty years of teaching in Bombay, then Madras, with Kumudini Lakhia and Durga Lal among his students; Delhi's akademi honoured him in 1958.","src":"Nad Sadhna (Institute of Indian Music) — 'Biography of Indian Dancers' (Jaipur gharana; son of Chunnilal, trained by uncle Durga Prasad and Bindadin Maharaj; taught c. 30 years in Mumbai, then Chennai; honoured in Delhi 1958)"},"acchanmaharaj":{"srcb":"James Kippen and Andrew Bel, 'Lucknow Kathak Dance', Bansuri vol. 13 (1996), quoting Sunil Kothari's 1989 monograph: 'Acchan Maharaj (1883-1947)'. Other years circulate and are worth knowing: the Oxford Encyclopaedia of the Music of India has a passing 'Achhan Maharaj (1888-1946)', nrityashiksha.com says 'born about 1893', and saraswatisangeetsadhana.in heads its page 1893-1950. The 1883 side rests on the standard Kathak reference","srcd":"Kippen and Bel, citing Kothari (1989), give 1947, and the same article adds 'Birju Maharaj (b. 1937), the son of Acchan Maharaj, studied with his father until the latter's death in 1947'; Indra Dhanush vol. 1 (2014) corroborates, 'With the passing away of Achhan Maharaj in 1947'; nrityashiksha.com gives the day, 11 May 1947. The Oxford Encyclopaedia's passing mention says 1946, which is what the research pass carried","blurb":"Eldest of Kalka Prasad's sons, trained by his father and his uncle Bindadin, he served as court dancer at Rampur, where his youngest brother Shambhu joined him at fourteen. A heavily built man who nevertheless took his chakkars with disarming ease, he trained Sukhdev Maharaj's daughters when their father asked — and his own son: Birju Maharaj, performing from the age of seven, learnt the gharana at his feet.","src":"Mahindra Sanatkada Lucknow Festival — 'Kalka-Bindadin Gharana' page (eldest son of Kalka Prasad, trained by Kalka Prasad and Bindadin; court dancer at Rampur); Sahapedia, Siddhi Goel — 'Kathak: Aesthetics, History and Influences' (worked at the Rampur court)"},"louishorst":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Louis Horst: born January 12, 1884, Kansas City, Missouri","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Louis Horst: died January 23, 1964, New York City","blurb":"A pianist who became modern dance's schoolmaster: a decade as Denishawn's musical director, then twenty-two years as Martha Graham's composer, accompanist and mentor. He held that choreography could be taught like counterpoint, drilling generations at Bennington and Juilliard in the old court forms — the argument of his Pre-Classic Dance Forms (1937) — and founded Dance Observer to give the young art a critical press of its own, editing it until his death in 1964.","src":"Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1"},"adolphbolm":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Adolph Bolm: Adolph Rudolphovich Bolm, September 25, 1884","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Adolph Bolm: April 16, 1951 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A Mariinsky dancer who had run a European tour for Pavlova before he was thirty, he was injured onstage in Thamar during the Ballets Russes' 1917 American tour and simply stayed. From Chicago he seeded ballet across the United States — teaching the teenage Ruth Page, making the film Danse Macabre with her in 1922, running summers at Seattle's Cornish School, then ballet master of the San Francisco Opera from 1933. His last appearance was as the Moor in Petrushka at the Hollywood Bowl, aged 58.","src":"Encyclopedia.com, 'Bolm, Adolph Rudolphovich': Imperial Ballet first-prize dancer; maitre de ballet at the Chicago Opera 1922; San Francisco Opera ballet master 1932; later directed a ballet school"},"suzannebing":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Suzanne Bing: 10 March 1885, founding member of Copeau's Theatre du Vieux-Colombier","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Suzanne Bing: 22 November 1967","blurb":"A founding member of Copeau's Vieux-Colombier company — its first Viola — she did much of the school's actual inventing: mask work, improvisation, and the training of adolescents at the École du Vieux-Colombier, where character was built from the body rather than rhetoric. Her students included Étienne Decroux, which makes her a quiet origin of modern mime; the remaking of French actor training she helped begin was carried on by Lecoq and Mnouchkine while her own name faded.","src":"Leach, Theatre Studies: The Basics (Routledge, 2008): Copeau"},"jailalmaharaj":{"srcb":"saraswatisangeetsadhana.in, page headed 'जयलाल जीवन परिचय Jayalal Biography In Hindi 1885-1949', giving जन्म तिथि 1885, his father Pt Chunnilal, his teacher Durga Prasad, the Jaipur, Jodhpur, Raigarh and Maihar courts and his daughter Jaikumari — every particular the atlas already holds, so it is the same man","srcd":"saraswatisangeetsadhana.in, 'जयलाल जीवन परिचय 1885-1949': मृत्यु 1949","blurb":"A pillar of the Jaipur house who made his living the old way, court to court — Jaipur, Jodhpur, Raigarh, Maihar — as a master of layakari and intricate parans. He put the gharana's future into his own children as much as his students: his daughter Jaikumari was reckoned among the finest dancers of her day, his son Ram Gopal taught after him, and he himself taught in Calcutta until his death there in 1949, aged sixty-four.","src":"Nad Sadhna (Institute of Indian Music) — 'Biography of Indian Dancers' (born 1885; Jaipur gharana pillar; attached to the courts of Jaipur, Jodhpur, Raigarh and Maihar; taught until his death in Calcutta, 1949)"},"marywigman":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Mary Wigman: born Karoline Sophie Marie Wiegmann, 13 November 1886","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Mary Wigman: died 18 September 1973","blurb":"She broke down in 1918 and came back in 1919 with a respelled name and a practice built on the figure in space rather than on the music. Her Dresden school headed a group with Palucca's and Trümpy's that joined the Nazi teachers' league and put racial science on the syllabus. Manning has her keeping Jewish students by permission; Karina and Kant record Pola Nirenska's claim that she dismissed most of them on her return from America. She mourned the dead hero with eighty women at the 1936 Olympics, then taught refugees free in Leipzig after 1942.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006)"},"bakuishii":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 石井漠: 1886年12月25日","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 石井漠: 1962年1月7日","blurb":"He began in Italian ballet and Japanese art song, toured France and Germany in the early 1920s, saw Wigman dance, and brought eurythmics home inside what he called dance poems. Ohno Kazuo took his classes in 1933 and left unconvinced — it was mainly like pantomime, he wrote, dance built like drama on a ballet base. Ishii also wrote the schoolbook that put dance-gymnastics into Japanese classrooms in the years when the slogans were about enriching the nation and building the army.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 13 (Schwellinger): Ohno took classes in Ishii Baku's studio from 1933; ch. 14: Ishii studied with Rosi and Yamada Kosaku, toured France and Germany in the early 1920s where he saw Wigman perform; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): Ishii Baku (1886-1962), one of the pioneers of modern dance in Japan; Hijikata said that by the fifth grade he had already attended Ishii's performances, which is Baird's evidence that he grew up with dance. Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): Dalcroze technique and German Neue Tanz were imported to Japan through Kosaku Yamada and sustained in Ishii Baku's teaching, and Ohno studied with Ishii Baku in 1933"},"kosakuyamada":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 山田耕筰: 1886年〈明治19年〉6月9日, 旧名 山田耕作","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 山田耕筰: 1965年〈昭和40年〉12月29日","blurb":"He wrote the first complete four-movement symphony composed in Japan, then went looking for bodies to put music into. With Baku Ishii and the director Kaoru Osanai he started a 'New Theatre' and the dance-and-poetry movement out of which Japan's first Western-style concert dance came; in New York he collaborated with Michio Ito. His own ballet on Maeterlinck's Maria Magdalena got as far as complete piano sketches, which are now lost.","src":"Brill Experimental"},"sukhdevmaharaj":{"srcb":"ClassicalClaps, Sunil Sunkara, 'The Trendsetting Kathak Visionary: Pt Sukhdev Maharaj' (5 December 2021): 'Pt Sukhdev Maharaj was born in August 1888 in Kathmandu, Nepal. His father Ramdas Mishra ji was an expert vocalist and sarangi player.' The same page lists his children Alaknanda, Tara and Sitara. Note the birthplace it gives is Kathmandu, not Varanasi","blurb":"A Brahmin Sanskrit scholar who earned his living by kathak: a Vaishnavite who studied the Natya Shastra and Bharatanatyam in depth, served at the Nepal court, and then did what respectable men did not — opened a dance school and put his own daughters in it. Alaknanda, Tara and Sitara learnt from him and from Achhan Maharaj, whom he asked to add the Lucknow polish; the daughter who at eight refused the marriage arranged for her grew into Sitara Devi.","src":"Sahapedia, Siddhi Goel — 'Kathak: Aesthetics, History and Influences' (leading guru of the Banaras gharana, court dancer in Nepal, father of Sitara Devi); Nad Sadhna — 'Biography of Indian Dancers' (had Achhan Maharaj and Shambhu Maharaj train his daughters)"},"suzanneperrottet":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Suzanne Perrottet: 13 September 1889","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Suzanne Perrottet: 10 August 1983","blurb":"Dalcroze's assistant at Hellerau, she quit to follow Laban, and it was partly her account that sent Wigman to Monte Verita. She played her own, Laban's and Schoenberg's compositions at the Cabaret Voltaire in April 1917, wrote the music for Wigman's Die Koenigin, and led dancing in Laban's all-night Sun Festival. She bore Laban a son; he took responsibility for none of his children.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 2 (Gestalt im Raum)"},"margarethdoubler":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Margaret H'Doubler: Margaret Newell H'Doubler, April 26, 1889","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Margaret H'Doubler: March 26, 1982","blurb":"A biology graduate who taught basketball and swimming at Wisconsin, she found her method in Alys Bentley's New York classes, where students moved to music lying on the floor. Back in Madison she taught dance as education rather than performance — beginners exploring on the floor, asked to describe their movements in scientific terms — and in 1926 built the first university dance major anywhere. Anna Halprin and Mary Hinkson both began in her classroom.","src":"Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"vaslavnijinsky":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Vaslav Nijinsky: '12 March 1889/1890' — the article gives both years; this map follows 1889","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Vaslav Nijinsky: 8 April 1950","blurb":"Cecchetti sponsored his application to the Imperial school; he graduated second in his class and became the dancer people still argue about. As a choreographer he made only four ballets in four years — Faune, whose closing gesture with a scarf had to be defended by Rodin and Proust, Jeux, the Rite of Spring that set off violence in the auditorium, and Till Eulenspiegel, which he improvised at the first performance. Diaghilev dismissed him by telegram when he married; he had never had a contract or wages.","src":"Moore 2013"},"asadatadafora":{"srcb":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 5 —","srcd":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 5 —","blurb":"Great-great-grandson of a man who returned to Freetown from slavery in Nova Scotia, he had sung at La Scala and toured Aida before reaching New York in 1929, where he first tried a career as a lyric tenor. Kykunkor opened at the Unity Theatre — a converted East 23rd Street beauty parlour — on 5 May 1934 to four almost empty houses, until the manager talked John Martin into coming and his review filled the place overnight. He spent his last years taking whatever bookings he could find, in nightclubs and zoos.","src":"Manning, Modern Dance, Negro Dance: Race in Motion (University of Minnesota Press, 2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search: Dafora and his family, who"},"jayammal":{"srcb":"Douglas M. Knight Jr., Balasaraswati: Her Art and Life (Wesleyan University Press, 2010), the family genealogy chart: 'Jayammal Srinivasan (1890-1967)'; the narrative adds that her great-grandmother 'died in 1890, the same year in which her great-granddaughter, Balasaraswati's mother, Jayammal, was born'","srcd":"Knight, Balasaraswati: Her Art and Life (2010), genealogy chart: 'Jayammal Srinivasan (1890-1967)'","blurb":"A singer of Veena Dhanammal's house — daughter of the era's most revered veena player — who encouraged her own daughter toward dance and then stood behind her for decades as her accompanist, the fixed musical point around which Balasaraswati improvised. The musicality critics heard in the dancer was the household's daily practice, and Jayammal was its working centre; she sang for Balasaraswati until her own death in 1967.","src":"Hari Krishnan, 'Introduction to Bharatanatyam and T. Balasaraswati' — Jacob's Pillow Dance Interactive (T. Jayammal, 1890-1967, singer; daughter of Veena Dhanammal, mother of Balasaraswati)"},"gertrudbodenwieser":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Gertrud Bodenwieser: 3 February 1890","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Gertrud Bodenwieser: 10 November 1959","blurb":"A Viennese professor of dance who taught out of a concert-hall basement and made Demon Machine, in which the dancers become the machinery. In 1938 she and her largely Jewish company got out; the route ran through Colombia, where she danced Bogota's four-hundredth anniversary to a full bullring, and ended in Sydney. There she built the first modern dance company Australia took seriously, and in 1944 answered the war with The Masks of Lucifer — intrigue, terror and hatred given bodies.","src":"Wikipedia, Gertrud Bodenwieser — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gertrud_Bodenwieser (professorship in Vienna, Demon Machine, 1938 flight via Colombia, Bodenwieser Ballet Sydney 1939-1959, The Masks of Lucifer 1944, students incl. Bain, Chapple, Kramer)"},"tedshawn":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ted Shawn: born Edwin Myers Shawn, October 21, 1891","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Ted Shawn: died January 9, 1972","blurb":"A Denver ministry student whose diphtheria left him temporarily paralyzed — dancing began as his physical therapy. With Ruth St. Denis he created the Denishawn School in 1915 and, after their separation dissolved it, spent the thirties proving dance a man's profession: Ted Shawn and His Men Dancers toured from 1933 until 1940, when the company dissolved into the armed forces. The Becket farm where he charged the public a small fee to watch rehearsals became the Jacob's Pillow Dance Festival.","src":"Ted Shawn papers finding aid, Jerome Robbins Dance Division, NYPL (archives.nypl.org/dan/23066): Ted Shawn (1891-1972), American dancer, choreographer, educator; created the Denishawn School of Dancing and Related Arts with Ruth St. Denis; founder of Jacob's Pillow and Ted Shawn and His Men Dancers (1933-1940)"},"bronislavanijinska":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Bronislava Nijinska: January 8, 1891 [O.S. December 27, 1890]","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Bronislava Nijinska: February 21, 1972","blurb":"She entered the Imperial school at nine, helped her brother build Faune and the Rite, and resigned in solidarity when Diaghilev cut him loose. On her own in revolutionary Kiev she opened a School of Movement, published a theory of choreography in 1920, and fled across the Bug into Poland when the Soviets closed it. Back with the Ballets Russes she made Les Noces and Les Biches — some seventy ballets over a lifetime, and Stravinsky called her 'endowed with a profoundly artistic nature'.","src":"Nijinska 1981"},"mylaporegowriammal":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Mylapore Gowri Ammal: (1892-1971), Bharatanatyam dancer, temple dancer at the Kapaleeshwarar Temple, Mylapore","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Mylapore Gowri Ammal: (1892-1971)","blurb":"The last devadasi to serve the Kapaleeshwarar temple in Mylapore: the 1947 act that abolished her order also took her temple residence, and she lived afterwards by teaching. Her abhinaya was the thing itself — widely noted, unreproducible — and it drew reform and resistance alike to her door: Rukmini Devi sought her out in 1936, Balasaraswati studied with her, Yamini Krishnamurthy and Kalanidhi Narayanan after them. The Akademi's award found her in 1959.","src":"Veejay Sai, 'Yamini Krishnamurthi: The Last Prima Donna of Indian Classical Dance' — Swarajya obituary, Aug 2024 ('the last of the traditional dancers dedicated to the Kapaleeshwarar Temple in Mylapore'); Kalakshetra Foundation — 'The Founder' page (initiated Rukmini Devi into bharatanatyam)"},"margretcraske":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Margaret Craske: 26 November 1892","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Margaret Craske: 18 February 1990","blurb":"Cecchetti's pupil, and the one who kept the shop: when he retired to Italy in 1923 she took over his studio in West Street, London, and with Cyril Beaumont wrote the allegro down so it could not be lost. Ashton, Fonteyn, Helpmann and Tudor came through her classes. She followed Meher Baba from 1931 and spent the war years in India, then came to New York and from 1950 taught at the Metropolitan Opera Ballet School, still teaching into her eighties.","src":"Cecchetti International Classical Ballet, 'Margaret Craske' (cicb.org): pupil of Enrico Cecchetti in London; from 1950 principal teacher of the resident school at the Metropolitan Opera under Tudor; taught the Cecchetti adagios and allegros so 'this method must not be lost'"},"valeskagert":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Valeska Gert: 11 January 1892","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Valeska Gert: 'c. 16 March 1978' — the article hedges the day, not the year","blurb":"Where Ausdruckstanz went looking for the universal, Gert danced prostitution, boxing, traffic and orgasm; the 1922 Berlin performance of Canaille brought the police. Jewish but holding a British passport, she was still in Germany in 1934 — powerless, Karina and Kant write, rather than gone. By 1941 she was running the Beggar's Bar in New York, where Tennessee Williams waited tables; later she kept a cabaret in a goat shed on Sylt and turned up in Fellini's Juliet of the Spirits.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), introduction to the new edition"},"hanyaholm":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Hanya Holm: born Johanna Eckert, 3 March 1893","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Hanya Holm: died 3 November 1992","blurb":"Wigman sent her to New York in 1931 to plant the school; American students quit over what they called a lack of technique, so Holm and Louise Kloepper bolted a warm-up, turnout and repetition onto a method built on improvisation. By 1936 the sign said Hanya Holm Studio and the communal choric class was gone. When leftist critics attacked Wigman's dealings with the Nazis, Holm insisted art and politics were separate and stayed at Bennington; two decades later she was choreographing Kiss Me Kate and My Fair Lady.","src":"Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 3; also Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006)"},"michioito":{"srcb":"Britannica, Michio Ito — gives \"1892?\", its own question mark; Perron and the Routledge companion give 1893, which this map follows","srcd":"Britannica, Michio Ito: died 1961, Tokyo","blurb":"The only Asian among Dalcroze's three hundred Hellerau students, he created the Hawk in Yeats's At the Hawk's Well in London in 1916, then carried his 'dance poems' to New York — where the teacher on the floor above passed his structural ideas into American modern dance — and on to Hollywood Bowl spectacles. Pauline Koner and Lester Horton were his students. Arrested as an enemy alien after Pearl Harbor, he was repatriated in 1943; he died in 1961 planning ceremonies for the Tokyo Olympics.","src":"Wendy Perron, 'Michio Ito (1893 to 1961)' (wendyperron.com): Japanese-born choreographer, trained at the Dalcroze Institute in Hellerau, career in London, New York and Los Angeles, taught and strongly influenced Lester Horton; interned after Pearl Harbor and repatriated to Japan 1943"},"pierrevladimiroff":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Pierre Vladimiroff (Pyotr Nikolayevich Vladimirov): February 13, 1893","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Pierre Vladimiroff: November 25, 1970","blurb":"When Nijinsky was gone his roles went to Vladimiroff — including the prince in Diaghilev's 1921 Sleeping Princess — and he ended his stage life as Anna Pavlova's last partner. From 1934 to 1967 he taught at the School of American Ballet, the first of its teachers to take the boys, and Maria Tallchief, Tanaquil LeClercq and Francisco Moncion came out of those classes. A grand duke had once shot him in the nose over a ballerina.","src":"School of American Ballet, '#SAB90 - Beginnings' (sab.org): Imperial School-trained Maryinsky premier danseur and Pavlova partner, on SAB's founding faculty when the school opened January 2, 1934"},"marthagraham":{"srcb":"Britannica, Martha Graham: born May 11, 1894, Allegheny county, Pennsylvania, U.S.","srcd":"Britannica, Martha Graham: died April 1, 1991, New York, New York","blurb":"From a Carnegie Hall studio in 1926 she built company, school, and technique in one: 181 ballets across seventy years, movement grounded in the torso's contraction and release, scores commissioned from Copland and Barber, sets from Noguchi. The company doubled as an incubator she could not contain — Cunningham, Taylor, and Tharp each trained inside it and left to build their own. TIME named her Dancer of the Century.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 7 (Mary Wigman and American Dance)"},"annaduncan":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority GND 129356700, Duncan, Anna (variant Denzler, Anna): date of birth 1894-12-12, place Zurich; 'Schweizer.-amerikan. Tanzerin'","srcd":"GND 129356700: died 1980, New York, NY","blurb":"One of the six Grunewald pupils Isadora Duncan raised from childhood — the Isadorables — she took the Duncan name, though the adoption the group claimed was never legalized; a New York court refused them the surname in 1920. When the troupe scattered she turned the style into a curriculum of her own, teaching at the Eastman School of Music and, from 1926, at Carnegie Hall, and acting in films besides.","src":"Kurth 2001"},"dorishumphrey":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Doris Humphrey: Doris Batcheller Humphrey, October 17, 1895","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Doris Humphrey: died December 29, 1958","blurb":"Denishawn's leading dancer from 1917 until 1928, when she and Charles Weidman broke away to build a company and a theory of their own: fall and recovery, dance located in the body's argument with gravity. When she stopped performing in 1944 she did something then unheard of, becoming artistic director of José Limón's new company — the first modern dance troupe not directed by its founder — and made her late works on her former student's dancers.","src":"Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"albrechtknust":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority n84208239, 'Knust, Albrecht', sourced to his own Dictionary of Kinetography Laban (Labanotation), 1979, v. 1, p. ii: 'b. 1896; d. 1978'. GND 1137531886 gives the days: 5 October 1896, Hamburg, and confirms the Folkwang affiliation","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority n84208239, from p. ii of his own Dictionary of Kinetography Laban: 'd. 1978'; GND 1137531886 gives 19 March 1978, Essen","blurb":"The first person to make a full-time job of writing dance down: by 1930 he and Laban's daughter Azra were running a notation office, and between 1946 and 1950 he wrote an eight-volume manual of Kinetography Laban that circulated in English as typed carbon copies. He took over the Folkwang dance school in 1934, the year after Jooss fled, and gave it up after twelve months. Der Tanz printed him teaching at Laban's Rangsdorf camp in September 1935, and Laban proposed him for the new state dance archive — the desk officer wrote no in the margin.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 5 notes"},"ludmillasperanzeva":{"blurb":"A Russian émigré out of Moscow's Kamerny Theatre and the Chauve-Souris cabaret, with a brief spell under Mary Wigman, she founded the Negro Dance Group with Katherine Dunham in Chicago; the press called its 1932 performance 'the first Negro ballet ever danced.' Dunham later said she got from her 'a lot of that explosive feeling, and also a freedom of sexual presentation' — cabaret theatricality and Wigman's ecstatic modernism folded into what became the Dunham style.","src":"Das, Katherine Dunham: Dance and the African Diaspora (2017), ch. 1; also Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 6 —"},"markturbyfill":{"srcd":"The Newberry Library's own finding aid for the Mark Turbyfill papers (askart.com)","blurb":"Katherine Dunham took ballet lessons from him in Chicago in years when a Black dancer had almost nowhere to get classical training. Das's biography of Dunham credits the two with trying to build a company out of that, the Ballet Nègre; by Dunham's own record the group she formed in 1931 danced once, at the Chicago Beaux Arts Ball, was well received, and then disbanded.","src":"Das, Katherine Dunham: Dance and the African Diaspora (2017), ch. 1"},"lottejacobi":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Lotte Jacobi: 17 August 1896","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Lotte Jacobi: 6 May 1990","blurb":"A fourth-generation photographer who ran the family's Berlin studio from 1927 and was passionate about dance and theatre photography — the Brooklyn Museum showed her Weimar-era dance pictures in 1937. She fled Germany in 1935 with her son, losing nearly all of her early work, and rebuilt in New York: Einstein at his desk in 1938, camera-less 'photogenics' in the 1940s, and at last a New Hampshire studio where she worked until her death at ninety-three.","src":"Metromod Archive"},"irmaduncan":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Irma Duncan: February 26, 1897","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Irma Duncan: September 20, 1977","blurb":"The Isadorable who followed Isadora furthest: to revolutionary Moscow in 1921, where she took over the school when Isadora left in 1924 and toured the Soviet Union and China with the Isadora Duncan Dancers of Moscow. At the end of their 1929 American tour she stayed in the United States; the pupils went back without her. She spent the rest of her life as the tradition's record-keeper, publishing her autobiography, Duncan Dancer, in 1966.","src":"Kurth 2001"},"etiennedecroux":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Etienne Decroux: 19 July 1898 in Paris","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Etienne Decroux: 12 March 1991 in Boulogne-Billancourt","blurb":"He gave fifty years to building a grammar for mime — pulling and pushing as the two essential human actions, drama placed inside the moving body rather than gesture substituting for speech — a system Lecoq found 'severe' and locked shut. He wanted mime autonomous, distinct from theatre on one side and dance on the other, and trained Marceau only to disavow the popular illusion-mime his pupil toured for four decades. He died in extreme poverty, his grammaire the deep structure under corporeal mime.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"joseamaya":{"blurb":"A poor guitarist who played taverns by day and by night and, when his daughter Carmen was four, began taking her along on the rounds — he strummed, she sang and danced, and they picked the audience's coins off the ground. When she became the century's most famous flamenco dancer, the troupe that toured behind her was his family: her brother Paco on guitar, her sisters dancing. The record keeps him mostly where her story requires him — the guitar, the poverty, the nightly rounds.","src":"Leblon 2003"},"ruthpage":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ruth Page (ballerina): March 22, 1899","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Ruth Page (ballerina): April 7, 1991 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"She toured South America with Pavlova's company at nineteen and then made Chicago, not New York, her base for forty years — the Allied Arts, Ravinia, the opera companies. In 1934 she handed the lead of La Guiablesse to Katherine Dunham, with Dunham's own dancers filling the ensemble, and in 1937 made An American Pattern, described as the first feminist ballet created in the United States. Her specialty was turning operas into ballets in which the body 'sang' the voice.","src":"Das, Katherine Dunham: Dance and the African Diaspora (2017), ch. 1"},"kandappapillai":{"srcb":"Ashish Khokar, Bharatanatyam (2002)","srcd":"Khokar, Bharatanatyam (2002), '(1899-1941)'; the Oxford Encyclopaedia adds the month, 'd. Chennai, Feb. 1941'","blurb":"A nattuvanar of the famed Thanjavur family who began drilling Balasaraswati at the age of four and conducted her art for the rest of his short life. His rhythmic compositions kept alive the abstract, courtly side of Thanjavur dance while the courtesan tradition that made it was being pushed from the stage; he died in 1945, in his mid-forties, but his son Ganesan was still conducting for Bala at her American debut at Jacob's Pillow in 1962.","src":"Hari Krishnan, 'Introduction to Bharatanatyam and T. Balasaraswati' — Jacob's Pillow Dance Interactive (Kandappa Pillai, 1899-1945, Balasaraswati's dance teacher)"},"kisakuito":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 伊藤熹朔: 1899年8月1日, 舞台美術家・美術監督","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 伊藤熹朔: 1967年3月31日; Kotobank's デジタル大辞泉 entry agrees — 「いとう‐きさく【伊藤熹朔】［1899〜1967］舞台美術家... 舞踊家伊藤道郎の弟、演出家千田是也の兄」, which also confirms he is Michio Ito's brother. The research pass had 1973","blurb":"Michio Ito's brother, and the man Michio brought in as scenic designer when he was running the Ernie Pyle Theatre in occupied Tokyo. His own career ran to film: he became one of the most prominent art directors in post-war Japanese cinema and won the Mainichi award for his work on Ugetsu in 1953.","src":"Wikipedia, 'Michio Itō' ('While at the Ernie Pyle, he brought his brother Kisaku Ito on as Scenic Designer') and Wikipedia, 'Kisaku Itō' (b. 1 August 1899 Tokyo, d. 31 March 1967; art director, among the most prominent set designers in Japanese cinema after WWII; Mainichi Film Award for Best Art Direction for Ugetsu, 1953)"},"irmgardbartenieff":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Irmgard Bartenieff: February 24, 1900","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Irmgard Bartenieff: August 27, 1981","blurb":"She took Laban's two-year course in space harmony, effort and notation, then left Germany and spent the polio years as chief physical therapist at a New York fever hospital, applying movement analysis to patients who could not move. Out of that came Bartenieff Fundamentals, a body training that treats anatomical function and personal expression as the same problem. The institute added her name in June 1981, a few months before she died.","src":"Wikipedia, Irmgard Bartenieff — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irmgard_Bartenieff (1900-1981, two-year Laban course, emigration 1933-36, Willard Parker polio service, Bartenieff Fundamentals, LIMS renamed June 1981)"},"marthahill":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Martha Hill: December 1, 1900","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Martha Hill: November 19, 1995","blurb":"Dance's institution-builder: she started teaching dance inside NYU's physical-education department in 1930, was founding director of the Bennington School of the Dance from 1934 and the Connecticut College summer school from 1948 — the precursors of the American Dance Festival — then built the dance division at Juilliard in 1951, training ballet and modern dancers side by side, a revolutionary mix for the 1950s. Wherever she directed, working choreographers did the teaching.","src":"Martha Hill Dance Fund, 'Her Legacy' (marthahilldance.org/bio): founding director of the Bennington School of the Dance (1934) and founding director of dance at The Juilliard School (1951), where she combined ballet and modern training"},"paulinelawrence":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Pauline Lawrence: Pauline Lawrence Limon, nee Lawrence, 1900, born in Los Angeles","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Pauline Lawrence: July 16, 1971","blurb":"She joined Denishawn in 1917 as a pianist and became the figure companies cannot run without: accompanist, business manager, and costume designer for Humphrey-Weidman, then for the company of José Limón, whom she married in 1941. Limón's dancers performed inside her costumes and under her contracts; the unphotographed labor of two landmark modern companies ran through her hands.","src":"Pauline Lawrence Limón collection finding aid, Jerome Robbins Dance Division, NYPL (archives.nypl.org/dan/19657): (1900-1971), joined Denishawn 1917 as pianist; Humphrey-Weidman pianist, business manager and costume designer; married José Limón October 4, 1941; designed costumes and represented the Limón company"},"aaroncopland":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Aaron Copland: November 14, 1900","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Aaron Copland: December 2, 1990","blurb":"He wrote the Graham commission under the working title Ballet for Martha, scored for a thirteen-piece chamber orchestra, with a Shaker tune — Simple Gifts — running through it as theme and variations; Graham premiered it as Appalachian Spring at the Library of Congress in October 1944. The score won the 1945 Pulitzer Prize for Music and, rescored for full orchestra, entered the concert repertory on its own.","src":"aaroncopland.com — Appalachian Spring works page (composed the ballet score in 1943-44 for Martha Graham and her dance company, 'who gave the premiere October 30, 1944 at the Library of Congress's Coolidge Auditorium')"},"takayaeguchi":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 江口隆哉: 1900年1月21日","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 江口隆哉: 1977年12月25日","blurb":"He went to Wigman for two years and came back with a method for generating movement rather than a style: Work No. 1 (1934) began with his own hand at rest — wrist bent square, two fingers standing — and the sharp sensation that gave. Ohno saw the photographs and thought Japanese dance had finally become abstract. His 1951 Japanese Drum reworked an Iwate deer dance for orchestra and proscenium, and he admitted the crouch was almost impossible for Tokyo-trained bodies to hold.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 5 (Inata): Eguchi Takaya (1900-1977), kingpin of Japanese modern dance, teacher of Hijikata's first teacher Masumura; ch. 14: studied under Wigman in Germany in the early 1930s, taught Ohno; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): Eguchi Takaya studied with Mary Wigman from 1931 to 1933 and carried her German Neue Tanz to Japan, where it was the first form of modern dance to arrive and, for its perceived potency, was dubbed"},"kurtjooss":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Kurt Jooss: 12 January 1901","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Kurt Jooss: 22 May 1979","blurb":"He wanted modern dance to take the opera house rather than the gymnasium, and coined the word Tanztheater for what he made there. The Green Table (1932) took its dance of death from the Luebeck frescoes and its politics from Tucholsky's warning that the peace talks were fake; its masked Gentlemen in Black were read as Nazis, later as the Vietnam-era power elite, and Jooss said in 1976 that he still did not know who they were. In 1933 he refused to dismiss his Jewish company members and slipped into Holland; the Gestapo arrived with a warrant the next day.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006)"},"charlesweidman":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Charles Weidman: July 22, 1901","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Charles Weidman: July 15, 1975","blurb":"The pantomimic half of American modern dance's sternest decade: a Nebraskan who entered Denishawn in 1920 — Doris Humphrey was his first teacher there — and left with her to co-found the Humphrey-Weidman company and school. He devised what he called kinetic pantomime, mime dissolved into pure motion, and aimed it at comedy and social commentary alike. He formed his own Charles Weidman Dance Theatre in 1948; he died in 1975.","src":"Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"lachhumaharaj":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Lachhu Maharaj: Pandit Bajinath Prasad, also known as Pandit Lachhu Maharaj (1901-1978)","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Lachhu Maharaj: (1901-1978)","blurb":"Ten years of training under his uncle Bindadin, then a leap the family had not made: Bombay. He set kathak into the cinema's memory — Mahal, Mughal-e-Azam, Pakeezah — choreographing for the camera what his ancestors had made for the Nawab, and Sitara Devi came to him for lasya, the soft expressive register. He returned to Lucknow in the end as founding director of the state's own Kathak Kendra; the Akademi had honoured him back in 1957.","src":"Mystica Music — Lachhu Maharaj artist profile (scion of the Lucknow kathak family; trained c. 10 years under his uncle Bindadin Maharaj; choreographed Mahal, Mughal-e-Azam and Pakeezah; Sangeet Natak Akademi Award)"},"gertrudkraus":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Gertrud Kraus (גרטרוד קראוס): 5 May 1901","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Gertrud Kraus: 13 November 1977","blurb":"One of the leading choreographers at the 1930 Dancers' Congress, and one of only two on Manning's list who left primarily because they were Jewish. She emigrated from Austria to Palestine, opened a school and started a company, and is one of the foundations Israeli concert dance was built on — the German line's Tel Aviv branch.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), introduction to the new edition"},"sigurdleeder":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Sigurd Leeder: born Carl Eduard Wilhelm Leder, 14 August 1902","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Sigurd Leeder: 20 June 1981","blurb":"Jooss's partner for twenty-three years, and the one who turned Laban's eukinetics and choreutics into daily classwork. At Dartington from 1934 the two of them ran the exile school that trained Birgit Cullberg and Ann Hutchinson Guest — the route by which the Essen method reached Sweden and the notation world. He later directed the dance department at the University of Chile, and ended his teaching in the small Swiss town of Herisau.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 5 notes"},"gretpalucca":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Gret Palucca: born Margarethe Paluka, 8 January 1902","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Gret Palucca: 22 March 1993","blurb":"A member of Wigman's first dance group who opened her own Dresden school in 1925 and spent the next decade as her rival. Half-Jewish under the racial laws, she applied for and was granted a special authorization — temporary and revocable — danced the Waltz solo at the 1936 Olympic dance festival, and danced for the SS, who celebrated her as the most German of all dancers. Her permission was withdrawn in 1939; her Dresden school then trained most of East Germany's choreographers.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006)"},"haraldkreutzberg":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Harald Kreutzberg: December 11, 1902; also Karina & Kant, Hitler's Dancers (2004), Documentary Appendix — a Propaganda Ministry document names 'Harald Kreutzberg, born 11 December 1902 in Reichenberg'","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Harald Kreutzberg: April 25, 1968","blurb":"The German column's leading man, and its most awkward case: his partnership with his composer Friedrich Wilckens was an open secret, and the ministry's permit for their foreign tours names them together; his thank-you letter closes Heil Hitler. He toured through the years gay men were being sent to the camps, praised by Nazi critics for the Germanness of his dancing. At the 1936 Olympic festival he led sixty men in a Weapons Dance that ended in the hero's death by the sword. His 1934 Japan tour helped push Ohno Kazuo toward dance.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006)"},"helentamiris":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Helen Tamiris: born Helen Becker, April 23, 1902","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Helen Tamiris: August 4, 1966","blurb":"A Metropolitan Opera corps dancer turned modernist without a school: her 1928 program manifesto demanded work contemporary, sincere and American, and the Dance Repertory Theatre she helped found briefly got Graham, Humphrey and Weidman onto one stage. She danced her Negro Spirituals cycle across fourteen years, made How Long Brethren? from protest songs — it won Dance Magazine's first group-choreography award — and campaigned dance into the WPA. Broadway later took her: Annie Get Your Gun, and a Tony for Touch and Go.","src":"McPherson 2012"},"yvonnegeorgi":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Yvonne Georgi: 29 October 1903","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Yvonne Georgi: 25 January 1975","blurb":"A Wigman student who went the opposite way from her teacher: by the late twenties she was ballet mistress at the municipal theatre in Hannover, arguing at the Dancers' Congress that modern dance had to leave the physical-culture studios, take on ballet's discipline and live in the opera house. She toured duets with Harald Kreutzberg and was seen in New York. She showed at Goebbels's German Dance Festival in 1934, as most of those who stayed did.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 4 (From Modernism to Fascism)"},"isamunoguchi":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Isamu Noguchi (野口勇): November 17, 1904","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Isamu Noguchi: December 30, 1988","blurb":"For more than thirty years Martha Graham danced her fiercest myths on and around his abstract sculpture — some twenty sets, from Frontier in 1935 through Appalachian Spring and Hérodiade in 1944 to Cave of the Heart in 1946. He made scenery that refused to be background: objects the dance had to touch, climb and answer. The partnership gave American modern dance its sculptural look, and gave the sculptor a stage.","src":"Hyperallergic — 'A Tactile Tour of Isamu Noguchi and Martha Graham's 1940s Dance Sets', 2016 (sculptor whose sets for Graham ran from Frontier 1935 through Appalachian Spring 1944, Hérodiade 1944 and Cave of the Heart 1946; 'For over three decades, Martha Graham danced her most compelling choreography on and around the abstract sculpture of Isamu Noguchi'; around 20 sets)"},"rukminideviarundale":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Rukmini Devi Arundale (nee Sastri): 29 February 1904","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Rukmini Devi Arundale: 24 February 1986","blurb":"A Theosophist who married at sixteen into scandal and met Anna Pavlova on a ship; Pavlova told her to look to her own country's dance, and she did — studying under Meenakshisundaram Pillai, debuting at the Theosophical Society's Diamond Jubilee in 1935, and building Kalakshetra to teach a sadir remade as Bharatanatyam, its eros pruned toward devotion. She composed some thirty dance-dramas, declined a nomination for India's presidency, and at the end refused medicines that had been tested on animals.","src":"Kalakshetra Foundation — 'The Founder' page (born 29 Feb 1904; raised in the Theosophical Society; historic 1935 stage debut; founded Kalakshetra)"},"georgebalanchine":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, George Balanchine: born Georgiy Melitonovich Balanchivadze, January 22, 1904 [O.S. January 9]","srcd":"en.wikipedia, George Balanchine: April 30, 1983","blurb":"Invited to America in 1933, he insisted the first thing built be a school; the School of American Ballet opened on 2 January 1934 and Serenade came out of its classes that year. He made the music, not the story, the subject — some 465 works, among them Apollo, which he called the turning point of his life, Agon, Jewels, and the 1954 Nutcracker danced every Christmas since. He began losing his balance in 1978; the Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease behind it was identified only after he died.","src":"New York City Ballet, 'George Balanchine' history page (nycballet.com): School of American Ballet, first product of the Balanchine-Kirstein collaboration, founded 1934; New York City Ballet born October 11, 1948; style described as neoclassic"},"ernstuthoff":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ernst Uthoff Biefang: (1904-1993), German-born Chilean, danced for Kurt Jooss, co-founded the Chilean National Ballet 1945","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Ernst Uthoff Biefang: (1904-1993)","blurb":"A Ballets Jooss soloist whose company was touring South America when the war closed Europe behind it. Chilean composers hired him, his wife Lola Botka and Rudolf Pescht; on 7 October 1941 they opened a dance school inside the University of Chile, and by 1945 it had a company, which he then directed for some twenty-three years. His ballet-oratorio on Orff's Carmina Burana was the piece that carried Chilean dance abroad.","src":"Wikipedia (es), Ballet Nacional Chileno — https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballet_Nacional_Chileno (1940-41 Jooss tour, Uthoff/Botka/Pescht hired by Carvajal and Santa Cruz, school opened 7 Oct 1941 at the Universidad de Chile, company debut 18 May 1945 with Coppelia, ~23 years' direction, Carmina Burana, 1984 National Art Prize); also Wikipedia, Chilean National Ballet"},"frederickashton":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Frederick Ashton: Sir Frederick William Mallandaine Ashton, 17 September 1904","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Frederick Ashton: 18 August 1988","blurb":"He saw Anna Pavlova dance in Lima in 1917, as a boy, and decided immediately that this was the job. Massine took him as a pupil, Rambert pushed him into choreographing, Craske gave him his Cecchetti; from 1935 he was de Valois's resident choreographer and from 1963 ran the company himself. Into piece after piece he folded the same small sequence, built from a step of Pavlova's — dancers call it the Fred step.","src":"Kavanagh 1996"},"laquica":{"srcb":"EcuRed, La Quica: 'Nombre: Francisca Gonzalez Martinez / Nacimiento: 1905, Sevilla, Espana / Fallecimiento: 1967, Madrid' — La Quica was a woman, not the male dancer the key had been read as; corroborated by Flamencas por Derecho, 'La Quica, maestria y temperamento (I)': 'Francisca Gonzalez Martinez, bailaora sevillana nacida en 1905 y conocida artisticamente como La Quica', which also places her academy at Plaza General Vara del Rey 5, the address this map already held","srcd":"EcuRed, La Quica: 'Estuvo dedicada a la ensenanza en la academia de Frasquillo desde 1932, hasta su muerte en 1967. Muere en Madrid en 1967' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A Madrid dance teacher who survives almost entirely in her students' biographies: El Güito was four years old when she began teaching him at the Plaza General Vara del Rey, and Emma Maleras and Ciro Diezhandino both name her among their masters. Of her own dancing the accessible record says nearly nothing — she is documented by what walked out of her studio, which was much of postwar Madrid's flamenco.","src":"ExpoFlamenco"},"mayodonnell":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, May O'Donnell: May 1, 1906, born in Sacramento, California","srcd":"en.wikipedia, May O'Donnell: February 1, 2004","blurb":"Graham's company held her twice — as a member through the 1930s, then as a guest from 1944, when she created the Pioneering Woman in Appalachian Spring. On her own she built spare concert works — Suspension in 1943, later the Dance Sonatas and Dance Energies — and at sixty-eight formed the May O'Donnell Dance Company to keep making them.","src":"O'Donnell-Green Music and Dance Foundation, 'May O'Donnell' (odonnellgreen.com): (1906-2004), Martha Graham Dance Company member 1932-1938, guest artist from 1944, created the Pioneering Woman in Appalachian Spring (1944); May O'Donnell Dance Company formed 1974"},"eleanorking":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Eleanor King: Eleanor Campbell King (1906-1991), member of the original Humphrey-Weidman company","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Eleanor King: (1906-1991)","blurb":"A Humphrey-Weidman dancer from the company's first years (1928–1935) who left the founders' shadow by way of the museum: her first large work, Icaro, premiered at the Brooklyn Museum's Dance Center in May 1937. She then carried modern dance away from New York for good — the Cornish School in Seattle, then twenty years at the University of Arkansas — one of the teachers who made the art national rather than a New York possession.","src":"NYPL Jerome Robbins Dance Division — Eleanor King papers finding aid, archives.nypl.org/dan/19776 (born Middleton, PA 8 Feb 1906, died 27 Feb 1991; trained at Denishawn from 1927; Humphrey-Weidman Company 1928-1935; first large-scale work Icaro premiered at the Brooklyn Museum Dance Center, May 1937; later Cornish School, Seattle and University of Arkansas 1951-1971)"},"lesterhorton":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Lester Horton: Lester Iradell Horton, January 23, 1906","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Lester Horton: November 2, 1953","blurb":"He built a complete modern-dance world in Los Angeles while the field looked the other way: a technique drawn from anatomical study and Native American dance, and, from 1946, the Dance Theater — a permanent house where school, stage and a racially integrated company shared one roof, among the first in America to insist on it. He had come west in 1927 to dance in a Hiawatha pageant and never left. Dead at forty-seven in 1953, he survives in the bodies he trained: Ailey, de Lavallade, Truitte, Lewitzky.","src":"Alvin Ailey Dance Foundation — Lester Horton profile, ailey.org/people/lester-horton (established the Lester Horton Dance Theater in Los Angeles in 1946, one of the first permanent theaters dedicated to modern dance; among the first US choreographers to insist on racial integration; technique built on Native American dances and anatomical studies; died 1953; Ailey, Carmen de Lavallade and James Truitte danced with him)"},"kazuoohno":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Kazuo Ohno (大野一雄): October 27, 1906","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Kazuo Ohno: June 1, 2010","blurb":"From the last row of the third balcony he watched the Spanish dancer La Argentina, and her magic hit him, he said, like a lightning bolt; fifty years later, at seventy-one, he returned to the stage to dance her. In between came a gymnastics post at a Baptist girls' school, the war in Manchuria and New Guinea, and his mother's last words — a flatfish swims in my body — out of which he built an entire cosmology of the womb. He taught no technique: some evenings the Kamihoshikawa class passed in silence and ended with him cooking a meal.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 13 (Schwellinger): born October 27, 1906 in Hakodate, died 2010; Ishii Baku studio 1933, Eguchi/Miya studio from 1936; own studio and students in Kamihoshikawa; core participant in Hijikata's 1960-1968 experiments per the Introduction; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born 1906; in his own six-page vita he records seeing Mary Wigman's student Harald Kreutzberg dance in 1934, which is what moved him to study with Eguchi Takaya and Eguchi's wife Miya Misako; studied with Ishii Baku in 1933 and with Eguchi from 1936; premiered the solo La Argentina Sho in 1977, directed by Hijikata, and won the Dance Critics' Circle Award for it; toured twenty-nine countries in his mid-seventies. Baird (2012): in September 1959 Ohno danced the solo role of Divine in Forbidden Colors 2, dressed as a down-and-out male prostitute after Genet's Our Lady of the Flowers — the role that became the prototype for Divinariana (1960) and, much later, for Admiring La Argentina; in 1949, while dancing with Eguchi and Miya, he met the physical education instructor Noguchi Michizo"},"trudakaschmann":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority n93012085, heading 'Kaschmann, Truda Fackenheim, 1906-1986', sourced to NUCMC data from the Connecticut Historical Society for the Hartford Conservatory records — the Hartford provenance matches her teaching there","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority n93012085: 'Truda Fackenheim Kaschmann; 1906-1986; involved with modern dance and ballet'","blurb":"She studied with both Laban and Wigman in the mid-twenties and could compare them from inside: Laban explained far more than Wigman ever did — perhaps he explained too much, she said. After emigrating to Hartford she became Alwin Nikolais's first modern dance teacher, and in 1939 the two of them took the commission that produced his first work, Eight Column Line.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 3 (Mask and Gemeinschaft)"},"bessieschonberg":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Bessie Schonberg: December 27, 1906","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Bessie Schonberg: May 14, 1997","blurb":"A knee injury in 1931 ended her dancing in Graham's early company — Heretic, Primitive Mysteries — and gave American dance its great composition teacher. From 1938 to 1975 she directed theater and dance at Sarah Lawrence College, mentoring a lineage that runs from Lucinda Childs to Meredith Monk; her monument is other people's dances, and the field said so by naming its New York awards, the Bessies, after her. She was scheduled to teach at Juilliard the day she died, at ninety.","src":"Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 3"},"trudepohl":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority GND 1209906325, 'Pohl, Trude, 1907-1975', life dates XX.XX.1907-07.09.1975, occupations Tanzerin/Tanzlehrerin, affiliation Folkwang Universitat der Kunste — Institut fur Zeitgenossischen Tanz, Wirkungsort Essen, source Deutsches Tanzarchiv Koln. The day comes from the Deutsche Digitale Bibliothek record for her denazification file at Landesarchiv NRW, titled 'Entnazifizierung Trude Pohl, geb. 21.02.1907 (Ballettmeisterin)', signature NW 1005-G","srcd":"GND 1209906325: died 07.09.1975 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A Jooss student who took over the Folkwang dance department in 1935, after Albrecht Knust had lasted a year, and held it until Jooss came back from exile in 1949. The school kept working through the Nazi period with the state's support. What she actually taught in those fourteen years — how much of the Laban syllabus survived intact — is the open gap in this stretch of the map.","src":"Folkwang Universitaet der Kuenste, Institut fuer Zeitgenoessischen Tanz, department history — https://www.folkwang-uni.de/en/home/dance/izt/history ('Trude Pohl, a student of Kurt Jooss, becomes head of the dance department' in 1935, after Knust's one-year tenure; ran the Folkwang Dance Studio through the Nazi period with state support until Jooss's 1949 return)"},"misakomiya":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 宮操子: 1907年4月15日 — but the National Diet Library authority record for 宮操子 gives 1909, and the disagreement is left standing here rather than settled by whichever page was read last; this map follows the 1907 the research pass took","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 宮操子: 2009年5月7日","blurb":"Eguchi's partner in the two years with Wigman and in the duets that came out of it — in Work No. 1 the two dancers were identical but for colour, one silver, one gold. Their Tokyo institute trained Ohno Kazuo, Kasai Akira and Hijikata's first teacher, which makes her a node the histories tend to name second. In 1995 she published a book on the dance troupe the army ministry sent to the front, which she and Eguchi had been part of.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 14 (Elswit et al.): went with Eguchi to study under Wigman in the early 1930s; Eguchi-Miya Dance Institute taught Ohno and Kasai; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): Miya Misako was Eguchi Takaya's wife, and it was to Eguchi and Miya together that Ohno Kazuo went to study after seeing Harald Kreutzberg dance in 1934"},"aleksandrpushkin":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority n97094020, heading 'Pushkin, Aleksandr (Aleksandr Ivanovich), 1907-1970', citing Koegler's Concise Oxford Dictionary of Ballet — 'b. Mikulino, 9/7/1907... Russian dancer and ballet teacher'. ru.wikipedia agrees: '25 августа [7 сентября] 1907, Микулино'. The poet is a separate heading","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority n97094020, citing Koegler: 'd. Leningrad, 3/20/1970'; ru.wikipedia: '20 марта 1970, Ленинград'","blurb":"He taught the men's classes at the Leningrad school for decades and produced, out of one studio, both Rudolf Nureyev and Mikhail Baryshnikov — along with Nikita Dolgushin, Oleg Vinogradov and Askold Makarov. Accounts of Baryshnikov's training call him the Vaganova's best-known teacher and describe him becoming a surrogate father to the boy. He died in Leningrad in 1970, aged sixty-two.","src":"EBSCO Research Starters, 'Mikhail Baryshnikov': Pushkin, 'the Vaganova's best-known teacher', mentored Baryshnikov at the Vaganova school and became a surrogate father to him"},"ednaguy":{"srcb":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 3 —","srcd":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 3 —","blurb":"She wrote to Ruth St. Denis at fifteen, signing 'Edna Guy, Colored Girl,' and got six years of Denishawn — classes, then tours as wardrobe mistress, never a place in the company. After a quarrel with the costume designer in 1930 St. Denis asked her to leave, and she kept herself as a maid, a cook and an artist's model. So she produced what she could not join: the First Negro Dance Recital with Winfield in 1931, and with Alison Burroughs the 1937 Negro Dance Evening that carried Dunham into New York.","src":"Manning, Modern Dance, Negro Dance (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search"},"hemsleywinfield":{"srcb":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 2 —","srcd":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 2 — after a","blurb":"An actor first: he took over Salome when his actress missed a performance, kept the role, and found he liked the solos. On 29 April 1931 he and Edna Guy packed the two-hundred-seat Theatre in the Clouds, fifty floors up the Chanin Building, with the First Negro Dance Recital in America; in 1933 his company danced The Emperor Jones at the Met, the first dance company engaged there since Pavlova. Asked at a Harlem forum what the Negro should dance about, he answered: 'What has anyone to dance about?'","src":"Manning, Modern Dance, Negro Dance (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search: Winfield directed"},"antonytudor":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Antony Tudor: born William Cook, 4 April 1908","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Antony Tudor: 19 April 1987","blurb":"He asked for advice in a ballet bookshop on Charing Cross Road, was pointed at Marie Rambert, and within a decade had made a new kind of ballet — about what people are keeping to themselves. Jardin aux lilas, Dark Elegies, then Pillar of Fire in New York, where he choreographed for Ballet Theatre for ten years. After 1950 he mostly taught, at the Met's ballet school and Juilliard, telling dancers to lose their mannerisms and find the character. A Zen Buddhist, he died on Easter Sunday 1987.","src":"Antony Tudor Ballet Trust, 'About Antony Tudor' (antonytudor.org): master of psychological ballet (Pillar of Fire, 1942); director of the Metropolitan Opera Ballet company and school off and on 1950-1963; student works for the Juilliard School from 1953"},"joselimon":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jose Limon: Jose Arcadio Limon, January 12, 1908","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Jose Limon: December 2, 1972","blurb":"A Mexican-born painting student who saw a visiting German expressionist soloist perform in 1929 and enrolled at the Humphrey-Weidman school instead. When he founded his own company in 1946 he hired his teacher: Doris Humphrey became its artistic director, the first in modern dance who was not the founder. The Moor's Pavane (1949) compressed Othello into four dancers and one court dance and won the Dance Magazine Award; from 1951 he taught at Juilliard and left a technique built on weight, fall and recovery.","src":"Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1"},"birgitcullberg":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Birgit Cullberg: Birgit Ragnhild Cullberg, 3 August 1908, born in Nykoping","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Birgit Cullberg: 8 September 1999","blurb":"Swedish, and trained at the Dartington school Jooss and Leeder ran in exile — the route by which the Essen method reached Scandinavia. Miss Julie (1950), after Strindberg, made her name by putting a play's worth of motive into a ballet. She founded the Cullberg Ballet out of that work, directed it until 1985, and handed it to her son Mats Ek.","src":"Wikipedia, Birgit Cullberg — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birgit_Cullberg (1908-1999, Jooss-Leeder training, Miss Julie 1950, Cullberg Ballet, retirement 1985, handover to Mats Ek); Dartington link corroborated by Wikipedia, Sigurd Leeder"},"kenjihinoki":{"srcb":"Kotobank, 新撰 芸能人物事典 明治〜平成, 檜健次 (ヒノキ ケンジ): 本名 檜堅二, 生年月日 明治41年 (1908), 出生地 徳島県徳島市, 大阪音楽学校卒","srcd":"Kotobank, 新撰 芸能人物事典, 檜健次: 没年月日 昭和58年12月2日 (1983年)","blurb":"One of Japan's second-generation modern dancers: in the late 1930s he crossed the Pacific to study with Ruth St. Denis and Adolph Bolm, carrying American modern dance home just before the war closed the route. In occupied Japan he taught creative dance at the Kamakura Academia, and his line reached forward to Kei Takei, who carried it back to New York. The record on him is thin; he survives mostly in the biographies of his students.","src":"Modernity in Japanese Dance"},"erickhawkins":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Erick Hawkins: Frederick 'Erick' Hawkins, April 23, 1909","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Erick Hawkins: November 23, 1994","blurb":"A Harvard classicist who saw Kreutzberg dance and changed careers: four years at the new School of American Ballet, then Balanchine's American Ballet and Lincoln Kirstein's Ballet Caravan, which performed his first piece in 1937. In 1938 he became the first man to dance with Martha Graham's company; he married her in 1948 and left in 1951. What he built afterwards was the reverse of contraction — movement derived from kinesiology, released rather than driven. 'The body is a clear place.'","src":"Dance Teacher, 'Erick Hawkins: Martha Graham's First Male Company Member and the Creator of the Hawkins Technique' (dance-teacher.com): four years at the newly founded School of American Ballet; first male dancer accepted into Graham's company"},"letitiaide":{"srcb":"Gale's Dictionary of Women Worldwide, entry 'Ide, Letitia (1909-1993)': 'American modern dancer. Name variations: Letitia Ide Ratner. Born June 2, 1909, in Springfield, IL... trained at Humphrey-Weidman studio in NY; m. Victor M. Ratner'. Library of Congress name authority n97858467 agrees on the person","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority n97858467, editorial note: 'Dancer with Humphrey-Weidman and Limon companies. Died in Hastings-on-Hudson, NY of cancer, Aug. 29, 1993, aged 84', sourced to Dance Magazine, November 1993, p. 116 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A Humphrey-Weidman dancer out of Springfield, Illinois, trained at the founders' studio and seasoned in the Concert Group's touring years. Two decades on, José Limón called her back as the mature female presence of his early-fifties repertory — The Exiles in 1950, then The Visitation and The Queen's Epicedium in 1952 — a performing life with a second act where most of her generation had none.","src":"Encyclopedia.com — 'Ide, Letitia (1909-1993)', Women in World History (born Springfield, IL 2 June 1909, died Hastings-on-Hudson 29 Aug 1993; trained at the Humphrey-Weidman studio and performed with the Humphrey-Weidman Concert Group; later danced in Limón's The Exiles 1950, The Visitation 1952 and The Queen's Epicedium 1952)"},"evegentry":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Eve Gentry: August 21, 1909, nee Henrietta Greenhood","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Eve Gentry: June 17, 1994","blurb":"A principal in Hanya Holm's company until 1942 — her signature solo, Tenant of the Street, premiered in 1938 — she went on to teach and serve on the board of the New Dance Group and to run a company of her own from 1944 to 1968. Then she codified a second discipline entirely: at eighty-two, in 1991, she co-founded the Institute for the Pilates Method, ending her working life as a founder twice over.","src":"University of Washington Dance Program — Eve Gentry profile, dance.washington.edu/people/eve-gentry (born Los Angeles 1909; principal performer with Hanya Holm's company until 1942; signature solo Tenant of the Street premiered 1938; board member and teacher for the New Dance Group; own company 1944-1968; co-founded the Institute for the Pilates Method, 1991)"},"polanirenska":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Pola Nirenska: born Pola Nirensztajn, 28 July 1910","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Pola Nirenska: 25 July 1992","blurb":"One of Mary Wigman's most prominent Dresden students until Wigman, home from their 1932 American tour, dismissed her Jewish dancers. Nirenska fled through Warsaw, Vienna, and London and settled in Washington as the city's leading modern dancer; more than seventy-five of her relatives were murdered in the Holocaust. Her answer came late — the 1980s tetralogy In Memory of Those I Loved... Who Are No More, completed for her eightieth birthday. Her husband Jan Karski bought a ticket to watch.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006)"},"annasokolow":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Anna Sokolow: February 9, 1910","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Anna Sokolow: March 29, 2000","blurb":"Trained at the Neighborhood Playhouse under Graham and Louis Horst and a Graham company member through the thirties, she turned the technique toward the city's shut-in lives: Rooms, her study of urban alienation, became the first modern dance work presented on national television. She planted the art abroad twice over, working in Mexico from 1939 and in Israel from 1953, and taught at Juilliard for decades.","src":"Sokolow Theatre/Dance Ensemble — Anna Sokolow biography, sokolowtheatredance.org (1910-2000; trained at the Neighborhood Playhouse with Martha Graham and Louis Horst; member of the Graham company in the 1930s; commentaries on humanity and social justice; Rooms the first modern dance work presented on national television; longtime Juilliard faculty; worked in Mexico from 1939 and Israel from 1953)"},"alwinnikolais":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Alwin Nikolais: November 25, 1910","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Alwin Nikolais: May 8, 1993","blurb":"Hanya Holm's student and assistant who concluded the dancer need not be the center of anything: his principle of decentralization made motion, light and shape the performers, with the human body one instrument among them. For twenty-two years from 1948 he ran the Henry Street Playhouse on the Lower East Side as a total-theatre laboratory. The Wigman line reached him through Holm and Truda Kaschmann; he took its space-thinking to the logical extreme, and lies buried in Père Lachaise.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 7 (Mary Wigman and American Dance)"},"louisekloepper":{"srcb":"Left as it is, and the paragraph calling her \"a Seattle girl\" is flagged rather than rewritten: the origins pass found her born in Washington, D.C. and raised in Tacoma, with Seattle the place she trained. A soft finding on a phrase rather than a field","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Louise Kloepper: December 15, 1996","blurb":"A Seattle girl who finished her dance training in Dresden and could describe exactly what was different there: in technique class you followed the teacher, but in the class lesson the group watched one student improvise and then criticised it. She joined Hanya Holm in New York for the second season and stayed ten years, and it was the two of them who added the warm-up, the turnout and the repetitions that made Wigman's method legible to Americans who wanted technique.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 7 (Mary Wigman and American Dance)"},"katherinedunham":{"srcb":"Britannica, Katherine Dunham: born June 22, 1909, Glen Ellyn, Illinois — and left at 1909 deliberately against a contradicting book this map also cites. Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 6, states","srcd":"Britannica, Katherine Dunham: died May 21, 2006, New York","blurb":"Trained in anthropology at Chicago and initiated into Vodou during her 1935-36 Caribbean fieldwork, she built a company, a technique, and a school out of what she had seen there. She premiered Southland, a lynching drama, in Chile in 1950; the State Department suppressed it and withheld support, and she toured six continents anyway, keeping no permanent US address. At fifty-seven she began again in East St. Louis, running its schools and museum for over thirty years.","src":"Manning, Modern Dance, Negro Dance (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search: Dunham led"},"shambhumaharaj":{"srcb":"Left at 1910, from en.wikipedia, and disputed three ways. Sahapedia, in Ashish Mohan Khokar's article, reports that the Padma and Sangeet Natak Akademi citations give 1912, while Kathak Kendra held his centenary in 2004, which implies 1904 — a year his nephew Munnalal Shukla also backs. Nobody has read the Akademi citation itself, which would settle it, so the map keeps the year it had and the disagreement is written down rather than hidden","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Shambhu Maharaj: 4 November 1970","blurb":"Bindadin tied the ganda on him at eight; at thirteen he was learning thumri from Ustad Rahimuddin Khan, at fourteen dancing beside his brother Achhan at the Rampur court — a singer's schooling that made the sung poem his instrument. From 1952 he headed dance at Delhi's Bharatiya Kala Kendra, where Birju Maharaj, Kumudini Lakhia, Maya Rao and Uma Sharma all passed through his hands; throat cancer took him in 1970.","src":"Sahapedia — Shambhu Maharaj profile (Lucknow gharana's foremost proponent of bhava; trained by Bindadin Maharaj and elder brother Achhan Maharaj; died Delhi, 4 Nov 1970)"},"nobutoshitsuda":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 津田信敏: 1910年1月16日","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 津田信敏: 1984年1月4日","blurb":"One of the dancers who carried German neuer Tanz into Japan, and the teacher in whose Tokyo studio Hijikata was working when he made Forbidden Colors in 1959 — the programme still called him a dancer of the Tsuda Nobutoshi Dance Studio. Mishima visited that studio and was asked to supply titles for improvisation exercises; he offered Dali's Melting Clock and The Marquis de Sade, and thought the second one a mistake.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), Introduction: one of Hijikata's Tokyo teachers; ch. 3 (Mishima): Hijikata presented Kinjiki as a dancer of the Tsuda Nobutoshi Dance Studio; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): Tsuda Nobutoshi had studied with the German expressionist dancer Max Terpis; he was striving for independence from the modern dance world and wanted to create a new genre of figurative art that would use the healthy body as its material and be neither dance, theatre, painting nor literature; his dance studio was in Meguro and later became Asbestos Hall, and the surviving studio photographs of Forbidden Colors were taken there in the summer of 1959; after the 1959 recital he left the All Japan Art Dance Association alongside Hijikata, Motofuji and Ohno"},"lolabotka":{"blurb":"A Ballets Jooss dancer who did not go home: when the company's 1940-41 South American tour left it stranded, she, her husband Ernst Uthoff and Rudolf Pescht were hired to teach in Santiago. The school they opened inside the University of Chile in October 1941 grew into the Ballet Nacional Chileno, and the Essen training became the base of Chilean concert dance.","src":"Wikipedia (es), Ballet Nacional Chileno — https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballet_Nacional_Chileno (Botka as Uthoff's spouse, one of the three Jooss dancers hired in Santiago, school founded 7 Oct 1941, company from 1945); also Wikipedia, Chilean National Ballet and Wikipedia, Ernst Uthoff"},"jeanlouisbarrault":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jean-Louis Barrault: Jean-Louis Bernard Barrault, 8 September 1910","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Jean-Louis Barrault: 22 January 1994","blurb":"At Dullin's Atelier he and Decroux began the years of somatic research into corporeal expression that founded modern mime's grammar; in 1945 he played the mime Deburau in Les Enfants du Paradis, the form's most famous appearance on film. Where Decroux narrowed, Barrault widened — 'a unique combination of the mysticism of Artaud, the techniques of Decroux and Dullin's concept of sincerity', one scholar wrote — running the Compagnie Renaud-Barrault with Madeleine Renaud from 1946 and directing at the Odéon.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"vazhuvoorramiahpillai":{"srcb":"The Vazhuvoor family site, reproducing a Sruti magazine profile: 'Ramiah Pillai was born to Parthiban and Bhagyamma on 24th December 1910 in Vazhuvoor'; vazhuvoorarparampara.com gives the same day; and narthaki.com's A. Seshan, 'Vadivelu and Vazhuvurar Centenaries' (12 March 2010), describes months of chasing it — 'Eventually it was found to be December 24, 1910'","srcd":"Contested. For 1994, which this map keeps: Ashish Khokar, Bharatanatyam (2002), 'Guru Ramiah Pillai (1910-94) hailed from Vazhuvoor, near Mayuvaram', and the Bibliotheque de la danse Vincent-Warren name authority, 'RAMIAH PILLAI, 1910-1994'. Against it, en.wikipedia gives 1991 — but that article carries a citations-needed banner and cites nothing for the date, and neither The Hindu's 'Remembering Vazhuvoor Ramaiah Pillai' nor Sruti's interview states a death year. ta.wikipedia's 1979 is refuted by the Sruti profile, conducted with him alive in 1986","blurb":"Heir through his maternal uncle to the nattuvangam of Vazhuvoor village, he turned a local bani into a school of stars — Kamala, Vyjayanthimala, the Padmini sisters, Padma Subrahmanyam — and its grace-forward, theatrically vivid style travelled through those students deep into Tamil cinema. His politics moved the same way: during the independence struggle his student productions performed Bharathiyar's banned songs. The Akademi honoured him in 1966.","src":"SADI Journal 2023"},"dorehoyer":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Dore Hoyer: 12 December 1911","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Dore Hoyer: 31 December 1967","blurb":"The best soloist of her generation in a country that had stopped buying solos: she took engagements in South America to fund the work, and in 1962 made Affectos Humanos, five dances on Spinoza's affects. Wigman cast her as the sacrificial virgin in the 1957 Sacre. She killed herself on New Year's Eve 1967, after a badly attended December concert and a knee injury, leaving a note saying that only in dance could she communicate; Susanne Linke rebuilt Affectos from film in 1987.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006)"},"beatriceseckler":{"srcb":"NYPL Jerome Robbins Dance Division finding aid: 1911","srcd":"NYPL Jerome Robbins Dance Division finding aid: 2002","blurb":"The dancer the thirties choreographers built on: across seven Humphrey-Weidman years she created roles in New Dance, With My Red Fires, Flickers and Lynchtown, and later originated in Anna Sokolow's Rooms. With José Limón and Dorothy Bird she toured as the trio out of which the Limón company grew. From 1948 she taught Humphrey-Weidman technique at the New Dance Group — a creator of first casts who became a keeper of the vocabulary.","src":"NYPL Jerome Robbins Dance Division — Beatrice Seckler papers finding aid, archives.nypl.org/dan/18543 (1911-2002; danced with Doris Humphrey, Charles Weidman & Company 1935-1942; created roles in New Dance, With My Red Fires, Flickers, Lynchtown and Sokolow's Rooms; toured with José Limón and Dorothy Bird as the original trio that became the José Limón Company; taught Humphrey-Weidman technique at the New Dance Group from 1948)"},"sophiemaslow":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Sophie Maslow: March 22, 1911, born in New York City","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Sophie Maslow: June 25, 2006 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"Twelve years in Martha Graham's company ran alongside a second, socially minded life: she taught at the New Dance Group, co-founded the Dudley-Maslow-Bales trio, and choreographed from American folk sources — Folksay in 1942, later The Village I Knew, remembered at her death as a vision of community as tender as any in the repertory.","src":"Village Voice, 'In Memoriam: Sophie Maslow (1911-2006)', July 4, 2006: twelve years in Martha Graham's company, New Dance Group teacher, Dudley-Maslow-Bales trio co-founder, choreographer of Folksay (1942) and The Village I Knew"},"tennesseewilliams":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Tennessee Williams: Thomas Lanier Williams III, March 26, 1911","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Tennessee Williams: February 25, 1983","blurb":"Before The Glass Menagerie made him one of the century's foremost American playwrights, he bussed tables at Valeska Gert's Beggar's Bar and was fired for refusing to pool his tips. He held no grudge: when Gert was called into Provincetown court in 1946 for throwing garbage out her window and failing to pay a dance partner, he appeared as her character witness, telling incredulous friends that he 'simply liked her.'","src":"Valeska Gert Wikipedia"},"lucashoving":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Lucas Hoving: September 5, 1912","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Lucas Hoving: January 5, 2000","blurb":"Formed in the European expressionist line — Yvonne Georgi's company, then Kurt Jooss's — he emigrated from Groningen to New York and joined José Limón's young company, creating the Iago figure opposite Limón's Moor in The Moor's Pavane. In the 1960s he directed a company of his own and choreographed Icarus — the Jooss line's man inside the American canon.","src":"Jacob's Pillow Dance Interactive — 'Lucas Hoving: Growing Up in Public' (1912-2000, born Groningen, Holland; performed with Yvonne Georgi and Kurt Jooss in the late 1930s before emigrating to New York; charter member of the Limón Company from 1949, creating roles in The Moor's Pavane; directed his own company in the 1960s, creating Icarus)"},"paulinekoner":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Pauline Koner: June 26, 1912","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Pauline Koner: February 8, 2001","blurb":"Trained by Fokine, Michio Ito, and Nimura rather than any modern-dance academy, she kept her independence on the billing itself: fourteen years a guest artist — never a member — with the Humphrey-Limón repertory, creating Emilia in The Moor's Pavane and a role in La Malinche. The Farewell (1962) honored the memory of Doris Humphrey; at sixty-three she formed a company of her own, the Koner Dance Consort.","src":"Pauline Koner papers finding aid, Jerome Robbins Dance Division, NYPL (archives.nypl.org/dan/19779): born June 26, 1912, New York; died February 8, 2001; trained with Fokine, Ito and Nimura; guest artist for the Humphrey-Limón repertory 1946-1960, creating roles in The Moor's Pavane and La Malinche; formed the Pauline Koner Dance Consort 1975"},"sybilshearer":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Sybil Shearer: Sybil Louise Shearer, February 23, 1912","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Sybil Shearer: November 17, 2005","blurb":"After Humphrey-Weidman and Agnes de Mille came the October 1941 solo debut at Carnegie Chamber Music Hall that her foundation still calls radical — and then refusal: in 1942 she left New York for Chicago to work independently, in her own unorthodox way, until her death in 2005. Hers was a six-decade argument that a dance career need not pass through New York at all.","src":"Morrison-Shearer Foundation, 'Sybil Shearer' (morrisonshearer.org/sybil): (February 23, 1912 - November 17, 2005), danced with Humphrey-Weidman and Agnes de Mille, radical solo debut at Carnegie Chamber Music Hall October 22, 1941, moved to Chicago 1942 to work independently in her own unorthodox way"},"johncage":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, John Cage: John Milton Cage Jr., September 5, 1912","srcd":"en.wikipedia, John Cage: August 12, 1992","blurb":"His 'Composition of Experimental Music' class at the New School (1956–60) seeded the Happenings; an earlier, freer class he taught at the Cunningham dancers' own request was their antidote to the prescriptive composition class the modern-dance schools were giving. In 1960 he picked his former student Robert Dunn to teach choreography at the Cunningham studio — the class that became Judson Dance Theater. What the dancers took from him was less technique than license: chance as a way of writing, and, in Dick Higgins's words, the sense 'that anything goes.'","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Ross, Anna Halprin: Experience as Dance (2007), ch. 5; also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1"},"janedudley":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jane Dudley: April 3, 1912","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Jane Dudley: September 19, 2001","blurb":"Trained by Hanya Holm at the Wigman School's New York branch, she co-founded the New Dance Group in 1932, danced as a Graham principal from 1937 to 1944, and from 1942 toured with the Dudley-Maslow-Bales Trio; her solo Harmonica Breakdown premiered in 1938. Late in life the line ran outward: artistic director of Batsheva in Tel Aviv (1968–69), then London, where she died in 2001.","src":"University of Washington Dance Program — Jane Dudley profile, dance.washington.edu/people/jane-dudley (born New York 3 Apr 1912, died London 19 Sept 2001; trained with Hanya Holm at the Mary Wigman School 1930-35; founder of the New Dance Group 1932; principal dancer with Martha Graham 1937-1944; Dudley-Maslow-Bales Trio from 1942; Harmonica Breakdown premiered 1938; artistic director of Batsheva 1968-69)"},"marcelhoste":{"blurb":"A Ghent painter, photographer and puppeteer who became best known as a mime. He founded the puppet theatre 't Maske in 1935, ran a mime school on the Lange Steenstraat, and in 1952 opened the Sabbattini pantomime theatre — named for a Renaissance stage architect, billed as the smallest theatre in Europe — which nonetheless acquired international fame and hosted Marceau and Decroux. His paintings kept returning to the same subjects: fairs, theatre and puppet play.","src":"Wikipedia (nl), 'Marcel Hoste': Ghent artist, 12 April 1912 - 17 October 1977, who painted, wrote and photographed but 'verwierf vooral faam als mimespeler'; founded the puppet theatre 't Maske in 1935, taught at his own mime school in the Lange Steenstraat, and in 1952 founded the Sabbattini pantomime theatre under Ghent's Sint-Pieterskerk, which despite being the smallest in Europe drew Marcel Marceau and Etienne Decroux"},"pilarlopez":{"srcb":"El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco, PILAR LOPEZ: 'PILAR LOPEZ JULVEZ... nacio el dia 4 de junio del ano de 1912, en San Sebastian (Guipuzcoa)'; es.wikipedia agrees exactly","srcd":"El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco: 'ha fallecido en la madrugada del 25 de marzo de 2008 en Madrid a la edad de 95 anos'; es.wikipedia agrees","blurb":"After her sister La Argentinita died in 1945, she formed her own Ballet Español and made it the seedbed of postwar Spanish dance: José Greco toured with her in the forties, Antonio Gades joined at fifteen and stayed eight years, El Güito entered at fourteen, Mario Maya danced with her from 1956. She danced la caña on film in 1952 and outlasted nearly all of her pupils' beginnings, dying at ninety-five in 2008, two years after Spain's theatre world gave her its lifetime Max Award.","src":"The Guardian 2008"},"sabicas":{"srcb":"es.wikipedia, Sabicas: 'Agustin Castellon Campos (Pamplona 16 de marzo de 1912-Nueva York, EE. UU., 14 de abril de 1990)'","srcd":"es.wikipedia, Sabicas: died New York, 14 de abril de 1990","blurb":"A Pamplona Romani prodigy who started guitar at five and was performing two years later, he left Spain in 1936 with Carmen Amaya and did not go back for thirty-one years. From exile in Mexico City and New York he did what accompanists were not supposed to do: made the flamenco guitar a solo concert instrument, with picado runs fast enough to draw praise from Chet Atkins. Paco de Lucía and Paco Peña both named him an influence; the modern concert guitar descends from his exile.","src":"Sabicas Wikipedia"},"jacksonpollock":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jackson Pollock: Paul Jackson Pollock, January 28, 1912","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Jackson Pollock: August 11, 1956","blurb":"In the lean years before Peggy Guggenheim signed him in 1943, he worked for the dancer Valeska Gert at her Beggar's Bar. The employment reads less strangely beside his method: he painted on the floor — 'more at ease... nearer, more part of the painting' — walking around and into the canvas, and on Hans Namuth's film the movements grow steadily faster and 'more dance like.' A dance atlas can claim him for that: action painting as choreography the canvas recorded.","src":"Valeska Gert Wikipedia"},"dorothybird":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority n97019538, heading 'Bird, Dorothy, 1912-1996', birth date 1912-10-17, taken from the data sheet for her own book Bird's Eye View (c1997)","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority n97019538: death date 1996-11-12, from the jacket of Bird's Eye View and the New York Times of 14 November 1996","blurb":"Sent to Seattle's Cornish School in 1930, she met the visiting Martha Graham there and followed her east, debuting with the Graham Group in Primitive Mysteries in 1931 and serving until 1937 as the company's demonstrator — the body on which the technique was shown. Then she crossed every border the field had drawn, dancing for Balanchine, de Mille, Humphrey, Tamiris, Sokolow, Limón and Robbins, concert and Broadway alike, and told it all in her memoir, Bird's Eye View.","src":"Project MUSE — Bird's Eye View: Dancing with Martha Graham and on Broadway, University of Pittsburgh Press book page (sent to the Cornish School in 1930 where she encountered Graham; debuted with the Graham Group in 1931 in Primitive Mysteries, company member and Graham's demonstrator until 1937; worked with Balanchine, de Mille, Doris Humphrey, Tamiris, Sokolow, José Limón and Robbins)"},"carmenamaya":{"srcb":"Two sources disagree on the year and agree on the day. El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco, CARMEN AMAYA: 'nacio en Barcelona el dia 2 de noviembre del ano de 1913'. Against it, es.wikipedia: 'Carmen Amaya Amaya (Barcelona, 2 de noviembre de 1918 - Bagur, Gerona, 19 de noviembre de 1963)', and Historias de Flamenco, 'Biografia de Carmen Amaya (1918-1963)', which adds 'Tan solo cuarenta y cinco anos de frenetica vida' — forty-five years is arithmetic for 1918, not 1913. This map keeps 1913 and records that the better-attested side is 1918","srcd":"es.wikipedia, Carmen Amaya: died Bagur, Gerona, 19 de noviembre de 1963; Historias de Flamenco gives the hour, 'el 19 de noviembre de 1963, a las nueve y cinco de la manana' — the death year is unanimous","blurb":"Her father carried her through Barcelona taverns from the age of four — he played, she danced, and afterward they gathered the coins off the floor. She took the fast, hammering footwork the trade reserved for its best men, danced it sometimes in high-waisted trousers, and drove it from the Somorrostro shanty to Carnegie Hall by 1941. Even her birth year is an argument — 1913 or 1918, the baptismal record burned with its church. Kidney disease killed her at forty-five.","src":"Leblon 2003"},"hanszullig":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority record GND 1126657727, Zullig, Hans: date of birth 1914-02-01, place Rorschach; occupations Tanzer, Tanzlehrer, Ballettmeister, Choreograf, with the note that he was one of the most influential teachers at the Folkwang Hochschule in Essen","srcd":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek GND 1126657727: date of death 1992-11-08, place Essen","blurb":"Swiss, trained at Folkwang by Jooss and Leeder, and in the company at eighteen — he went into exile in Devon with it and stayed until 1947. Back in Essen he ran the dance department from 1969 and taught both Reinhild Hoffmann and Susanne Linke, then spent nearly twenty years as training director at Pina Bausch's Wuppertal company. The school's new dance building carries his name.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 6 (From Ausdruckstanz to Tanztheater)"},"ninafonaroff":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Nina Fonaroff: 3 March 1914, early member of the Martha Graham Dance Company","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Nina Fonaroff: 14 August 2003 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A Graham soloist for eleven years — she danced in the premieres of Letter to the World and Appalachian Spring — and, overlapping that, thirteen years as Louis Horst's assistant in his composition courses. Her own small repertory ran from Of Tragic Gesture to Mr. Puppet, made in 1949 for the ballet stars Alicia Markova and Anton Dolin; she died in London in 2003.","src":"Encyclopedia.com, 'Fonaroff, Nina (1914-2003)': born March 3, 1914, New York; died August 14, 2003, London; soloist in Martha Graham's company 1936-47, danced premieres of Letter to the World and Appalachian Spring; assistant to Louis Horst 1937-50"},"batshevaderothschild":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Bethsabee de Rothschild: 23 September 1914; assumed the name Batsheva after immigrating to Israel in 1951","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Bethsabee de Rothschild: 20 April 1999","blurb":"A London-born Rothschild who studied at Martha Graham's school and became, instead of a dancer, the art's banker and architect: patron of Graham's company, then founder of its Israeli sister. She conceived a new company in 1963, brought Graham over as artistic consultant, and established Batsheva in 1964; four years later she added a second school and company, Bat-Dor, alongside it.","src":"Batsheva Dance Company — 'Batsheva de Rothschild', batsheva.co.il (born London 1914, died 1999; studied dance with Martha Graham and became her company's patron; conceived a new Israeli dance company in 1963 and invited Graham as artistic consultant; established Batsheva Dance Company 1964 and the Bat-Dor school and company from 1968)"},"eileenkramer":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Eileen Kramer: 8 November 1914, Australian; joined the Bodenwieser Ballet after seeing it in 1940","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Eileen Kramer: 15 November 2024","blurb":"She saw the Bodenwieser company dance in 1940, auditioned, and got a decade of touring out of it; then India, Paris as an artist's model, and eighteen New York years nursing a filmmaker after his stroke. She came back to Australia at ninety-nine because she missed the kookaburras and the smell of gum trees, and went on making dances and films until she died at 110 — the last person alive who had worked in Bodenwieser's Sydney studio.","src":"Wikipedia, Eileen Kramer — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eileen_Kramer (1914-2024, saw the Bodenwieser Ballet in 1940 and auditioned, toured c. 1943-1953, India/Paris/New York years, return to Australia at 99, work continuing past 110); also michellepotter.org, Bodenwieser Ballet tag"},"bonniebird":{"srcb":"York University Libraries, Clara Thomas Archives and Special Collections, authority record 'Bird, Bonnie', dates of existence 1914-1995, parallel names Bird Gundlach, Bonnie / Gundlach, Bonnie, with a history quoted from the New York Times obituary of 13 April 1995","srcd":"York University Libraries authority record 'Bird, Bonnie': 1914-1995, sourced to the New York Times obituary of 13 April 1995, headed 'Bonnie Bird, Graham Protegee and Noted Dance Teacher, 80'","blurb":"A Graham company dancer of the thirties who mattered most as a hinge: heading dance at Seattle's Cornish School from 1937, she taught the young Merce Cunningham and Syvilla Fort and hired an unknown John Cage as accompanist — his first prepared-piano piece was written there. Four decades later she did it again for another country, building Britain's first BA in dance theatre at London's Laban Centre, which named its theatre for her.","src":"Bell-Kanner 1998"},"michizonoguchi":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 野口三千三: 1914年〈大正3年〉11月16日, 野口体操の創始者, 東京芸術大学名誉教授","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 野口三千三: 1998年〈平成10年〉3月29日","blurb":"A dancer and physical education instructor whose taiso starts from the claim that the body is fundamentally liquid: movement ripples through it, and stiffness is what catches the ripple. The programme is to hang the weight from the vertical bones and then remove force, taught through imagery exercises with names like Slither Roll and Buttocks Walk. He came to Asbestos Hall to teach, and Hijikata then inverted him — muscles held artificially contracted, weight shifted off the core — which is why butoh looks nothing like the exercises behind it.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 20 (Aihara): Noguchi Michizo (1914-1998), founder of Noguchi Taiso; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): Noguchi Michizo (1914-1998), a dancer and physical education instructor and the founder of Noguchi Exercises (Noguchi taiso); Ohno Kazuo met him in 1949 while dancing with Eguchi and Miya, and Maro Akaji and Kara Juro met him sometime after 1965, after which he came to Asbestos Hall to teach movement classes; he held that bodies are fundamentally liquid and interconnected, that inertia carries movement from one part into the next, and that overuse renders a faculty insensate; his programme was to hang the weight from the vertical bones and then remove stiffness, and his terms are chikara wo nuku (removing force) and hogusu (loosening), taught through imagery exercises with names like Slither Roll, Buttocks Walk and"},"mitsukoando":{"blurb":"Eguchi's student, and the teacher who ran programmes in two halves: serious dances on social subjects, and jazz. Ohno Kazuo took the artistic pieces and Hijikata the jazz ones — and it was at her 1954 recital, where Hijikata debuted as a jazz dancer, that butoh's founding pair met. The next year he played the bartender and Ohno the customer in her Cocktail.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), chs. 5 (Inata) and 14: Eguchi's student, known for jazz-music work; Hijikata danced in her troupe in the 1950s, and Ohno and Hijikata met at her 1954 recital (ch. 1, Kuniyoshi); also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): Hijikata was in the Ando Mitsuko Dance Studio from 1953 to 1958 and appeared in the"},"maryanthony":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority nr98030883, 'Anthony, Mary': birth date 1916-11-11, birth place Newport (Ky.), citing the New York Times obituary published 5 June 2014","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority nr98030883: death date 2014-05-31, citing the New York Times obituary of 5 June 2014 — 'd. Saturday, Manhattan, aged 97'","blurb":"From Newport, Kentucky, to a scholarship at Hanya Holm's school: she danced in Holm's company from 1943 to 1949, took Martha Graham's classes besides, and in 1956 founded the company she would teach from for decades. Threnody, drawn from Synge's Riders to the Sea, became her most acclaimed dance. She died in 2014 at ninety-seven, remembered as dancer, choreographer, and teacher at once.","src":"Boston Globe, 'Mary Anthony, at 97; acclaimed modern dancer, choreographer, teacher', June 8, 2014: born 1916, Newport, Kentucky; scholarship at Hanya Holm's school, danced in the Holm company 1943-1949, studied with Martha Graham 1946, founded her company in 1956"},"rayharrison":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority n2009061261, heading 'Harrison, Ray, 1916-1981', sources: the DLC copy of the Little Mary Sunshine 1959 theater program ('staged and choreographed by Ray Harrison'), the Internet Broadway Database, the New York Times obituary of 14 August 1981 ('Ray Harrison, Broadway dancer, choreographer and dance teacher, died on July 27 in Boston. He was 64 years old'), and the Social Security Death Index ('Born: 7 November 1916; last residence Boston'). Not the English footballer Raymond William Harrison","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority n2009061261, citing the New York Times obituary of 14 August 1981: 'Ray Harrison... died on July 27 in Boston. He was 64 years old', and the Social Security Death Index, 'Died: July 1981' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"One of Hanya Holm's dancers on Broadway: Sorell's survey records him as leading male dancer of the musical Holm choreographed in 1950 — Cole Porter's Out of This World, where the season's Theatre World annual lists him — and he had already survived Allegro's 1947 tryout, carried from the stage with torn knee ligaments. By 1959 he had crossed over, directing and choreographing the off-Broadway Little Mary Sunshine. Beyond the playbills, the record on him runs thin.","src":"Walter Sorell, The Dance Has Many Faces (via Open Library full-text search): the Hanya Holm chapter names Ray Harrison among her dancers (with Valerie Bettis, Marc Breaux, Elizabeth Harris, Nancy McKnight Hauser, Dorothy Jarnac, Murray Louis) and records Holm 'choreographing a Broadway musical with Ray Harrison as leading male dancer' in 1950; Daniel Blum, Theatre World season 1950-51 (via Open Library): Harrison listed in the Cole Porter musical Out of This World (book by Dwight Taylor and Reginald Lawrence, choreography by Hanya Holm, designs by Lemuel Ayers); Stanley Green and Kay Green, Broadway Musicals Show by Show (via Open Library): Harrison co-director (with Rick Besoyan) and choreographer of Little Mary Sunshine; Wikipedia, 'Allegro (musical)': dancer Ray Harrison tore knee ligaments in the 1947 tryout and was carried from the stage; Wikipedia, 'Little Mary Sunshine': the 1959 Orpheum Theatre production was directed and choreographed by Ray Harrison. No source found for his birth year; the atlas's 1915 stays inferred."},"bellalewitzky":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Bella Lewitzky: Bella Rebecca Lewitzky, January 13, 1916","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Bella Lewitzky: July 16, 2004 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"Lester Horton's chief dancer and co-conspirator — he built his technique, a colleague said, 'on her body. Specifically' — and with him a founder of Los Angeles's Dance Theater in 1946. Called before HUAC in 1951 she refused to name names, telling reporters 'I am a dancer, not a singer,' and was blacklisted; in 1990 she refused a $72,000 NEA grant over its anti-obscenity pledge, sued, and won. Between the two refusals she ran her own company from Los Angeles for thirty-one years.","src":"Online Archive of California — Lewitzky Dance Company records finding aid, oac.cdlib.org/findaid/ark:/13030/kt9q2nf7x6 (born Los Angeles 13 Jan 1916; joined Lester Horton's modern dance company in 1934, rising from dancer to lead performer; established the Dance Theater of Los Angeles with Horton in 1946; own company 1966, active 31 years; National Medal of Arts 1996; died Pasadena 16 July 2004)"},"alfredocorvino":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority no2009051111, heading 'Corvino, Alfredo, 1916-2005', citing the New York Times of 26 March 2009: 'b. 1916 in Montevideo, Uruguay'","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority no2009051111, citing the New York Times: 'd. 2005 in Manhattan'","blurb":"He arrived from Montevideo by way of the Jooss Ballet and the Ballet Russe de Monte Carlo, took his Cecchetti from Margaret Craske and Antony Tudor, and became ballet master of the Metropolitan Opera Ballet. Then he taught, for a very long time: 42 years at Juilliard, nearly twenty at the Met's school, twenty-five more at his own Dance Circle. At his death he was ballet master to Pina Bausch's Tanztheater Wuppertal.","src":"New Jersey Dance Theatre Ensemble, 'Alfredo Corvino' (njdte.org): born Montevideo, Uruguay; ballet master of the Metropolitan Opera Ballet; Juilliard faculty for 42 years; founded the Dance Circle school; credited Margaret Craske and Antony Tudor among his teachers"},"laviniawilliams":{"srcb":"NYPL Archives, Schomburg Center for Research in Black Culture, finding aid for the Lavinia Williams collection 1954-1983","srcd":"NYPL Schomburg Center finding aid, Lavinia Williams collection: '(1916-1989)'; en.wikipedia gives 19 July 1989","blurb":"A Dunham dancer who remembered Hollywood as hunger — one fifteen-cent meal a day at Father Divine's, walking past the door rather than be seen going in. In San Francisco in August 1942 she began filling notebooks with line drawings and descriptions of Dunham's daily class, which is why the technique of those years is documented at all. Invited to Haiti by its government, she founded the national school of folkloric dance; her students' word for what she brought was discipline.","src":"Das, Katherine Dunham: Dance and the African Diaspora (2017), ch. 7; also Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 6 — Williams is the reason the Dunham technique of the early 1940s is documented at all"},"marjoriemazia":{"srcb":"Jewish Women's Archive, Shalvi/Hyman Encyclopedia of Jewish Women, 'Marjorie Guthrie, October 6, 1917-1983' by Irma Commanday: 'Marjorie Guthrie was born in Atlantic City, New Jersey, on October 6, 1917... Under her professional name, Marjorie Mazia, she was featured in Graham's first company'","srcd":"Jewish Women's Archive, Shalvi/Hyman Encyclopedia: 'After her death on March 13, 1983, a series of lectures by leading scientists was established in her honor at the National Institutes of Health' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A Graham company dancer from 1935 — Primitive Mysteries, American Document, a Follower in Appalachian Spring — she became Graham's assistant for fifteen years and the first company member permitted to teach the technique outside Graham's own school. She married Woody Guthrie in 1945; after Huntington's disease killed him she founded the committee that became the Huntington's Disease Society of America, her second life's work.","src":"Marjorie Guthrie Project, 'Martha Graham Dance Company' (marjorieguthrie.com): won a 1935 scholarship to study under Martha Graham and was invited into the company; danced in Primitive Mysteries, American Document (1938), and as a Follower in Appalachian Spring (1944) alongside Fonaroff, Lang and Yuriko"},"syvillafort":{"srcb":"Gottschild, Joan Myers Brown & the Audacious Hope of the Black Ballerina (2012) — via Internet Archive full-text search","srcd":"Gottschild, Joan Myers Brown & the Audacious Hope of the Black Ballerina (2012) — via Internet Archive full-text search","blurb":"The Cornish School's first Black student, she was the dancer for whom John Cage wrote Bacchanale (1940), his first prepared-piano piece. She danced in Dunham's company, then directed the dance division of the Dunham School in New York; when she opened her own studio a block away in 1954, most of the students followed her and the school closed within a month. There she taught her 'Afro-Modern' blend for two more decades — Eartha Kitt, Marlon Brando, and James Dean among her students.","src":"Gottschild, Joan Myers Brown & the Audacious Hope of the Black Ballerina (Palgrave Macmillan, 2012) — via Internet Archive full-text search"},"davidzellmer":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority n97863649, 'Zellmer, David', sources: 'found: *MGTB Morgan, Barbara. Martha Graham 1941, p 114' and 'found: The spectator, 1999: CIP t.p. (David Zellmer) CIP data sheet (b. 04-20-18)' — 20 April 1918, from the Cataloging-in-Publication sheet for his own book; the Barbara Morgan 1941 citation ties the record to the Graham dancer. Data contributed by the Dance Heritage Coalition for the NYPL Dance Collection. The inferred 1915 was three years early","blurb":"A dancer in Martha Graham's company whose place in the record is a correspondence: Graham wrote to him throughout his World War II military service, and he kept the letters — 1942 to 1944 — for half a century. When he sold them to the New York Public Library in 1996 he supplied his own apologia and a subject index, a dancer annotating his choreographer's private voice for the archive. Of his stage career little else is documented; the letters are the monument.","src":"NYPL Jerome Robbins Dance Division — Letters from Martha Graham to David Zellmer finding aid, archives.nypl.org/dan/19744 (member of Graham's dance company; Graham wrote to him during his military service in World War II; letters 1942-1944 sold to NYPL by Zellmer, 1996)"},"annhutchinsonguest":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ann Hutchinson Guest (nee Hutchinson): 3 November 1918","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Ann Hutchinson Guest: 9 April 2022","blurb":"She learned Labanotation from Sigurd Leeder at Dartington and then spent a career on the problem of how a dance survives its dancers: the Dance Notation Bureau in New York, a survey of more than eighty competing notation systems, some twenty of them translated into Laban's. From Nijinsky's own notation she and Claudia Jeschke recovered his Faune — the score, not the version handed down through rehearsal rooms. She died in 2022, aged 103.","src":"Wikipedia, Ann Hutchinson Guest — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ann_Hutchinson_Guest (1918-2022, Dartington 1936-39 under Sigurd Leeder, Dance Notation Bureau, 80+ notation systems surveyed and ~20 translated, Nijinsky's Faune with Claudia Jeschke, Language of Dance, MBE 2021)"},"talleybeatty":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Talley Beatty: 22 December 1918, born in Cedar Grove, Louisiana","srcd":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 8 —","blurb":"At twelve he stopped to watch a class through the door of a converted carriage house on Chicago's South Side, and Katherine Dunham waved him in. Studios that took him afterward would not let him in the room — one had him follow class from the office, another taught him in a dressing room — and when Balanchine sent him to his own school he was told it did not accept Negro students. He made the refusal into a style: unrelenting speed and attack, and Mourner's Bench, one man's grief worked out along a plank.","src":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search: in the years before Ailey founded his own troupe, the companies of"},"tbalasaraswati":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Balasaraswati: Tanjore Balasaraswati, 13 May 1918","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Balasaraswati: 9 February 1984","blurb":"Veena Dhanammal's granddaughter, given an arangetram at seven in 1925, she built her art on improvised abhinaya — metaphor found in the moment of the song, not fixed in advance. While reformers scrubbed sringara from the repertoire as disreputable, she kept dancing it, holding the erotic to be spiritual rather than shameful; the establishment came around in the language of awards, Padma Bhushan to Padma Vibhushan. Her 1962 Jacob's Pillow debut opened two decades of teaching America.","src":"Hari Krishnan, 'Introduction to Bharatanatyam and T. Balasaraswati' — Jacob's Pillow Dance Interactive (hereditary devadasi-family dancer; granddaughter of Veena Dhanammal; upheld improvised abhinaya practice)"},"mercecunningham":{"srcb":"Britannica, Merce Cunningham: born April 16, 1919, Centralia, Washington, U.S.","srcd":"Britannica, Merce Cunningham: died July 26, 2009, New York, New York","blurb":"A soloist in Graham's company for six years from 1939, he left to build, with John Cage, a theater in which music and dance are made separately and share only the same stage and span — and in which chance operations, not taste, could settle a phrase's order. He made some 180 dances over seventy years; Robert Dunn's 1960 composition class, taught at his studio, seeded Judson Dance Theater.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1"},"valeriebettis":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Valerie Bettis: Valerie Elizabeth Bettis, December 1919","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Valerie Bettis: September 26, 1982","blurb":"A Houston-born soloist in Hanya Holm's company who led her own ensemble from 1941, she took modern dance where it had not yet been allowed: Virginia Sampler (1947) was the first work by a modern choreographer for a classical company, the Ballet Russe de Monte Carlo, and her Streetcar Named Desire (1952) turned the Williams play into a ballet. Her solo The Desperate Heart stayed her best-known piece; she also choreographed for Broadway and for NBC and CBS television.","src":"Handbook of Texas Online, 'Bettis, Valerie Elizabeth' (tshaonline.org): born December 19, 1919, Houston; died New York September 26, 1982; performed with the Hanya Holm and Dance Group 1937-1940; directed her own ensemble from 1941; choreographed for Broadway, NBC-TV and CBS-TV"},"pearlprimus":{"srcb":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 7 —","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Pearl Primus: October 29, 1994","blurb":"A Hunter biology graduate pressed into dance as an understudy on a federal youth project, she took a New Dance Group scholarship in 1941 as its only Black student and asked what people meant by warming up. Barney Josephson booked her into Cafe Society for ten days and kept her ten months. In 1944 she went south disguised as a migrant and picked cotton to learn what her protest dances were about; the Rosenwald Fund's last and largest grant then sent her to Africa, where Nigeria named her Omowale, child returned home.","src":"Manning, Modern Dance, Negro Dance (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search, from the chapter"},"bertramross":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority n88227071, heading 'Ross, Bertram, 1920-2003', birth place Brooklyn, citing Cohen-Stratyner's Biographical Dictionary of Dance p. 772, 'born 11/13/1920 N.Y.'. en.wikipedia gives 14 November; both give 1920","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority n88227071: death date 2003-04-20, Manhattan, citing the New York Times obituary — 'died April 20, 2003 at the age of 82... performed with the Martha Graham Dance Company from 1949-1973'","blurb":"Graham's partner through her great mythic decade: he joined the company in 1949, first danced opposite her in 1953 as Oedipus in Night Journey — son and lover in a single role — and was her principal male dancer from 1957 to 1973. He was named the company's co-director in 1966, the years when the repertory's male halves rested largely on him.","src":"NYPL Jerome Robbins Dance Division — Bertram Ross papers finding aid, archives.nypl.org/dan/19862 (born Brooklyn 13 Nov 1920, died 23 Apr 2003; joined the Graham company 1949, first partnered Graham 1953 as Oedipus in Night Journey; principal male dancer 1957-1973; named co-director 1966)"},"yuriko":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Yuriko (dancer): Yuriko Kikuchi, nee Amemiya, February 2, 1920","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Yuriko (dancer): March 8, 2022","blurb":"Born in San Jose in 1920 and incarcerated at the Gila River camp in the wartime removal of Japanese Americans, she was released in 1943 and inside Martha Graham's company by 1944 — said to be its first nonwhite dancer. She stayed with the work for the rest of a very long life, dancing Graham's roles and then preserving them as teacher and stager; she died in 2022, at 102.","src":"Pacific Citizen, 'Choreographer Yuriko Kikuchi Dies' (2022): born 1920, San Jose; incarcerated at the Gila River War Relocation Center, released 1943; joined the Martha Graham company in 1944, said to be its first nonwhite dancer; died March 8, 2022 at 102"},"marioncuyjet":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Marion Cuyjet: Marion Durham Cuyjet, July 29, 1920","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Marion Cuyjet: October 22, 1996","blurb":"Essie Marie Dorsey's scholarship student, she was fair enough to train unremarked in Philadelphia's Littlefield Ballet, which did not realize she was Black until her friends came to visit her at the studio. Cuyjet attributed her exclusion to body type — she was tall and not supple — an account her biographer found surprising. In 1948 she split from Sydney King and opened the Judimar School, running it twenty-three years and giving Black children the training the city's studios refused them.","src":"Gottschild, Joan Myers Brown & the Audacious Hope of the Black Ballerina (Palgrave Macmillan, 2012) — via Internet Archive full-text search"},"annahalprin":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Anna Halprin: born Hannah Dorothy Schuman, July 13, 1920","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Anna Halprin: May 24, 2021","blurb":"On the outdoor dance deck below Mount Tamalpais she ran the 1960 summer workshop through which Forti, Rainer, Trisha Brown, and Ruth Emerson passed on their way to Judson; she worked from tasks after noticing that non-dancers moved functionally. When Parades and Changes was danced naked in New York, a warrant was issued for her arrest. Cancer at fifty-two remade the work: she changed her name from Ann to Anna and turned to communal healing rites — Circle the Earth, danced annually from 1981.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"fernandschirren":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority GND 102433645X, Schirren, Fernand: date of birth 1920-01-21, place of birth Nice; occupations Komponist, Musiker, cited to his book Le rythme (1996) — the rhythm teacher of Mudra and P.A.R.T.S.","srcd":"GND 102433645X: died 2001-08-25, Auderghem — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A percussionist and silent-film accompanist, son of a fauve painter, who taught rhythm instead of steps — from 1970 at Béjart's Mudra, and after it closed at De Keersmaeker's P.A.R.T.S. Maguy Marin, De Keersmaeker and the De Mey siblings all sat in those classes. His treatise Le Rythme primordial et souverain was published in facsimile because the handwriting itself looked like a score. He regretted to the end never registering the music he wrote for Béjart, and earned nothing from it.","src":"CINEMATEK dossier, 'Fernand Schirren (1920-2001)' (cinematek.be, 2021): musician and silent-film pianist; music director of Maurice Bejart's ballet and star rhythm teacher at his school Mudra; teacher of Anne Teresa De Keersmaeker"},"sitaradevi":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Sitara Devi: born Dhanlakshmi, 8 November 1920","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Sitara Devi: 25 November 2014","blurb":"Born Dhanlakshmi and promised in marriage at eight, she refused and insisted on school; at eleven she danced before Rabindranath Tagore, who named her 'Nritya Samragni' — empress of dance — and gave her a shawl and fifty rupees. She spent a career proving the title: Bombay films from the age of twelve, a recital that once ran thirteen hours without stopping, and in 2002 the refusal of a Padma Bhushan as too small — 'I will not accept any award less than Bharat Ratna.'","src":"Gulf News — 'Sitara Devi, Kathak legend, dead', 25 Nov 2014 obituary (born 1920 in Kolkata to Sukhdev Maharaj; kathak legend, pioneer of the form in Bombay cinema); Nad Sadhna — 'Biography of Indian Dancers' (Tagore's title 'Queen of Kathak dance')"},"masumurakatsuko":{"blurb":"Hijikata's first dance teacher, in Akita, before the 1952 move to Tokyo. She had trained with Eguchi and Miya, which puts butoh's first lesson inside the German line — Wigman's method at two removes, taught in a northern provincial studio. The book that names her gives her no dates at all; her row here is placed from her approximate teaching years and drawn as inferred.","src":"The Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018)"},"helenmcgehee":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Helen McGehee: Helen Gray McGehee Umana, May 10, 1921","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Helen McGehee: April 9, 2020","blurb":"A Lynchburg, Virginia dancer who spent nearly thirty years inside the Graham company: she originated the girl in yellow in Diversion of Angels and danced Electra in Clytemnestra. From the Juilliard Dance Division's founding in 1951 until 1982 she taught the technique she embodied, and choreographed works of her own — I Am the Gate and El Retablo de Maese Pedro among them.","src":"Umaña family obituary — 'Helen McGehee', umana.us/helen-mcgehee-obituary (born Lynchburg, Virginia 10 May 1921, died 9 Apr 2020; key member of the Martha Graham Dance Company for nearly 30 years; originated the girl in yellow in Diversion of Angels and danced Electra in Clytemnestra; Juilliard Dance Division faculty from its inception in 1951 until 1982; choreographed I Am the Gate and El Retablo de Maese Pedro)"},"pearllang":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Pearl Lang: May 29, 1921","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Pearl Lang: February 24, 2009","blurb":"Graham made nine roles on her and then let her go first into her own: Lang was the first dancer permitted to assume Graham's principal parts, seven of them in the end. Her own sixty-odd works drew half on Jewish sources — Shirah, from a mystical Hasidic parable, the full-evening The Possessed — made for the company she led alongside her standing as the technique's acknowledged guardian.","src":"Dance Teacher, 'Pearl Lang: Guardian of Martha Graham's technique' (dance-teacher.com); Broadway World, 'Pearl Lang, Dancer and Teacher, Dead at 87' (Feb 2009): joined Graham's company at 19, Graham created nine roles for her, first to assume seven of Graham's own principal roles; founded the Pearl Lang Dance Theater; about half her ~60 works on Jewish themes"},"jacqueslecoq":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jacques Lecoq: 15 December 1921","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Jacques Lecoq: 19 January 1999","blurb":"A physical-education teacher who came to theatre through sport and rehabilitation — he watched how a paralysed man could organise his body to walk, and taught him to — and never stopped studying how athletes move. Eight years in Italy gave him commedia, Sartori's neutral mask moulded on his own face, and sixty productions at Milan's Piccolo; from 1956 his Paris school taught not a style but a lexicon of movement and dispositions. An actor's question in Padua — fine, but where is it going? — convinced him that mime standing alone goes nowhere.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"manjachmiel":{"srcb":"de.wikipedia, Manja Chmiel: 'Marie Anna „Manja“ Chmiel (* 5. Februar 1922 in Opava (Troppau), Tschechoslowakei; † 5. Mai 2006 in Hannover)'; Deutsche Nationalbibliothek GND 1222113929 gives the same dates with the note 'Assistentin von Mary Wigman', place of death Hannover. The research pass had 1906, sixteen years early","srcd":"de.wikipedia and GND 1222113929: died 5 May 2006 in Hannover — the research pass had 1999","blurb":"When Eiko and Koma wrote to Mary Wigman from Japan in 1972, Wigman was too ill to teach and they were sent instead to her student in Hannover — and Wigman then wrote back approving the substitution: good that you are studying with Manja. The German line's contact with the butoh generation is documented right here, in a letter about the teacher who could take them when Wigman could not.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 14 (Elswit et al.): Mary Wigman's student in Hannover who taught Eiko & Koma from 1972, with Wigman's written approval; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): Eiko and Koma studied in the tradition of German modern dance with Manja Chmiel in Germany, which is the route by which butoh's German ancestry passes into their work"},"jamestruitte":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority nr2004021857, 'Truitte, James': birth date 1923, birth place Chicago (Ill.), sourced to the NYPL Dance Division's own obituary files — 'American modern dancer; b. 1923, Chicago; danced with Lester Horton and Alvin Ailey; dance educator at University of Cincinnati'","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority nr2004021857: death date 1995-08-21, Cincinnati, from the NYPL Dance Division obituary files","blurb":"He danced in Lester Horton's interracial Los Angeles company beside Carmen de Lavallade, Bella Lewitzky and Joyce Trisler, and after Horton died suddenly in November 1953 became the recognized authority on his technique. With Ailey's company he danced the 'Fix Me, Jesus' duet in Revelations opposite Minnie Marshall. He spent his last decades teaching at the University of Cincinnati's conservatory, so that Horton technique reached dancers who never saw Horton.","src":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search: Horton's company"},"marcelmarceau":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Marcel Marceau: born Marcel Mangel, 22 March 1923","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Marcel Marceau: 22 September 2007","blurb":"He came to Paris during the war to train at Dullin's Atelier, learned the grammar of illusion mime from Decroux, and spent the rest of his life perfecting it as Bip, a white-faced clown descended from Pierrot. Where his teacher wanted mime pure and autonomous, Marceau wanted it seen: he shared with Barrault the desire to reach the public, toured the world for over four decades, and opened his own Paris mime school in 1978. Decroux disavowed the popular result and the two grew distant; the public never did.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"margaretchapple":{"blurb":"When Bodenwieser died in 1959 the company folded, and Chapple and Keith Bain kept the training alive by founding a centre at 18 City Road, Chippendale — for decades the room where Sydney's professional dancers were made and visiting companies rehearsed. She taught and choreographed there; Philippa Cullen was one of the children who danced her work. The building was sold shortly before Chapple's death in 1996.","src":"change.org petition, 'Let's get Bodenwieser Dance Centre back for the Sydney dance community' — https://www.change.org/p/city-of-sydney-let-s-get-bodenwieser-dance-centre-back-for-the-sydney-dance-community (Bain and Chapple founded the Bodenwieser Dance Centre after Bodenwieser's 1959 death; 18 City Road, Chippendale; sold shortly before Chapple's death in 1996); also Wikipedia, Gertrud Bodenwieser and Wikipedia, Philippa Cullen (Chapple's choreography danced at the Bodenwieser studio)"},"antoniaamaya":{"srcb":"El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco, ANTONIA AMAYA, read in full: 'ANTONIA AMAYA AMAYA, bailaora de la mas pura escuela del baile flamenco de su propia hermana Carmen Amaya... nacio en Barcelona el dia 16 de septiembre del ano de 1923. Hija de El Chino, llamado Jose Amaya Amaya y de Micaela Amaya Moreno, segun nos revela su pasaporte' — the page reproduces the passport. The research pass had 1886, from AncientFaces genealogy, which the atlas's own blurb already flagged as sitting oddly against the family's dates; 1886 would have made her older than her father","blurb":"One of the dancing Amaya siblings — the Spanish record lists Carmen, Antonia, Leonor, Antonio and María as the children who went to the stage — she worked inside the family company that toured behind her sister. The archive holds her in rosters and genealogy pages; what her dancing actually looked like went unrecorded. Even her birth date is genealogy-site data rather than documentation, and it sits oddly against the family's other dates.","src":"AncientFaces"},"ethelwinter":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority no2008068026, 'Winter, Ethel', citing the New York Times obituary published 17 March 2012: 'b. June 18, 1924, Wrentham, Mass.'","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority no2008068026, citing the New York Times obituary published 17 March 2012: 'd. Mar. 10, Manhattan, aged 87'. The same LC page's structured death field reads 2009-03-10, which is a data-entry error — the obituary it cites was published in 2012, and 87 against an 18 June 1924 birth gives 2012. en.wikipedia also gives 10 March 2012","blurb":"A Graham company member from the 1940s through the 1960s with the repertory's most delicate assignment: she was given roles Graham had originally choreographed for herself — the maker's own parts passing to another body while the maker lived. She then became the technique's keeper by sheer patience: fifty years on the Juilliard dance faculty, retiring only in 2003, nine years before her death.","src":"Wikipedia — Ethel Winter (18 June 1924 - 10 Mar 2012; member of the Martha Graham Dance Company from the 1940s through the 1960s; given roles Graham had originally choreographed for herself; taught at the Juilliard School Dance Division for fifty years, retiring 2003)"},"stuarthodes":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Stuart Hodes: November 27, 1924","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Stuart Hodes: March 15, 2023","blurb":"A Second World War bomber pilot who found Graham's studio on his return, he became her stage partner — Appalachian Spring, Deaths and Entrances, Every Soul Is a Circus, Errand into the Maze — and danced in the company from 1946 to 1958. At ninety-six he published Onstage with Martha Graham, his inside account of the company; he died in 2023.","src":"University Press of Florida, 'Onstage with Martha Graham' by Stuart Hodes (2021), publisher's page (floridapress.org): (1924-2023), WWII bomber pilot; danced with the Martha Graham Dance Company 1946-1958, Graham's partner in Appalachian Spring, Deaths and Entrances, Every Soul Is a Circus and Errand into the Maze"},"robertcohan":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Robert Cohan: Sir Robert Paul Cohan, 26 March 1925","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Robert Cohan: 13 January 2021","blurb":"Graham's soloist and stage partner from 1946, co-director of her company from 1962, he then transplanted the whole ecology: from 1967 he was founding artistic director of The Place — the London Contemporary Dance School and London Contemporary Dance Theatre — through which Graham-based training entered Britain wholesale. He advised Batsheva through the 1980s and died in London in January 2021.","src":"Robert Cohan official biography (robertcohan.co.uk/biography): born New York 1925, joined the Graham company 1946, soloist and partner to Graham, co-director with Bertram Ross from 1962; founding artistic director of The Place, London Contemporary Dance School and London Contemporary Dance Theatre (1967); artistic advisor to Batsheva 1980-1990; died London, January 2021"},"luciadlugoszewski":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Lucia Dlugoszewski: June 16, 1925","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Lucia Dlugoszewski: April 11, 2000","blurb":"She invented her own instrument — the timbre piano, played from inside, strings and frame struck with mallets and objects — and composed the sound of Erick Hawkins's company as his primary musical collaborator. She was also, secretly, his wife: married in 1962, she acknowledged it publicly only in her eulogy for him in 1994. Then she took the company itself, as artistic director and principal choreographer — the composer finishing the choreographer's sentences.","src":"Bowerbird — 'Pure Lucia: The Life and Work of Lucia Dlugoszewski', bowerbird.org (born Detroit 16 June 1925, died Manhattan 11 Apr 2000; timbre piano practice of playing the strings and frame with mallets and objects; married Erick Hawkins in 1962, acknowledged publicly only at his 1994 eulogy; named artistic director and principal choreographer of the Erick Hawkins Dance Company after his death)"},"matturney":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority no98009424, 'Turney, Matt': fuller name Matt Hart, birth date 1925, birth place Americus (Ga.), citing the New York Times of 30 December 2009 — 'Matt Turney, 84, Martha Graham dancer... b. Matt Hart Turney, 1925, in Americus, Ga.' A documented year replacing the build's activity-minus-25 estimate, which had landed on the same year","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority no98009424: death date 2009-12-20, citing the New York Times of 30 December 2009, 'd. Dec. 20, 2009, Poughkeepsie, N.Y.' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"From Americus, Georgia, with a Wisconsin dance degree, she joined Martha Graham's company in 1951 — one of its first Black dancers — and stayed twenty-one years, creating the Martyr in Seraphic Dialogue, Cassandra in Clytemnestra, and Lilith in Embattled Garden. What reviewers dwelt on was her stillness: as the Pioneer Woman in Appalachian Spring she could hold the stage without moving at all.","src":"Boston Globe, 'Matt Turney, at 84; Martha Graham dancer', December 31, 2009: born 1925, Americus, Georgia; joined the Graham company in 1951, left 1972; created the Martyr in Seraphic Dialogue, Cassandra in Clytemnestra, Lilith in Embattled Garden"},"maryhinkson":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority no2008068031, 'Hinkson, Mary': fuller name Mary De Haven, birth date 1925-03-16, birth place Philadelphia (Pa.), citing the New York Times obituary published 28 November 2014; en.wikipedia gives the same day. A documented date replacing the build's estimate","srcd":"Library of Congress name authority no2008068031: death date 2014-11-26, death place Manhattan, citing the New York Times obituary of 28 November 2014, 'd. Wednesday, Manhattan, aged 89' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"Trained in Margaret H'Doubler's Wisconsin program, she joined Martha Graham's company in 1951 alongside her college classmate Matt Turney — among its first African American dancers — and was a principal by 1953. Graham built Circe on her; in Seraphic Dialogue she danced the warrior and, later, the maid. After leaving in 1973 she taught at Juilliard, Dance Theatre of Harlem, and the Ailey School, carrying the technique to a generation of students.","src":"On Wisconsin Magazine, 'Dance, Dance Revolutionary' (onwisconsin.uwalumni.com): trained under Margaret H'Doubler at the University of Wisconsin; invited by Martha Graham to join the company in 1951, principal dancer by 1953, one of Graham's most important leading dancers; later taught at Juilliard and Dance Theatre of Harlem"},"davidwood":{"srcb":"University of California Academic Senate, In Memoriam, 'David K. C. Wood, Professor of Dance, Emeritus, Berkeley, 1925—2002': 'Born in 1925, raised in Fresno, California'. Corroborated by Library of Congress name authority n2007077322, 'Wood, David, 1925-2002'. Note the UC site holds a different David Wood (an entomologist, 1931-2024) at a similar path; the Wood who founded Berkeley's dance program is the DavidK.C.Wood file","srcd":"University of California Academic Senate In Memoriam: 'David K. C. Wood, founder of the University of California, Berkeley dance program, passed away on Sunday, April 21, 2002, at his home of complications from Parkinson's disease and muscular dystrophy. He was 77' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"He danced for nearly everyone the American century produced — Limón, Humphrey, Weidman, Tamiris, the Dudley-Maslow-Bales Trio, Nikolais — before fifteen years as a Graham soloist for whom she made roles: the Messenger of Death in Clytemnestra, the whip master in Acrobats of God. In 1968 Berkeley recruited him, and he built the university's dance program — a performing lineage converted, late, into a curriculum.","src":"University of California Academic Senate — In Memoriam: David K. C. Wood (1925-2002; fifteen-year association with the Martha Graham Dance Company as soloist for whom Graham created roles, incl. the Messenger of Death in Clytemnestra and the whip master in Acrobats of God; earlier performed with Limón, Humphrey, Weidman, Tamiris, the Dudley-Maslow-Bales Trio and Nikolais; recruited to UC Berkeley 1968, built its dance program)"},"elainesummers":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Elaine Summers: Lillian Elaine Summers, February 20, 1925","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Elaine Summers: December 27, 2014","blurb":"She came to Dunn's class with an arthritic hip and a conviction, formed by teaching nondancers, that differently shaped bodies made different dances and that what she loved to do didn't count as dance. She made Fantastic Gardens (1964) — by her account the first full-evening intermedia concert in New York, the audience holding hand mirrors to light the dancers — and evolved Kinetic Awareness, whose first dance grew from two and a half months of not getting up off the floor.","src":"Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), chs. 1, 5-6"},"kanzehisao":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 観世寿夫: 1925年11月12日, 観世流の能楽シテ方, 七代観世銕之丞(雅雪)の長男","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 観世寿夫: 1978年12月7日, 53歳で早世","blurb":"A shite of the Kanze school who decided noh's centuries were a reason to talk to the rest of theatre, not a wall against it. He went to Paris in 1962 as the first Japan-France theatre exchange fellow to study under Jean-Louis Barrault, gave noh seminars with his brother Hideo at Eugenio Barba's Holstebro laboratory between 1966 and 1972, and founded the Mei no Kai group to stage modern interpretations of Western classics. He died in 1978 at fifty-three, the experiment unfinished.","src":"Kotobank encyclopedia entry, Kanze Hisao (kotobank.jp) - Kanze-school shite, 12 Nov 1925 - 7 Dec 1978; first Japan-France theatre exchange fellow (1962, studied under Jean-Louis Barrault); founded Mei no Kai staging modern interpretations of Western classics"},"nolandingman":{"blurb":"One of the two names that appear whenever William Forsythe's beginnings are described: Forsythe was 'raised in New York and initially trained in Florida', by him and one other, before the Joffrey and Stuttgart. Beyond that first teaching the record reachable here is thin — he is in the atlas as the man who gave a future deconstructor of ballet his classical grammar.","src":"William Forsythe biography, williamforsythe.com (Forsythe official site): Forsythe was 'raised in New York and initially trained in Florida with Nolan Dingman and Christa Long'"},"christalong":{"blurb":"Named second of two in the single sentence that covers William Forsythe's training: he was 'initially trained in Florida' with her and one other teacher. She is here as a first teacher; what else she taught, and to whom, is not documented in any source reachable in this pass.","src":"William Forsythe biography, williamforsythe.com (Forsythe official site): Forsythe was 'raised in New York and initially trained in Florida with Nolan Dingman and Christa Long'"},"oikawahironobu":{"blurb":"A physician's son from Aomori who studied medicine, then ballet, then spent 1954–56 in Paris — ballet in the mornings, Decroux's school in the afternoons, half-hoping to find traces of Artaud. Back in Tokyo he was among the few who could teach the Decroux system in Japan: Hijikata, Ohno Yoshito, Kasai Akira and Ishii Mitsutaka all studied with him as butoh took form. In 1968 he founded the Artaud-kan, having thrown away, by his own account, his Paris mime and ballet to pursue Artaud's body through breath and Eastern practice.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 15 (Yoshida): brought the Decroux system and Artaudian body ideas to Japan; taught mime to Hijikata, Ohno Yoshito, Kasai Akira and Ishii Mitsutaka; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012), notes: Oikawa had trained in mime in France and was the founder of the Artaud-kan (Artaud Studio); he taught mime to Hijikata, Ohno Kazuo and Ohno Yoshito during the late 1950s, and Baird interviewed him and used his account of the 1956-60 period. Fraleigh, Butoh (2010) records Ohno Yoshito's study of classical ballet and pantomime with Hironobu Oikawa"},"robertrauschenberg":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Robert Rauschenberg: Milton Ernest Rauschenberg, October 22, 1925","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Robert Rauschenberg: May 12, 2008","blurb":"Cunningham's lighting designer and stage manager from 1961, he wanted theater's 'awful feeling of being on the spot' that painting no longer gave him. His first choreography, Pelican (1963), put himself on roller skates beside Carolyn Brown on pointe — the skates, he said, a gimmick to disguise the fact that he couldn't dance. The auditions taught him the price of training: dancers who had skated as children froze on wheels, because 'they couldn't hear the floor any more.'","src":"Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), chs. 1, 4, 6"},"julianbeck":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Julian Beck: May 31, 1925","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Julian Beck: September 14, 1985","blurb":"An abstract-expressionist painter and poet who worked at Valeska Gert's Beggar's Bar before founding the Living Theatre with Judith Malina in 1947. The company carried its anarchist-pacifist message through The Connection, The Brig, and the audience-participation Paradise Now, and paid for it: a tax dispute closed their theater and jailed them both, and a 1971 tour of Brazil ended in months of prison and deportation. The work, he said, 'had always striven to stress the sacredness of life.'","src":"Valeska Gert Wikipedia"},"bettyjones":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority record GND 132818529X, Jones, Betty: date of birth 1926, place Meadville, Pa.; occupation Tanzerin; variant name Ludin — the married name that identifies her as the Limon dancer who partnered Fritz Ludin","blurb":"A charter member of the José Limón company from 1947, she created Desdemona opposite Limón's Moor in The Moor's Pavane — the innocent at the quartet's center. She assisted Limón in the Juilliard studio and stayed on its faculty more than two decades, teaching the repertory's roles to the bodies that would inherit them; she died in Honolulu in 2020, at ninety-four.","src":"Dance Teacher — 'Former Limón Star and Teacher Betty Jones, 94, Has Died', 2020, dance-teacher.com/betty-jones (born Meadville, PA 11 June 1926, died Honolulu 17 Nov 2020; charter member of the José Limón Dance Company from 1947; created Desdemona opposite Limón's Moor in The Moor's Pavane; assisted Limón at Juilliard and stayed on its faculty more than two decades)"},"ruthcurrier":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ruth Currier: January 4, 1926","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Ruth Currier: October 4, 2011","blurb":"A principal with José Limón for two decades, she was twice handed the lineage's unfinished business: she completed Doris Humphrey's Brandenburg Concerto No. 4 after Humphrey's death — the beginning of her own choreography — and when Limón himself died she directed his company from 1972 to 1978, through the first years of its life without him.","src":"The Dance Enthusiast, 'Ruth Currier, Remembered: 1926-2011' (October 6, 2011): joined the José Limón company in 1949, principal dancer for two decades; completed Doris Humphrey's Brandenburg Concerto No. 4; directed the Limón company 1972-1978 after Limón's death"},"glentetley":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Glen Tetley: February 3, 1926","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Glen Tetley: January 26, 2007","blurb":"Trained on both sides of the American divide — five years in Hanya Holm's company, ballet with Margaret Craske and Antony Tudor, a season with Graham — he declined to choose, and made the refusal a career. His first program as a choreographer, in 1962, closed with Pierrot Lunaire; The Anatomy Lesson, Embrace Tiger and Return to Mountain and Voluntaries followed, carrying modern-dance weight into the ballet repertory.","src":"Glen Tetley Legacy — biography, glentetleylegacy.omeka.net/bio (born Cleveland 3 Feb 1926, died West Palm Beach 26 Jan 2007; scholarship student of Hanya Holm, danced in her company 1946-1951; ballet training under Margaret Craske and Antony Tudor; Martha Graham Dance Company 1958-59; first choreography program 1962 closed with Pierrot Lunaire; later The Anatomy Lesson 1964, Ricercare 1966, Embrace Tiger and Return to Mountain 1968, Voluntaries 1973)"},"murraylouis":{"srcb":"University of Washington Department of Dance, Murray Louis: 'Born Brooklyn, New York, 1926'; Deutsche Nationalbibliothek GND 119125544 gives the day, 1926-11-04; en.wikipedia gives November 4, 1926","srcd":"University of Washington Department of Dance, Murray Louis: 'Died New York City, New York, February 1, 2016'; the New York Times ran 'Murray Louis, Choreographer With a Comic Flair, Dies at 89' on 2 February 2016. The research pass had 2015","blurb":"He met Alwin Nikolais at Hanya Holm's Colorado College summer session and stayed for life: soloist and associate director of the Nikolais Dance Theatre, and Nikolais's partner and primary collaborator. He also led a company of his own from 1953 and choreographed more than a hundred works; the two men's papers now sit together in a single archive.","src":"Ross, Anna Halprin: Experience as Dance (2007), ch. 4"},"keithbain":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority record GND 1073949052, Bain, Keith: date of birth 1926, with the note 'For many years leader of the movement studies course at the National Institute of Dramatic Arts (NIDA), Kensington, Australia'","srcd":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek GND 1073949052: date of death 2012","blurb":"A Bodenwieser dancer who became the movement teacher for Australian actors: NIDA students learned to move from him, and Baz Luhrmann, who grew up in his town, drew part of Strictly Ballroom from his life. With Margaret Chapple he kept the Bodenwieser studio going after 1959. The informal dancers' picnic he started in 1986 turned into the Australian Dance Awards.","src":"Wikipedia, Gertrud Bodenwieser — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gertrud_Bodenwieser (Bain named among the dancers her teaching produced); Wikipedia, Australian Dance Council (Bain OAM among the 1977 founders; Australian Dance Awards originate in his 1986 Dancers' Picnic); Wikipedia, Strictly Ballroom (film drew on Bain's life); Wikipedia, Lillian Crombie (invited to NIDA by Bain); change.org Bodenwieser Dance Centre petition (co-founding the Centre with Chapple)"},"kundanlalgangani":{"srcb":"narthaki.com, Dr Chitra Sharma, 'The Pinnacle of Kathak — Pandit Kundan Lal Gangani' (13 July 2026): 'Born on 16 July 1926 into the distinguished Gangani family as the son of Pandit Ganeshi Lal, he represented the seventh generation of this celebrated lineage'; punarjjanibharat.com's birth-centenary piece agrees, 'born in Sujangarh, in the Churu district of Rajasthan... on 16 July 1926'","srcd":"punarjjanibharat.com (16 July 2026): 'He became a part of the Delhi Kathak Kendra in 1970 as a Guru and continued working there for the next 15 years until his passing on 16 July 1984' — he died on his birthday","blurb":"A Jaipur-gharana guru whose record is his students. He initiated Prerana Shrimali in Jaipur and carried her training on at Delhi's Kathak Kendra, where the gharana's court art was being remade as curriculum, and he started his son Rajendra on the form at the age of four. Both now head the line he taught them — Rajendra as its noted technician, Shrimali as a Kathak Kendra repertory chief and senior guru — so his teaching is audible wherever Jaipur kathak is danced.","src":"Nritya Shiksha"},"judithmalina":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Judith Malina: June 4, 1926","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Judith Malina: April 10, 2015","blurb":"A Beggar's Bar employee of Valeska Gert's who co-founded the Living Theatre with Julian Beck in 1947 and kept it alive through jailings at home and abroad. For the Judson generation her theater was infrastructure: it shared a building with the Cunningham studio, hosted James Waring's composition class and the July 1961 program that gave one of the Judson founders her choreographic debut, and it was there that the young dancers first saw the peace symbol and what one of them called 'the rise of political consciousness' arriving in art.","src":"Valeska Gert Wikipedia"},"jeancebron":{"srcb":"fr.wikipedia, Jean Cebron: 'Jean-Maurice Cebron, ne le 29 avril 1927 a Paris'","srcd":"fr.wikipedia, Jean Cebron: 'mort le 1er fevrier 2019 aux Sables-d'Or-les-Pins'","blurb":"A Paris-born carrier of the Jooss-Leeder method: he studied with Sigurd Leeder twice over, danced, choreographed and taught in Kurt Jooss's Folkwangballett in Essen (1961–64), and from 1976 professed modern dance at the Folkwang-Hochschule, bracing the method with Cecchetti ballet. As training director of the Folkwang-Tanzstudio and of Pina Bausch's Wuppertal company he became the technique teacher standing behind German Tanztheater.","src":"PACT Zollverein — Jean Cébron artist biography, pact-zollverein.de (born in Paris; studied with Sigurd Leeder 1947-48 and again in London 1954-57; choreographer, soloist and teacher with the Folkwangballett Essen under Kurt Jooss 1961-1964; professor of modern dance at the Folkwang-Hochschule Essen from 1976; training director of the Folkwang-Tanzstudio and Wuppertaler Tanztheater, both directed by Pina Bausch; teaching based on the Jooss-Leeder method with Cecchetti technique)"},"mauricebejart":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Maurice Bejart: 1 January 1927","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Maurice Bejart: 22 November 2007","blurb":"Son of the philosopher Gaston Berger, he ran the Ballet du XXe Siècle in Brussels from 1960 to 1987 and made his Boléro there in 1960, for the Yugoslav ballerina Duška Sifnios. He was as interested in schools as in companies: Mudra in Brussels from 1970, Mudra Afrique in Dakar from 1977 with Léopold Sédar Senghor and Germaine Acogny, and, after he moved everything to Lausanne, Rudra, which he ran until his death in 2007.","src":"International Dance Day, 'Maurice Bejart' (international-dance-day.org): formed the Ballet du XXe Siecle in Brussels 1960; founded the Mudra School in Brussels in 1970, then in Dakar seven years later"},"johncranko":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, John Cranko: John Cyril Cranko, 15 August 1927","srcd":"en.wikipedia, John Cranko: 26 June 1973","blurb":"Prosecuted in Britain for homosexual activity in 1961, he took the Stuttgart directorship the same year and turned a provincial house into a destination — Romeo and Juliet, Onegin, The Taming of the Shrew, built on Marcia Haydée and Richard Cragun. He collected choreographers as well as dancers: MacMillan came and made five ballets, and he pulled the young Jiří Kylián out of the Royal Ballet School. He died in 1973 on the flight home from an American tour, reacting to a sleeping pill.","src":"Stuttgart Ballet, 'The Cranko Era 1961-1973' (stuttgart-ballet.de): appointed director and choreographer of the ballet in Stuttgart 1961; 1969 New York tour triumph crowned by Clive Barnes's phrase 'The Stuttgart Ballet Miracle'"},"robertellisdunn":{"srcb":"Movement Research Performance Journal #14 (Spring 1997), the memorial issue, titled throughout 'The Legacy of Robert Ellis Dunn (1928-1996)'; and in the Dunn text the issue opens with — his own journal entry of 30 March 1980, reprinted from Contact Quarterly Winter '89 — he counts his own age across the Judson years: 'In the fall of 1960, I began a series of four courses ... at the Merce Cunningham Studio ... For me, the \"Judson\" experience began with the first of these classes and ended in October of 1964 ... (I moved from 31 to 35 years of age during this period, most of the rest were in their 20s.)' Thirty-one in the autumn of 1960 puts his birth in 1928 or 1929, which is the year the map already held, now on his own word; also en.wikipedia, Robert Ellis Dunn: 1928","srcd":"Movement Research Performance Journal #14 (Spring 1997), whose every page is headed 'The Legacy of Robert Ellis Dunn (1928-1996)' — an issue put together by his colleagues in the year after he died; also en.wikipedia, Robert Ellis Dunn: July 5, 1996","blurb":"A musician, not a dancer — Cunningham's accompanist, conservatory-trained — he taught the choreography course John Cage asked him to give in 1960, charging twelve to fifteen dollars 'for each solvent student.' He replaced Horst's verdicts with one question, 'How did you make that dance?', banishing good-and-bad in favor of analysis, and the two-year workshop emptied into the concert that became Judson Dance Theater. He taught, Paxton said, 'almost by neglecting us... leaving with a smile.'","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"aaleath":{"srcb":"No birth year in any source opened; Ross places him in San Francisco in August 1953 as a doctoral student, which the estimate is built back from","blurb":"H'Doubler sent him to Halprin in 1953 — a biology doctoral student who had been auditing her Wisconsin dance classes — and he became the Dancers' Workshop's other teacher, insisting that 'we were educators first' and the productions 'by-products of our discoveries of teaching.' Compact, muscular, 'delightfully unpredictable' as a dancer, he recruited Simone Forti after class one day ('Look, you're an improviser') and played himself in Apartment 6 until that trio's honesty broke up the company.","src":"Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"tatsumihijikata":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Tatsumi Hijikata (土方巽): March 9, 1928; ja.wikipedia 土方巽 gives 1928年3月9日","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Tatsumi Hijikata: January 21, 1986; ja.wikipedia gives 1986年1月21日","blurb":"His 1959 debut Forbidden Colours — an older man, a boy, a white chicken killed in half-darkness — is where butoh is usually said to begin, and he supplied the name: ankoku butoh, the dance of utter darkness. Born in Akita in 1928 as Yoneyama Kunio, he spent the fifties acquiring ballet, jazz, tap, flamenco and mime in Tokyo studios and the sixties turning all of it against the imported upright body. From 1972 he largely stopped dancing and choreographed his apprentices word by word instead, which is how butoh-fu came to exist.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), Introduction (Baird & Candelario): born 1928 in Akita as Yoneyama Kunio, died 1986; first lessons under Masumura Katsuko, then Ando, Horiuchi, Oikawa, Tsuda in Tokyo; Kinjiki debut 1959; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): born in Akita prefecture in 1928 as Yoneyama Kunio, the tenth of eleven children; joined Masumura Katsuko's modern dance studio at roughly eighteen; shuttled between Akita and Tokyo 1947-1952 and first saw Ohno Kazuo dance in 1949; in Ando Mitsuko's studio 1953-1958 studying ballet, jazz and modern dance; used the stage name Hijikata Kunio from about 1954 and Hijikata Genet, Hijikata Nue and Hijikata Nero thereafter, first appearing as Hijikata Tatsumi in 1958; debut in his own choreography with Forbidden Colors, 24 May 1959; left the stage in 1973 and choreographed more than thirty dances after Story of Smallpox; died 1986. Fraleigh, Butoh: Metamorphic Dance and Global Alchemy (2010) gives 1928-86 and records that he read Genet's The Thief's Journal and Our Lady of the Flowers in the mid-1950s in fresh Japanese translation"},"akikomotofuji":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 元藤燁子: 1928年9月22日, 本名 元藤明子, 土方巽記念資料館館長","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 元藤燁子: 2003年10月19日","blurb":"She had placed fourth in an international dance competition in Vienna in 1955 before she became the person who paid for butoh. From 1959 she and Hijikata worked the nightclubs as a duo, the Blue Echoes, and she ran the gold-paint cabaret through a production company called Garmela Mercantile — named for the caramel they sold from a snow-cone stand by day, and used by Hijikata as a code word for Asbestos Hall itself. Her 1990 memoir is still the primary source for what nobody else wrote down.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 2 (Arimitsu): Hijikata's wife and lifelong manager, danced in the early works; her 1990 memoir Hijikata Tatsumi to tomoni is used as a primary source; ch. 42 note 2: her father gave her the Asbestos building in 1952; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): a student of Tsuda Nobutoshi, and an accomplished dancer in her own right — she placed fourth in an international dance competition in Vienna in 1955; in 1959 she and Hijikata formed a nightclub dance duo, the Blue Echoes, to fund the avant-garde work, which never paid for itself; she managed the Gold Dust Show through a production company called Garmela Mercantile, which was also Hijikata's code name for Asbestos Hall; she is credited as"},"paulsanasardo":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Paul Sanasardo: 'Paul Savior Sanasardo (September 15, 1928 - July 23, 2026) was an American dancer, choreographer and dance teacher of Italian descent'","srcd":"Les Humanités obituary: 23 July 2026","blurb":"Chicago-born, trained by Graham and Antony Tudor, he ran with Donya Feuer a New York company from 1957 whose significance surfaced elsewhere: the young Pina Bausch danced in it and made her first choreography under its roof. His own works ran from Metallics (1963) to the solo Pain (1970). He died in July 2026 at ninety-eight — 'the forgotten one, except by Pina Bausch,' as a French obituary put it.","src":"Les Humanités, 'Paul Sanasardo, the forgotten one (except by Pina Bausch)' (leshumanites-media.com, 2026): born Chicago 1928, died July 23, 2026; trained with Graham and Tudor; founded the Paul Sanasardo-Donya Feuer Dance Company with Donya Feuer in 1957, where Pina Bausch made her first choreographic creations"},"antoniomarin":{"blurb":"A Madrid maestro whose academy sat in the path of the postwar generation: El Güito joined his group at eleven and was dancing soleá by twelve, La Chunga trained under him, and Ciro Diezhandino paired his lessons with those of another Madrid maestra. Like hers, his is a teacher's record — preserved in the opening paragraphs of other dancers' biographies rather than in any surviving account of his own dancing.","src":"ExpoFlamenco"},"marioescudero":{"srcb":"es.wikipedia, Mario Escudero: 'Mario Escudero Valero (Alicante, 11 de octubre de 1928 - Miami, 19 de noviembre de 2004)'; en.wikipedia agrees on both dates","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Mario Escudero: November 19, 2004","blurb":"He accompanied Vicente Escudero's dancing from 1944 and Carmen Amaya's tours from 1950, then stepped off the USS Constitution in New York in January 1955 and decided the following year not to go home. As a soloist he took the flamenco guitar to Carnegie Hall, Town Hall and the Kennedy White House, and cut more than thirty albums between 1952 and 1969. With Sabicas he recorded guitar duets that tested what two flamenco guitars could do with no dancer to serve.","src":"Fine Fretted"},"donredlich":{"srcb":"His published obituary, Winona Daily News, 5 May 2026, headed 'Donald Redlich 1929 - 2026': 'Born August 17, 1929, in Winona Minn. to Walter and Kathryn Redlich... Don spent his summers learning from pioneering choreographer Hanya Holm... In 1966, he founded the nationally touring Don Redlich Dance Company'. The NYPL Don Redlich papers finding aid agrees: 'Don Redlich (born 1929)'. GND 1329544609 gives the same day but the year 1933, which the obituary's stated age of 96 rules out","srcd":"Winona Daily News obituary, 5 May 2026: 'Donald ‘Don’ Redlich passed away peacefully on Tuesday, April 28'; the Echovita notice gives 'August 17, 1929 - April 28, 2026 (96 years old)'. The research pass had 2020 — he outlived it by six years","blurb":"Hanya Holm's disciple, dancer and assistant, he came to New York in 1954 and performed in productions by Holm, Doris Humphrey and Anna Sokolow before founding the Don Redlich Dance Company in 1966. His repertory kept Holm's Rota alive beside his own works — Passin' Through, Earthling, Patina, Traces — the Holm line held deliberately on stage.","src":"NYPL Jerome Robbins Dance Division — Don Redlich Dance Company photographs finding aid, archives.nypl.org/dan/185535 (born 1929; modern dancer, choreographer and teacher who studied under Hanya Holm; moved to New York 1954, performing in productions by Holm, Doris Humphrey and Anna Sokolow; founded the Don Redlich Dance Company 1966, whose repertoire included Holm's Rota; works incl. Passin' Through 1959, Earthling 1963, L'Histoire du Soldat 1966, Patina 1974, Traces 1975)"},"paultaylor":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Paul Taylor: Paul Belville Taylor Jr., July 29, 1930","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Paul Taylor: August 29, 2018","blurb":"A principal in Graham's company for seven years while making his own work on the side: his 1957 concert Seven New Dances included Duet, in which he and Toby Glanternik simply stood still — Louis Horst reviewed it with a blank column. Aureole (1962) turned him full-time to the troupe he had founded in 1954, and Esplanade (1975) built a whole dance from running, walking, sliding, and falling; he left 147 works at his death in 2018.","src":"EBSCO Research Starters, 'Paul Taylor (choreographer)': born July 29, 1930, Wilkinsburg, PA; died August 29, 2018; Juilliard study; principal dancer with Martha Graham's company for seven years; formed the Paul Taylor Dance Company in 1954; Aureole (1962), Esplanade (1975)"},"donaldmckayle":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Donald McKayle: July 6, 1930","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Donald McKayle: April 6, 2018","blurb":"He saw Pearl Primus dance as a teenager and auditioned, untrained, into a New Dance Group scholarship in 1947. Games (1951) made choreography from street games and play songs and the childhood poverty underneath them; Rainbow 'Round My Shoulder (1959) set a chain gang's bondage against its dream of freedom. He became the first Black man to direct and choreograph major Broadway musicals, taking Tony nominations for Golden Boy and Raisin, and ended as an endowed professor of dance at UC Irvine.","src":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search: the index entry"},"johngraham":{"srcb":"No birth year in any source opened, and the name defeats search — a Scottish actor and a Seattle architect of the same name fill the results. Banes's Democracy's Body ch. 1 has him at Halprin's Union Street studio in 1947 as a San Francisco State freshman, which the estimate is built back from; it draws as an estimate","blurb":"An actor who began at Halprin's Union Street studio in 1947 as a San Francisco State freshman and joined her first improvisation class, where the instruction was to curl one finger and follow it. He brought speech into the work: 'We'd start talking as part of the movement. That's how we started to use voice.' In Apartment 6 (1965) he, Halprin, and A. A. Leath played themselves — making pancakes, reading the paper, improvising their real relationships — until the piece split the company.","src":"Ross, Anna Halprin: Experience as Dance (2007), ch. 4; also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1"},"horiuchikan":{"srcd":"Sogi.jp's death notice, 堀内完 氏（舞踊家）: 命日 2016年5月20日 15時, 年齢 85歳, 出身 愛知県名古屋市, 事由 老衰 — matching the Mainichi and Sankei obituaries of 22 May 2016, which give the same date, the same age and the same funeral temple, and name his eldest son 堀内元, artistic director of the Saint Louis Ballet. The research pass had no death year","blurb":"One of the Tokyo teachers Hijikata passed through after the war, in the run of studios — Ando, Horiuchi, Oikawa, Tsuda — that supplied him with ballet, jazz, tap and mime. Horiuchi had himself trained under Oikawa at Ballet Tokyo, and ran the Unique Dance Group with Ando Mitsuko: at its first performance in 1955 Hijikata played a bartender and Kazuo Ohno a customer, the second time those two met. The record of him is about two index entries thick.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), Introduction: one of Hijikata's Tokyo teachers; ch. 15 (Yoshida): himself a student of Oikawa at Ballet Tokyo"},"kumudinilakhia":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Kumudini Lakhia: 17 May 1930, founder of Kadamb School of Dance and Music, Ahmedabad","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Kumudini Lakhia: 12 April 2025 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"She learnt kathak from teachers across four gharanas, danced in Ram Gopal's touring company at seventeen, then spent her life dismantling the soloist's monopoly: at Kadamb, founded in Ahmedabad in 1964, she made kathak an ensemble art, and works like Dhabkar and Atah Kim moved it past mythological storytelling into pattern and line. Aditi Mangaldas came out of her school; so did the dances of Umrao Jaan (1981). The Padma Vibhushan reached her in 2025, months before she died at ninety-four.","src":"Kothari 1989"},"gladysbailin":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Gladys Bailin: born 1930","blurb":"She met Alwin Nikolais at the Henry Street Playhouse in 1948 and became one of the original dancers of his total theatre, touring with his company and Murray Louis's across the United States and abroad — the human instrument inside the light, shape and sound. She taught at NYU's School of the Arts from 1966, and in 1972 carried the lineage to Ohio University, directing its School of Dance from 1983 to 1995 and becoming the university's first female Distinguished Professor.","src":"Bearnstow Journal 2004"},"lindahodes":{"srcb":"Batsheva Archive: 1931","srcd":"Batsheva Archive: 2025","blurb":"A Graham dancer from 1953 who performed lead roles across the repertory and then became the technique's courier: sent to Israel in 1964 as Graham's assistant, she prepared the infant Batsheva company for its premiere of Graham's choreographies and stayed, guarding that repertory until 1975 and serving in turn as co-artistic director. Two companies, one body of work; she is a large part of how it crossed the ocean.","src":"Batsheva Archive — Linda Hodes, archive.batsheva.co.il/en/people/linda-hodes (1931-2025; joined Graham's company in 1953 and performed lead roles in many of her works; arrived in Israel 1964 as Graham's assistant to prepare Batsheva's premiere of Graham choreographies; joined Batsheva as teacher and dancer; responsible for Graham's repertoire at Batsheva until 1975; co-artistic director alternately 1971-1974)"},"alvinailey":{"srcb":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search","srcd":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search","blurb":"At five he picked cotton beside his mother in segregated Texas; church and the blues lodged as what he called blood memories, and Revelations (1960) set them down and became one of the world's most performed modern dances. Brought to Lester Horton's studio in 1949 by his classmate Carmen de Lavallade, he founded his company in 1958 as a home for Black dance that hired on talent alone. In 1987 he mounted an evening of Dunham, repaying the Tropical Revue that had first made him want to dance.","src":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations: Alvin Ailey's Embodiment of African American Culture (Oxford University Press, 2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search"},"carmendelavallade":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Carmen de Lavallade: March 6, 1931","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Carmen de Lavallade: December 29, 2025 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A Los Angeles teenager on scholarship in Lester Horton's interracial company, dancing beside James Truitte, Bella Lewitzky and Joyce Trisler, she brought her classmate Alvin Ailey to Horton's Hollywood studio in 1949. Ailey followed her to New York in December 1954 to appear with her in the Broadway musical House of Flowers, and for a time the two toured under the banner of the De Lavallade-Ailey American Dance Company. She taught movement at the Yale School of Drama for decades afterward.","src":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search: Horton's company"},"joanmyersbrown":{"srcb":"Gottschild, The Black Dancing Body (Palgrave Macmillan, 2003) — via Internet Archive full-text search","blurb":"She came up through Philadelphia's Black dance lineage — Essie Marie Dorsey down to Marion Cuyjet and Sydney King — and Syvilla Fort, a principal teacher out of Dunham's school, watched over 'the skinny little girl'. She founded her own school in 1960 and the Philadelphia Dance Company in 1970, when Black dancers had few companies to join. Then she organized the field, founding the conference of Black dance companies in 1988 and the International Association of Blacks in Dance in 1991.","src":"Gottschild, Joan Myers Brown & the Audacious Hope of the Black Ballerina (Palgrave Macmillan, 2012) — via Internet Archive full-text search"},"robertmorris":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Robert Morris (artist): February 9, 1931","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Robert Morris (artist): November 28, 2018","blurb":"He no longer called himself an artist when he and Simone Forti reached New York in 1959, and his stage debut had no visible performer: at the Living Theater a grey column stood for three and a half minutes, then fell and lay as long. Objects were 'means for dealing with specific problems' — a javelin, an adjustable T-form, a light swung overhead in Arizona — movement generators that refused the pulled-up, turned-out 'dancer.' His task-attitude became the paradigm of Judson's minimalist strand.","src":"Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"hansvanmanen":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Hans van Manen: Hans Arthur Gerard van Manen, 11 July 1932","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Hans van Manen: 17 December 2025 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"He trained first as a make-up artist, and became the Netherlands' classicist of clarity: about 150 ballets, mostly short, most built straight out of the music with everyday gesture and social dancing folded in. He directed Nederlands Dans Theater through the 1960s and then spent decades at Dutch National Ballet, teaching steps by dancing them himself. He was also a serious art photographer, and died in Amsterdam in December 2025, aged 93.","src":"Wikipedia, 'Hans van Manen' (b. 11 July 1932 Nieuwer-Amstel, d. 17 December 2025 Amsterdam; trained as a make-up artist from 1945, studied ballet with Sonia Gaskell from 1949; founding member of Nederlands Dans Theater, its artistic director 1961-1970; ballet master of Dutch National Ballet 1974-1987; c. 150 mostly short ballets driven by the music, integrating everyday movement and social dance; 'danced the movements for the dancers'; also an art photographer; Grosse Fuge 1971, Adagio Hammerklavier 1973, 5 Tango's 1977; Hans van Manen Foundation established 2021)"},"anniefratellini":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Annie Fratellini: Annie Violette Fratellini, 14 November 1932","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Annie Fratellini: 1 July 1997","blurb":"Fourth generation of one of Europe's most illustrious clown dynasties, she formed a classic duo with her husband, the filmmaker Pierre Étaix — he the whiteface Clown, she the Auguste, the comic role a woman rarely held. In 1975 the pair opened the École Nationale du Cirque, one of Europe's first two professional circus schools, replacing family apprenticeship with formal training. It outlived her as the Académie Fratellini, one of France's two major state-sponsored circus schools.","src":"Peak Performances 2019"},"judithdunn":{"srcb":"NYPL Jerome Robbins Dance Division, Judith Dunn collection finding aid: 'Judith Dunn (nee Goldsmith, 1933-1983) was an American dancer, choreographer, and founding member of the Judson Dance Theater'; her New York Times obituary of 3 July 1983 is headed 'JUDITH DUNN, 49', an age that fits 1933","srcd":"NYPL Jerome Robbins Dance Division finding aid: Judith Dunn (1933-1983); NYT obituary 3 July 1983","blurb":"She wanted to be a professional basketball player, majored in anthropology, and was in Cunningham's company by 1958; when her husband Robert taught the workshop that hatched Judson she assisted, and students simply called it 'the Dunns' class.' She called his teaching 'outrageous' — 'interminable rambling discussion,' evaluation eliminated, analysis in its place. She later formed a company with trumpeter Bill Dixon — one of the first sustained meetings of free jazz and improvised dance.","src":"Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1"},"jerzygrotowski":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jerzy Grotowski: Jerzy Marian Grotowski, 11 August 1933","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Jerzy Grotowski: 14 January 1999","blurb":"From the Theatre of 13 Rows in Opole he redefined what actors are for: 'poor theatre' stripped away spectacle until only the actor's direct confrontation with the spectator remained, codified in Towards a Poor Theatre (1968). At the height of his fame he abandoned productions altogether — paratheatre's communal rites, the Theatre of Sources' ritual techniques, finally 'art as vehicle' at Pontedera, where the performing served the doers rather than an audience. He passed the work, deliberately, to Thomas Richards.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (Routledge, 2003), Name Glossary"},"ehudbendavid":{"srcb":"Batsheva Archive: 1933","srcd":"Batsheva Archive: died in a car accident, 1976","blurb":"A founding dancer of Batsheva, joining from the Lyric Theater in 1963 before the company had performed at all, and its charismatic leading man in the Graham repertory. He took the Golden Star Medal in Paris in 1971, choreographed pieces of his own — By Order of Creon, 1966 — and married Linda Hodes, the woman Graham had sent to build the repertory around him. A car accident killed him in 1976, at forty-three.","src":"Batsheva Archive — Ehud Ben-David, archive.batsheva.co.il/en/people/ehud-ben-david (1933-1976; joined Batsheva in 1963 from the Lyric Theater as one of the company's first founding members; one of its charismatic dancers, Golden Star Medal in Paris 1971; choreographed several pieces incl. By Order of Creon, 1966; married to Linda Hodes; died in a car accident, 1976)"},"joankerr":{"srcd":"Philadelphia Dance Projects, Local Dance History Project, 'First Horton Based Companies 1960-80's', essay by Terry Fox: 'A little later she was diagnosed with a brain tumor. She died in January 1981. A memorial service was held in her Race Street Studio on one of the coldest days of that winter' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"Nine years a dancer in Lester Horton's Los Angeles company, she came home to Philadelphia in 1961 carrying the technique east: she taught it at the Philadelphia Dance Academy — Judith Jamison among her students — then opened her own studio and the Joan Kerr Dance Company. Like her teacher she made integration the point, establishing one of Philadelphia's first racially integrated dance companies. She died in January 1981.","src":"Philadelphia Dance Projects — 'First Horton Based Companies 1960-80s', philadanceprojects.org (member of the Lester Horton Dance Theater in Los Angeles 1952-1961; returned to Philadelphia 1961; taught at the Philadelphia Dance Academy before opening her own studio and the Joan Kerr Dance Company; established one of the first racially integrated dance companies in Philadelphia; students of her Horton technique include Judith Jamison; died Jan 1981)"},"arthurhall":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Arthur L. Hall: 'Arthur Lee Hall was born on April 18, 1934 in Memphis, Tennessee'; founded the Arthur Hall Afro-American Dance Ensemble in 1958","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Arthur L. Hall: 'Arthur Hall died on July 6, 2000', citing the Philadelphia Inquirer obituary (Rusty Pray, 15 July 2000, p. C08); corroborated in Brenda Dixon Gottschild, Joan Myers Brown and the Audacious Hope of the Black Ballerina: 'Arthur Hall (1934-2000)' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"He came up at Marion Cuyjet's Judimar School — where, he said, there were no stars — and later taught there for her, and in 1958 founded the Afro-American Dance Ensemble in Philadelphia, directing it about thirty years. In 1968 he opened the Ile Ife Black Humanitarian Center at 2544 Germantown Avenue, putting African dance, art and neighborhood work under one North Philadelphia roof. He founded the school to promote a positive image, and saw the ensemble as a way to empower African Americans.","src":"Long, The Black Tradition in American Dance (Rizzoli, 1989) — via Internet Archive full-text search"},"yvonnerainer":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Yvonne Rainer: born November 24, 1934","blurb":"She began training at twenty-four — too late, she judged, for 'mastery as a performer of other artists' work' — so she made her own dances from charts of options, one voice option being 'First of all, it is a contribution to history,' and it was her suggestion that sent Dunn's class to Judson Memorial Church. The No Manifesto (1965) and Trio A — no repeated movements, gaze averted — turned refusal into a style. Between 1972 and 1975 she moved to film, citing among other urgencies her aging body.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"donyafeuer":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Donya Feuer: 31 October 1934","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Donya Feuer: 6 November 2011","blurb":"Graham-trained and a dancer with Paul Taylor, she ran the Studio for Dance in New York with Paul Sanasardo, where she coached the young Pina Bausch in dramatic performance; in Stockholm, her 1962 course gave Mats Ek his first dance training. She then disappeared into Ingmar Bergman's theatre, choreographing his productions for decades and co-directing The Magic Flute and Face to Face — an American modern dancer working deep inside European theatre and film.","src":"TheaterMania — 'Choreographer/Director Donya Feuer Has Died', Nov 2011 (died 16 Nov 2011 at 77; trained with Martha Graham, performed with Paul Taylor; formed the Studio for Dance company and school in New York with Paul Sanasardo; choreographed numerous Ingmar Bergman productions and co-directed The Magic Flute and Face to Face)"},"mosheefrati":{"srcb":"he.wikipedia משה אפרתי: 'משה אפרתי (1934 – 26 בספטמבר 2020) היה רקדן וכוריאוגרף ישראלי' — born 1934 (the en.wikipedia infobox for this man is scrambled, reading '26 - 1934 September 2020')","srcd":"he.wikipedia משה אפרתי: died 26 September 2020","blurb":"A lead dancer from Batsheva's 1964 founding, schooled in New York under Louis Horst and Antony Tudor at Graham's school, he made his first piece for the company in 1969 — Sin Lieth at the Door — and found his life's work two years later: Demama, from 1975 Kol Demama, a company in which deaf and hearing dancers performed together. He became an Israel Prize laureate.","src":"Batsheva Archive, 'Moshe Efrati' (archive.batsheva.co.il): (1934-2020), lead dancer from Batsheva's 1964 founding alongside Rina Schenfeld and Ehud Ben David; studied at Martha Graham's New York school under Louis Horst and Antony Tudor; established the Demama company of deaf dancers in 1971, renamed Kol Demama 1975; Israel Prize laureate"},"kamalalakshman":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Kumari Kamala: 'Kumari Kamala (16 June 1934 - 23 November 2025), also known as Kamala Lakshman'","srcd":"narthaki.com, Obituaries 2025: 'KAMALA LAKSHMINARAYANAN (June 16, 1934 - November 24, 2025) — Bharatanatyam dance icon Kamala Lakshminarayanan (Kumari Kamala as she was popularly known) passed away in the early hours of November 24, 2025 (IST) in California at her son's place. She was 91.' en.wikipedia gives 23 November; she died in California, so the two are the same moment across time zones. The research pass had no death year","blurb":"Discovered at four and famous by ten for the paampu attam — the snake dance she coiled through Jagathalapratapan (1944) — she became the Vazhuvoor bani's proof on screen: Nam Iruvar (1947), thick with Gandhian anthems, is credited with restoring bharatanatyam's respectability for Tamil filmgoers. She danced in Queen Elizabeth II's coronation festivities at nineteen and in nearly a hundred films, then taught the style from Long Island for four decades, a National Heritage Fellow in her adopted country.","src":"SADI Journal 2023"},"valdasetterfield":{"srcb":"Alastair Macaulay's New York Times obituary, syndicated in full on Artdaily: 'Valda Setterfield was born Sept. 17, 1934, in Margate, England, in the county of Kent'","srcd":"Alastair Macaulay's New York Times obituary of April 2023, syndicated in full on Artdaily: 'Valda Setterfield, a leading figure in the New York dance world for 60 years and the wife and muse of renowned choreographer David Gordon, died April 9 in Manhattan. She was 88.' Born 17 September 1934, she was 88 in April 2023 — the research pass had no death year at all","blurb":"Marie Rambert trained her; New York took her at full height — she had heard that there, unlike in English ballet, her tallness would not be a handicap. Arriving in 1958 she met James Waring, who asked her something about Zen and shortly asked her to dance. She sat in Dunn's class with David Gordon 'in it but not of it,' danced as a soloist for Cunningham, and spent six decades as Gordon's central performer — the two of them called 'the Barrymores of post-modern dance.'","src":"Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1"},"deloresbrownabelson":{"srcb":"Obituaries: 1935","srcd":"Obituaries: 2023","blurb":"Trained at Marion Cuyjet's Judimar school in Philadelphia — 'I still fall back on my Judimar training' — she was chosen in a national search in 1956 as a principal of the New York Negro Ballet, and danced its four-month British tour in 1957, opening in Glasgow. The company folded in 1960 when its sponsor died. She taught for decades afterwards: the first scholarship programme at the Ailey School, then years at the Philadelphia School of Dance Arts.","src":"MoBBallet, 'Marion Cuyjet's Orbit: Delores Browne' (mobballet.org, 2021): studied at the Judimar School under Marion Cuyjet ('I still fall back on my Judimar training'); ran the first scholarship program at The Ailey School and taught for many years at Philadelphia School of Dance Arts"},"simoneforti":{"srcb":"Il Giornale dell'Arte obituary, 'Addio a Simone Forti, l'artista che ha cambiato la danza contemporanea' (29 July 2026): 'Nata a Firenze il 25 marzo 1935 in una famiglia ebrea, dovette lasciare l'Italia nel 1938 in seguito alle leggi razziali fasciste'; en.wikipedia gives March 25, 1935","srcd":"Il Giornale dell'Arte, 29 July 2026: 'E morta a Los Angeles, a 91 anni, Simone Forti'. The obituary gives no day, only the date of the announcement — the research pass had no death year at all","blurb":"Four years improvising on Halprin's deck left her allergic to New York training: at the Graham school, holding her stomach in repelled her, and Cunningham's speed bewildered a sensibility 'still very close to the holistic and generalized response of infants.' Her Dance Constructions, shown at Yoko Ono's loft in 1961 like works in a gallery — Huddle, a human jungle gym; Slant Board, a ramp climbed for ten minutes — replaced phrases and climaxes with rules that generate activity.","src":"Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"gopikrishna":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Gopi Krishna (dancer): 22 August 1935","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Gopi Krishna (dancer): 18 February 1994","blurb":"Raised and trained from the age of eleven by his grandfather Sukhdev Maharaj, dancing through chronic asthma, he held the title 'Nataraj' at fifteen and by seventeen was staging dances for Madhubala — among the youngest choreographers Hindi film had seen. Jhanak Jhanak Payal Baaje (1955) made him classical dance's face on screen, and the engine never slowed: a nine-hour-twenty-minute kathak marathon, the dances of Umrao Jaan, a Bombay academy of his own, a Padma Shri at forty.","src":"Mystica Music — Gopi Krishna artist profile (born in Kolkata; raised and trained from age 11 by maternal grandfather Pt Sukhdev Maharaj of the Banaras gharana; danced in and directed dances for Indian films)"},"antoniomontoyaflores":{"srcb":"es.wikipedia, Farruco: 'Antonio Montoya Flores conocido como Farruco o El Farruco (Pozuelo de Alarcon, 18 de abril de 1935 - Sevilla, 17 de diciembre de 1997)'; El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco agrees on both years","srcd":"es.wikipedia, Farruco: died Sevilla, 17 de diciembre de 1997","blurb":"He took his stage name from his mother, La Farruca, and his losses early: widowed at sixteen when a train struck the car carrying his first wife, the dancer Pastora Amaya, and driven from the stage for a time by his son's death on a motorcycle. Between the griefs he danced in the companies of Lola Flores and Manolo Caracol, took the 1970 Premio Nacional de Baile, and filmed for Saura's Flamenco in 1995. The patriarch of gitano baile ran his Seville escuela until the day he died in 1997; his grandchildren are still its output.","src":"Instituto Andaluz del Flamenco (Junta de Andalucia), biographical notes PDF 'Antonio Montoya Flores Farruco' (juntadeandalucia.es/cultura/flamenco) - b. Pozuelo de Alarcon 18 Apr 1935, d. Seville 17 Dec 1997; patriarch of gitano baile, Premio Nacional de Baile 1970, directed his escuela taller until his death"},"yoneyamamamako":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia ヨネヤマママコ: 1935年3月15日, 本名 米山ママコ, パントマイミスト・振付師","srcd":"ja.wikipedia ヨネヤマママコ: 2023年9月20日","blurb":"Trained in ballet by her father and in modern dance in Eguchi's line, she debuted in pantomime in 1954, the year before Marceau first played Japan. Hijikata passed through the Tokyo studio she ran with Imai Shigeyuki and choreographed a scene of their 1958 Ainu epic Hanchikik. She taught mime at UCLA through the 1960s, came home in 1972 to found Mamako the Mime Studio, and built a form of her own folding pantomime into song and story, practised until her death in 2023, at 88.","src":"Dance Archive Network"},"josecarrasco":{"blurb":"A gitano bailaor from Triana, remembered as the trunk of the Carrasco dance dynasty. His daughter Manuela — who taught herself from what she watched at home, over her parents' initial objections — became 'La Diosa' of flamenco dance, and the record of El Sordo's own dancing thinned to a patronymic in her biographies. The style he housed now outweighs every surviving account of the man.","src":"Vida Flamenca"},"trishabrown":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Trisha Brown: November 25, 1936","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Trisha Brown: March 18, 2017","blurb":"At Halprin's she thrust a broom forward so hard the momentum carried her whole body through the air; Forti still held the image decades later. Against turning out the lights and going 'gah-gah in circles' she argued for structured improvisation — rules first, freedom inside them — then spent the seventies making gravity negotiable: Man Walking Down the Side of a Building (1970), Walking on the Wall, and the Accumulation series' slow arithmetic of gestures.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"davidgordon":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, David Gordon (choreographer): July 14, 1936","srcd":"en.wikipedia, David Gordon (choreographer): January 29, 2022","blurb":"His first choreography, made under James Waring in 1960, opened with him dropping an armful of rubber balls, waiting until they stopped rolling, and walking off. In Dunn's class he 'sought ways to beat the system,' answering a Satie assignment by ignoring the music. The Pick Up Performance Company he founded in 1971 ran on talk, metal folding chairs, and pedestrian movement — 'mundane means to a magical end' — with Valda Setterfield at its center, from Chair (1974) to an Emmy for Made in USA.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1; also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1; also Banes, Terpsichore in Sneakers (1980)"},"eugeniobarba":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Eugenio Barba: born 29 October 1936","blurb":"An Italian who worked as a welder and sailor in Norway, went to Warsaw for theatre school, and left after a year to spend three with Grotowski in Opole. Back in Oslo he and the writer Jens Bjørneboe assembled a company from students who had failed the State Theatre School's admission test: Odin Teatret, founded 1 October 1964, later permanently based in Holstebro, Denmark. His ISTA, founded 1979, made the comparison of performers across cultures into a discipline — theatre anthropology.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (Routledge, 2003), Name Glossary"},"antoniogades":{"srcb":"es.wikipedia, Antonio Gades: 'Antonio Esteve Rodenas (Elda, Alicante, 14 de noviembre de 1936-Madrid, 20 de julio de 2004)'. El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco gives the same year but the 16th of November","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Antonio Gades: 20 July 2004","blurb":"Born Antonio Esteve Ródenas in Elda, he joined Pilar López's company at fifteen and spent eight years absorbing her discipline. He co-founded the Ballet Nacional de España in 1978, and with Carlos Saura and Cristina Hoyos fixed his Blood Wedding and Carmen on film — the reference recordings of staged flamenco. A communist to the end, he received the Order of José Martí from Fidel Castro weeks before his death in 2004; his ashes rest in a revolutionary mausoleum in Santiago de Cuba.","src":"The Guardian 2004"},"eleopomare":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Eleo Pomare: 20 October 1937","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Eleo Pomare: 8 August 2008","blurb":"Raised in Cartagena and Panama until he reached New York at ten, he trained at a High School of Performing Arts that barred jazz classes outright and forbade its students to choreograph to jazz. He had his own company by 1958; a Whitney fellowship then took him to Europe from 1961 to 1964, which ended badly — 'I was expelled from the Joos school. I couldn't take that dogmatic bull.' Blues for the Jungle (New York, 1966) put slave auction, prison, tenement and junkie onstage in six unsoftened scenes.","src":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search"},"rodrodgers":{"srcb":"Encyclopedia.com's entry 'Rodgers, Rod 1937-': 'Born on December 4, 1937, in Cleveland, Ohio, Rodgers grew up in Detroit, in a family of professional dancers'. The Los Angeles Times obituary of 30 March 2002 is headed 'Rod Rodgers, 64' and says he 'was born in Cleveland and grew up in Detroit' — 64 in March 2002 fits a December 1937 birth","srcd":"Los Angeles Times, Lewis Segal, 'Rod Rodgers, 64; Black Dance Pioneer', 30 March 2002, read in full: 'Choreographer Rod Rodgers, an important transitional figure in the history of African American concert dance, has died. He was 64. Rogers died Sunday in a Manhattan hospital of complications from a stroke' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"His parents were professional dancers who took their three children round the chitlin circuit, and he grew up in Detroit studying with teachers trained by Dunham, Primus and Holm. In New York he worked with Mary Anthony, Erick Hawkins and Charles Weidman, then made rhythm the subject: in Tangents (1968) three dancers handle yard-long sticks, and for stretches the only sound is the sticks swishing and striking the floor. Black artists, he argued, must stay in the front rank of experiment.","src":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 8 —"},"ryszardcieslak":{"srcb":"pl.wikipedia, Ryszard Cieslak: 'ur. 9 marca 1937 w Kaliszu', aktor Teatru Laboratorium","srcd":"pl.wikipedia, Ryszard Cieslak: 'zm. 15 czerwca 1990 w Houston'","blurb":"Grotowski's model actor. He joined the Theatre of 13 Rows in 1961; his Constant Prince is remembered as the first realisation of the 'total act', and after the Laboratory Theatre's 1969 American season New York critics named him the year's best off-Broadway actor. He stayed until the company dissolved in 1984, led training and workshops across the world, then built the blind king Dhritarashtra for Brook's Mahabharata. He died in Houston in 1990; many believed the years without Grotowski's work had already devastated him.","src":"Brockett, Century of Innovation, 2nd ed. (Allyn and Bacon, 1991): Grotowski"},"mariomaya":{"srcb":"es.wikipedia, Mario Maya: Mario Maya Fajardo, Cordoba, 23 de octubre de 1937","srcd":"es.wikipedia, Mario Maya: Sevilla, 27 de septiembre de 2008","blurb":"Raised in Granada's Sacromonte caves, where his mother La Rana performed, he reached Madrid on an English painter's patronage and returned a maker of protest theatre. Camelamos naquerar (1976), built on a text by the poet José Heredia Maya, put the marginalization of his fellow Gitanos on the flamenco stage. Between the Trío Madrid with El Güito and Carmen Mora, the school he opened in Seville in 1983, and his dancer daughter Belén, he treated flamenco as a company art with something to say; Spain gave him the National Dance Award twice.","src":"RomArchive"},"dudleywilliams":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Dudley Williams (dancer): 'Dudley Eugene Williams (August 18, 1938 - May 2015) was an American modern dancer who performed with the Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater from 1964 to 2005'; the NYPL finding aid for the Dudley Williams papers gives '(1938-2015)'","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Dudley Williams (dancer): died May 2015 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A New Yorker who was studying and performing with May O'Donnell while still in high school, joined Martha Graham's company in 1961, and from 1963 to 1968 danced for Graham and Alvin Ailey at once — two of the field's most demanding repertories in a single working week. He became famed for the solo I Wanna Be Ready in Ailey's Revelations, and died in 2015 at the Westbeth Artists Community.","src":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 8 — Talley Beatty, listing the cast on whom he built The Road of the Phoebe Snow"},"barbaradilley":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Barbara Dilley: Barbara Dilley (Lloyd), born 1938; Merce Cunningham Dance Company 1963-1968","blurb":"A Cunningham dancer (as Barbara Lloyd, 1963–68) who sat with her baby during Paxton's Afternoon — 'a sweet transaction between mother, son, sky, and trees,' Jill Johnston wrote. She improvised with the Grand Union and was the colleague Paxton invited into the first contact improvisation workshop in 1972. She took the Judson inheritance where no peer did: Naropa, the Buddhist university in Boulder — directing dance from 1974, president 1985–93, teaching 'embodied awareness.'","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), chs. 2, 6; also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), chs. 2, 6; also Banes, Terpsichore in Sneakers (1980)"},"ruthemerson":{"srcb":"Susan McKenzie's memorial page 'Ruth Emerson Wortis 1938-2015', reproducing the family memoriam: 'The youngest of four children, Ruth was born on Mar. 16, 1938, in Palo Alto, CA, to Robert (Bob) Emerson, noted biophysicist...'. Her papers are the Ruth Emerson Wortis fonds at Simon Fraser University (MsC-185), whose biographical note gives the same date","srcd":"Susan McKenzie's memorial page: 'Ruth Emerson (Wortis), dancer, choreographer, dance educator, and one of the pioneers of Postmodern dance, died Sept. 13, 2015, in New Westminster, BC Canada, from the effects of Alzheimer's disease' — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A Radcliffe mathematics graduate, math-textbook coauthor, and a Quaker active in the General Strike for Peace, she arrived in Dunn's class still wanting 'to be pretty and famous and do hard steps' — and found it a relief to work from paper without being told she was doing it wrong. She danced Timepiece at the hot-evening audition Al Carmines approved in ten minutes, and performed a score Elaine Summers made from a two-year-old's scrawled drawing, adding a minute of stillness to each section.","src":"Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"yoshitoohno":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 大野慶人: 1938年〈昭和13年〉7月15日, 大野一雄の次男","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 大野慶人: 2020年〈令和2年〉1月8日","blurb":"He was the boy in Forbidden Colours in 1959, twenty-one and barely trained — Hijikata preferred amateur bodies — and it was he who placed the white chicken. He studied mime with Oikawa, danced Ophelia in a studio Hamlet, left the stage for years alongside his father, then helped design the 1977 solo that made Kazuo Ohno world-famous. As master teacher at the family studio he declined to hand anything down: study by yourself, he tells students, then make your own revolution.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 13 (Schwellinger): born 1938, Ohno's second son and dance partner; ch. 2 (Arimitsu): danced the 1959 Kinjiki duet with Hijikata; chs. 43/45: master teacher at the Kazuo Ohno Dance Studio; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born 1938 in Tokyo, son of Ohno Kazuo; danced the young boy in Kinjiki (1959), directed and choreographed by Hijikata; his studies included classical ballet and pantomime with Hironobu Oikawa; performed his first solo recital in 1969 and then suspended stage appearances; returned to performance in The Dead Sea with Ohno Kazuo in 1985 and has directed all his father's performances since; in 1998 performed a solo recital based on manuscripts left unpublished by the kabuki scholar Gunji Masakatsu; teaches at the Yokohama studio he and his father established. Baird (2012): he was twenty-one at Forbidden Colors, dancing the Young Man against Hijikata's Man, and in the butoh world he is known by his given name rather than his surname to avoid confusion with his father"},"birjumaharaj":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Birju Maharaj: born Brijmohan Nath Mishra, 4 February 1938","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Birju Maharaj: 17 January 2022","blurb":"On stage at seven and the presiding heir of the Kalka-Bindadin line: trained by his father Achhan Maharaj and his uncles, he made the Lucknow style — the animated face, the light feet under heavy bells — the international image of kathak. Counted by peers as that rare artist equally good as performer and teacher, he taught at India's premier institutions for decades before founding his own school, Kalashram, in Delhi in the late 1990s, and died in 2022 with the Padma Vibhushan among his honours.","src":"Al Jazeera — 'Birju Maharaj, legend of India's Kathak dance, dies at 83', 17 Jan 2022 (born into the family of kathak masters, trained by his father Acchan Maharaj; founded Kalashram in Delhi); Eastern Eye obituary, Jan 2022"},"rinaschenfeld":{"srcb":"he.wikipedia רנה שינפלד: 'נולדה ב-15 בדצמבר 1938'; en.wikipedia, Rina Schenfeld gives the bare year 1938","blurb":"Batsheva's founding leading dancer, Tel Aviv-born and trained in New York at Graham's school and Juilliard under Horst, Tudor, and Craske, she carried the company's principal roles — Graham's, Robbins's, Cranko's, Tetley's, Cohan's — for its first fourteen years while choreographing for it herself, from Jephthah's Daughter in 1965. In 1979 she left and founded the Rina Schenfeld Dance Theatre.","src":"Batsheva Archive, 'Rina Schenfeld' (archive.batsheva.co.il) and official biography (rinaschenfeld.co.il): born Tel Aviv; studied at Graham's School and Juilliard, with Louis Horst, Antony Tudor and Margaret Craske; Batsheva founding member and leading dancer until 1978, performed main roles by Graham, Robbins, Cranko, Tetley, Cohan; founded the Rina Schenfeld Dance Theatre in 1979"},"lindsaykemp":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Lindsay Kemp: Lindsay Keith Kemp, 3 May 1938","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Lindsay Kemp: 24 August 2018","blurb":"He studied dance with Hilde Holger and mime with Marceau, then made himself a genre between mime, dance and theatre. Flowers (1974), his mime-and-music staging of Genet's Our Lady of the Flowers with himself as Divine, toured the world for years. As a teacher he marked pop as deeply as theatre: Bowie and Kate Bush were students, he staged and performed in the Ziggy Stardust concerts of 1972, and Bush wrote 'Moving' as his tribute. He left England in 1979 and died in Livorno in 2018.","src":"The Guardian 2018"},"vazhuvoorsamaraj":{"srcd":"narthaki.com, Obituaries 2004: 'Bharatanatyam guru Vazhuvoor R Samraj, aged 66, passed away in Chennai on July 17, 2004. He was the son of noted guru Vazhuvoor Ramiah Pillai'; the site's master obituary index repeats 'Vazhuvoor R Samraj, Bharatanatyam guru, July 17, 2004 aged 66'. The research pass had no death year","blurb":"A bharatanatyam guru of the Vazhuvoor name known to the record almost entirely through one student: Shobana Jeyasingh, whose company biography names him as her teacher. Like many nattuvanars of his generation he left little paper behind him — no reliable dates, no documented roster of students — but the training he gave is audible in an unexpected register: it became the classical ground under one of Britain's most formally adventurous choreographers.","src":"Shobana Jeyasingh Dance — 'Shobana Jeyasingh CBE' company profile (names Vazhuvoor Samaraj Pillai as her Bharata Natyam teacher)"},"johannkresnik":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Johann Kresnik: Johann 'Hans' Kresnik, 12 December 1939","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Johann Kresnik: 27 July 2019","blurb":"He called it choreographic theatre, not dance theatre, and made about a hundred full-length pieces on subjects dance was not supposed to touch: Ulrike Meinhof, Sylvia Plath, Frida Kahlo, and the Germany that produced them. By his own account he was three when he saw Slovenian partisans shoot his father, a Wehrmacht soldier. He ran the Bremen company for a decade from 1968, then Heidelberg and the Volksbuehne, and was nicknamed the Berserker.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 6 (From Ausdruckstanz to Tanztheater)"},"stevepaxton":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Steve Paxton: Steven Douglas Paxton, January 21, 1939","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Steve Paxton: February 20, 2024","blurb":"A Tucson gymnast who danced for Limón and Cunningham and spent Dunn's class flushing out his 'why-nots' — one dance was him sitting on a bench eating a sandwich. Grand Union confirmed his sense that even the postmoderns ran 'alchemical dictatorships,' so in 1972 he set eleven Oberlin students hurling into falls, named the work contact improvisation because it 'accurately and objectively described what we were doing,' and policed nothing but safety — mysticism, he wrote, was 'horse-drivel.'","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1; also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 1; also Banes, Terpsichore in Sneakers (1980)"},"arianemnouchkine":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ariane Mnouchkine: born 3 March 1939","blurb":"She studied psychology at the Sorbonne and trained with Lecoq in 1967–68, but her real instrument was the collective: Théâtre du Soleil, founded 1964 and run on the principles of a socialist people's theatre after Jean Vilar. In the 1970s she turned to Asian performance — not to resuscitate ancient forms for Western audiences, she insisted, but to reinvent their rules for the contemporary world — and for twenty years made her theatre with the writer and philosopher Hélène Cixous.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"pinabausch":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Pina Bausch: Philippine 'Pina' Bausch, 27 July 1940","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Pina Bausch: 30 June 2009","blurb":"Trained by Jooss at Folkwang, then Juilliard, then Wuppertal from 1973, where she covered the stage in dirt, leaves and water and made audiences watch what people do to each other. Her 1975 Sacre answers Wigman's: the same libretto, but the victim dies for nothing, and the same gesture reads as authority on a man and as vulnerability on a woman. Manning suspects her covert subject is not gender but history — the parents' generation, in mid-century spike heels and evening gowns, playing victim and victimiser.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 6 (From Ausdruckstanz to Tanztheater)"},"lucindachilds":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Lucinda Childs: born June 26, 1940","blurb":"Judith Dunn's student at Sarah Lawrence, she made Judson solos Paxton remembered as 'like haikus. Terse.' In Street Dance (1964) she disappeared down the freight elevator; a tape sent the audience to the window to find her tiny figure in a doorway on East Broadway, her recorded voice still in the room. Her company (1973) turned the coolness geometric — diagrammed paths, repeating phrases — through Einstein on the Beach (1976) and Dance (1979), live dancers doubled by Sol LeWitt's filmed ones.","src":"Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 3"},"yaminikrishnamurthy":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Yamini Krishnamurthy: Mungara Yamini Krishnamurthy, 20 December 1940","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Yamini Krishnamurthy: 3 August 2024","blurb":"Kalakshetra-schooled from the age of five under Rukmini Devi, she then went back out of the institution to the hereditary masters — Ellappa Pillai, Kittappa Pillai, Gowri Ammal among them — and from her 1957 Madras debut made herself the presiding soloist of both bharatanatyam and kuchipudi, carrying the southern forms north to Delhi. Tirupati's temple named her asthana nartaki, dancer to the deity; the state gave her every Padma in ascending order, ending with the Vibhushan in 2016.","src":"The Tribune (India) — 'End of an era: Bharatanatyam legend Yamini Krishnamurti dies at 84', Aug 2024 (born 20 Dec 1940; trained at Kalakshetra; Padma Vibhushan, Sangeet Natak Akademi Award 1977)"},"deborahhay":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Deborah Hay: born 1941","blurb":"Her mother taught ballet, tap, and acrobatics in a Brooklyn synagogue basement; at Connecticut College she snuck into the balcony at night, lying on her stomach, to watch Cunningham rehearse. The self-described baby of the Judson group, she toured Japan with Cunningham's company in 1964, then walked away from the centers: in Vermont she made ten Circle Dances on consecutive nights with no audience, and in Austin from 1976 she developed 'playing awake,' choreography made on untrained performers.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 2; also Banes, Democracy's Body (1993), ch. 2; also Banes, Terpsichore in Sneakers (1980)"},"twylatharp":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Twyla Tharp: born July 1, 1941","blurb":"She studied with Graham, Cunningham, and Taylor while still at Barnard, then built a language that ignored the border war: ballet's discipline threaded with running, walking, and skipping. Push Comes to Shove (1976), which set Baryshnikov loose on Mozart interleaved with Joplin rags at American Ballet Theatre, made the crossover between modern dance and ballet a fact rather than a manifesto.","src":"Academy of Achievement, 'Twyla Tharp' (achievement.org): born July 1, 1941; studied with Graham, Cunningham, Taylor and Hawkins while at Barnard; fused classical discipline with natural movement, combining ballet technique with running, walking, skipping; Push Comes to Shove for Baryshnikov at American Ballet Theatre (1976)"},"carlottaikeda":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Carlotta Ikeda: born Sanae Ikeda, 19 February 1941","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Carlotta Ikeda: 24 September 2014","blurb":"Born Ikeda Sanae, Graham-trained, and inside Dairakudakan when Maro told his members to go and start their own troupes — so in 1974 she started Ariadone, all women, with Ko Murobushi directing and choreographing for it. In January 1978 she, Murobushi and Yumiko Yoshioka gave the first recorded butoh performance in Europe; Baudrillard called what he saw a theatre of revulsion, convulsion and repulsion. From 1979 she worked out of France, and stayed a fixture there until her death in 2014.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 20 (Aihara) table: 1941-2014, Ariadone 1974; ch. 24 (Centonze): born Ikeda Sanae, Graham-trained, Dairakudakan member, moved to Europe 1979; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): Murobushi choreographed for the all-female company Ariadone and for Ikeda specifically after moving to Paris; in 1978 Ikeda performed with Murobushi and Yoshioka Yumiko in Le Dernier Eden in Paris; Furukawa Anzu was among the artists who worked with her"},"meredithmonk":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Meredith Monk: Meredith Jane Monk, born November 20, 1942","blurb":"She reached New York just as the Judson collective was dissolving — Philip Corner said she 'used the structure that had been set up' — and turned its permissions back toward the spectacle the founders had forsworn. Juice (1969) was a theater cantata for eighty-five voices; The House (1968) became her company; and the voice itself, stretched through extended techniques she first developed alone, became her primary instrument, from Dolmen Music to the opera Atlas (1991).","src":"Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"douglasdunn":{"srcb":"His own company site, douglasdunndance.com/about: 'Douglas Dunn (born October 19, 1942, Palo Alto, California) is an American dancer and choreographer based in New York City'. Not to be confused with the Scottish poet Douglas Dunn, who was also born in 1942 and who dominates the search results","blurb":"A Californian who did not start dancing until he was in college, he danced for Cunningham and for Yvonne Rainer alongside him from 1968 to 1970, then gave the Grand Union six years. He answered improvisation's noise with stillness: for 101 (1974) he lay motionless, eyes closed, on a scaffold for hours daily over two months, a Performance Exhibit visitors wandered through. Lazy Madge, begun in the spring of 1976, went the other way — dancers chose from accumulated material and placed themselves, and the rules shifted as the group decided.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990)"},"pratappawar":{"srcb":"narthaki.com, Obituaries 2026: 'Kathak guru PRATAP PAWAR (20 May 1942 - 23 June 2026) passed away in London. Born in Dewas, Madhya Pradesh, he was the first disciple of Pandit Birju Maharaj'; Ashish Mohan Khokar's tribute of 26 June 2026 on the same site repeats the dates. Not the industrialist Pratap Govindrao Pawar, who has the en.wikipedia article under that name","blurb":"One of twenty-six children from Dhar who became Birju Maharaj's first ganda-bandha disciple — the tied thread that makes a student kin — and then took the Lucknow gharana abroad the hard way: three years teaching in Guyana, then Trinidad, then London, his home for over thirty years. There he trained Akram Khan in kathak from the age of seven; the Padma Shri found him overseas.","src":"Ashish Mohan Khokar, 'Pratap Pawar: Global Guru of Kathak' — The Dance History Column, narthaki.com (born 20 May 1942; first ganda-bandha student of Birju Maharaj; settled in London; trained Akram Khan)"},"rahamimron":{"srcb":"he.wikipedia רחמים רון: 'רחמים רון (נולד ב-1942) הוא רקדן לשעבר, כוריאוגרף ומורה למחול' — born 1942; one of Batsheva's first dancers","blurb":"Born in Cairo in 1942, a Batsheva founder from the company's first 1964 performances, he was sent on scholarship to the Martha Graham School and crossed all the way over, dancing in Graham's own company in 1967 and 1968 before returning to Tel Aviv. For Batsheva he later choreographed his own dances, Fantasia (1977) and Daughters of Shiloh (1979) among them.","src":"Batsheva Archive, 'Rahamim Ron' (archive.batsheva.co.il): born 1942 in Cairo; founding member of Batsheva from its first 1964 performances; studied at the Martha Graham School on scholarship and danced in Graham's company 1967-1968; choreographed Fantasia (1977) and Daughters of Shiloh (1979) for Batsheva"},"elguito":{"srcb":"ABC, 'Muere El Guito, la ultima leyenda del baile flamenco' (9 January 2025): 'este arte, que el empezo a practicar poco despues de nacer en el barrio madrileno de El Rastro en 1942'. The same piece calls him 83, which does not divide — 1942 to January 2025 is 82, the age this map's blurb gives","srcd":"ABC, 9 January 2025: 'Eduardo Serrano Iglesias, conocido como El Guito, ha muerto en Villalba... por una neumonia que no ha podido superar'","blurb":"A Rastro kid nicknamed by his sister for his dark skin, dancing from age four with La Quica, in Antonio Marín's group at eleven, in Pilar López's ballet at fourteen. Critics made him the reference for the soleá — 'presence, seriousness, elegance' — and Paris named him best dancer of the Theatre of Nations season in 1959. Through the Trío Madrid with Mario Maya and Carmen Mora and Gades's Ballet Nacional, he carried a whole era of male dancing; his death in January 2025, at eighty-two, was mourned as the closing of it.","src":"ExpoFlamenco"},"reinhildhoffmann":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Reinhild Hoffmann: born 1 November 1943 in Sorau","blurb":"Folkwang-trained, taught by Jooss's dancer Hans Zuellig, and one half of the pair — with Susanne Linke — who ran the Folkwang Tanzstudio in the mid-seventies after Bausch left for Wuppertal. She then took the Bremen theatre for eight years and Bochum after that, working inside the subsidised opera-house system that Tanztheater inherited, as Manning notes, from the Nazi state's reorganisation of German dance.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 6 (From Ausdruckstanz to Tanztheater)"},"judithjamison":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Judith Jamison: Judith Ann Jamison, May 10, 1943","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Judith Jamison: November 9, 2024","blurb":"Marion Cuyjet's Judimar student from the age of six, she was spotted by Agnes de Mille in a 1964 master class, danced for American Ballet Theatre, then joined Alvin Ailey's company in 1965. Cry (1971), the sixteen-minute solo Ailey made as a birthday gift for his mother and dedicated to Black women everywhere, became her signature. When Ailey died in 1989 she took over the company and led it for twenty-two years; Kennedy Center honors and the National Medal of Arts arrived while she ran it.","src":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search: Ailey made"},"akirakasai":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 笠井叡: 1943年11月25日 — butoh dancer, choreographer and eurythmist (the en.wikipedia 'Akira Kasai' is a Communist Party politician, a different man)","blurb":"He is why the word exists in its current sense: in 1966 he asked the group to drop buyo for butoh, to mark a dance that paid as much attention to the body as to the making of the dance. He had met Hijikata in 1963 and spent their first three hours on Huysmans, danced in the early works, then left for fifteen years of eurythmy in Germany. He holds that butoh is not a genre but an attitude, rejects some of Hijikata's own late dances as butoh, and argues the form is Western at root, because it needs a Western right and wrong to rebel against.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), Introduction and ch. 21 (Nicely): born 1943; claims to have suggested the term butoh; Tenshi-kan 1971-1979; Stuttgart Eurythmeum from 1979; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): Kasai claimed credit for the resurrection of the word butoh — in 1966 he wanted to designate a dance in which one paid as much attention to the body as to the making of the dance, and proposed that the group use a word other than the standard buyo; he first met Hijikata in 1963 and spent three hours discussing Huysmans's Against Nature with him; danced a rubber-hose duet with Ishii Mitsutaka in Rose-colored Dance (1965); he holds that butoh is not a formal genre but an attitude, rejects some of Hijikata's own later dances as butoh, and argues that butoh is fundamentally Western in origin because it needs a Western idea of right and wrong to rebel against. Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): he teaches what he calls floating power and the community body, and told her that if we are not dancing with a consciousness of community we are not dancing at all"},"natsunakajima":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Nakajima Natsu: 'Nakajima Natsue (中島夏枝; 1943 - 3 March 2024), known professionally as Nakajima Natsu (中嶋夏), was a Japanese dancer, choreographer and teacher, known for being one of the first female butoh dancers. Nakajima founded the Muteki-sha dance group in 1969'","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Nakajima Natsu: died 3 March 2024 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"A student of both Hijikata and Ohno, she founded Muteki-sha in 1969 and carried butoh out of Japan earlier than her teachers did. Eugenio Barba watched her working in Bologna in 1990 and wrote the method down: immobile poses sculpted into the body, assembled without a break into a single sequence, then danced three times over as though the surrounding space were stone, then liquid, then air. Her first choreography Niwa (1982) and the Noh-derived Sumida River she made for a Canadian dancer in 2000 both work that interval. She died in Mexico City in 2024.","src":"Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): a student of Hijikata and Ohno and one of the core founders of butoh; her first butoh choreography was Niwa (The Garden, 1982); she carried butoh out of Japan early, through performances and teaching in Europe, the United States and Canada; she made Sumida River (2000) for Denise Fujiwara of Toronto; Hijikata wrote her a letter in 1984, two years before his death, on the 'collected unconscious body'. Baird (2012): Eugenio Barba watched her work at ISTA Bologna in July 1990 and described her method — a series of immobile poses sculpted into the body, assembled one after another without interruption, then repeated three times as though moving through stone, through liquid and through air"},"maroakaji":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 麿赤兒: 本名 大森宏, 1943年2月23日 — actor, butoh dancer, head of Dairakudakan","blurb":"He quit Waseda almost at once for drama school, made his name as an actor in Kara Juro's tent theatre, and founded Dairakudakan in 1972 under a motto he coined so as not to borrow Hijikata's word: tenputenshiki, being born into this world is a talent in itself. What he took from Hijikata was a lesson in walking that left him unable to walk at all, and out of it the idea of the stray body. From 1974 he pushed his own members out to found companies; Sankai Juku, Ariadone and Byakko-sha all came from that instruction.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 20 (Aihara): born 1943; Jokyo Gekijo actor; founded Dairakudakan 1972; tenputenshiki; one person, one troupe from 1974; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): a friend and disciple of Hijikata who, with the playwright Kara Juro, met Noguchi Michizo sometime after 1965 — Hijikata's association with the two of them, from around 1965-66, coincides with the turn of the nightclub routine into the Gold Dust Show; on Noguchi's liquid-body idea Maro said that what they actually studied was the way movements flowed in order to be able to interrupt them, which is the fragmentation of the body in butoh"},"philippegaulier":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Philippe Gaulier: 4 March 1943","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Philippe Gaulier: 9 February 2026","blurb":"A one-time teacher at Lecoq's school who left to run his own in Paris and then London, where the first course is always Le Jeu: four weeks on the pleasure of play, without which he considers a student unequipped for any dramatic territory. His via negativa can be harsh in the moment, but the failure that draws his verdict is almost always a failure to play; he demands lightness even in tragedy and detests mime outright — 'moi, je déteste le mime'. With Monika Pagneux he trained much of Complicité's casts.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"tonibasil":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Toni Basil: Antonia Christina Basilotta, born September 22, 1943","blurb":"Daughter of an orchestra leader and a vaudeville performer, she assembled and managed the 1973 group that became The Lockers, putting street dancers on national stages beside her own film and television choreography. For Saturday Night Live in 1978 she set a 'Swan Lake' that spliced ballet onto street dance; in 1982 her video for 'Mickey' hit number one with Basil wearing her own high-school cheerleading uniform, two decades after graduation. At seventy-five she was still choreographing, for Tarantino's Once Upon a Time in Hollywood.","src":"The Campbellock Timeline, campbellock.dance (official Don Campbell site) - Basil assembled, managed and danced in the 1973 Campbellockers who became The Lockers; CBS News Sunday Morning profile, Feb 2020 (six-decade choreography career, American Graffiti to Once Upon a Time in Hollywood); Chicago Sun-Times Shabba-Doo obituary, Dec 2020, lists her among The Lockers' founders"},"susannelinke":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Susanne Linke: born 19 June 1944","blurb":"She got to Wigman at the end — an old woman who could hardly stand, and who gave her a correction she still remembered twenty years later — then moved to Folkwang for the ballet the Berlin school could not give her. Her solos work against objects: a cast-iron bathtub as partner, uterus and grave; a length of cloth she drowns in. On the day Germany reunified she told an audience in Chicago that Central Europeans do not know who they are, and so their dance expresses what they are not and want to be.","src":"Manning, Ecstasy and the Demon (2006), ch. 6 (From Ausdruckstanz to Tanztheater)"},"mitsurusasaki":{"srcb":"Folkwang Universitat der Kunste obituary 'Zum Tod von Mitsuru Sasaki' (29 January 2026), which names his solo 'I was born in 1944'; Tanzweb.org states it outright: 'Mitsuru Sasaki, 1944 in Japan geboren, hat von 1980 bis 1988 als Choreograph mit dem Folkwang Tanzstudio gearbeitet'","srcd":"Folkwang Universitat der Kunste, 'Zum Tod von Mitsuru Sasaki': 'Die Folkwang Universitat der Kunste trauert um den Tanzer und Choreographen Mitsuru Sasaki, der am 21. Januar 2026 im Alter von 81 Jahren verstorben ist'","blurb":"He danced butoh with Ohno Kazuo for years before meeting Susanne Linke in Paris in 1980, who brought him to Essen; there he took classes from Jean Cebron and Hans Zuellig, the Jooss line's teachers. He danced in Linke's work and became the Folkwang studio's other house choreographer. The traffic on this map mostly runs Germany to Japan; Sasaki is one of the places where it runs back.","src":"Oriente Occidente, artist page for 'Gilles et Jeannes' — https://www.orienteoccidente.it/en/people/events/gilles-et-jeannes-marathon (Japanese; danced butoh with Kazuo Ohno; met Susanne Linke in Paris in 1980 and was invited to the Folkwang-Tanzstudio; studied with Jean Cebron, Hans Zuellig and Brian Bertscher; one of the studio's two main choreographers alongside Linke); also Folkwang Universitaet, Folkwang Tanzstudio history (Sasaki listed among its choreographers)"},"blondellcummings":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Blondell Cummings: October 27, 1944","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Blondell Cummings: August 30, 2015","blurb":"A founding member of Meredith Monk's company The House: in Monk's Paris/Venice/Milan/Chacon trilogy she was the exotic visitor who comes back as the sorceress. She found her own signature alone in a kitchen — Chicken Soup (1981), built from memories of her grandmother, a kitchen chair, a scrubbed floor, a frying pan — which the NEA named an American Masterpiece in 2006. Raised in Harlem, trained at NYU and the Graham school, she formed the Cycle Arts Foundation by 1978 to pull artists of different disciplines toward each other.","src":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 8 — on the breadth of her training"},"thomasleabhart":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Thomas Leabhart: born 1944; studied with Etienne Decroux in Paris 1968-1972","blurb":"An American who studied under Decroux at his Paris school from 1968 to 1972 and has spent the decades since making corporeal mime teachable and legible from a California campus: resident artist and professor of theatre at Pomona College, editor of Mime Journal, and author of Modern and Post-Modern Mime, the standard account that traces the art from Copeau's experiments of the 1920s through Decroux, Barrault, Marceau and Lecoq.","src":"Pomona College - faculty page for Thomas G. Leabhart (pomona.edu): 'Emeritus Professor of Theatre'; studied at the 'Ecole de Mime Etienne Decroux' with Decroux 'from 1968-1972'; 'Editor, Mime Journal'; author of Modern and Post-Modern Mime (1989, 2nd ed. 1998), Etienne Decroux (2008) and A Decroux Sourcebook (2009)"},"bernieschurch":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority record GND 1073144054, Schurch, Bernie (variant Schurch, Berchtold): date of birth 1944-08-03, place Thun; occupations Schauspieler, Pantomimus, Clown, with the note 'Mitbegrunder der Gruppe Mummenschanz'","blurb":"He met Andres Bossard at Lecoq's school in 1967; they performed as clowns, then with a show called Masks and Buffooneries, and in 1972 — joined by Floriana Frassetto — founded Mummenschanz, whose masks are everyday materials and whose form has barely needed changing in thirty years. His memory of Lecoq's teaching: criticism in two words, never showing how, never praising — 'just gave you a push in the right direction. That was his genius as a teacher.'","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"andresbossard":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority record GND 11364129X, Bossard, Andres: date of birth 1944-08-09, place Zurich","srcd":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek GND 11364129X: date of death 1992-03-25","blurb":"One third of Mummenschanz: he met Bernie Schürch at Lecoq's school in 1967, lived through Paris's upheavals of 1968–69, and from 1972 performed behind masks made of everyday materials — a slinky among them — rather than faces. Until his early death in March 1992 he was part of the trio that stayed the company's permanent creative centre; the loss threw Schürch and Frassetto into serious doubt about continuing at all. They rebuilt the work, in his absence, on the form the three had fixed.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"germaineacogny":{"srcb":"Dictionary of African Biography (Oxford University Press, 2012) — via Internet Archive full-text search","blurb":"She had pressed President Senghor to put dance inside Senegal's arts education, and he sent her to Brussels to be assessed by Maurice Bejart, who made her head of Mudra Afrique in Dakar. European staff there claimed to know African dance better than she did and tried to take over her part of the curriculum; she fought until Bejart confirmed her as sole director. In 1998 she opened Ecole des Sables at Toubab Dialaw, forty miles down the coast, where dancers from across Africa train.","src":"Susan Leigh Foster"},"dannylepkoff":{"srcb":"No birth year and no age anywhere on movementresearch.org. Both of his primary pieces there were read in full — 'The Movement of Attention: An Interview with Daniel Lepkoff' by Simone Forti (MRPJ #29, Spring 2006, the conversation dated December 2004) and his own essay 'What is Release Technique?' (MRPJ #19, Fall/Winter 1999) — and both date his training rather than his birth: he studied 'intensively' with Mary Fulkerson 'between the years of 1970-1974', met Paxton's contact improvisation about a year into that, and his contributor note gives 'Anatomical Release Technique with Mary Fulkerson 1970-75' and contact improvisation 'since 1972'. The map's 1947 remains first activity minus twenty-five and still draws as an estimate","blurb":"A student of Mary Fulkerson's release technique at Rochester, he arrived at the 1972 Chinatown loft already knowing how to fall: the loft 'was a massage hospital after every performance,' he remembered, except for the Rochester people. In Rome the next summer Paxton maneuvered and carried him at length until the balance reversed and he lifted Paxton. He danced in Free Lance Dance and in 1977 co-founded Contact Collaborations, the nonprofit behind Contact Quarterly and the growing video archive.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990)"},"mintanaka":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Min Tanaka (田中泯): born March 10, 1945","blurb":"A basketball player who trained ten years in ballet and modern dance and then, in 1974, walked away from all of it to dance naked in public — as often as five times a day, once with his wife and child seated naked on the lawn outside the hall. He called it hyperdance; the line that survives in several translations is that he does not dance in the place, he dances the place. In 1985 he moved company and training to a farm at Hakushu and spent eleven years farming for a living, which he treats as the real experiment.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), Introduction and ch. 50 (Fuller): born 1945; abandoned modern dance career 1974 for naked hyperdance; Body Weather; Maijuku 1981-1996; Hakushu farm 1985; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): Tanaka"},"matsek":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Mats Ek: born 18 April 1945","blurb":"Son of Birgit Cullberg and the actor Anders Ek, he came to dance sideways, after years directing marionette and dramatic theatre in Stockholm. He joined the Cullberg Ballet as a dancer, made his first piece in 1976, then took the company over — first alongside his mother from 1978, later alone, until 1993. What made his name was rewriting the classics from inside: Giselle (1982), the title role created on Ana Laguna, then Swan Lake (1987) and Carmen (1992).","src":"Opera national de Paris, 'Mats Ek' artist page (operadeparis.fr): son of choreographer Birgit Cullberg and actor Anders Ek; joined the Cullberg Ballet 1973; international recognition for reinterpretations of Giselle (1982) and Swan Lake (1987)"},"diannemcintyre":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia: 'Dianne McIntyre (born July 1946)'. The HistoryMakers' biography record gives the day: 'Born: July 18, 1946 / Birth Location: Cleveland, Ohio', from its interview of 18 June 2004","blurb":"She started ballet at four in Cleveland after seeing Janet Collins dance in Aida, and in 1972 formed Sounds in Motion — an ensemble of musicians and dancers, not just dancers — which became Harlem's only modern dance studio for most of two decades. She and her company spent long hours improvising with free jazz players, and that work sent her back into the histories of earlier jazz dance forms. Jawole Willa Jo Zollar trained there before founding Urban Bush Women.","src":"Susan Leigh Foster"},"maryfulkerson":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Mary Fulkerson: 'Mary O'Donnell Fulkerson (1946-2020)'. Weak: the same article is filed under Category:1940 births, and its only footnote — the Dartington Trust notice — carries no birth year at all. Treat 1946 as soft","srcd":"Dartington Trust, 'Mary Fulkerson at Dartington', 14 July 2020 (read via the Wayback Machine, the live page now 404s): 'Everyone at Dartington has been saddened at the news of the death of dancer, teacher and choreographer, Mary Fulkerson'","blurb":"She taught 'release technique' at the University of Rochester — movement from anatomical imagery, softness, flow — and came to Paxton's 1972 John Weber Gallery workshop with two of her students; the releasing people, Nancy Stark Smith recalled, 'were very soft and light, very sensitive,' and David Woodberry credited her work with strongly shaping contact improvisation's development. A onetime Halprin student, she later taught extensively at Dartington College in Devon.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990); also Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"patcatterson":{"srcb":"Dictionary of Women Worldwide: 25,000 Women Through the Ages, entry 'Catterson, Pat (1946-)', via Encyclopedia.com: 'American dancer and choreographer. Born Feb 20, 1946, in Indianapolis, Indiana; dau. of ballroom dancers.' Her own site (patcatdance.com/bio) matches on both particulars: 'With parents who were a professional ballroom dancing team... Born in Indianapolis'","blurb":"Granddaughter of a vaudeville tap dancer, she taught tap in New York for two decades while making 111 works of her own. She first danced Yvonne Rainer's work in 1969, performed Trio A backwards in 1971, and folded part of it into her own Serial II in 1975; since 1999 she has been Rainer's dancer, rehearsal assistant and custodian of the early repertory. Fifty years of New York performing ended with ten farewell performances of Rainer's early works at MoMA.","src":"Movement Research, artist page 'Pat Catterson' (movementresearch.org)"},"koichitamano":{"srcd":"Dancers' Group memorial notice: 13 November 2025","blurb":"He arrived at Asbestos-kan in 1964 at eighteen and stayed ten years, earning the nickname bow-legged Nijinsky; in one Hijikata piece Nakanishi Natsuyuki painted a vagina in full colour on his back and he danced with it there. With Hiroko he carried Harupin-Ha from Tokyo to Berkeley at the end of the seventies, and the two of them became the root of West Coast butoh — a generation of Bay Area dancers date their careers to the Tamanos' kitchen. He died in 2025.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 33 (Flournoy): born near Shimada, began with Hijikata at Asbestos-kan in 1964 at age 18, studied ten years; with Hiroko root of West Coast butoh in Berkeley from 1978; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born in Shimada, Shizuoka, in 1946; his first butoh performance was in Hijikata's Barairo Dansu (Rose-Colored Dance) in 1965 in Tokyo; Hijikata anointed him"},"ichikawadanjuro12":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ichikawa Danjuro XII (十二代目市川團十郎): August 6, 1946","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Ichikawa Danjuro XII: February 3, 2013","blurb":"Twelfth bearer of kabuki's weightiest name, he climbed the ladder himself — on stage at seven in 1953, Shinnosuke in 1958, Ebizō X in 1969, Danjūrō in 1985, with naming festivities that ran for months and traveled abroad. He kept the Naritaya house's aragoto repertory, Sukeroku and Kanjinchō among it, running through a 2004 leukemia diagnosis and a return to the stage. Pneumonia killed him in February 2013 at sixty-six; the press called him a pillar of kabuki and meant the word structurally.","src":"Nippon.com, 'Kabuki Fights for its Future After Two Tragic Losses' (2013) - Ichikawa Danjuro XII died 3 Feb 2013 aged 66, twelfth bearer of the historic name and a pillar of kabuki; his son Ebizo to take over his stage name"},"osukaisamu":{"blurb":"An experienced actor before he was a butoh dancer, and one of the men around Maro when Dairakudakan formed in 1972; when the members were sent out to build their own companies, he built the one that took root in Kyoto and became Byakko-sha. Its spectacles travelled — it was Byakko-sha in the courtyard of Mexico City's anthropology museum that turned a Mexican researcher of Mesoamerican ritual to butoh on the spot. The record contradicts itself: the same book gives his birth as 1943 and as 1946, and the company's founding as 1976 and as 1977.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 20 (Aihara): Dairakudakan founding member; founded Toho Yaso Kai 1977, renamed Byakkosha 1980; ch. 38 (Seetoo) notes his Byakkosha's Asian touring; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012), notes: Osuka Isamu, the founder of the company Byakkosha; his parents lived in Hiroshima at the time of the bomb, and he has consistently maintained that his dances addressed the bomb — Baird cites him as the counter-example to his own argument that butoh is not about the atomic bombings"},"nitalittle":{"srcb":"No birth year on movementresearch.org. Her one piece there, 'Lisa Nelson in conversation with Nita Little' (Critical Correspondence, 9 January 2006, recorded at the Side Step Festival in Helsinki in January 2005), has Little as the interviewer, not the subject; the only thing in it that touches her chronology is Nelson placing her at Bennington 'a couple of years' after Nelson's own first years there, which Nelson gives as '66-'68. That is a college arrival, not a birth year, and nothing here is built back from it. The map's 1947 remains first activity minus twenty-five and still draws as an estimate","blurb":"A Bennington student in the 1972 John Weber Gallery group, she toured the West Coast in 1973 under the banner 'You come. We'll show you what we do' and re-formed with the others as ReUnion in 1975 — the year she floated the name Contact Core and a friend's advice to stamp '© Steve Paxton et al.' on publicity, proposals the group let die as 'not in the spirit.' One of the founding cohort's last practicing members, she later took a PhD on embodied attention and the articulation of presence.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990)"},"curtsiddall":{"blurb":"An Oberlin student in contact improvisation's first group, he arranged the reunion in Amherst at the end of the summer of 1972 that kept the scattered dancers working, toured the West Coast in 1973, and co-formed ReUnion in 1975. His definition stuck — 'the beauty of natural movement combined with full communication' — and by 1978 he had left to become a carpenter, an exit in keeping with a form that never asked anyone to stay.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990)"},"yokoashikawa":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 芦川羊子: 1947年2月23日, 千葉県出身, 本名は洋子","blurb":"She joined Asbestos-kan in 1967 at twenty, out of cabaret and night-club work, and became the body Hijikata thought through for the next twenty years: the butoh-fu were tested and fixed on her, and she took the notes. She led Hakutobo's dancers and taught its students; in rehearsal she would shout choreography from the wings, or throw five-yen coins onto the stage when someone got it wrong. People who never saw Hijikata dance say they understood his work by watching her.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 22 (SU-EN on Ashikawa): born 1947 in Chiba, joined Asbestos-kan in 1967, the body through which Hijikata realized his vision for some 20 years; led Hakutobo's dancers and choreographed for Gnome-line students; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): an art student before she joined Hijikata's dance troupe, which she did in 1967 or 1968; she and Hijikata together studied paintings and observed things not usually thought worthy of dance representation — animals, the elderly — to elicit new poses, the programme that became the"},"komurobushi":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 室伏鴻: 1947年6月14日, 東京都生まれ, 1969年土方巽に師事","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 室伏鴻: 2015年6月18日, died of myocardial infarction in a Mexico City hospital while touring Latin America","blurb":"He turned up at Hijikata's studio in 1969 with a school friend and was immediately put into a horror film; his training was films and gold-paint cabaret rather than the studio discipline the core apprentices got, and he said so himself. From 1970 he went to the mountains with the yamabushi ascetics instead — fasting, waterfalls, caves — and built out of that a butoh of the outside that he carried to Europe and Latin America. He edited the butoh newspaper Violent Season, directed Ariadone, and danced his late solos half naked and silver-painted.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 24 (Centonze): 1947-2015; brief training under Hijikata only in films and gold-paint cabaret from 1969; yamabushi practice from 1970; Dairakudakan founding member 1972; directed and produced Ariadone; ch. 42 note 3 table: born 1947, apprenticeship 1969-1971; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born 1947 in Tokyo; a founding member of the long-running Dairakudakan; left the stage in 1970 to practise ascetic life and shamanism for two years as a yamabushi in the Japanese mountains; later based in Paris, where he choreographed for the all-female company Ariadone and for Carlotta Ikeda and Endo Tadashi; after 1988 focused on duo productions with Urara Kusanagi; his solo En took him to Mexico and South America, and he was invited to India in 2002; he leads the Edge company from a base in Japan, and gave the United States premiere of his solo Quick Silver at the Theater for the New City, New York, on 9 November 2007"},"jirikylian":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jiri Kylian: born 21 March 1947","blurb":"Cranko spotted him at the Royal Ballet School and took him to Stuttgart; within a few years he was running Nederlands Dans Theater, and did so for most of twenty-five years. He gave the company its shape as much as its repertory — a second troupe for young dancers, and in 1992 a third for dancers over forty, which was then unheard of. The 'black and white' ballets, Petite Mort among them, made for Mozart's bicentenary at Salzburg in 1991, are what other companies still dance.","src":"Nederlands Dans Theater, 'Jiri Kylian' (ndt.nl): trained from age nine in Prague; Royal Ballet School scholarship 1967; joined Cranko's Stuttgart company; artistic director of NDT from 1975, leading it for nearly 25 years"},"ulyssesdove":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ulysses Dove: January 17, 1947 in Columbia, South Carolina","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Ulysses Dove: June 11, 1996 in Manhattan","blurb":"A South Carolina premed who left Howard for dance, he passed through Merce Cunningham's company, joined Alvin Ailey's in 1973 and stopped performing in 1980 to assist at the Paris Opera Ballet. Of everyone who made work for the Ailey company in Ailey's lifetime, its historian judges that Dove came closest to putting queer Black desire on the concert stage. He set dances famous for speed, force and eroticism on companies across two continents, and died of AIDS in 1996, at forty-nine.","src":"DeFrantz, Dancing Revelations (2004) — via Internet Archive full-text search: of the choreographers who worked"},"tadashiendo":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Tadashi Endo: 1947, Japanese butoh dancer resident in Gottingen","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Tadashi Endo: 25 May 2025 — the research pass had no death year","blurb":"Born in Beijing to Japanese parents and trained at Vienna's Max Reinhardt Seminary from 1973 to 1976, he came to butoh out of European theatre rather than out of Japan. He met Ohno in 1989 and was asked to interpret for him; when Ohno answered a question about butoh with a dream of his mother turning into a caterpillar, Endo abandoned literal translation and rendered what he thought was meant. He apologised, and Ohno told him that was butoh. He has run MAMU in Göttingen since 1992 on that idea of the between.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 29 (van Hensbergen): met and translated for Ohno Kazuo at ImPulsTanz 1989; MA/MAMU concept and manifesto; MAMU festival in Goettingen from 1992; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born 1947, and began his life as a Japanese national living in Beijing; from 1973 to 1976 he studied at the Max Reinhardt Seminary in Vienna; in 1989 he met Ohno Kazuo, who profoundly influenced his development; he calls his work Butoh-MA and explained at the London Butoh Festival in 2005 that in Zen Buddhism ma means emptiness as well as the spaces between things — the moments when one figure changes into the next matter more to him than the figures; he heads the Butoh Center MAMU in Gottingen and directs the MAMU Festivals in Gottingen and Tokyo through his MAMU Dance Theater, and has produced work with jazz musicians including Toshinori Kondo, Aki Takase, Peter Kowald and Gunter Sommer; he says in class that he"},"dariahalprin":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Daria Halprin: born December 30, 1948","blurb":"She grew up inside her mother's work — at eleven she was lifted and carried in Birds of America — and at nineteen was recruited out of a Berkeley ceramics studio to star opposite another non-actor in Antonioni's Zabriskie Point, playing under her own name a self the Dancers' Workshop had trained: emotion wedded to action, nothing theatrical. In 1978 she co-founded the Tamalpa Institute with her mother, having trained in Gestalt therapy and built her own approach to expressive arts therapy.","src":"Worth & Poynor, Anna Halprin (2004), ch. 1"},"joanlaage":{"blurb":"She saw Dairakudakan in Tokyo in 1982 and spent the rest of the decade getting inside the line rather than alongside it: study with Kazuo Ohno and with Yoko Ashikawa, and performances in Ashikawa's student group Gnome in 1988-89, which almost no Westerner managed. She took it to Seattle in 1990, founded Dappin' Butoh, and wrote a doctorate on the body in butoh. A certified Laban analyst, she works the seam between early German modern dance and butoh, and performs as kogut, rooster, a name picked up while living in Poland.","src":"Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born 1948; first encountered butoh in 1982 when she saw Dairakudakan in Tokyo; studied under Ohno Kazuo and Ashikawa Yoko in Japan and performed in Ashikawa's group Gnome in 1988-89; moved to Seattle in 1990 and founded Dappin' Butoh; her Texas Woman's University doctoral dissertation is titled 'Embodying the Spirit: The Significance of the Body in the Contemporary Japanese Dance Movement of Butoh'; she has performed under the name kogut, Polish for rooster, since 2005, and resettled in Seattle in November 2008 after two years each in Krakow and South Korea; a certified Laban Movement analyst, she is drawn to the relationship between early German modern dance and butoh and has used her own German roots as an entrance to it"},"bishopyamada":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia ビショップ山田: 別名義 山田一平, 1948年2月17日, 舞踏家・演出家・振付家","blurb":"He walked out of a good Tokyo high school, apprenticed at Asbestos-kan, helped found Dairakudakan — and then in late 1973 left the city entirely for Tsuruoka in the north, at the foot of Mt Haguro, to test Hijikata's mythology of Tohoku by living inside it. He rented a two-storey rice granary among the paddies and rebuilt it into a studio, finished in September 1974. The first performance there, Shiokubi in October 1975, put poets on trains from Tokyo and filled the barn to the walls.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 23 (Kosuge): Hijikata apprentice from about 1968, Dairakudakan 1972, Tsuruoka end of 1973, studio completed September 1974, Shiokubi October 1975; ch. 42 note 3 table: born 1948; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012), notes: Yamada Ippei danced under the stage name Bishop Yamada; his 1992 memoir Dansaa (Dancer, Ota Shuppan) is one of Baird's first-hand sources for Hijikata's 1969-71 nightclub, Space Capsule and Art Village period, including the origin of Hijikata's stage name Nero and a dance in which a woman wore a chastity belt and a headdress of blinking lights. Fraleigh, Butoh (2010) also names him"},"katsurakan":{"blurb":"He came up through Byakko-sha, the Kyoto company of the Dairakudakan line, and has since spent eight or nine months of most years abroad — working with what he calls minority dancers in Africa, the Mediterranean, Asia, Australia and the United States, and leaving small butoh communities where none existed. Ledoh, born into Burma's Karen minority and now working out of San Francisco, calls him his butoh mentor. He also argues in public about what counts: he has said in print that Kasai's dancing is butoh only as metaphor.","src":"Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): Katsura Kan is named as a member of the radical 1970s butoh collective Byakko-Sha, and as the butoh mentor in Japan of the Karen-born San Francisco artist Ledoh, who studied intensively with him before returning to the United States; Marie-Gabrielle Rotie of Butoh UK has performed duets with him, and Lani Fand Weissbach names him among her butoh teachers"},"durgalal":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Durga Lal: Pandit Durga Lal (1948 - 21 January 1990), Kathak dancer of the Jaipur gharana, born in Mahendragarh, Rajasthan","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Durga Lal: 21 January 1990","blurb":"A Jaipur-gharana dancer from Mahendragarh, trained first by his elder brother Devi Lal and formally under Sunder Prasad, he sang and played pakhawaj as fluently as he danced. The national akademi honoured him in 1984, the Padma Shri followed, and in 1989 he danced the principal role in Ghanashyam to Ravi Shankar's score; months later he was gone, dead in January 1990 at forty-one, leaving Uma Dogra and an annual memorial festival to keep the line.","src":"Nad Sadhna (Institute of Indian Music) — 'Biography of Indian Dancers' (Jaipur gharana kathak dancer; honoured by Rajasthan Sangeet Natak Akademi 1983 and the national Sangeet Natak Akademi 1984; Padmashri)"},"richardalston":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Richard Alston (choreographer): Sir Richard Alston CBE, born 30 October 1948","blurb":"He was the first choreography student at the London Contemporary Dance School, and in 1972 founded Strider, the UK's first independent dance company. After studying at Merce Cunningham's studio in New York he took over Rambert's choreography and then its direction, turning the company towards Cunningham technique and abstraction and inviting Brown, Childs and Cunningham himself in. He was out at the end of 1992, built a second company at The Place for 26 years, and saw that one cease activity in 2020.","src":"Rambert, 'Our history' (rambert.org.uk): under Alston's direction Rambert became known for Cunningham technique and mainly abstract works; he invited Trisha Brown, Lucinda Childs and Merce Cunningham, and left Rambert at the end of 1992"},"mikhailbaryshnikov":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Mikhail Baryshnikov: Mikhail Nikolayevich Baryshnikov, born January 27, 1948","blurb":"Trained by Alexander Pushkin in Leningrad, he defected in Toronto in 1974 mid-tour and then spent a career declining the obvious next step. He was ABT's principal, took fifteen months at New York City Ballet to work under Balanchine, ran ABT for nine years, and at 42 left to make new work with Mark Morris in the White Oak Dance Project, a company formed for older dancers. Since 2005 he has run the Baryshnikov Arts Center in New York.","src":"Baryshnikov Arts Center, 'Mikhail Baryshnikov' (baryshnikovarts.org): born 1948 in Riga; spectacular Kirov career; came to the West in 1974; director and dancer of the White Oak Dance Project 1990-2002, co-founded with Mark Morris"},"komaotake":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority GND 1016441851, Yamada, Takashi Koma (variant Koma): date of birth 1948, with the note that he performs with his wife Eiko Otake as Eiko & Koma","blurb":"Half of Eiko & Koma, a duo for over forty years, and one of the two people who took Hijikata's and Ohno's studios as a starting point and then left Japan entirely. In 1972, the German teacher they had written to being too ill to take them, the pair went to Hannover on her recommendation to work with her student Manja Chmiel — an episode Eiko remembers partly as Koma throwing eggs. They have never called their work butoh, and the record of Koma apart from the duo is thin.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 14 (Elswit et al. roundtable): with Eiko went to Manja Chmiel in Hannover in 1972 on Wigman's endorsement; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): Koma, born 1948, studied with both Ohno Kazuo and Hijikata Tatsumi and, with Eiko, in the tradition of German modern dance with Manja Chmiel in Germany; the two were named MacArthur Fellows in 1996 and have received two Bessie Awards, the 2004 Samuel H. Scripps American Dance Festival Award and the 2006 Dance Magazine Award; the record Fraleigh gives is entirely of the duo, never of Koma apart from it"},"lisanelson":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Lisa Nelson: 'She was born in New York City in 1949 and currently lives in Northern Vermont'","blurb":"Improviser and videographer, she edited the founding tapes of contact improvisation — Chute, Soft Pallet — with Paxton and Steve Christiansen, remembered the early form as 'a complete folk form — social form at first' — and still never wanted to be called a contact improviser. From 1973 she developed the Tuning Scores, spontaneous composition grown from a decade of video and dance; from 1978 she co-edited Contact Quarterly, and danced the duets PA RT (1978) and Night Stand (2004) with Paxton.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990)"},"ushioamagatsu":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 天児牛大: 1949年12月31日, 本名 上島正和, 舞踏カンパニー山海塾主宰; en.wikipedia, Ushio Amagatsu gives 31 December 1949","srcd":"ja.wikipedia: 2024年3月25日, heart failure","blurb":"Three years in Dairakudakan, then Sankai Juku in November 1975 and a method he describes as subtraction — peeling off concepts, aesthetics and semiotics rather than adding them, and working the same few themes over and over. From 1980 he was in Europe, and from 1982 he made new work for the Théâtre de la Ville in Paris every other year for three decades, which is why for most of the world the white bodies suspended head-down are what butoh looks like. He died in 2024.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 25 (Iwaki): born 1949 as Ueshima Masakazu; three years in Dairakudakan; formed Sankai Juku November 1975; France from 1980, Theatre de la Ville co-productions from 1982"},"petalily":{"blurb":"An Australian in London who helped shape 1980s physical theatre — first in Three Women Mime Company, one of Britain's first all-women mime companies — then spent years working out what a clown is when comedy stops protecting it. Her answer, the Dark Clown: where the red-nose clown has no past and bounces back from every slap, the Dark Clown 'has seen it all, feels it all' and must sell its own pain for our entertainment. Solo shows from Wendy Darling (a Fringe First, 1988) to Chastity Belt (2012) carried the research.","src":"Banks, The Joke's on Us: Women in Comedy from Music Hall to the Present Day (Pandora, 1987)"},"nolarae":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Nola Rae: the article disagrees with itself — the lead says 'born 1950', the biography says 'Rae was born in Sydney, Australia, in 1949 and migrated to London with her family in 1963'. This map follows the 1949 in the body","blurb":"A Sydney-born Royal Ballet School dancer who defected to mime by way of Marceau's Paris teaching, founded the London Mime Theatre in 1974, and with Joseph Seelig instigated the London International Mime Festival, running every January since 1977. From 1990 she developed full-evening wordless comic dramas — one silent character, often a Shakespeare or a political figure, sustained over a whole night. Her MBE in 2008 was for services to drama and to mime, in that order.","src":"Lust, From the Greek Mimes to Marcel Marceau and Beyond (Scarecrow Press, 1998): the London mime festival was founded"},"williamforsythe":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, William Forsythe (choreographer): born December 30, 1949","blurb":"He kept ballet's vocabulary and discarded its assumptions — the fixed vertical, the fixed centre — so that a phrase could start anywhere in the body and keep falling. Ballett Frankfurt was the twenty-year laboratory, from Artifact (1984) through Impressing the Czar and Eidos:Telos to Decreation (2003). He wanted the thinking portable too: Improvisation Technologies, made with ZKM, teaches his analysis to anyone with a screen, and his 'choreographic objects' put it in museums, where the visitor does the moving.","src":"William Forsythe biography, williamforsythe.com (Forsythe official site): Joffrey and Stuttgart Ballet (Resident Choreographer 1976); director of Ballett Frankfurt 1984-2004; directed The Forsythe Company 2005-2015"},"hisauzawa":{"blurb":"Born into a noh family in 1949, she trained under her father Uzawa Masashi, Kanze Hisao and Kanze Tetsunojō VIII and became a shite of the Kanze school's Tessenkai — a woman holding principal roles in a profession organized around male succession. Japan designated her a holder of Important Intangible Cultural Property, and she has carried the repertory abroad, staging the warrior play Atsumori at the University of British Columbia; the prize named for her teacher Kanze Hisao is among her honors.","src":"University of British Columbia, Dept. of Theatre & Film, 'The Warrior Atsumori: Uzawa Noh' (theatrefilm.ubc.ca) - Uzawa Hisa b. 1949 into a noh family, shite of the Kanze Tessenkai school, holder of Important Intangible Cultural Property, Kanze Hisao-Hosei University Noh Theater Prize recipient"},"donaldbyrd":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Donald Byrd (choreographer): 'Byrd was born July 21, 1949, in New London, North Carolina' (the jazz trumpeter Donald Byrd, 1932-2013, is a different man)","blurb":"He calls his work a theater of disruption: dance meant to make audiences confront racism rather than observe it. The Minstrel Show (1991) put blackface itself onstage and won a Bessie; Shot (2017) and Greenwood (2019) took up a police shooting and the Tulsa massacre. After running Donald Byrd/The Group for twenty-four years in Los Angeles and New York, he moved in 2002 to lead Seattle's Spectrum Dance Theater, and took The Color Purple to Broadway in 2005.","src":"Spectrum 2023"},"takashimorishita":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 森下隆: 1950年 - , 慶應義塾大学アート・センター所員","blurb":"The reason Hijikata's work is checkable. He looked after the Asbestos-kan library and then built it into the Hijikata Tatsumi Archive at Keio University Art Center — the scrapbooks, the notation, Otsuji's Forbidden Colours photographs, the material nearly every chapter of the current scholarship cites. He has published on Hijikata's notational butoh and taken the photographs abroad himself, lecturing and mounting exhibitions at festivals as far away as Mexico City.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 32 (Rosales): director of the Hijikata Archive; archive figure credits throughout name the Hijikata Tatsumi Archive, Keio University Art Center; also Baird, Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh (2012): the photographs throughout the book are credited jointly to the Morishita Takashi Butoh Materials, NPO, and the Research Center for the Arts and Arts Administration at Keio University, which is the collection nearly every image of Hijikata's dances comes through; Morishita edited Hijikata Tatsumi no buto: Nikutai no shururearisumu, shintai no ontoroji (Okamoto Taro Museum of Art and Keio University Research Center for the Arts and Arts Administration, 2004) and co-edited the 1997 Ikeda Museum exhibition catalogue Bijutsu to buto no Hijikata Tatsumi ten; Baird takes up Morishita's reading that if one argues by analogy from anti-art, Hijikata's dance was anti-dance"},"stevenwasson":{"srcb":"Theatre de l'Ange Fou / International School of Corporeal Mime, its own Biographies page (read via the Wayback capture of 7 Sept 2024; the live site's certificate has expired): 'Born in the United States (1950), Steven Wasson is the director of the Theatre de l'Ange Fou... Coming to Paris, he was a student of Etienne Decroux and later became his assistant'. The BnF authority record ark:/12148/cb17955789f agrees: 'Wasson, Steven, 1950-....', 'Fondateur et directeur du Theatre de l'ange fou'. Not the Dayton, Ohio composer Steven Wasson (LC n2003083879, b. 17 June 1946)","blurb":"One of Étienne Decroux's last assistants, he founded Théâtre de l'Ange Fou with Corinne Soum in Paris in 1984 and moved it — touring company and International School of Corporeal Mime together — to London in 1995, then to the White Church Theatre Project in Spring Green, Wisconsin. From 1992 the two took up the reconstruction of Decroux's own repertoire, re-introducing to the public pieces such as The Man Who Preferred to Stand, Passage of Man on Earth and A Promethean Dream.","src":"Duchateau, Devenir comedien (L'Etudiant, 2001), on the corporeal-mime school"},"florianafrassetto":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority record GND 1197162690, Frassetto, Floriana: date of birth 1950; occupations Schauspielerin, Artistin, with the note 'Pantomimin; kunstlerische Leiterin von Mummenschanz'","blurb":"Mummenschanz's third founder and its Roman recruit: trained at an acting academy and Roy Besier's mime school in Rome, she met Schürch and Bossard when they performed there in 1971 and joined them to create the company — first show at the 1972 Avignon Festival. Through every touring configuration the creative centre stayed Bossard, Frassetto and Schürch; after Bossard's death in 1992 she and Schürch chose, after considerable turmoil, to rebuild rather than stop. Play, for the two of them, is what the masks are for.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"jawolezollar":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jawole Willa Jo Zollar: born December 21, 1950","blurb":"Taught from age seven in Kansas City by a teacher trained in the Dunham technique, she moved to New York in 1980, trained under Dianne McIntyre in Harlem, and in 1984 founded Urban Bush Women — the first major American company made entirely of Black women. Her work runs on weight and fluidity rather than sharp shapes and takes its subjects — homelessness, racism, sexism — from Black women's perspectives. A MacArthur Fellowship found her in 2021, thirty-seven years into the experiment.","src":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 8 — Zollar"},"bebemiller":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Bebe Miller: born 1950","blurb":"Brooklyn-born, she danced six years for Nina Wiener before founding the Bebe Miller Company in 1984 and working out a movement vocabulary that departed from the era's postmodern conventions. Bessie awards, a Guggenheim, and a Doris Duke Artist Award followed; from 2000 to 2017 she taught at Ohio State, retiring as a Distinguished Professor in the Arts and Humanities.","src":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 8 —"},"doncampbell":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Don Campbell (dancer): 'Don ‘Campbellock’ Campbell (January 8, 1951 - March 30, 2020)', born in Saint Louis, Missouri; corroborated in Guzman-Sanchez, Underground Dance Masters","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Don Campbell (dancer): died March 30, 2020","blurb":"The lock began as a stall — a nineteen-year-old trying the Robot Shuffle who kept sticking between moves and turned the sticking into the dance. He rode it onto Soul Train in 1971, cut 'The Campbellock' on the Stanson label in February 1972, and was off the show by 1973 for organizing dancers to ask for pay — so he founded the Campbellock Dancers, renamed The Lockers under legal pressure in 1974, whose first TV booking backed Roberta Flack. Locking is the rare dance whose inventor, name and paycheck dispute are all on the record.","src":"USC Annenberg Media obituary, 10 Apr 2020 - b. 1951, invented the Campbellock (locking), Soul Train regular banned in 1973 for organizing dancers, founded the Campbellock Dancers (1973) who became The Lockers, d. 30 Mar 2020; The Campbellock Timeline, campbellock.dance ('The Campbellock' single, Stanson label, 1972)"},"micheldallaire":{"blurb":"A Québécois clown whose working life ran through the European circus revival: he crossed from Quebec to Belgium to direct for Cirque Gosh, then settled in France at Alès, directing Archaos — the company that put contemporary circus in front of the French public in the mid-1980s — and staging productions for Gosh, Les Arrosés and CirkologioM through the 2000s. He died in December 2018, leaving a written record far thinner than his circulation among clowns.","src":"The Place 2025"},"nancystarksmith":{"srcb":"Contact Quarterly's own memorial page, headed 'Honoring Nancy Stark Smith / 11 Feb 1952 - 1 May 2020'","srcd":"Contact Quarterly's own memorial page, headed 'Honoring Nancy Stark Smith / 11 Feb 1952 - 1 May 2020': 'WITH SORROW, we announce the passing of our beloved cofounder, codirector, and coeditor, Nancy Stark Smith, on Friday, May 1st, at home, of ovarian cancer'","blurb":"A gymnast among the self-described 'jocks... rolling around and crashing about' at the first contact improvisation performances in 1972, she became the form's memory by refusing to institutionalize it: when the founders flirted with trademark in 1975, her newsletter announced the retreat — no policing, communication instead; 'It feels a lot better this way.' The newsletter became Contact Quarterly (1976), co-edited by her until her death in 2020; from 1990 her Underscore gave jams a shared map.","src":"Novack, Sharing the Dance (1990)"},"yukiowaguri":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 和栗由紀夫: 1952年(昭和27年)2月26日","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 和栗由紀夫: 2017年(平成29年)10月22日","blurb":"He apprenticed with Hijikata from 1972 to 1980, the years the butoh-fu were being fixed; his right-wing father accepted the arrangement, he said, as though his son were being taken by the military and sent to war, which Hijikata thought was wonderful. He then spent decades turning his rehearsal notes into something transmissible — Butoh Kaden, a CD-ROM of 88 movements, later an iPhone app — so the words, the images and the dances they produce could be studied by people who never met the man.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 42 (Coker) note 3: born 1952, apprenticeship 1972-1980; ch. 44 (van Hensbergen): Butoh Kaden records 88 butoh movements choreographed by Hijikata in the 1970s; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): a disciple of Hijikata from 1972 and the person who codified and saved Hijikata's butoh-fu in electronic form; he founded the Hijikata Method Study Group in 1992 and performed Butoh Kaden, a journey through seven butoh-fu worlds, in 1996; he tours with his group Butoh Kohzensha and made the solo Komachi for Denise Fujiwara; asked by Fraleigh to perform the butoh-fu"},"hirokotamano":{"blurb":"She saw one Hijikata performance and it broke open her idea of what a body was for; she danced in his company through the sixties and seventies, then moved Harupin-Ha to Berkeley with Koichi Tamano. She has taught the West Coast largely from her own house — inviting strangers in for tea and Asbestos-kan videotapes — and is one of the few first-generation women whose American teaching line runs unbroken. In 1996 she danced in San Francisco's Women in Butoh programme, on a bill of four that included Hijikata's widow.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 33 (Flournoy): danced in Hijikata's company through the 1960s and 1970s before emigrating with Koichi to Berkeley; ch. 37 (Mezur): early female trainee of Hijikata; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born in Fukuoka in 1952; her butoh debut was in Hijikata's Gibasa in 1972 in Kyoto, a precursor to his Summer Storm (1973); she joined Harupin-Ha in 1973 in Tokyo; her first performance in the United States was for the Isamu Noguchi exhibition at the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art in 1979; though Koichi is the company's artistic director it is often Hiroko who teaches the classes and supplies the images that guide a dancer's movement, and she has influenced a generation of dancers, musicians and artists"},"ohadnaharin":{"srcb":"he.wikipedia אוהד נהרין: 'נולד ב-22 ביוני 1952'; en.wikipedia, Ohad Naharin gives the bare year 1952","blurb":"He did not start dancing until he was 22, and Martha Graham, visiting Israel, saw him in his first year at Batsheva and pulled him to New York. Back as director from 1990, he replaced technique class with Gaga — a language of images and sensation, mirrors discouraged so dancers stop grading themselves. In 1998, told to put long underwear on his dancers so the state's fiftieth-anniversary gala would offend nobody, he pulled Batsheva out of the performance instead.","src":"Batsheva Dance Company, 'Ohad Naharin, House Choreographer' (batsheva.co.il): joined Batsheva 1974; Martha Graham invited him to her company; artistic director 1990-2018, then House Choreographer; developed the Gaga movement language"},"billtjones":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Bill T. Jones: born February 15, 1952","blurb":"He met Arnie Zane at Binghamton in 1971, and for seventeen years they danced as an openly interracial male couple, formalizing their company in 1982. After Zane died of AIDS in 1988, Jones made D-Man in the Waters for the dying dancer Demian Acquavella, then Still/Here (1994), built from workshops with the terminally ill — the piece the critic Arlene Croce refused to see, calling it 'victim art.' Tonys for Spring Awakening and Fela! and a Kennedy Center Honor followed.","src":"Perpener, African-American Concert Dance (2001), ch. 8 — for Still/Here (1994)"},"alonzoking":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority GND 1089593163, King, Alonzo: date of birth 1952, place of birth Georgia, affiliation Alonzo King Lines Ballet, Wirkungsort San Francisco","blurb":"A son of the Georgia civil rights movement — his parents were the activists Slater King and Valencia King Nelson — he founded LINES Ballet in San Francisco in 1982 and has made more than eighty works on the conviction that everyone can grow through dance. Seven years in, he added a community dance center to the company; his ballets entered repertories from the Joffrey to Ailey's company, and Juilliard and CalArts answered with honorary doctorates.","src":"LINES 2023"},"eikootake":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority GND 1016442602, Otake, Eiko (variants Yamada Eiko, Eiko): date of birth 1952-02-14, place of birth Tokyo, with the note that she performs with her husband Takashi Koma Yamada as Eiko and Koma","blurb":"She studied with Hijikata and with Ohno, and then in 1972 went to Hannover to work with Manja Chmiel, out of the Wigman line — a route that makes butoh's German ancestry literal rather than theoretical. She has never accepted the butoh label for her own work. Since 2014, after four decades as Eiko & Koma, she has performed alone in places rather than theatres, including irradiated Fukushima, and teaches a university course on the atomic bombings.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 14 (Elswit et al. roundtable): trained with Hijikata and Ohno and with Wigman's student Manja Chmiel in Hannover from 1972, though she does not identify as a butoh artist; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): Eiko, born 1952, and Koma, born 1948, studied with both Ohno Kazuo and Hijikata Tatsumi, performing early on with Hijikata in Tokyo and later with Ohno in the 1970s, and also studied in the tradition of German modern dance with Manja Chmiel in Germany; they were named MacArthur Fellows in 1996 and have two Bessie Awards, the 2004 Samuel H. Scripps American Dance Festival Award and the 2006 Dance Magazine Award; Fraleigh lists them among artists who do not necessarily call their dance butoh but credit it in their training and outlook"},"anzufurukawa":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 古川あんず: 本名 古川杏子, 1952年2月, 東京都生まれ","srcd":"ja.wikipedia 古川あんず: 2001年10月23日","blurb":"One of the women inside Dairakudakan, and one of the few who left to co-run a company rather than to dance in someone else's: Dance Love Machine, started with Tamura Tetsuro in 1974. From 1989 she worked out of Berlin, where her teaching shaped an entire German butoh scene, and travelled as far as Brazil, where she built a piece out of what each performer's own body brought rather than out of set material. She died in 2001, at forty-nine.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), Introduction: 1952-2001, co-started Dance Love Machine with Tamura Tetsuro, to Berlin 1989; ch. 24 (Centonze): female member of Dairakudakan; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): 1952-2001, born in Tokyo and died in Berlin; she studied from 1972 to 1975 under Professor Yoshiro Irino at the Toho-gakuen College of Music, and was a studio pianist for ballet companies as well as a ballet and modern dancer; she worked with Carlotta Ikeda, Murobushi Ko and Amagatsu's Sankai Juku, and cofounded Dance Love Machine with Tamura Tetsuro; her all-female Berlin company was called Verwandlungsamt, a German wordplay meaning"},"lutzforster":{"srcb":"de.wikipedia, Lutz Forster: 'geboren 2. Januar 1953 in Solingen'; Deutsche Nationalbibliothek GND 139012400 gives the same date","blurb":"The dancer who performs a recorded song in sign language in Bausch's Nelken. He trained at Folkwang under Hans Zuellig and Jean Cebron, joined Tanztheater Wuppertal in 1975, spent stretches of the eighties in New York dancing Limon, Sokolow and Meredith Monk, and has taught at Folkwang since 1991. He directed the Wuppertal company from 2013 to 2016 — the job of deciding what a repertory does once its author is gone.","src":"Wikipedia (de), Lutz Foerster — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lutz_F%C3%B6rster (b. 1953 Solingen, Folkwang under Zuellig and Cebron, Tanztheater Wuppertal from 1975, sign-language solo in Nelken, Limon company in New York 1981-86, Folkwang professorship 1991, artistic direction 2013-2016); also Folkwang Universitaet, Folkwang Tanzstudio history"},"yumikoyoshioka":{"srcb":"San Francisco Zen Center teacher page, Yumiko Yoshioka, read in full: \"Yumiko Yoshioka is a dancer, choreographer, teacher, and art director. She was born in Tokyo in 1953 and has been based in Berlin since 1988. In 1974 Yumiko was a member of Ariadone, the first female Butoh company, founded by Carlotta Ikeda and Ko Murobushi\"","blurb":"She danced in Dairakudakan and Ariadone as Mizelle Hanaoka, and in January 1978 was one of three who turned up in Paris with a three-month plan and no money — the promised backing had evaporated, and shame kept them from going home. What they scraped together, The Last Eden, packed a ninety-nine-seat theatre nightly and became the first butoh Europe saw. She stayed, co-founded Tatoeba-théâtre Danse Grotesque in Berlin in 1987, and teaches a method she calls body resonance.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 24 (Centonze): Dairakudakan/Ariadone member as Mizelle Hanaoka, premiered The Last Eden in Paris January 1978; ch. 29 (van Hensbergen): co-formed Tatoeba-theatre Danse Grotesque 1987 in Berlin, eX...it! festival with delta RA'i from 1995; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born 1953, raised in Tokyo; a member of Ariadone, which Fraleigh calls the first Japanese women's butoh company; in 1978 she performed with Murobushi Ko and Carlotta Ikeda in Le Dernier Eden in Paris; in 1995 she founded the art formation group TEN PEN CHii with the German visual artist Joachim Manger and the American composer Zam Johnson, based at Schloss Broellin, a castle in the former East Germany about two hours from Berlin that they converted into five dance studios; she has art-directed the EXIT butoh and contemporary dance project with delta RA'i since 1995; she explains her Body Resonance method through the word jibun, ji (nature) and bun (a part) —"},"moeyamamoto":{"blurb":"He was commuting to an agricultural college while training with Hijikata, then moved into Asbestos-kan in 1974 to absorb it faster; he stayed three years, straight through the period when the choreography was being written down. What makes him load-bearing is the notebooks. His record of the choreography Hijikata made on him for Costume en Face (1976), published bilingually in 2015, is one of the very few first-hand transcripts of butoh-fu being made.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 42 (Coker) note 3: born 1953, apprenticeship 1974-1977; Introduction: his scrapbooks record Hijikata's choreography on him for Costume en Face (1976)"},"tessdequincey":{"blurb":"Australian, and the person who brought Body Weather to Australia in 1989 after six years in Min Tanaka's Mai-Juku in Japan. Her company has since put the training onto the continent itself: the Triple Alice labs of 1999-2001 took dancers into central Australia to find out what the method does in that landscape rather than in a Tokyo studio. She is one of the teachers Australian dancers name when asked where they learned butoh.","src":"De Quincey Co, About page: dancer with Min Tanaka's Mai-Juku performance group 1985-91, introduced the Body Weather philosophy and methodology into Australia in 1989, ran the Triple Alice Labs in central Australia 1999-2001"},"maureenfleming":{"blurb":"She was born in Japan to an American Navy lieutenant and at the age of two was in a serious car accident with her mother; the aftermath, physical and emotional, is what she says sent her to the study of movement. She assembled her practice out of two incompatible teachers — Ohno's imagery and Min Tanaka's body work — and calls what she makes surreal movement poetry. Descending from the air onto a reflecting pool in Decay of the Angel, commissioned and premiered in Tokyo in 2000, she says her aim is to become the inside of the audience.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 33 (Flournoy): New York-based solo artist whose teaching combines movement styles from Ohno Kazuo and Min Tanaka; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born in Japan, the daughter of an American Navy lieutenant, and now living in New York; at the age of two she was in a serious car accident with her mother, and the effects, physical and emotional, ran through her life and shaped her devotion to the study of physical movement; she was one of the first and most consistent Western students of Ohno Kazuo and Ohno Yoshito, and her study with Tanaka Min took her toward Hijikata's surrealism and his idea of dance as experience; she calls her multimedia work surreal movement poetry and says she dances archetypes and wants to become"},"karolearmitage":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Karole Armitage: born March 3, 1954","blurb":"She danced Balanchine at the Grand Théâtre de Genève and toured leading roles with Cunningham (1976–81), then collided the two lineages with rock: Drastic-Classicism (1981) earned her the 1980s tag of 'punk ballerina.' Her range ran wherever technique travels — Madonna's Vogue and Michael Jackson's In the Closet videos, a Tony nomination for the Hair revival, Cirque du Soleil's Amaluna — before Armitage Gone! Dance (2004) resettled her in New York.","src":"Wikipedia, 'Karole Armitage'"},"tomoeshizune":{"blurb":"A composer and musician, not a dancer, who has been artistic director of Hakutobo since the mid-1980s — an outsider's ear installed at the centre of Hijikata's own company. He defined the dancing body in relation to sound and space, which took Ashikawa's choreography somewhere it had not been and produced the work that carried it abroad. He also does the naming: SU-EN received her stage name from him at her company's 1992 debut.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), chs. 22/30 (SU-EN): composer and musician, artistic director of Hakutobo since the mid-1980s, trained SU-EN as apprentice; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): SU-EN was an apprentice with"},"analaguna":{"srcb":"Sources disagree by a year and the atlas's 1955 has the Swedish side. sv.wikipedia, Ana Laguna: 'Ana Maria Laguna Caso, fodd 16 maj 1955 i Zaragoza i Spanien, ar en spansk-svensk balettdansos och hovdansare'. Against it, en.wikipedia: 'Ana Maria Laguna Caso was born 16 May 1954 in Zaragoza, Spain'. The German National Library holds the split as two records — GND 1121924700 'Laguna, Ana' 1954 and GND 1075739829 'Laguna Caso, Ana Maria' 1955. This map keeps 1955","blurb":"She came from Zaragoza to the Cullberg Ballet as a teenager and became the body Mats Ek thought through: the 1982 Giselle was created on her, and her Carmen won an Emmy. She once learned Miss Julie for Nureyev in two and a half weeks. In 2006 she was made a Swedish court dancer, the first from outside the Royal Swedish Ballet.","src":"Royal Swedish Opera, 'Ana Laguna' (operan.se): born in Zaragoza, Spain; moved to Sweden in 1974 at 19 to join the Cullberg Ballet, dancing there until 1993; later guested with Baryshnikov and Pina Bausch; danced Mats Ek's Julia & Romeo and A Cup of Coffee"},"adolfoquinones":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Shabba Doo: Adolfo Quinones, May 11, 1955","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Shabba Doo: December 29, 2020","blurb":"Raised by a single mother in Chicago's Cabrini-Green towers, he became a founding member of The Lockers alongside Don Campbell and Toni Basil and one of locking's recognized innovators. Breakin' (1984) made him Ozone, the style's first movie star — the film opened at number one — and he spent the following decades choreographing for Madonna's Who's That Girl tour, Lionel Richie, and Three 6 Mafia's Oscar-night performance. He died in December 2020 at sixty-five.","src":"Chicago Sun-Times obituary, 30 Dec 2020 - Adolfo 'Shabba-Doo' Quinones, d. Dec 2020 aged 65; founding member of The Lockers alongside Don Campbell and Toni Basil; starred in Breakin' and Breakin' 2: Electric Boogaloo (1984)"},"corinnesoum":{"srcb":"Theatre de l'Ange Fou, its own Biographies page (Wayback capture of 7 Sept 2024, and a 2006 capture eighteen years earlier gives the same year): 'Born in France (1956), Corinne Soum is the co-director of the Theatre de l'Ange Fou... She studied corporeal mime with Etienne Decroux, and became his assistant. Decroux created three pieces on Corinne: La Femme Oiseau, Le Fauteuil de l'Absent and Le Duo dans le Parc St. Cloud'. She has no authority record in LC, GND, BnF or Wikidata; this company page is the only source","blurb":"The other of Decroux's last assistants: with Steven Wasson she co-founded Théâtre de l'Ange Fou in 1984 and has carried corporeal mime through three homes — Paris, London from 1995, and the White Church in Spring Green, Wisconsin. Her share of the work has been the technique's growing repertoire and dramaturgy, taught through the International School of Corporeal Mime, and the reconstruction, begun in 1992, of most of Decroux's major pieces, returned to the stage in the company's performances.","src":"Duchateau, Devenir comedien (L'Etudiant, 2001), on the corporeal-mime school"},"alainplatel":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Alain Platel: born 9 April 1956; founded les ballets C de la B in 1984","blurb":"A remedial educationalist who worked with disabled children and began directing self-taught — the company started on a dare in 1984, first piece made in his loft with his sister and a filmmaker friend, the name les ballets C de la B adopted ironically. His stages mix trained dancers with amateurs, children and the elderly, and his 'bastard dance' hunts the tics, spasms and uncontrolled movement that schooling suppresses; vsprs, pitié! and Gardenia build such bodies around classical scores performed by the cast. He calls himself a child of Bausch.","src":"Boisseau, Danse et art contemporain (Nouvelles editions Scala, 2011)"},"markmorris":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Mark Morris (choreographer): Mark William Morris, born August 29, 1956","blurb":"A Seattle child of flamenco lessons and the Koleda Balkan folk ensemble, he founded his own group in 1980 and at thirty-one succeeded Béjart at Brussels's Théâtre de la Monnaie, with an orchestra and chorus at his disposal; L'Allegro, il Penseroso ed il Moderato, made there in 1988, took the Olivier Award a decade on. With Baryshnikov he founded the White Oak Dance Project in 1990, and his own company has now danced more than 150 of his works.","src":"Mark Morris Dance Group, 'Mark Morris' biography (markmorrisdancegroup.org): born August 29, 1956, Seattle; formed the Mark Morris Dance Group in 1980, over 150 works for the company; founded the White Oak Dance Project with Mikhail Baryshnikov in 1990; Olivier Award 1998 for L'Allegro, il Penseroso ed il Moderato"},"dougvarone":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Doug Varone: 'born 1955/1956' — the article will not choose between them; this map keeps 1956","blurb":"Out of the Limón company's ranks — he danced there in the late seventies — he founded Doug Varone and Dancers in 1986 and built a body of work praised for its emotional range and kinetic breadth. The concert stage never contained him: he has directed and choreographed at the Metropolitan Opera — Salome, Le Sacre du Printemps, Les Troyens — and in Broadway and Off-Broadway theatre, and his company has collected eleven Bessie Awards over four decades.","src":"Varone 2023"},"teresecapucilli":{"blurb":"She joined the Graham company in March 1979 and was its principal instrument for twenty-six years; the aging Graham made late roles on her — the Chosen One in The Rite of Spring among them — and gave her the lead in Maple Leaf Rag, her final ballet. With Christine Dakin she co-directed the company through its rebirth from 2002 to 2005, and she now teaches the technique at Juilliard, where she staged Appalachian Spring in 2008.","src":"Juilliard 2023"},"diegopinon":{"blurb":"In 1993 he watched Byakko-sha perform in the courtyard of Mexico City's anthropology museum and changed direction on the spot; he had been researching surviving Mesoamerican ritual, the kind that reaches ecstasy through entheogens. He went to Japan repeatedly after that, studying with Min Tanaka, Nakajima Natsu and both Ohnos, and built Butoh Ritual Mexicano in Michoacán out of the collision. He argues butoh gives Mexicans a way to say what the colonisations left in the body.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), chs. 26 (Candelario) and 32 (Rosales): studied in Japan with Ohno Kazuo, Ohno Yoshito, Tanaka Min and Nakajima Natsu; developed Butoh Ritual Mexicano in Michoacan; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010), from her interview with him in Chicago in March 2006: born in Mexico City in 1957; he comes from the Purepecha culture, now identified with the Tarascan Indians, who resisted Spanish culture and kept their own language, and was exposed to their dances as a child; he began formal study of dance and somatic modalities in 1975 with Mexican teachers of a form called Energetic Movement, completed studies in the social sciences in 1979 and began dancing at the Centro Superior de Coreografia in Mexico City; his butoh teachers are named as Nakajima Natsu, Ohno Kazuo, Ohno Yoshito, Min Tanaka, Hisako Horikawa and Mitsuyo Uesugui; in 1993 he was invited to perform with Byakko-Sha in Hibari to Nejaka and in 1994 with Min Tanaka's Maijuku in The Ancient Woman, and presented his first butoh ritual piece, Zacuala, in Kyoto; Ohno gave him a single instruction —"},"lloydnewson":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Lloyd Newson: born 1957; formed DV8 Physical Theatre in 1986","blurb":"A psychology graduate from Melbourne who concluded much contemporary dance was 'conning' its audiences about depth, and founded DV8 Physical Theatre in 1986 to say things instead: Dead Dreams of Monochrome Men out of the Nilsen murders, Strange Fish on the need to believe, later verbatim works built from interviews — To Be Straight With You, Can We Talk About This? — on religion, homosexuality and free speech. Fifty-five awards and an OBE later, he ended the company with his retirement in 2022.","src":"University of Bristol Theatre Collection - 'DV8 Archive' (bristol.ac.uk): 'Led by choreographer and Artistic Director Lloyd Newson since its inception, DV8 Physical Theatre was formed in 1986 by a group of independent dancers who had become frustrated and disillusioned with the preoccupations and direction of (most) contemporary dance'; Getz, Dancers and Choreographers: A Selected Bibliography (Asphodel Press, 1995) gives 'Newson, Lloyd (1957- )'"},"davidglass":{"blurb":"Lecoq-trained, with study under Boal, Grotowski, Brook, Alvin Ailey and Mike Alfreds behind him, he performed solo from 1979 and founded the David Glass Ensemble in 1990 — Gormenghast, La Dolce Vita with Alfreds, the first stage Popeye. In 1997 he pointed the devising machinery elsewhere: the Lost Child Project has made theatre with over 10,000 street children in 22 countries, seeding a Centre for Creative Development in Cambodia, and his five-stage Creative Practice is used by UNICEF and Save the Children.","src":"David Glass Ensemble - 'About' (davidglassensemble.co.uk): 'Since 1979 David Glass has been performing and creating across the world, and then in 1990 he established the David Glass Ensemble as a pioneering physical, devised and applied theatre company'; 'Trained at the Lecoq School in Paris he has also studied with Augusto Boal, Growtowski, Peter Brook, Alvin Ailey and Mike Alfreds'"},"simonmcburney":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Simon McBurney: Simon Montagu McBurney, born 25 August 1957","blurb":"He met Marcello Magni at Lecoq's school in 1980, and with Magni and Annabel Arden launched Théâtre de Complicité in 1982 — named for the collusion between celebrants that Lecoq taught as the outcome of successful play. A decade of bleakly comic devised work (A Minute Too Late began from his father's death) opened into adaptations devised just as hard: Street of Crocodiles, The Three Lives of Lucy Cabrol, Mnemonic. Most actors who pass through the company share the Lecoq–Gaulier–Pagneux vocabulary.","src":"Murray, Jacques Lecoq (2003)"},"shobanajeyasingh":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Shobana Jeyasingh: born 26 March 1957 in Chennai","blurb":"Chennai-born, trained in bharatanatyam by Vazhuvoor Samaraj Pillai and in Shakespeare at Sussex, she founded her London company in 1989 and has treated the classical grammar as living syntax rather than heritage: more than sixty works set in theatres, Venetian monasteries, historic churches (TooMortal) and London fountains, alongside a reimagined Bayadère and collaborations with scientists. Faultline answered the London of the tube bombings; the CBE came in 2020.","src":"Shobana Jeyasingh Dance — 'Shobana Jeyasingh CBE' company profile (born in Chennai; trained in Bharata Natyam under Vazhuvoor Samaraj Pillai; founded Shobana Jeyasingh Dance in 1989)"},"boogaloosam":{"srcb":"Thomas Guzman-Sanchez, Underground Dance Masters (2012) — the atlas's own source for this person — via Open Library full-text search: 'Sam Solomon, who was born on November 19, 1957 in Fresno'. The research pass had 1955, which no page states; the same book gives his brother Timothy Earl Solomon as born 5 August 1961, matching Wikipedia's Popin' Pete exactly","blurb":"A Fresno dancer who saw The Lockers on television, learned from the Tulare project dancers he met at Roeding Park, and built something else: the muscle-snap technique called popping and the rolling boogaloo that carries his crew's name. His Electronic Boogaloo Lockers, founded in 1977, dropped the 'Lockers' within a year and, as the Electric Boogaloos, became popping's official school — though rival accounts trace the style through Oakland's boogaloo groups without naming him. The origin is still an argument; his crew is still its establishment.","src":"Underground Dance Masters"},"umadogra":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Uma Dogra: born 23 April 1957, Kathak, senior disciple of Pt. Durga Lal of the Jaipur gharana","blurb":"Twelve years a disciple of Durga Lal, after early training with Reba Vidyarthi and three years with Birju Maharaj, she has now spent longer keeping her guru's memory than she had him: the Samved Society she founded in 1990, the year he died, has run a Pandit Durga Lal festival for more than three decades. A Sangeet Natak Akademi awardee in 2014, she teaches the Jaipur style from Mumbai, her students ranging from kathak soloists to film actors.","src":"Wikipedia"},"wendyhoustoun":{"srcb":"The Guardian, 'Wendy Houstoun: the death that made me question everything' (26 May 2014), opens 'Last year was a good one for Wendy Houstoun. Not only was the 55-year-old nominated for outstanding female dancer in the National Dance awards' — 55 in May 2014 puts the birth between May 1958 and May 1959. UK Companies House pins it: its single Wendy HOUSTOUN, British, director of THE INCREDIBLY STUPID COMPANY LTD (09647967), has 'Date of birth: September 1958'. The atlas's 1962 was activity-minus-25 built on a 1987 start; her own site says she has been making work 'since 1980'","blurb":"A movement/theatre artist committed, in her own billing, to finding new forms — combining movement with text and meaning with humour — across other people's companies (Lumiere & Son, DV8, Forced Entertainment) and a long duet partnership with Nigel Charnock. Her solos, Haunted, Daunted and Flaunted, Happy Hour and The 48 Almost Love Lyrics, toured Europe; two BBC films with David Hinton, Touched and Diary of a Dancer, carried the talking-while-moving approach to screen.","src":"Performance Perspectives: A Critical Introduction (Palgrave Macmillan, 2011), contributor note: 'Wendy Houstoun is a London-based artist who has been working with companies such as Ludus Dance Company, Lumiere & Son Theatre Company, DV8 Physical Theatre, Forced Entertainment'; University of Bristol Theatre Collection - 'DV8 Archive': early members of DV8 (formed 1986) 'included Michelle Richecoeur, Nigel Charnock and Wendy Houstoun'"},"tobysedgwick":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Toby Sedgwick: born 16 August 1958; devised the horse choreography for War Horse (2007)","blurb":"Two years at Lecoq's Paris school, then the Moving Picture Mime Show, which he co-founded in London. His lasting invention is movement for things that are not human: he devised the 'horse choreography' for the life-size puppets of War Horse, taking the 2008 Olivier for Best Theatre Choreographer and a Dora Award for the work in 2012, and assisted Danny Boyle with choreography for the opening ceremony of the London 2012 Olympics.","src":"Lust, From the Greek Mimes to Marcel Marceau and Beyond (Scarecrow Press, 1998): of the Lecoq-trained Moving Picture Mime Show"},"janfabre":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jan Fabre: born 14 December 1958","blurb":"A visual artist who made theatre at inhuman lengths — an eight-hour piece in 1982, a twenty-four-hour one in 2015 — and whose company was the room a generation of Flemish dance came out of; Wim Vandekeybus spent two years touring in it as one of two naked kings before starting his own. In 2018 twenty former members accused him of harassment and abuse of power. A Belgian court convicted him in April 2022 on five counts under worker-welfare law and one of assault on decency, and gave him eighteen months, suspended.","src":"The Art Newspaper 2022"},"manuelacarrasco":{"srcb":"Sources split on the year. For 1954: es.wikipedia, 'Manuela Carrasco Salazar (n. Sevilla, 1954)', and El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco, 'nacio en Sevilla en el ano de 1954'. For 1958, and with a full date: andalucia.org (Junta de Andalucia), 'Nacio en el barrio sevillano de Triana el 21 de octubre de 1958', with Vida Flamenca — this atlas's own source for her — 'Manuela Carrasco was born in Triana, Seville, in 1958' and ExpoFlamenco 'Manuela Carrasco (Seville, 1958)'. This map keeps 1954 and records that its own cited source says 1958","blurb":"Daughter of the Triana bailaor El Sordo, she formed herself by watching her family dance — the record lists no academy and no teacher — and debuted at ten in a Torremolinos tablao. At eighteen she was proclaimed 'La Diosa', the Goddess of flamenco dance, and the name never came off. The prizes ran from the Premio Nacional Pastora Imperio in 1974 to Spain's Gold Medal for Fine Arts in 2018; even her birth year, 1954 or 1958, is argued.","src":"Vida Flamenca"},"davidparsons":{"srcb":"University of Washington Department of Dance, David Parsons: 'Born Rockford, Illinois, October 29, 1959. David Parsons was raised in Kansas City, Missouri. From 1978 to 1987, Parsons was a lead dancer with the Paul Taylor Company'","blurb":"A leading Paul Taylor dancer from 1978 to 1987 who made his signature on the side: Caught (1982), a solo to Robert Fripp's music in which bursts of light catch the dancer only in mid-air, so that he seems to float and soar without ever landing. In 1985 he founded Parsons Dance with the lighting designer Howell Binkley — light a founding partner, not a service — and built it a repertory of more than seventy-five works touring five continents.","src":"Parsons Dance 2023"},"preranashrimali":{"srcb":"Sangeet Natak Akademi's own award citation PDF, 'PRERANA SHRIMALI / Akademi Award: Kathak': 'Born in 1959 at Banswara in Rajasthan, Shrimati Prerana Shrimali was initiated in Kathak dance by Shri Gauri Shanker. Later, she was groomed in the art at Kathak Kendra, New Delhi, under Shri Kundan Lal Gangani of the Jaipur gharana'","blurb":"From Banswara in southern Rajasthan to the front rank of the Jaipur gharana: initiated by Kundan Lal Gangani in Jaipur and kept under his eye at Delhi's Kathak Kendra, she stayed to lead the institution's repertory and teach as its senior guru. Her dancing reached the BBC's Far Pavilions; the Sangeet Natak Akademi's award reached her in 2009; and in 2021 she went home, founding her own kathak institute, Kalaavart, in Jaipur.","src":"Testbook"},"nigelcharnock":{"srcb":"The Guardian obituary footer, verbatim: 'Nigel Charnock, performer and choreographer, born 23 May 1960; died 1 August 2012'; the Library of Congress authority n97878468 cites the same page. GND 135187141 gives 1974 for a 'Nigel Charnock', which cannot be the DV8 founder — it would make him eleven at his 1985 Dance Umbrella debut","srcd":"The Guardian obituary, 7 August 2012: 'born 23 May 1960; died 1 August 2012'","blurb":"'A maverick and totally captivating performer' in his company's farewell, 'body poet and word dancer... a virtuoso in daring' in The Independent's words. He began in Ludus and Extemporary Dance Theatre, co-founded DV8, then made the joyful, uncompromising, all-singing-and-dancing solo his form — Human Being, Hell Bent, Original Sin, Resurrection, Frank — through Nigel Charnock + Company from 1995 and the Helsinki Dance Company, which he directed 2002–05. He died in August 2012, at 52, midway through an ensemble piece called 10 Men.","src":"University of Bristol Theatre Collection - 'DV8 Archive' (bristol.ac.uk): DV8 Physical Theatre 'was formed in 1986 by a group of independent dancers... Early members of the company included Michelle Richecoeur, Nigel Charnock and Wendy Houstoun'; The Rough Guide to Choreography (Rough Guides, 2004), entry on Charnock: 'From his roots as DV8 co-founder, Renaissance man Charnock has gone on to star', 'whether in his DV8 days, or working solo (as in the aptly-named Frank)'"},"annateresadekeersmaeker":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Anne Teresa De Keersmaeker: born 1960 in Mechelen, Belgium","blurb":"A flute student who began formal dance training only in her last school year, she built choreography like counterpoint: Fase (1982), four movements on Steve Reich, made her name, and Rosas danst Rosas (1983) launched her company. From residency at La Monnaie she co-founded P.A.R.T.S. in 1995, sixty students from twenty-five countries a year. When Beyoncé's Countdown video used her Rosas danst Rosas movement without permission in 2011, it set off a public argument over what in dance can be owned.","src":"Wikipedia, 'Anne Teresa De Keersmaeker'"},"aditimangaldas":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Aditi Mangaldas: born 1960, Kathak dancer and choreographer","blurb":"Trained by the two poles of modern kathak — Kumudini Lakhia's ensemble modernism at Kadamb, Birju Maharaj's Lucknow classicism at Kathak Kendra — she built the Drishtikon company into a place where the form thinks about the present: Within answered the 2012 Delhi gang rape, Forbidden takes on female desire and its policing. In 2013 she declined the national Sangeet Natak Akademi award — the record says only that her reasons were 'compelling'.","src":"Gavroche"},"frankvandeven":{"blurb":"Dutch, and one of the people through whom Min Tanaka's Body Weather left Japan: he spent his formative years with Tanaka and the Maijuku company, then co-founded Body Weather Amsterdam with Katerina Bakatsaki in 1993. Since 1995 he has run an annual project with the Czech artist Miloš Šejn that takes the training out of the studio into landscape, with geology and architecture as working partners. Sources disagree on his Japan years — 1983-92 by his own bio, 1985-93 elsewhere.","src":"Yumi Umiumare, Butoh and Body Weather residential workshop page (2016), carrying van de Ven's biography: formative years in Japan with Min Tanaka and the Maijuku Performance Company (1983-92), founded Body Weather Amsterdam with Katerina Bakatsaki in 1993, has led the annual interdisciplinary Bohemiae Rosa project with Czech artist Miloš Šejn since 1995"},"jimmyd":{"blurb":"A Bronx b-boy who founded Rock Steady Crew with Jojo in 1977 and made its growth his real work: battles doubled as auditions, and in 1979 he recruited Richard 'Crazy Legs' Colón and Lenny Len, the pair who carried the crew's name into Manhattan and then everywhere. The founder stepped back as the crew went global; the institution he started is now breaking's most famous, run by the generation he picked.","src":"Bronx Walk of Fame, Rock Steady Crew inductee page (2008) - crew founded in 1977 by Jimmy D and Jojo in the Bronx; OldSchoolHipHop.com, 'The Rock Steady Crew' history"},"jojo":{"blurb":"A former hustler who discovered that dancing earned recognition the street otherwise reserved for riskier work, he co-founded Rock Steady Crew with Jimmy D in the Bronx in 1977. He is 'often credited' with inventing the backspin — the hedge belongs to the histories themselves, compiled from memory decades after the fact. The crew the two of them started was inducted into the Bronx Walk of Fame in 2008, thirty-one years and several generations of b-boys later.","src":"Bronx Walk of Fame, Rock Steady Crew inductee page (2008) - crew founded in 1977 by Jimmy D and Jojo in the Bronx; OldSchoolHipHop.com, 'The Rock Steady Crew' history (credits Jo Jo with inventing the backspin)"},"popinpete":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Popin' Pete: 'Timothy Earl Solomon (born August 5, 1961)', member of the Electric Boogaloos; corroborated in Guzman-Sanchez, Underground Dance Masters: 'Timothy Earl Solomon, who was born on August 5, 1961 in Fresno'","blurb":"Sam Solomon's younger brother, who chose to learn popping first because he found the family boogaloo too hard, and joined the Electric Boogaloos in 1978. He built his own vocabulary — 'crazy legs', 'ET', 'sleepy style' — and after Michael Jackson approached the crew in 1982 about the Thriller and Beat It videos, worked inside Jackson's orbit for nearly two decades. Four decades on he is popping's itinerant professor, teaching and judging from Europe to Japan, Korea and China.","src":"Underground Dance Masters"},"ticknwill":{"srcb":"Thomas Guzman-Sanchez, Underground Dance Masters: Final History of a Forgotten Era (2012), read through Open Library full-text search of the Internet Archive copy: 'William Green Jr. was born on September 1, 1961 in Merced, California, and moved to West Fresno when he was two' — replaces the build's activity-minus-25 estimate of 1951","blurb":"In the apartments of Fresno's Tulare housing project, he and Ricky Darnell McDowell worked out foundational popping moves in long sessions the local history remembers as weed-fueled — the 'old man', the 'twist-o flex', the 'neck-o flex'. Sam Solomon met the pair at Roeding Park in 1976, was impressed enough to recruit them on the spot, and when Sam moved to Long Beach they came down to drill the new recruits. The style went around the world; the two names mostly stayed in Fresno.","src":"Tropics of Meta"},"thomasrichards":{"blurb":"Grotowski chose him — son of the American director Lloyd Richards, arrived through the Objective Drama project — as his 'essential collaborator', and in 1996 put his name on the institution itself: the Workcenter of Jerzy Grotowski and Thomas Richards. He worked at Grotowski's side from 1985, followed him to Pontedera, directed the Workcenter after his death in 1999 — One Breath Left, Dies Irae, the Focused Research Team in Art as vehicle — and wrote the books, beginning with At Work with Grotowski on Physical Actions, that record what was transmitted.","src":"Schechner, Performance Studies: An Introduction (Routledge, 2006): Thomas Richards"},"eduardotorroja":{"blurb":"Madrid-trained in dance and theatre, he moved to Brussels to start Ultima Vez with Wim Vandekeybus, and was choreographer, trainer and performer on the pieces that made the company's name — What the Body Does Not Remember and Les Porteuses de Mauvaises Nouvelles. He has since directed and restaged both repeatedly and teaches the repertory in workshops, which makes him the keeper of a body of work built on risk and impulse.","src":"Ultima Vez, 'Eduardo Torroja' artist page (ultimavez.com): moved to Brussels to found Ultima Vez with Wim Vandekeybus, working as choreographer, trainer and performer on the first productions (What the Body Does Not Remember, Les Porteuses de Mauvaises Nouvelles); later artistic assistant and director of restagings"},"rosariomontoyamanzano":{"srcb":"Canal Sur archive and El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco: Seville, November 1962","blurb":"Farruco's daughter and the hinge of the dynasty: she danced beside El Güito, Manuela Carrasco and Antonio Canales, stood on the Royal Festival Hall stage in 1997 when her son Farruquito premiered Raíces Flamencas, and raised three bailaores — Farruquito, Farru and El Carpeta. When her husband Juan el Moreno died she kept three years of mourning offstage, then returned in 2005 as a headliner. The record calls her the transmitter of the family's baile puro; the sons are the evidence.","src":"El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco, bailaora profile 'La Farruca' (elartedevivirelflamenco.com/bailaores103.html) - b. Seville 1962, daughter of Farruco, mother of Farruquito; Canal Sur Memoranda archive (blogs.canalsur.es) - b. Nov 1962, sons Farruquito, Farru and El Carpeta all bailaores"},"michaelclark":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Michael Clark (dancer): Michael Duncan Clark CBE, born 29 May 1962, born in Aberdeen","blurb":"Scottish country dancing at four, the Royal Ballet School at thirteen, a choreographic prize on his last day there, then Rambert with Richard Alston — and then he took that training somewhere it was not meant to go. From 1984 his own company worked with Leigh Bowery, BodyMap, The Fall and Laibach; Swamp (1986) later won an Olivier. He choreographed Caliban for Prospero's Books, gave three years to Stravinsky, and returned to rock with 'to a simple, rock 'n' roll . . . song' in 2016.","src":"Michael Clark Company, 'Michael Clark' biography (michaelclarkcompany.com): born in Scotland, trained at the Royal Ballet School 1975-1979; joined Ballet Rambert 1979 working primarily with Richard Alston; Michael Clark and Company launched 1984"},"darnellmcdowell":{"srcb":"Thomas Guzman-Sanchez, Underground Dance Masters (2012), via Open Library full-text search: 'This is where Will met Ricky Darnell McDowell, who was born on July 24, 1962' — replaces the build's estimate of 1951","blurb":"The other half of the Tulare housing project sessions: with William 'Tick'n Will' Green he invented moves — the 'old man', the 'twist-o flex' — in apartment practice years before the style had a name with a market. Recruited by Sam Solomon at Roeding Park in 1976 into what became the Electric Boogaloo Lockers, he perfected routines at the Mary Ella Brown Community Center and later helped train the Long Beach generation. Histories of popping written outside the Central Valley tend to skip him; the Fresno accounts do not.","src":"Tropics of Meta"},"oguri":{"blurb":"He danced with Mai-Juku from the mid-eighties and came to Los Angeles in 1990 to join the Body Weather Laboratory Roxanne Steinberg and Melinda Ring had begun there. The training was advertised on a telephone pole and held in an old church in South LA: crawling and jumping the length of the floor, exploring the block blindfolded, carrying each other into human sculptures against the architecture. He is careful about the label — he says he began with Min Tanaka and was not thinking about butoh at all.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 51 (Lu interview): Mai-Juku participant from the mid-1980s; came from Japan in 1990 to join Body Weather Laboratory Los Angeles"},"sashawaltz":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Sasha Waltz: Sasha Alexandra Waltz, born 8 March 1963, Karlsruhe","blurb":"Her first teacher, when she was five, had come out of the Wigman line — which is how a choreographer trained in Amsterdam and downtown New York still belongs in this column. With Jochen Sandig she founded a company in 1993 and turned a derelict Berlin building into the Sophiensaele; from 1999 she ran dance at the Schaubuehne, opening with Koerper in 2000. She has since made a run of choreographic operas, starting with Dido and Aeneas in 2005.","src":"Wikipedia, Sasha Waltz — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sasha_Waltz (b. 1963 Karlsruhe; first teacher Waltraud Kornhass, a Wigman student; SNDO Amsterdam 1983-86 and New York 1986-87; Sasha Waltz & Guests founded 1993 with Jochen Sandig; Sophiensaele; Schaubuehne 1999-2004 opening with Koerper in 2000; Dido and Aeneas 2005; Staatsballett Berlin from 2019)"},"wimvandekeybus":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Wim Vandekeybus: Herenthout, 30 June 1963","blurb":"He was studying psychology when Jan Fabre cast him, and spent two years touring the world as one of the two naked kings in The Power of Theatrical Madness. In 1986 he withdrew to Madrid for months with a group of novice dancers and came back with What the Body Does Not Remember — bodies working at the edge of reflex, to music by Thierry De Mey and Peter Vermeersch, and a Bessie in 1988. A second Bessie followed for Les porteuses de mauvaises nouvelles.","src":"Ultima Vez, 'Wim Vandekeybus' artist page (ultimavez.com): founded his dance company Ultima Vez in the mid-eighties; debut What the body does not remember (1987) won a Bessie Award"},"danacaspersen":{"blurb":"Forsythe's principal collaborator for nearly three decades — onstage in Ballett Frankfurt and The Forsythe Company, and co-maker of Eidos:Telos and I don't believe in outer space. She then took the same attention to how people meet each other and turned it into conflict work: a master's in conflict studies and mediation, a book of seventeen principles, public dialogue projects on racism and polarisation, and once, for Artangel in London, an enormous bouncy castle.","src":"Center for Ballet and the Arts at NYU, 'Dana Caspersen' fellow bio (balletcenter.nyu.edu): leading artist with the Ballet Frankfurt and the Forsythe Company; primary collaborator of William Forsythe over nearly three decades, co-creating Eidos: Telos and I Don't Believe in Outer Space"},"thomasmcmanus":{"srcb":"Library of Congress name authority no2007026290, authorized heading 'McManus, Thomas, 1963-', sourced on the record to the Forsythe CD-ROM 'Improvisation technologies: a tool for the analytical dance eye' (2003), printed guide colophon ('Thomas McManus; dancer, Frankfurt Ballet'), and to the timelapses project website, 7 March 2007 ('Thomas McManus; dancer, choreographer, b. 1963, U.S.A.')","blurb":"A dancer of the Ballett Frankfurt years who became one of the people the repertory now runs on: schools and companies engage him as an expert in the technique, repertory and improvisational methods of William Forsythe's work. The sources reachable in this pass document that transmission rather than his own performing career.","src":"Ballet West Academy, Professional Workshop faculty note (academy.balletwest.org): McManus, 'recognized expert on the repertory, technique, and improvisational methods' of William Forsythe's work"},"frostyfreeze":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Frosty Freeze: Wayne 'Frosty Freeze' Frost, December 4, 1963","srcd":"en.wikipedia, Frosty Freeze: April 3, 2008","blurb":"A second-generation Rock Steady b-boy whose trademark, the 'dead man drop', began as a backflip he botched onto his back and then kept. He was on the cover of the Village Voice in 1981 when downtown discovered breaking, then in Style Wars, Wild Style and Flashdance when the world did. Comedic, acrobatic and inventive by every account, he died in Manhattan in 2008 at forty-four, after a long illness the obituaries left undisclosed.","src":"New York Times 2008-04-04"},"seisaku":{"blurb":"A Hakutobo dancer under the name Ashikawa Seisaku, and the lead in Milky Woman (1988), the piece where Ashikawa's choreography and Tomoe Shizune's sound came together. His documented afterlife is as a clarifier: SU-EN credits him with settling what the words and meanings of the training actually were when she came to write them down. His birth year here is a guess from the years he was dancing.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), chs. 22/30 (SU-EN): Hakutobo dancer under the name Ashikawa Seisaku, lead dancer in Nyushoku no Onna (1988) choreographed by Ashikawa"},"mariobiagini":{"blurb":"He joined the Workcenter in Pontedera in 1986, its first year, was a group leader by 1987, and is considered — with Thomas Richards — Grotowski's official successor. A protagonist of Action, the central opus of Art as vehicle, he also worked at Grotowski's side as translator and editor of his texts. From 2007 he directed the Workcenter's Open Program, carrying the practice outward into performances woven from Allen Ginsberg's poetry, I Am America and Electric Party among them.","src":"Mroz, The Dancing Word (Rodopi, 2011)"},"rennieharris":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Rennie Harris: Lorenzo Harris, born January 28, 1964; founded Rennie Harris Puremovement in 1992","blurb":"A North Philadelphia dancer who found his form watching the Soul Train lockers and was teaching for the Smithsonian at fifteen, he founded Rennie Harris Puremovement in 1992 — the first hip-hop dance company built for concert touring — to prove the form was theater without cutting it from its roots. Rome & Jewels turned Romeo and Juliet hip-hop; Facing Mekka and Heaven followed; the Samuel H. Scripps Award for lifetime achievement came in 2023.","src":"Puremovement 2023"},"mrwiggles":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Mr. Wiggles: 'Steffan ‘Mr. Wiggles’ Clemente (born 1965)', member of the Rock Steady Crew and the Electric Boogaloos","blurb":"A South Bronx dancer with a double membership that maps street dance's two coasts: Rock Steady Crew for the b-boying, the Electric Boogaloos for the popping he became known for. He put both styles to work as a co-choreographer of hip-hop theatre productions and as a judge and teacher on the international circuit. Almost nobody holds standing in both founding institutions at once; his career is the bridge between them.","src":"Foundation for Contemporary Arts"},"suen":{"srcb":"sv.wikipedia, Su-En: 'Susanna Maria Margareta Akerlund, fodd 1 november 1966, ar en svensk koreograf och dansare, utbildad inom danskonsten butoh'","blurb":"A Swede who walked into a Hakutobo performance in Tokyo in 1988 and by the end of that same night was a student of Ashikawa and an apprentice of Tomoe Shizune. Five years of it: floors to clean, props and costumes to build, night-club burlesque with the other dancers, and finally a stage name — SU-EN — bestowed at her company's 1992 debut. She moved the company to a converted village school in rural Sweden in 1994 and has been working out what a nordic butoh body is ever since, in snow and in scrap yards.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 30 (SU-EN): trained 1988-1993 with Ashikawa, Tomoe Shizune, Gnome and Hakutobo; stage name bestowed at Kaze no Cho (1992); moved company to Sweden 1994; also Fraleigh, Butoh (2010): born 1966; lived in Tokyo from 1986 to 1994 and was an apprentice with the Tomoe Shizune and Hakutobo group from 1988, the years Ashikawa Yoko was choreographing; the SU-EN Butoh Company was formally started in Tokyo in 1992 and is now based at Haglund Skola in rural Sweden; she also holds a nattori licence in the traditional dance form Jiuta-mai, under the name Kei Izumo of the Yoh Izumo school; curator of the Friktioner International Performance Festival in Uppsala since 2006; her Chicken Project (2003) is a five-hour event in which humans live as chickens, performed at the Stockholm state theatre and aimed at how chickens are treated globally; in her teaching she asks dancers to twist their faces like rubber and become demons, skeletons and discarded junkyard metal"},"richardcolon":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Crazy Legs (dancer): 'Richard Colon (born January 1, 1966)', president of the Rock Steady Crew","blurb":"Recruited into Rock Steady Crew in 1979 at thirteen, he became its president and its engine: the Manhattan expansion, the Flashdance finale — where he doubled for Jennifer Beals — Beat Street, and a 1992 Bessie Award for the crew's choreography. The critic Sally Banes documented his accidental invention of the 'W', legs double-backed beneath him; the continuous backspin is also laid to him. He retired from battling in 2012 at the crew's 35th anniversary, still running the institution he had joined as a boy.","src":"National Puerto Rican Day Parade, historical honoree page for Richard 'Crazy Legs' Colon (nprdpinc.org) - b. 1966 in the Bronx, president of Rock Steady Crew, Flashdance and Beat Street, Bessie Award, Hispanic Heritage Award; OldSchoolHipHop.com (recruited into RSC in 1979, crew's Manhattan expansion)"},"kenswift":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ken Swift: Kenneth James Gabbert, born August 13, 1966; began b-boying in 1978 at the age of twelve","blurb":"He started breaking at twelve in 1978, joined Rock Steady Crew in 1980, and spent the Flashdance-and-Wild Style years putting b-boying on film. His deeper work was grammatical: original footwork and freezes, and the standardized terminology b-boys worldwide now argue in — b-boys themselves widely name him the single greatest influence on the dance. He founded VII Gems in 1996, a school of 'hip hop fundamentals' in 2004, and has taught dance studies at UCLA since 2018.","src":"B-Boy Summit speaker bio (bboysummit.com) - Kenneth James Gabbert, b. 13 Aug 1966, longtime Rock Steady Crew member and former Vice President; president of Breaklife and VII Gems in New York City"},"ronaldkbrown":{"srcb":"Gottschild, The Black Dancing Body (2003) — via Internet Archive full-text search","blurb":"He founded Evidence, A Dance Company, in New York in 1985, before he turned twenty, and has spent the decades since making one continuous argument: that modern dance, Senegalese sabar, Afro-Caribbean movement, and contemporary urban dance belong in a single body. His dances entered the repertories of Ailey's company and Philadanco; a Bessie, a Guggenheim, and an Audelco award for the musical Crowns followed.","src":"Evidence 2023"},"imrethormann":{"blurb":"A Swiss dancer trained under Kazuo Ohno, and one of the European practitioners who became teachers in their own right — he recurs as a guest at the New York Butoh Kan alongside Murobushi, Kasai and Waguri, and appears in Italian scholarship on butoh's European transplants beside Yvonne Pouget and Xavier Le Roy. Beyond that the record I could open is thin: the training years, and what he made of the practice, are not documented in the sources at hand.","src":"Athens24 2023"},"rajendragangani":{"srcb":"Sangeet Natak Akademi's own award citation PDF, 'RAJENDRA KUMAR GANGANI / Akademi Award: Kathak': 'Born in 1966 in Jodhpur, Rajasthan, Shri Rajendra Kumar Gangani inherited the tradition of the Jaipur gharana of Kathak dance from his father Guru Kundan Lal Gangani.' The build's activity-minus-25 estimate had him at 1958","blurb":"Started on kathak by his father Kundan Lal at four, he came through Delhi's Kathak Kendra in 1983–84 and grew into the Jaipur gharana's presiding technician — 'technical wizardry' is the phrase that follows him — while composing group works like Tribandhi and Maharaas that give a solo gharana an ensemble life. President Kalam handed him the Sangeet Natak Akademi award in 2003; he now stands where his father stood, the Jaipur line's reference point in Delhi.","src":"Sahapedia"},"koenaugustijnen":{"srcb":"nl.wikipedia, Koen Augustijnen: 'Koen Augustijnen (Mechelen, 23 juni 1967) is een Vlaams danser en choreograaf'","blurb":"He studied history at Ghent while taking theatre courses in Antwerp and Brussels, then spent two decades inside les ballets C de la B, working as choreographer with Alain Platel's company from 1990. His own pieces — Bâche, Import/Export, Ashes — mix dance styles with performance, theatre, acrobatics and music, often set against baroque song, Purcell especially, and run humour and intensity unusually close together. Collaborations stretched from the band dEUS to Toneelgroep Amsterdam and the Sydney Opera House.","src":"Making Contemporary Theatre: International Rehearsal Processes (Manchester University Press, 2010)"},"franckchartier":{"srcb":"Peeping Tom's own artist page: \"Franck (°1967, Roanne, FR)\"","blurb":"Sent at fifteen by his mother to Rosella Hightower's ballet school, he danced for Maurice Béjart from 1986 to 1989, then Preljocaj, then Brussels: Rosas' Kinok, Needcompany, and les ballets C de la B (La Tristeza Complice, Iets op Bach, Wolf). With Gabriela Carrizo he founded Peeping Tom in 2000 and has co-signed its uncanny interiors since, directing The Lost Room and The Hidden Floor for Nederlands Dans Theater — the former took the Dutch 'Swan' for most impressive dance production of 2016.","src":"Peeping Tom, artist page (via the Internet Archive, 2019 and 2021 snapshots)"},"mukaikumotaro":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 向雲太郎: 1967年4月22日, 舞踏家集団デュ社の主宰者","blurb":"He joined Dairakudakan in 1994 and became the senior dancer who leads the company classes; in 2001 he made the first piece for Kochuten, the company-inside-the-company Maro built so that members could learn to direct rather than only to dance. He left in 2012 and founded his own troupe, Deux Shrine, in 2014, opening it with a work about his grandfather, who died of radiation exposure at Hiroshima.","src":"Routledge Companion to Butoh Performance (2018), ch. 47 (Vessey): Dairakudakan senior member who leads company classes and choreographed the first Kochuten piece, Paradise in a Jar Odyssey (2001)"},"jacopogodani":{"blurb":"He studied fine art at Carrara and dance at Béjart's Mudra, then spent 1991 to 2000 as a lead performer in Ballett Frankfurt, working on Forsythe's pieces from inside them. His own productions are total designs — he takes the lighting, video, projections, costume styling and music as well as the steps. From 2015 to 2023 he directed the Dresden Frankfurt Dance Company, the ensemble descended from Frankfurt, and handed it to Ioannis Mandafounis.","src":"Gaertnerplatztheater, 'Jacopo Godani' (gaertnerplatztheater.de): studied at Bejart's international dance centre Mudra; leading soloist of William Forsythe's Ballet Frankfurt working closely with Forsythe; appointed artistic director of the Dresden Frankfurt Dance Company 2015"},"crystalpite":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Crystal Pite: born December 15, 1970","blurb":"Tap at four, first professional ballet at nineteen, and then a stretch inside Ballett Frankfurt, where Forsythe's improvisation film used her as one of its four dancers. Kidd Pivot, founded in Vancouver in 2002, is where she works the mixture out: dancers in kuroko black handling marionettes in Dark Matters, recorded speech lip-synced in Revisor, and Betroffenheit, made with Jonathon Young out of the shock that follows catastrophe.","src":"Kidd Pivot, 'Crystal Pite' (kiddpivot.org): former company member of Ballet British Columbia and William Forsythe's Ballett Frankfurt; formed Kidd Pivot in Vancouver 2002; Associate Artist at Nederlands Dans Theater, Sadler's Wells and Canada's National Arts Centre"},"waynemcgregor":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Wayne McGregor: Wayne McGregor CBE, born 12 March 1970","blurb":"He founded Random Dance in 1992 and in 2006 became the Royal Ballet's first resident choreographer to come from contemporary dance, making Chroma, Infra, Woolf Works and The Dante Project there. He treats choreography as a research problem: Living Archive was an AI experiment trained on his own movement record, and he has worked with Random International and, in another register, on a Harry Potter film.","src":"Studio Wayne McGregor, 'Wayne McGregor' (waynemcgregor.com): founded Random Dance (now Company Wayne McGregor) in 1992; Resident Choreographer at The Royal Ballet since 2006, the first from a contemporary dance background"},"gabrielacarrizo":{"srcb":"Peeping Tom's own artist page (Wayback capture 14 Aug 2020): 'Gabriela (°1970, Cordoba, AR) was ten years old when she started dancing...' — the same page series that dates Franck Chartier","blurb":"From a multidisciplinary school in Córdoba, Argentina — dancing at ten, making her first choreographies there — she came to Europe at nineteen and worked with Caroline Marcadé, Alain Platel, Koen Augustijnen and Needcompany. In 2000 she and Franck Chartier founded Peeping Tom, building worlds where hyperrealism gradually slips into the surreal; her own directions include Moeder, The Missing Door for Nederlands Dans Theater and La Ruta, which took the Olivier for Best New Dance Production in 2024.","src":"Peeping Tom - company artist page for Gabriela Carrizo (peepingtom.be, archived 2021): 'Gabriela (1970, Cordoba, AR) was ten years old when she started dancing at a multidisciplinary school'; she 'moved to Europe when she was nineteen' and worked with Caroline Marcade, Les Ballets C de la B (La Tristeza Complice, 1997; Iets op Bach, 1998), Koen Augustijnen and Needcompany; 'Gabriela has been the artistic director of Peeping Tom, along with Franck Chartier, since they co-founded the company in 2000'"},"adesolaosakalumi":{"blurb":"Born in the Bronx to a mother who danced principal roles in his uncles' Africa I Dance Theater, he trained on scholarship at Dance Theatre of Harlem and made his name popping and locking. As co-founder of GhettOriginal he helped carry hip-hop from cipher to script: Jam on the Groove opened Off-Broadway at the Minetta Lane in November 1995 and drew a Drama Desk nomination. He later fronted Fela! — ensemble and understudy in 2009, the title role by the 2012 Broadway return — and choreographed for films from School of Rock to Enchanted.","src":"Fjord Review"},"evayerbabuena":{"srcb":"es.wikipedia, Eva Yerbabuena: born 1970 in Frankfurt, raised in Ogíjares near Granada","blurb":"Born in Frankfurt in 1970 and raised in Ogíjares, outside Granada, she began flamenco at eleven and was working professionally at fifteen. She founded her own company in 1998 with the guitarist Paco Jarana, her husband, as musical director, took Spain's National Dance Prize in 2001, and drew collaborators from far outside the form — Pina Bausch, Mikhail Baryshnikov. In 2025 she became the first flamenco dancer in the Olivier Awards' history to win its prize for outstanding achievement in dance.","src":"Revista La Flamenca"},"scottgraham":{"blurb":"Co-founder and artistic director of Frantic Assembly, formed in 1994 by three Swansea University students none of whom had studied drama: 'we began with little more than a fierce work ethic and a desire to do something different and to do it differently.' The company's movement-and-music devising, always kept in service of story, reached its widest audience through The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-Time with the National Theatre — Tony for Best Play, 2015 — and is now standard study for UK drama students.","src":"Graham & Hoggett, The Frantic Assembly Book of Devising Theatre, 2nd ed. (Routledge, 2014): CIP entry"},"sharoneyal":{"srcb":"he.wikipedia שרון אייל: 'נולדה ב-2 בדצמבר 1971'","blurb":"Twenty-three years inside Batsheva — dancer from 1990, associate artistic director, then house choreographer until 2012 — before she left with Gai Behar, a Tel Aviv party producer she met at an electronic music event. L-E-V, founded in 2013, took Naharin's Gaga into the register of the club and the rave; the company has gone by S-E-D since 2022.","src":"Sadler's Wells, 'Sharon Eyal' Associate Artist page (sadlerswells.com): danced with Batsheva Dance Company 1990-2008; House Choreographer 2005-2012; launched L-E-V in 2013 with long-time collaborator Gai Behar"},"rachidouramdane":{"srcb":"fr.wikipedia, Rachid Ouramdane: 'ne en 1971 a Nimes'","blurb":"Born in Nîmes to Algerian parents, he built a choreography out of testimony — Des témoins ordinaires (2009), Sfumato (2012) — and out of people who are not dancers: twelve young athletes from Gennevilliers made up Surface de réparation. He co-ran the Grenoble choreographic centre with Yoann Bourgeois, and since April 2021 has directed Chaillot, the French national theatre given over to dance.","src":"French Wikipedia, 'Rachid Ouramdane' (b. 1971 Nîmes to parents of Algerian origin; graduated from the CNDC Angers 1992; danced for Hervé Robbe, Emmanuelle Huynh, Odile Duboc and Meg Stuart; co-founded Fin novembre with Julie Nioche 1996, own company L'A. 2007; projects at the border of dance and documentary; Superstars 2006, Surface de réparation 2007 with twelve young athletes from Gennevilliers, Des témoins ordinaires 2009, Sfumato 2012; co-directed CCN2 Grenoble with Yoann Bourgeois 2016-2021; appointed director of the Théâtre national de Chaillot 5 April 2021; Officier de l'ordre des Arts et des Lettres 2022)"},"amitlahav":{"srcb":"UK Companies House officers register for GECKO THEATRE, company no. 05243558, registered at Warehouse Studios, Paul's Road, Ipswich: 'LAHAV, Amit Jason / Date of birth: July 1972 / Appointed on 28 September 2004', the company's incorporation date. He is the only Lahav on the register. Gecko's own bio page confirms he is the founder and artistic director but carries no year","blurb":"Born in Israel and raised in London, trained with Lindsay Kemp and David Glass, he spent four years facilitating theatre with street children in Southeast Asia before founding Gecko in 2001. The Ipswich-based ensemble devises through what he calls total theatre — emotion, physicality, metaphor, breath and musicality — and treats no show as finished: each keeps evolving through years of touring. Eight works, from Missing and Institute to The Wedding and Kin, staged at the National Theatre in 2024, tour worldwide.","src":"Gecko Theatre Company - 'About Gecko' (geckotheatre.com): 'In 2001, Amit established the company as its Artistic Director'; the company is based at The Warehouse, Paul's Road, Ipswich"},"borischarmatz":{"srcb":"fr.wikipedia, Boris Charmatz: 'ne en 1973 a Chambery en Savoie'","blurb":"Trained at the Paris Opera school, he made his name in 1993 with À bras-le-corps, a duo danced close enough for the audience to touch. Since then he has kept inventing institutions rather than joining them: Bocal, a nomadic, temporary school that questioned how dance is handed on; and from 2009 the Rennes choreographic centre, which he renamed the Musée de la danse and took to MoMA and Tate Modern. He got Tanztheater Wuppertal in 2022 and announced his departure three years in.","src":"Festival de Marseille, 'Boris Charmatz' (festivaldemarseille.com): dancer and choreographer, creator of the Bocal school and Musee de la Danse (2009-2018); launched [terrain] 2019; took over the directorship of Tanztheater Wuppertal Pina Bausch in September 2022"},"trajalharrell":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Trajal Harrell: born 1973","blurb":"His long series asks a single counterfactual: what would have happened in 1963 if someone from the Harlem voguing balls had come downtown to perform alongside the early postmoderns at Judson Church? He worked the question out from 2009 to 2017, staging it in different sizes, from the intimate to the spectacular, and it carried him from MoMA to Antigone Sr. Since 2019 he has been at Zurich's Schauspielhaus, where in 2024 he founded a dance ensemble inside a theatre company.","src":"Wikipedia, 'Trajal Harrell' (b. 1973 Douglas, Georgia; Yale BA in American Studies; 'Twenty Looks or Paris is Burning at The Judson Church' — 'What would have happened in 1963 if someone from the voguing ball scene in Harlem had come downtown to perform alongside the early postmoderns at Judson Church?' — begun 2009, completed 2017, staged in different sizes; Antigone Sr.; The Ghost of Montpellier Meets the Samurai; resident choreographer at Schauspielhaus Zurich from 2019, founded the Zürich Dance Ensemble 2024; work shown at MoMA, MoMA PS1 and the New Museum; Guggenheim, Foundation for Contemporary Arts and Doris Duke fellowships)"},"akramkhan":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Akram Khan (dancer): Akram Hossain Khan MBE, born 29 July 1974","blurb":"A Lambeth-born son of a Dhaka family, Pratap Pawar's kathak disciple at seven and a teenager touring the world in Peter Brook's Mahabharata. The company he founded in 2000 with Farooq Chaudhry turned his double schooling — kathak's spun precision, contemporary dance's weight and fall — into a language of its own, from Kaash (with Anish Kapoor and Nitin Sawhney) to DESH to XENOS, with a duet for Juliette Binoche and a section of the 2012 London Olympics opening ceremony along the way.","src":"Sanjoy Roy, 'Step-by-step guide to dance: Akram Khan' — Guardian dance series, 2009 (sanjoyroy.net) (born south London 1974 to Bangladeshi parents; kathak with Pratap Pawar from age seven; Northern School of Contemporary Dance; X-Group at PARTS)"},"salvasanchis":{"blurb":"He was in the first class to come out of P.A.R.T.S., and became one of the few people De Keersmaeker signs work with rather than commissions from: Desh (2004) and A Love Supreme (2005) are jointly credited. He had joined Rosas as a dancer in 2003, for Bitches Brew / Tacoma Narrows. Beyond the Rosas collaborations the record reachable here is thin — a Catalan choreographer and teacher, born 1974.","src":"Cankarjev dom press release, 'Rosas: A Love Supreme' (cd-cc.si): Sanchis (Catalonia, 1974) graduated among the first generation of P.A.R.T.S. students; joined Rosas 2003 for Bitches Brew / Tacoma Narrows; co-signed Desh (2004) and A Love Supreme (2005) with De Keersmaeker"},"jamesthierree":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, James Thierree: 'James Spencer Henry Edmond Marcel Thierree (born 2 May 1974)'; fr.wikipedia adds the place: 'ne le 2 mai 1974 a Lausanne'","blurb":"Grandson of Charlie Chaplin and great-grandson of Eugene O'Neill, son of Victoria Chaplin and Jean-Baptiste Thierrée, he made his stage debut at four in his parents' Cirque Imaginaire and toured its successor, the Cirque Invisible, until 1994. In 1998 he founded the Compagnie du Hanneton; La Symphonie du Hanneton and its successors — La Veillée des Abysses, Au Revoir Parapluie, Raoul — blend contemporary circus, mime, dance and music, and took four Molière awards in 2006. Cinema keeps borrowing him back: a César for Chocolat in 2017.","src":"Peak Performances 2019"},"hofeshshechter":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Hofesh Shechter (חופש שכטר): born 3 May 1975","blurb":"He auditioned for the Jerusalem academy as a pianist and switched to dance on arrival, which explains everything after: he writes his own scores, and they are mostly drums. Three years at Batsheva, then a rock band and percussion study in Paris, then London in 2002 — and Uprising, In your rooms and Political Mother, dance delivered at gig volume. Of his conscription at eighteen he has said it was 'like an electrical short circuit in my brain'.","src":"Opera national de Paris, 'Hofesh Shechter' artist page (operadeparis.fr): career debut as a dancer with the Batsheva Dance Company; moved to London 2002; founded the Hofesh Shechter Company in 2008, resident at the Brighton Dome"},"megurodaiji":{"blurb":"He came to butoh late in the first generation's life, studying from 2000 with Akiko Motofuji — Hijikata's widow — and performing at Asbestos-kan in its final years before it closed. In 2003 he joined Ko Murobushi's male company Ko&Edge. Since then he has run his own group, worked solo on a fellowship in New York, and built formats in Kyoto that put visitors inside a butoh performance rather than in front of one.","src":"Kyoto Butoh 2023"},"sidilarbicherkaoui":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Sidi Larbi Cherkaoui: born 10 March 1976","blurb":"At nineteen he won a national prize for a solo mixing vogueing, African dance and hip-hop, which is a fair description of his method ever since. Out of les ballets C de la B and P.A.R.T.S. came zero degrees with Akram Khan, Sutra with Shaolin monks, Dunas with María Pagés, and Babel(words) with Damien Jalet. He founded Eastman in 2010 and has since run two ballet companies, Flanders and the Grand Théâtre de Genève.","src":"Grand Theatre de Geneve, 'Sidi Larbi Cherkaoui, future Director of Ballet' (gtg.ch): trained at P.A.R.T.S., the school founded by Anne Teresa De Keersmaeker; founded his own company Eastman in 2010; directs the Ballet of the Grand Theatre from season 2022-23"},"damienjalet":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Damien Jalet: born 17 August 1976","blurb":"Cherkaoui's collaborator since 2000 and co-author of Babel(words), he works most often where dance meets a sculptor's problem. Vessel was made with Kohei Nawa during a residency in Kyoto; Skid puts seventeen performers on a ten-metre stage tilted at 34 degrees and lets gravity take part in the choreography for forty minutes. He also made the dancing in Guadagnino's Suspiria and Paul Thomas Anderson's Anima.","src":"Wikipedia, 'Damien Jalet' (b. 17 August 1976 Uccle, Belgian-French; studied theatre at the national institute of the performing arts in Brussels, then modern dance, completing his training in New York; collaborator of Sidi Larbi Cherkaoui since 2000 within les ballets C de la B; BABEL (words) 2010 with Cherkaoui and Antony Gormley, Laurence Olivier Award and Benois de la danse 2011; Vessel 2016 with Kohei Nawa, developed during a Villa Kujoyama residency in Kyoto; Skid 2017 for GöteborgsOperans Danskompani, 17 performers on a 10-metre stage sloping at 34 degrees over roughly 40 minutes; film choreography for Suspiria 2018, Anima 2019, Emilia Pérez)"},"maartenvancauwenberghe":{"blurb":"The musician half of Voetvolk, the company he founded with Lisbeth Gruwez in 2006. He composes and designs the sound, which in their pieces is not accompaniment: the company calls its signature a refined but razor-sharp dialogue between dance and sound design. From Forever Overhead (2007) through It's going to get worse and worse and worse, my friend (2012) to Piano Works Debussy (2020), the work is credited to the pair, out of what they describe as a shared artistic DNA.","src":"Voetvolk, 'over' (voetvolk.be): Voetvolk (2006) is the dance company of dancer/choreographer Lisbeth Gruwez and musician/composer Maarten Van Cauwenberghe; hallmark is 'a refined yet razor-sharp dialogue between dance and sound design'; the two 'share a unique artistic DNA' developed consciously and intuitively; productions from Forever Overhead (2007) and Birth of Prey (2008) through It's going to get worse and worse and worse, my friend (2012), AH/HA (2014), We're pretty fuckin' far from okay (2016), The Sea Within (2018), Piano Works Debussy (2020), Nomadics (2023) and WASCO! (2024); the company is attached to the KVS"},"ichikawadanjuro13":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Ichikawa Danjuro XIII (十三代目市川團十郎): born 6 December 1977","blurb":"He collected the Naritaya names in order — stage debut at five, Shinnosuke VII in 1985, Ebizō XI in 2004 — then had to wait: the Danjūrō XIII succession announced in 2019 was delayed by the pandemic until late 2022, when he took the name in the same Kabukiza programme in which his nine-year-old son debuted as Shinnosuke VIII. Between the names came a Japan Academy best-actor prize, stages from Paris to New York, and the public loss of his wife Mao Kobayashi in 2017. The aragoto line now runs through him.","src":"Seiko Group ambassador profile of Ichikawa Danjuro XIII Hakuen (seiko.co.jp) - eldest son of Ichikawa Danjuro XII; stage debut as Shinnosuke VII 1985, succeeded to Ebizo XI 2004, succeeded to Danjuro XIII Hakuen Nov 2022; Kabuki Web (Shochiku) succession announcement 2022"},"kyleabraham":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Kyle Abraham: born August 14, 1977","blurb":"Raised in Pittsburgh's Lincoln-Larimer neighborhood, trained through performing-arts schools to an MFA at NYU, he danced for Bill T. Jones before founding A.I.M in 2006, a company for work 'galvanized by Black culture and history.' Pavement (2012), drawn from Boyz n the Hood, was the breakthrough; The Runaway (2018) carried his blend of ballet, hip-hop, and West African movement into New York City Ballet, and Untitled America into Ailey's company. A MacArthur Fellowship came at thirty-six.","src":"AIM 2023"},"lisbethgruwez":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority GND 1061653153, Gruwez, Lisbeth: date of birth 1977, occupations Tanzerin/Schauspielerin","blurb":"Dancer and choreographer of Voetvolk, founded in 2006 with the musician Maarten Van Cauwenberghe on what the two call a shared artistic DNA. Her solos are the company's best-known works — It's going to get worse and worse and worse, my friend (2012), We're pretty fuckin' far from okay (2016). The repertory since has gone looking for audiences that do not attend dance: Bob Dylan listeners, Debussy listeners, a walk with the public before the show begins.","src":"Voetvolk, 'over' (voetvolk.be): Voetvolk (2006), the company of dancer/choreographer Lisbeth Gruwez and musician/composer Maarten Van Cauwenberghe; expressive, sensitive, accessible dance language with detailed visual imagination; productions incl. It's going to get worse and worse and worse, my friend (2012), AH/HA (2014), Lisbeth Gruwez dances Bob Dylan (2015), We're pretty fuckin' far from okay (2016), Penelope (2017), The Sea Within (2018), Piano Works Debussy (2020), Into the Open (2022), Nomadics (2023, which opens with a walk so audience and artists connect before the show begins); stated aim of reaching music audiences and people 'just outside the expected public'; presented at Festival d'Avignon, Ruhrtriennale, Tanz im August, Venice Biennale and others"},"simonaorinska":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Simona Orinska: born 18 August 1978 in the town of Ergli, Latvia","blurb":"Latvia's butoh artist of record — some twenty years of performance work rooted in what she calls the archetypal and healing processes of body and mind, and in Baltic identity as a crossroads of cultures. She has built institutions as much as dances: a performance-art festival in Riga from 2018, an artist residency, a university programme in multimedia performing arts. Which butoh teachers she trained with I could not source.","src":"Creatrix Mag 2021"},"marlenemonteirofreitas":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority GND 1030173842, Freitas, Marlene Monteiro: date of birth 1979, place of birth Sao Vicente, Cape Verde","blurb":"She did rhythmic gymnastics until she was thirteen, quit because she disliked competing, and started making dances with friends in Cape Verde — the group Compass. Her work runs on metamorphosis and deformation, faces held in grimace; awarding her a Silver Lion in 2018, after Bacantes, the Venice Biennale called it a Dionysian power that erases the borders of what is aesthetically correct. She co-founded P.OR.K in Lisbon and has worked with Boris Charmatz, Emmanuelle Huynh and Loïc Touzé.","src":"Portuguese Wikipedia, 'Marlene Monteiro Freitas' (b. São Vicente, Cape Verde, 1979; practised rhythmic gymnastics until thirteen, left the sport because she disliked its competitiveness, then began dancing and choreographing with friends, giving rise to the group Compass in Cape Verde; co-founder in Lisbon of the cultural structure P.O.R.K, signing Paraíso-coleção privada 2012-13 and marfim e carne - as estátuas também sofrem 2014; has worked with Emmanuelle Huynh, Loïc Touzé, Tânia Carvalho and Boris Charmatz; Silver Lion for career at the 12th Venice Dance Biennale 2018, awarded after Bacantes - Prelúdio para uma purga, the Biennale citing an 'electrifying presence', a 'Dionysian power', an interest in 'metamorphosis and deformation' and the erasure of the borders of what is aesthetically correct; Medal of Merit of the Government of Praia, Cape Verde, 2017)"},"jeftavandinther":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Jefta van Dinther: born 4 March 1980 in Utrecht","blurb":"Eighteen full-length pieces since 2008, each one a staged research problem: what a body becomes when light, sound and material work on it as hard as choreography does. Plateau Effect was made for the Cullberg Ballet; Dark Field Analysis and The Quiet followed. He works between Stockholm and Berlin with the same two collaborators — Minna Tiikkainen on light, David Kiers on sound — and opened a workspace, DIORAMA, in Berlin in 2022.","src":"jeftavandinther.com, 'about' (graduated as a dancer in 2003; since 2008 has created 18 full-length performances, incl. IT'S IN THE AIR 2008, Kneeding 2010, GRIND 2011, THIS IS CONCRETE 2012, Plateau Effect 2013, As It Empties Out 2014, Dark Field Analysis 2017, The Quiet 2019, Unearth 2022, Mercury Rising 2025; rigorous physical approach and staged research of movement itself, the moving body in relation to light, sound and materials; collaborators Minna Tiikkainen on light and David Kiers on sound; senior lecturer and artistic director of the MA Choreography at Uniarts Stockholm 2012-2014; founder of the DIORAMA workspace in Berlin since 2022; Birgit Cullberg Grant 2012, Swedish Theatre Critics' Dance Prize 2013; works between Stockholm and Berlin)"},"ioannismandafounis":{"srcb":"Deutsche Nationalbibliothek authority GND 1060907410: preferred name Ioannis Mandafounis, date of birth 1981, place of birth Athen, occupations Tanzer, Choreograf. Tanz im August's artist biography agrees: 'Ioannis Mandafounis, 1981 in Athen geboren, studierte Tanz an der National School of Dance in Athen und am Conservatoire de Paris... gehorte von 2005 bis 2009 zur Forsythe Company'","blurb":"A dancer of The Forsythe Company who since 2023 has run the company descended from it, Dresden Frankfurt — and runs it on improvisation, inside an institution built for fixed repertory. His stated aim is to give dancers back their agency and to move improvisation out of the studio into performance itself, addressing the audience the way actors do. He has invited Forsythe back to make new work on that same principle.","src":"Springback Magazine, 'Ioannis Mandafounis: taking improvisation centre stage' (springback.org, 2025): former dancer with The Forsythe Company; artistic director of Dresden Frankfurt Dance Company since 2023"},"juanmanuelfernandezmontoya":{"srcb":"es.wikipedia, Farruquito: 'Juan Manuel Fernandez Montoya (Sevilla, 15 de agosto de 1982)'; El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco agrees on the year","blurb":"On Broadway at four in Flamenco Puro beside his grandfather, in Saura's Flamenco as a boy, head of the family lineage at fifteen when El Farruco died in 1997 — his life ran ahead of itself, then into catastrophe: in 2003, driving without a licence, he struck and killed a pedestrian in Seville, drove on, and later served fourteen months for it. He returned to the stage with the inherited baile intact; the New York Times had already called him the finest artist to visit the city. The dynasty's standard-bearer carries both records.","src":"Instituto Andaluz del Flamenco 'Farruco' biographical notes PDF - names grandson Juan Manuel Fernandez Montoya (1982), artistic name Farruquito, who inherited the name and the baile of his grandfather; KQED Arts, 'Farruquito Channels Dramatic Life Events into Forceful Flamenco'"},"bobbijenesmith":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Bobbi Jene Smith: born November 7, 1983","blurb":"Ten years inside Batsheva, mentored by Naharin — and then the subject of a documentary about the decision to leave. Bobbi Jene (2017) follows her choice to give up the company and try to make her own work in America. She had gone to Juilliard and not graduated; she went back to the United States anyway, choreographed the climactic dance in Annihilation, and co-founded the American Modern Opera Company with Or Schraiber.","src":"Jerusalem Post, 'After a decade with Batsheva, Bobbi Jene Smith dances her way home' (jpost.com): a decade-long member of Batsheva Dance Company, performing in Naharin's Sadeh"},"asainobuyoshi":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 浅井信好: 1983年12月10日, 月灯りの移動劇場主宰, Cie PIERRE MIROIR芸術監督","blurb":"He won a world hip-hop championship in 2002 and four years later was inside Sankai Juku, touring seventy cities in twelve countries over five years with the slowest dancing on earth. He left in 2011 for his own choreography — including work with Damien Jalet and the sculptor Kohei Nawa, and music videos — and runs an outdoor theatre platform out of Nagoya.","src":"TEDxAnjo 2019"},"ishiinorihito":{"srcb":"ja.wikipedia 石井則仁: 1984年9月25日, 東京都出身, 舞踏カンパニー山海塾所属","blurb":"A dancer in Sankai Juku, named in the casts of the company's recent repertory including the 2019 premiere ARC. Almost nothing else about him is documented in the sources I could open. He is on this map as a member of the generation now carrying Amagatsu's work rather than as a figure with a separate record of his own.","src":"UMS 2010"},"raphaelleboitel":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Raphaelle Boitel: born 1984","blurb":"A contortionist from age eight, street-performing with her siblings until Annie Fratellini noticed and the family moved to Paris for circus school. At thirteen she joined James Thierrée's Symphonie du Hanneton — critics called her 'boneless' — and stayed with his productions into the 2010s. In 2012 she founded Cie L'Oublié(e), where her stagings (5es Hurlants, La Chute des Anges) cross circus with theatre and dance under cinematic light, and she has smuggled circus into opera at La Scala and the Opéra-Comique.","src":"Peak Performances 2019"},"alexanderekman":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Alexander Ekman: Karl Wilhelm Alexander Ekman, born 1984","blurb":"He danced at the Royal Swedish Opera, NDT II and Cullberg, where he first choreographed, and has since made around fifty pieces, several built around one outsized idea. Cacti (2010) mocks the snobbery of the art world that commissions it and is now in twenty-odd companies' repertories; A Swan Lake (2014) put 5,000 litres of water on the Oslo opera stage. In 2024 he staged the opening ceremony of the Paris Paralympics.","src":"Wikipedia, 'Alexander Ekman' (b. 1984 Stockholm; Royal Swedish Ballet School 1994-2001; Royal Swedish Opera 2001-2002, Nederlands Dans Theater II 2002-2005, Cullberg Ballet 2005-2006, where he made his debut as a choreographer; around 50 pieces for companies worldwide; Cacti 2010, 'a comment on the high browed snobbish art world', performed by over 20 companies; A Swan Lake 2014 with 5,000 litres of water on stage for Norwegian National Opera & Ballet, music by Mikael Karlsson; Midsummer Night's Dream 2015 for the Royal Swedish Ballet; PLAY 2017 for Paris Opera Ballet, 25 sold-out performances at the Palais Garnier; in 2024 chosen as artistic director and choreographer of the opening ceremony of the Paris Paralympic Games)"},"rociomolina":{"srcb":"rociomolina.net, her own site, Biografia: 'Rocio Molina nace en Malaga en 1984... a los diecisiete se gradua en el Real Conservatorio de Danza de Madrid'; es.wikipedia, Rocio Molina Cruz gives '(Torre del Mar, Espana, 1984)' and El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco 'nacio en Malaga, en el ano de 1984'","blurb":"A construction painter's daughter from Vélez-Málaga, improvising dances at three, in Madrid's conservatory at fifteen, in María Pagés's company at seventeen. Her own works — El eterno retorno (2006) took its cue from Nietzsche — treat fans, shawls and the bata de cola as raw material for experiments flamenco had not licensed. Spain's National Dance Prize came at twenty-six, the Gold Medal for Merit in Fine Arts in 2022.","src":"Revista La Flamenca"},"guillaumepige":{"blurb":"A Frenchman in London who studied with Decroux's line at one remove — Steven Wasson, Corinne Soum, Daniel Stein, Thomas Leabhart — and founded Theatre Re, where ensemble devising and live music turn 'intimate, difficult to talk about and sometimes taboo life experiences' into physical performance. The Nature of Forgetting, the company's best-travelled piece, has played seventeen countries, from the Hong Kong Arts Festival to the London International Mime Festival. He has taught at RADA since 2011.","src":"Mime London 2023"},"julianspooner":{"blurb":"Co-artistic director of Rhum + Clay, the company begun at the École Jacques Lecoq in Paris that he now leads with Matt Wells from London's New Diorama Theatre. Their devising is collective from the first day — 'embracing the magical, the comical and the surreal, but getting down in the earth and the dirt where the real stories are found' — in shows like The War of the Worlds and Project Dictator. His own Mistero Buffo, after Dario Fo, won him The Stage Award for Acting Excellence.","src":"Rhum and Clay 2023"},"viggovenn":{"srcb":"en.wikipedia, Viggo Venn: born 22 March 1989 in Kongsberg","blurb":"A Norwegian who left economics for École Philippe Gaulier, spent four years in the duo Zach & Viggo — their Thunderflop won Best Comedy at the 2016 Brighton Fringe — and went solo with The Life of Pepito. In 2023 he won Britain's Got Talent by putting on and taking off high-visibility vests, one after another, until the nation surrendered the £250,000 and a Royal Variety slot; the stripping was real enough to give him a repetitive strain injury in the elbow.","src":"Wikipedia 2023"},"charliewheeller":{"blurb":"A breakdancer with A-level dance who went through the National Centre for Circus Arts and came out a Cyr wheel specialist and founder member of Barely Methodical Troupe — the experimental acrobatic circus company that folds hand-to-hand, Cyr, parkour, b-boying and tricking into something closer to theatre. Bromance, their first show, opened at the Edinburgh Fringe in 2014 and toured internationally ever since; KIN premiered at London's Roundhouse in 2016.","src":"B-QL 2023"},"berendamico":{"blurb":"A childhood in Taekwondo led to the National Centre for Circus Arts, where he specialised in hand-to-hand with Louis Gift, and to founding Barely Methodical Troupe with Gift and Charlie Wheeller. He combines tricking and free running with contemporary dance — the same fusion BMT practises as a company, acrobatics run through games and relationships. Bromance opened at the Edinburgh Fringe in 2014 and kept touring; KIN followed at the Roundhouse in 2016.","src":"B-QL 2023"},"yannfrisch":{"srcb":"fr.wikipedia, Yann Frisch: 'ne en 1990'","blurb":"A juggler at nine, a magician at ten or eleven, self-taught before the Lido circus school in Toulouse; in 2008 he met Raphaël Navarro and became a figure of the magie nouvelle movement. His card-and-ball act Baltass made him French, European, then world champion — the FISM Grand Prix for close-up at Blackpool in 2012, the video passing a million views inside a week. His stage shows, Le Syndrome de Cassandre (2015) and Le Paradoxe de Georges (2018), carried the act into clowning and theatre.","src":"The Place 2025"},"manuelfernandezmontoya":{"srcb":"El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco and EFE: Seville, August 1997","blurb":"The youngest of La Farruca's three bailaor sons, nicknamed by his grandfather Farruco because, in the family's telling, the child absorbed the art of the house like a sponge. He was on stage from childhood — Jerez at twelve, Manhattan and San Francisco at fourteen, Saura's Flamenco, Flamenco on film — and waited until 2024 to premiere a show alone. In this family, a solo debut at twenty-six counts as patience.","src":"El Arte de Vivir el Flamenco, bailaor profile 'El Carpeta' (elartedevivirelflamenco.com/bailaores401.html) - b. Seville Aug 1997, grandson of Farruco, son of La Farruca, brother of Farruquito and Farru; EFE via Infobae, 'El Carpeta, de la saga de los Farrucos, se estrena en solitario', 6 Feb 2024"},"ichikawashinnosuke8":{"blurb":"Born Horikoshi Kangen in 2013, he stepped onto the Kabukiza stage in November 2022 as Ichikawa Shinnosuke VIII in the very programme that made his father Danjūrō XIII — a double ceremony staged as one event, the house's future announced alongside its present. Kabuki's name system is what makes a nine-year-old legible: Shinnosuke is the first rung of the ladder that leads through Ebizō to Danjūrō, and the debut is the promise that the ladder will be climbed.","src":"Kabuki Web (Shochiku official), 'The name succession of Ichikawa Danjuro XIII, Hakuen, and the stage debut of Ichikawa Shinnosuke VIII' (2022) - Horikoshi Kangen, eldest son of Ebizo XI, made his stage debut as Ichikawa Shinnosuke VIII at the Kabukiza, Nov-Dec 2022"}},"THINGS":{"1789":{"blurb":"The company plays a troupe of 1789 fairground barkers re-enacting the Revolution — a device that let the actors pass judgement on the characters instead of disappearing into them. Trestle stages ringed a standing audience who moved between them; the company built a full-size mock-up first to find the right platform height for people on their feet. Actors dressed themselves from heaps of junk-shop cast-offs, like children playing pirates, and the designers fixed afterwards whatever they had chosen. — 1970-11-11"},"1980":{"blurb":"Made in the year Rolf Borzik — Bausch's partner, designer of the company's sets and costumes until his death — died of leukemia. Peter Pabst stepped in, contributing the set and video projections, with Raimund Hoghe as dramaturg: the hinge between the Borzik era of the Tanztheater and everything Bausch made after. — 1980-05-18"},"thegreentable":{"blurb":"Masked diplomats argue across a long table under green cloth, then draw pistols from their pockets and fire into the air: war declared. Between those bookends, in eight scenes, a skeletal Death advances with a robot-like tread and claims them one by one. Jooss set the diplomats' posturing to a tango and won the Paris choreography competition with it. After his death his daughter controlled the staging for thirty years, which is why it survives intact rather than as a reconstruction. — 1932-07-03"},"cafemuller":{"blurb":"Figures move through a stage strewn with café chairs, several dancing with eyes closed while others rush to clear the furniture from their blind paths — dependence, manipulation and trust played out to Purcell arias from Dido and Aeneas and The Fairy-Queen. Bausch, who as a child watched her father work in his café, danced in it herself, and it has toured the world ever since. — 1978-05-20"},"afectoshumanos":{"blurb":"A cycle of solos by the dancer widely considered the most talented soloist of her generation — one who by then relied on engagements in South America to support her art, as Ausdruckstanz lost its public to the postwar ballet boom. Susanne Linke rebuilt it in 1987 from a film record and left the seams showing: where Hoyer changed costume offstage, Linke changed in full view, keeping open the gap between her own presence and the persona she was assuming. — 1962"},"ulrikemeinhof":{"blurb":"A typewriter hammers behind a black-red-gold curtain before it opens. Three performers share the title role; one of them works at a desk on a lighting bridge high above the audience for the whole ninety minutes, folding her typed pages into paper aeroplanes and throwing them down at the society below, and by the end flinging them as crumpled scraps. Underneath, the company whirls without a break towards Meinhof's violent death. — 1990"},"totenmal":{"blurb":"A memorial to the dead of the First World War, made with the poet Albert Talhoff: a speaking choir borrowed from the working-class theatre movement joined to a movement choir borrowed from the populist wing of modern dance, about a hundred performers in a hall built for the occasion. Wigman subordinated herself to a male Demon, war given human form. Most spectators took the work as pacifist; Manning argues it was a prototype for Nazi theatre, above all in its strategy of not appearing political. — 1930"},"totenklage":{"blurb":"The women's lament in Heroic Struggle and Death Lament, the fourth section of the Olympic Youth spectacle. Sixty men fought a mock war while a speaker recited that the fatherland's highest command in need is sacrificial death; then eighty women led by Wigman mourned them. No male leader was left standing on the field — only Hitler, watching from a box while the spectators watched him watching. It was the last commission the Cultural Ministry ever gave her. — 1936"},"vomtauwind":{"blurb":"Laban's movement choir for the 1936 Olympics, cancelled after its dress rehearsal. Manning's chapter on the Games treats the spectacle that did go ahead, Olympic Youth, and not this; in the introduction to the new edition she names the comparison — Laban's suppressed choir, the boycotted Tanzfestspiel, Riefenstahl's Olympia — as work still to be done. What the piece looked like is not established there. — 1936"},"callas":{"blurb":"Eight tableaux, and not a biography of the singer: Hoffmann used the name to get at the theatre itself — life on a stage, the manufactured world, the star system. Thirty years on it was rebuilt with the original designers, Johannes Schütz and Joachim Herzog, and the reconstruction was documented at every stage by dance scholars at the Freie Universität and published as a book and a DVD, an unusually complete paper trail for a piece of Tanztheater. — 1983"},"appalachianspring":{"blurb":"A frontier couple's wedding day, danced in skips, curtsies, square-dance figures and a grand right and left around Noguchi's spare set with its Shaker rocking chair, to the Copland score that keeps returning to 'Simple Gifts.' Made as the war in Europe wound down, it landed as a promise of new life in a new land — 'shining and joyous,' the reviewers said. — 1944-10-30"},"primitivemysteries":{"blurb":"Three sections — Hymn to the Virgin, Crucifixus, Hosanna — each of which begins and ends in silence with a measured processional. A group of women walk in unison as celebrants, framing a single figure, and the shapes they assemble around her are the pietà, the crucifixion, the assumption. Graham drew it from the Catholic ritual the Spanish brought to the Southwest and the way it wound into the ceremonies of the indigenous villages there. — 1931-02-02"},"nightjourney":{"blurb":"The Oedipus story told from Jocasta's side, so that the action happens where only her heart and mind are real. In the central duet with her son, lovemaking keeps being broken into by maternal memory — the infant at her breast, the child in her arms — and one rope serves at once as marriage bond, umbilical cord and the instrument of her death. It belongs to Graham's Greek cycle, made when Freud and Jung had become the common language for family and desire. — 1947-05-03"},"diversionofangels":{"blurb":"Three couples in white, red and yellow — mature, romantic and adolescent love — in a plotless dance Graham distilled after the first performance, cutting the original title Wilderness Stair and Noguchi's desert set. She wanted Kandinsky in motion: the Girl in Red crossing the stage like 'a bold slash of red across a blue background.' — 1948-08-13"},"themoorspavane":{"blurb":"Subtitled Variations on the Theme of Othello, and by the score's own account not a choreographic version of Shakespeare's play at all but the tragedy of Everyman, timeless in its implications. Four dancers only — the Moor, his wrongfully suspected wife, his treacherous friend and that friend's wife — hold the murder inside the measured steps of a pavane and other dances of the high Renaissance, to Purcell compiled and arranged by Simon Sadoff. — 1949-08-17"},"trend":{"blurb":"The work that established Holm, in John Martin's words, beyond question as one of the important figures in the American dance world. Martin read it as a synthesis of the dynamic quality of American dance with the more subjective qualities of the German — its sense of space, its fluency, its unbroken relationship of movement to emotion. Wallingford Riegger wrote for it, and it was through this piece that Varèse's Ionisation reached a Bennington audience. — 1937-08-13"},"withmyredfires":{"blurb":"Two parts to a Wallingford Riegger score, and the section titles carry the shape: a Ritual of Hymn to Priapus, Search and Betrothal, Departure, then a Drama of Summons, Coercion and Escape, Alarm, Pursuit and Judgment. Sixteen women and seven men, so the pursuit is a mob's. It is one panel of the trilogy that also holds New Dance and the now-lost Theatre Piece. — 1936-08-13"},"dayonearth":{"blurb":"Three dancers, one box and a length of cloth, and Copland's Piano Sonata. The notation score describes it as depicting, in a non-literal and poetic way, the age-old cycle of life on earth. Humphrey made it after arthritis ended her own dancing in 1944 and she took over as director of Limón's company — one of the works she made on other people's bodies rather than her own. — 1947"},"lynchtown":{"blurb":"One of the three dances of Weidman's Atavisms: a choreographic depiction of 'a carnal and bloodthirsty mob acting like vultures about to devour their prey.' The frenzy it studies is the crowd's, not the victim's — American modern dance in the 1930s looking directly at American violence. — 1936"},"hereandnowwithwatchers":{"blurb":"An evening-length duet made for the company Hawkins had just founded, with Ralph Dorazio's designs and Lucia Dlugoszewski playing her own timbre piano live; Hawkins danced it with Barbara Tucker. Dlugoszewski never set the score down in full — she notated only what she needed for her own playing — so reviving the music means working from film and the dance score alongside her scattered pages. She also performed it on its own, as Archaic Timbre Piano Music. — 1957-11-24"},"kissmekate":{"blurb":"Brooks Atkinson's notice singled out the dances — 'Under Hanya Holm's direction, the dancing is joyous' — but the show's lasting first came on paper: Holm's Labanotation score of the choreography became the first choreography copyrighted in the United States. The success launched her on roughly a dozen more musicals. — 1948"},"passacagliaandfugue":{"blurb":"Bach, thirteen women, five men, and no story whatever. 'The Passacaglia is like a procession through some idealized and noble city,' says the introduction to the notation score. 'The dancers, the celebrants themselves, become the architecture of the city. The architecture dances.' Humphrey's assistant notator reached for a single word for it: grandeur, without any aristocratic or haughty connotation. — 1938-08-05"},"rooms":{"blurb":"Chairs stand in for rooms — nameless city dwellers living their dreams and frustrations in quietly desperate isolation, each stuck inside the few feet a chair allows. Kenyon Hopkins conducted his own jazz score on the first night, played by a small band that included Charles Mingus and Teo Macero. Walter Terry wrote at the time that Sokolow 'makes you care about her characters to the degree that their problems replace your own'. The chair-as-room has been borrowed by choreographers many times since. — 1955-02-24"},"americandocument":{"blurb":"Spoken word set against movement to ask 'What is America?', built from essential American texts. It brought Erick Hawkins in as the first man among Graham's women — Jane Dudley, Sophie Maslow, May O'Donnell. The dance itself faded; a libretto, Barbara Morgan's photographs and a short film of the original cast at Bennington seeded a 2010 reimagining. — 1938"},"caveoftheheart":{"blurb":"Medea after Jason has abandoned her: 'a psychological study of the destructive powers of love, the dark passions that guard the human heart, coiled like a serpent ready to strike.' First titled Serpent Heart, it moves from betrayal through the murders the Chorus foresees but cannot stop, until the sorceress, cleansed by flames, is returned to her father the Sun. — 1946-05-10"},"lagya":{"blurb":"Dunham's Martinique fieldwork staged: a three-part love-triangle ballet built around the ag'ya, the island's fighting dance, folded into American ballet vocabulary and designed by John Pratt, whom she later married. It became a signature of the Dunham repertory — anthropology translated into theater without surrendering its sources. — 1938-01-27"},"southland":{"blurb":"An anti-lynching dance drama. A chorus asks whether it is true what they say about Dixie; a white woman falsely accuses a black field hand of raping her, a mob hangs him from the magnolia tree, and mourners carry the body through a nightclub where the patrons answer with dances of violence, sorrow and bewilderment. Dunham had insisted for years that she never went into a direct social message; she staged this abroad and never in the United States, and the State Department moved to suppress it. — 1950-12-09"},"kykunkor":{"blurb":"Anne Kennedy, business manager of the Unity Theatre, saw Dafora's group at Madison Square Garden and invited them to her own house — a makeshift room on East Twenty-third Street that had lately been John's Beauty Parlor. It opened there on 5 May 1934 and played to four near-empty houses before John Martin's review of the ninth turned it into a hit; the run then took in other theatres including the Chanin and went on to tour New England and New Jersey. The first African dance-drama to be received in New York as concert art rather than as spectacle. — 1934-05-05"},"strangefruit":{"blurb":"Primus first called it A Man Has Just Been Lynched, and that is the situation the solo occupies — not the killing, but one person's response in the minutes after it. The later title comes from the Lewis Allan poem that Billie Holiday had made famous four years earlier; the dance has been given both with the text spoken and with it merely implied, and by contemporary accounts it held the room either way. — 1943-02-14"},"southernlandscape":{"blurb":"A five-part suite drawn from Howard Fast's novel Freedom Road, about the destruction of thriving Black communities in the South during Reconstruction. \"I was so horrified by the information I got from Fast's book,\" Beatty said; \"I couldn't believe man could be guilty of such horrible behaviour.\" What survives of it is the solo Mourner's Bench, danced on and around an eight-foot wooden bench: the figure lies across one end with his head to the audience and rolls, balances, pleads. Dayton Contemporary and Philadanco keep it. — 1949"},"mournersbench":{"blurb":"A solo of grief, danced on and along a long wooden bench with the posture erect and the focus inward — Beatty called it a personal expression of grief. It survives as the fragment of Southern Landscape that outlived the whole: a larger work built on Howard Fast's novel Freedom Road, about black and white farmers in the Reconstruction South and the terror used to break them. A 1948 film of Beatty performing it exists, and he oversaw a reconstruction in 1992. — 1949"},"games":{"blurb":"Three sections — Play, Hunger and Fear — made from the street games McKayle remembered from a Harlem childhood, danced to traditional songs: children of a city's slum district at play and never quite free of what stands over them. What critics found remarkable is that he used no children in it, and not even very young dancers. It was his first choreographic work and it made his name at once; the Christian Science Monitor called it a minor masterpiece. — 1951"},"rainbowroundmyshoulder":{"blurb":"Incarcerated men on a Southern chain gang, and one woman crossing among them as the figure they hold on to — sweetheart, mother, wife by turns, the whole emotional supply of the piece. The score is traditional songs out of the Lomax collections, arranged for the stage by Robert de Cormier and Milton Okun. The lyrical and the athletic are pitched at the same level, so the physical trial and the despair carry equal weight; it is usually named as McKayle's masterpiece. — 1959"},"thebeloved":{"blurb":"Eight minutes, one man and one woman, and by Britannica's account it came out of a newspaper story: a husband who suspected his wife of infidelity and beat her to death with a Bible. Horton meant it, the notation score says, as a statement in pure dance terms of 'the bigotry and sexual chauvinism that held women subservient in fin-de-siecle New England, a kinetic projection of the social savagery of the double standard'. Bella Lewitzky shares the choreography; the score is Judith Hamilton's. — 1948"},"bluessuite":{"blurb":"One long, sultry night in a sporting house out of the barrel houses and fields of Ailey's southern childhood — Saturday-night sinning before the Sunday churchgoing — its people drinking, dancing and flirting until a train and church bells call time. The work with which his company began, from a choreographer who called the blues 'hymns to the secular regions of the soul.' — 1958-03-30"},"revelations":{"blurb":"Ailey made it out of what he called blood memories — a Texas childhood, the Baptist church, spirituals and song-sermons, gospel and holy blues. Three sections carry it from grief to celebration: Pilgrim of Sorrow, the baptism of Take Me to the Water, and the closing Move, Members, Move. Sometimes sorrowful, sometimes jubilant, but always hopeful was his own description of the material; his company calls it the most widely seen modern dance work in the world. — 1960-01-31"},"cry":{"blurb":"A sixteen-minute solo in white leotard and long ruffled skirt, made in a few studio days as a birthday gift for Ailey's mother and dedicated 'to all Black women everywhere — especially our mothers.' Judith Jamison, on whom it was made, read its three sections as the passage from slavery's hardships through grief and depression toward overcoming. — 1971-05-06"},"bluesforthejungle":{"blurb":"A survey of African-American oppression running from the slave auction to the ghetto, in sections whose titles carry the temperature: Slave Auction, Behind Prison Walls, Preaching the Gospel, View from a Tenement Window, Junkie, Riot. Pomare began it during the summers of social unrest and it premiered in the middle of them. Confrontational where his contemporaries were lyrical, it has been called one of the American dance masterpieces and left vivid impressions on the people who saw it. — 1966"},"narcissusrising":{"blurb":"Pomare's signature solo, and by several accounts more harrowing than Blues for the Jungle: an S&M biker in a leather jockstrap, bare-chested, in sunglasses and a rakish cap, revving an imaginary motorcycle and asserting his right to be a rebel - Black, queer and self-defined. Don McDonagh called the character menacing, until police searchlights hunt him down. Critics doubted the piece could survive a transfer off Pomare's own body; in 2001 Donna Clark danced it as a woman and proved otherwise. — 1968"},"rhythmritual":{"blurb":"One of the pieces Rodgers grouped as his \"rhythm dances,\" in which the performers make their own accompaniment - playing hand-held instruments, or handling props so that at moments the only sound in the theatre is the one the dancing produces. Perpener names it in that genre beside Percussion Suite and Tangents without describing it further, so what this particular piece looked like is not established there. — 1973"},"trioa":{"blurb":"Energy spread evenly through every movement, with none of the attack and recovery that shapes ordinary dancing, and no phrase ever repeated. The performer never meets the audience's eye: facing front, the eyes close or the head is already turning away. It began as the opening section of a longer work, was first done by three dancers running it at once and out of step with each other, and has since been taught on and on until it became the most widely transmitted single dance of its century. — 1966-01-10"},"trioapressured":{"blurb":"Rainer's five-minute 1966 sequence put under load, four ways in one evening. Pat Catterson danced Trio A backwards; Rainer, Douglas Dunn and Steve Paxton danced it as a trio; Rainer and Colin Beatty danced it as a duet in which every move of his was predicated on keeping her face in view - which is the exact inverse of Trio A's own rule that the performer's gaze never meets the audience; and Catterson and Rainer danced it to the Chambers Brothers' In the Midnight Hour. — 1999-10-04"},"continuousproject":{"blurb":"Workshop process pushed onstage: the piece changed as it went, audiences watching revision itself rather than a finished object, the line between rehearsing and performing deliberately erased. Out of its open method grew Grand Union, the improvisational collective in which Rainer then danced for two years. — 1970"},"magnesium":{"blurb":"On wrestling mats, men in loose clothes stagger, crash into each other, fall, roll and lurch up again, with a lot of hand-clasping and dragging, the body used as one piece and the falling pursued with a task-like attitude and no orientation toward the audience. It closes in the stand: standing still and noticing the smallest shifts of weight and balance. Contact improvisers cite it as the seminal work of the form, made before the form had a name, which is why 1972 is counted as the start. — 1972-01"},"contactimprovisations":{"blurb":"Five afternoons of continuous practice in a Manhattan gallery, spectators drifting in and out: dancers giving and taking weight, rolling, colliding, falling — grown from Magnesium and the standing 'small dance' Paxton had developed at Oberlin months before. Less a repertory work than a founding demonstration: from it, contact improvisation spread to jams on every continent except Antarctica. — 1972-06"},"manwalking":{"blurb":"A man in street clothes walks, at ordinary walking pace, straight down the front of a seven-storey building. Brown called it a natural activity under the stress of an unnatural setting: strip out every eccentric choreographic option, she argued, and what remains is that you actually see the movement. Joseph Schlichter was the one who walked, and the whole thing was over in a couple of minutes. — 1970"},"roofpiece":{"blurb":"Half an hour of dancing distributed across downtown rooftops, strung from 53 Wooster Street to 381 Lafayette, performed without music by ten or so dancers — Valda Setterfield, Douglas Dunn and David Gordon among them. Brown ran it again in 1973 over a different stretch of SoHo, from 420 West Broadway to 35 White Street, and it was that repeat that got widely publicised. In the company's own records it carries a second title, Roof and Fire Piece. — 1971-05"},"danceconstructions":{"blurb":"Task pieces for plywood and rope first shown in Yoko Ono's loft: a rope-climb up a steep ramp as 'an isolated action that lacks a purpose,' six to nine people massed into a Huddle 'to be a strong unit.' Paxton said the work 'was like a pebble tossed into a large, still, and complacent pond'; the ripples ran to Judson, and in 2016 MoMA acquired seven of them. — 1961-05"},"rollers":{"blurb":"Two performers sat in shallow wagons and were hauled about by three ropes, so that the vehicle and not the dancer settled what the movement could be. It was one of a pair Forti built out of children's playthings; the companion piece, See-Saw, took its structure from a seesaw with a toy attached that mooed. The programme also carried work by Jim Dine and Claes Oldenburg. — 1960"},"pastime":{"blurb":"A solo in three parts, each taking a different angle on the body. Childs walked a predetermined pathway around the gym with the movement isolated in her right leg; in the section she called the bag, a length of stretched fabric flattened out and lengthened along her body, which she meant as comic. Paxton remembered her early work as terse, like haikus. Reviewing it at the YMHA, Louis Horst complained that Philip Corner's water score sounded as though all the plumbing at the Y was out of order. — 1963-01-30"},"carnation":{"blurb":"A solo made entirely of handling objects. Childs sat at a small table with foam-rubber curlers and sponges, set a salad colander on her head, pulled each curler out with a neat swift tug and hung it on the colander's prongs, then packed the sponges into her mouth and dumped the lot into a blue plastic bag. Jill Johnston gave it a whole column; Banes reads the curlers, the sponges and the garbage apparatus as the housewife's own tools turning on her. — 1964-04-24"},"ten":{"blurb":"A band plays and ten performers work a radically simple score, using each other and a set of poles to build strict geometric figures. It came out of the year Hay spent making group configurations rather than solos, and the original accompaniment was live, by the rock band The Third Eye - a Judson choreographer with a rock band in the pit. — 1968"},"juice":{"blurb":"A theatre cantata in three installments, staged a month apart and shrinking each time. It opened on the Guggenheim's spiral with some eighty-five performers, four red figures beginning the climb up the ramp and a woman on horseback in the rotunda; moved to a proscenium stage at Barnard; and ended in Monk's own loft, where the audience walked among the objects and costumes the earlier parts had used. The piece was the audience's memory of it as much as anything on stage. — 1969"},"birdsofamerica":{"blurb":"Halprin's first long work, pulling two years of improvisation into fifty minutes: seven directives for five dancers, charted by age and size, each set of tasks paired with an encumbering prop — the eight-year-old assigned to kick and carry beach balls. The one filmed section, Child and Tall Man, Lifting and Carrying, instructs only lie down, stand, sit, be lifted, roll, still. Just before the premiere she handed every dancer a ten-foot bamboo pole and made them do the dance holding it. — 1960"},"kinjiki":{"blurb":"A duet on Mishima's novel of forbidden love: the older man hands a white chicken to a boy, the boy scissors it between his legs and sinks to the floor, and the two roll together in the dark miming sodomy. Both Motofuji and Yoshito Ohno spent decades insisting the bird survived — killing it was beyond what any of them could afford. Hijikata was not banned either: he, Motofuji, Ohno and Tsuda walked out of the sponsoring association themselves, and Mishima, who came to complain about the stolen title, stayed to become his patron. — 1959-05-24"},"nikutainohanran":{"blurb":"A collection of solos in which Hijikata danced a bride, an old woman, a male burlesque dancer, a flamenco dancer, a scoliosis patient, a nattily dressed sailor and a young girl — read either as the people who had shaped him or as the selves the prevailing norms would not let surface. A chicken was killed onstage; he worked a giant golden phallus, then rose out of a canopied carriage in a kimono worn backwards. From here he began building dances out of images and words rather than steps. — 1968-10"},"hosotan":{"blurb":"It opens on wind and the cawing of crows and the songs of goze — the poor blind women who travelled the country playing the samisen — placing the scene in the impoverished northeast Hijikata came from. Its twenty-minute climax keeps him supine throughout: half naked, skin chafed and unhealthy, legs moving feebly, quivering in a way that recalls Minamata disease as much as the smallpox of the title. What he assimilated in preparation, by fasting, was the deformed state of leprosy patients. — 1972-10-25"},"admiringlaargentina":{"blurb":"At 71, Ohno came back to the stage in homage to the Spanish dancer Antonia Mercé, 'La Argentina,' whom he had seen perform half a century earlier; Hijikata directed. It won the Dance Critics' Circle Award, and from the Nancy festival in 1980 to La MaMa in 1981 it carried butoh — and Ohno — onto the world's stages. — 1977-11-26"},"lederniereden":{"blurb":"Ten tableaux alternating trios with solos and duets, and the performance by which butoh became visible in Europe. Murobushi danced his mummy — a living corpse identified with Miroku, the Buddha still to come — appearing in the second tableau inside an ignited coffin; Ikeda danced material from Mesukazan. French audiences read the burnt skin falling from that mummy as the bodies of Hiroshima, an identification Murobushi later opposed hard. — 1978-01-27"},"amagatsusho":{"blurb":"A metaphorical tale of an amagatsu, the old Japanese doll left beside a child's bed as a talisman, and the piece from which Amagatsu took the method he has used ever since — peeling away redundant concepts and images rather than throwing them pell-mell into the pot as Hijikata did. Maro Akaji, who had given the dancer his stage name and the company its name, guest-directed and appeared. Amagatsu was not satisfied with it and carried much of its content into the next year's piece. — 1977"},"kinkanshonen":{"blurb":"Shaved heads, white-powdered bodies and a live peacock onstage. Its 1980 performances at Nancy and Avignon were Sankai Juku's European debut, helping introduce butoh to Western audiences; it took the Grand Prix at the Belgrade International Theatre Festival in 1982 and, recreated in 2005, was still touring decades on. — 1978"},"sholiba":{"blurb":"Dancers are lowered head-down on ropes from the top of a building, down its face, to the crowd below — the company's signature image in public space, described at the time as suggesting childbirth. It made the company famous outdoors and then cost it: in Seattle on 10 September 1985 a rope broke and Yoshiyuki Takada, 31, fell some eighty feet to his death, and the rest of a ten-city American tour was cancelled. — 1979"},"niwa":{"blurb":"Nakajima's first butoh choreography, and the piece she carried out of Japan with. Fraleigh describes a woman's spiritual transfiguration made out of ma, the interval between things: mental anguish condensed into floods of gesture that coalesce and dissolve within seconds, like ripples in a pool, with the face held at an intensity that vibrates with that interval. Fraleigh saw its North American premiere in Montreal in 1986 and began writing about butoh from then on. — 1982"},"kazenocho":{"blurb":"The performance at which a Swedish apprentice was given the stage name SU-EN and presented to the world as part of the Hakutobo lineage — a hata-age kōen, a raising of the flag. Ashikawa Yoko choreographed it, Tomoe Shizune directed. Five years of training came first, in a world where the teacher is loved and feared and demands everything, and where the humblest and most important task is cleaning floors. SU-EN calls it the point of no return. — 1992"},"butohfunotebooks":{"blurb":"Not a performance but the archive underneath one. From the late 1960s Hijikata filled notebooks with reproductions of European and American paintings and sculptures cut from art magazines, with notes scrawled alongside on the poses and qualities to be taken from each. He then altered the resulting steps by prompt: imagine a young girl doing this, or an old woman; imagine the movement in water rather than air; imagine being eaten by a given number of bugs. This is the material that became butoh-fu. — c.1970"},"deaddreamsofmonochromemen":{"blurb":"Four men in a work drawn from Killing for Company, the book about the serial killer Dennis Nilsen, from DV8's earliest and most physically intense period. It took the Time Out Dance Award and the Evening Standard Ballet Award, and David Hinton's 1990 television version, made for the South Bank Show, began the company's run of dance films. — 1988"},"mysexourdance":{"blurb":"The one DV8 piece Lloyd Newson did not choreograph: he made and performed it with Nigel Charnock, and the company began from it. Craine and Mackrell describe a duet in which the physical risk-taking mirrored the emotional challenges of a male relationship. The New York Times, seeing it in New York two years later, called it a homo-erotic duet for two men that was more brutal than tender. — 1986"},"enterachilles":{"blurb":"In a dimly lit British pub, men with pints and cigarettes act out stereotypical male behaviour until the vulnerability underneath shows through — 75 minutes on masculinity that The Observer found 'heartening,' with 'DV8 blazing a fresh, dangerous trail.' The film version made with Clara van Gool won the 1997 Emmy for the Performing Arts, and a new production with Rambert followed in 2020. — 1995-06-07"},"thestreetofcrocodiles":{"blurb":"Built out of the life and stories of Bruno Schulz, a Galician Jew shot by a Gestapo officer in 1942. Joseph peers into books and conjures his childhood back: a flock of flapping books becomes the rare birds in the attic, crapping on a dining table made from a row of school desks, and his father returns first as a giant wooden puppet that is sawn into pieces and then as a ghost dripping sawdust. In the opening minutes a man walks casually down the back wall of the stage. — 1992-08-13"},"mnemonic":{"blurb":"A devised piece about dislocation — from our ancestors and from each other — cutting between the examination of a prehistoric body found in the Alps and a man in London whose lover has vanished across Europe. McBurney played both the living man and the corpse. The published text carries the input of at least fifty named contributors, a measure of how the company builds a show. — 1999-12-18"},"lagedor":{"blurb":"Subtitled \"première ébauche\", first draft. The actors invented commedia figures rather than reviving them: Philippe Caubère's Arlequin became Abdallah, a Maghrebi building labourer newly landed at Marseille, crossing a France of Pantalons, Doctors and Brighellas. The company's programme was blunt that it was not resurrecting commedia or Chinese opera but hunting rules of play that would show daily life as astonishing and changeable rather than familiar and fixed. — 1975"},"akropolis":{"blurb":"Wyspiański's national epic moved to a concentration camp — Auschwitz as 'our Acropolis.' Actors with faces frozen into grimace-masks build the camp around themselves as the play proceeds, until a final procession bears a corpse into the grave-crematorium they have made; the audience shares their space, held at absolute distance. Toured to Edinburgh, Paris and New York and filmed for US television with an introduction by Peter Brook, it stands as a canonical work of twentieth-century theatre. — 1962-10-10"},"theconstantprince":{"blurb":"Ryszard Cieślak played the Prince almost naked in a white loincloth while the Court, in dark clothes and army boots, circled him like a flock of birds and made a martyr of him through the iconography of the Passion. Grotowski rehearsed Cieślak alone, calling himself an obstetrician to what the actor did to himself. The Court left without a curtain call and the body stayed on its wooden chest until the last spectator had gone. The film was shot at Spoleto in 1967 with a hidden camera. — 1965-04-25"},"apocalypsiscumfiguris":{"blurb":"Grotowski's last theatre production, four years in the making. The improvisations produced enough material for a full day, shown once as an internal premiere; the piece is what survived the compression. Over ten years of playing he stripped its theatrical signs away one at a time — white costumes replaced by everyday coloured clothes, the black plaster taken off the walls to show bare brick, the benches removed so spectators simply stood in a ring, the actors entering after the audience and greeting people they knew. — 1969-02-11"},"minfarshus":{"blurb":"The turn in Barba's method: the first Odin production not built on a narrative text written before rehearsals. It grew instead out of improvisations on the actors' reading of Dostoevsky's novels and his biography, and no Barba production since has begun from a prior text. Danish television filmed forty-two minutes of excerpts. The company toured it until January 1974, and its international standing dates from it. — 1972-04-11"},"theovercoat":{"blurb":"Gogol's clerk becomes Akakki, played by Lahav himself, marched about a set of right angles and chiaroscuro by ice-cold office superiors who regard the workers' lives as hardly worth a pin. Penny Francis, reviewing it at the Lyric, found the movement mathematically planned and sometimes gravity-defying, and the spoken text a soup of muttered phrases in French and Spanish and probably Esperanto — which did not worry her, though she thought the storyline was not entirely clear. — 2009"},"taylorsdummies":{"blurb":"Boy meets girl, boy says nothing, girl passes by — that is the whole story. Taylor is played by three performers plus a couple of life-size dummies and a baby doll or two, and most of the few words spoken are expletives coming from one of the dolls. Three Taylors sit at separate nightclub tables, two on the floor and the third jutting out at right angles halfway up the back wall; the woman is literally unattainable, an aerialist who spends most of the show in a flying harness. — 2003-07-10"},"mummenschanz1972":{"blurb":"Three performers who had met at Lecoq's school and in Rome built a theatre out of materials instead of stories: lycra, foam rubber, styrofoam, plastic tubes, putty and dough, gathered from markets, skips and industrial suppliers and played with until an object suggested a scene. There is no script, no theme and no topical issue. One writer summed up the first production as a curious blend of masks, body-disguising costumes, anthropomorphic creatures and symbolic confrontations. — 1972"},"othello":{"blurb":"Half of Shakespeare's text is cut and the rest set in the shabbiest of twenty-first-century Yorkshire pubs: grubby walls, a fruit machine, and a pool table that doubles as the marital bed. Desdemona has tattoos, smokes dope and drinks WKD from the bottle. Laura Hopkins's walls concertina open and shut, and the best of the movement is a drunken dance in which the whole company leans and sways as Cassio gets more inebriated. — 2008"},"inthemiddlesomewhatelevated":{"blurb":"Two cherries hang suspended over the stage — the title points at them — while classical technique is pushed in unclassical directions against Thom Willems's electronic score, first danced by Sylvie Guillem, Isabelle Guérin, Laurent Hilaire and Manuel Legris. Later folded into Impressing the Czar, it spread into repertories worldwide and earned an Olivier for Royal Ballet Flanders' staging. — 1987-05-30"},"artifact":{"blurb":"Ballet turned on itself: in the second act the curtain is raised and lowered in the middle of the dance, the environment onstage drastically changed at each rise — interruption used as a compositional element rather than stage machinery. A signature of Forsythe's deconstruction of classical convention at the start of the Frankfurt era. — 1984-11-29"},"duoforsythe":{"blurb":"Forsythe invented it in a theatre foyer in under three weeks, moving in front of Jill Johnson and Regina van Berkel with a camera running so they could learn the material back off the tape. Ten to twenty minutes for two dancers side by side who never touch and never catch each other’s eye: unison giving way to rhythmic counterpoint over Thom Willems’s spare score or none at all, the breathing audible. He calls it a project rather than a piece, because it kept changing for two decades with each new cast. — 1996-01-20"},"fase":{"blurb":"Four movements to Reich's phase-shifting music — Piano Phase, Come Out, Violin Phase, Clapping Music — for two dancers alternating circles and straight lines through mathematically rigorous repetition; De Keersmaeker danced it with Michèle Anne De Mey. Its immediate success led to the founding of Rosas, and it has been performed over 200 times worldwide. — 1982-03-18"},"rosasdanstrosas":{"blurb":"Four women dance themselves, over and over: small everyday gestures set against abstract movement, with repetition in the leading role and the dancers' visible exhaustion doing the work drama usually does. Thierry De Mey and Peter Vermeersch wrote the score alongside the choreography rather than for it. Some five hundred performances and five generations of dancers later, De Mey's film is as familiar as the stage piece, and an online project teaches anyone who wants it the chair sequence. — 1983-05-06"},"drumming":{"blurb":"Twelve dancers to Steve Reich's percussion score, played live by Ictus. Reich's single rhythmic cell multiplies and phases against itself, and the choreography does the same thing with a single movement phrase - handed round, displaced, overlaid, until the stage is one machine of overlapping canons. De Keersmaeker laid the dancers' paths over a spiral built from the Fibonacci sequence, each dancer given a square of it as a home to leave and return to. — 1998"},"whatthebodydoesnotremember":{"blurb":"Vandekeybus's first choreography, built to find out what a body does under threat: dancers hurl bricks at each other and dive clear, barrel through combat rolls, throw themselves like missiles. Under the apparent chaos the structure is exact — one quartet passes clothing around at high speed while executing folksy little steps. In New York he and his composers, Thierry De Mey and Peter Vermeersch, took a Bessie for what the citation called a brutal confrontation of dance and music. — 1987-06-12"},"gisellematsek":{"blurb":"Ek keeps the story and moves it: Giselle becomes 'a solitary, vulnerable girl in a claustrophobic society on a remote island,' and in the second act the Wilis become patients of a psychiatric clinic. Made on Ana Laguna, whose filmed performance took the French Video Dance Prize — one of the reworked classics on which Ek's reputation rests. — 1982-07-06"},"carmenmatsek":{"blurb":"A decade after his Giselle, Ek reworked Carmen — another of the 'sensational reworks of classics' from his Cullberg directorship. Ana Laguna counted it among her favorite roles, and her filmed performance of it won an Emmy Award, carrying the production far beyond the stage. — 1992"},"korper":{"blurb":"Thirteen dancers, topless for most of it, treat each other as material: measuring one another's height and weight, drawing red circles round each other's organs and estimating what they would cost in euros, dragging partners across the floor by folds of skin, piling into a mound of bodies that an audience cannot help reading as a grave. A vast black diagonal wall holds a glass window they squirm behind until their breath clouds it. Hans Peter Kuhn's score is built from amplified clicking, hiccupping and gurgling. — 2000"},"desh":{"blurb":"A full-length solo Khan made and danced himself — the point where his kathak-rooted contemporary language turned from ensemble pieces toward work spun out of personal and cultural narrative. It won the Laurence Olivier Award for Best New Dance Production in 2012. — 2011"},"chroma":{"blurb":"Ten dancers, entirely off pointe, inside John Pawson's white minimalist set — the architect's first for a stage — to Joby Talbot's score with orchestrated White Stripes songs. Made in three weeks, it won the Olivier for Best New Dance Production and made McGregor resident choreographer, the first without formal ballet training in the line that ran from Ashton and MacMillan. — 2006"},"wolf":{"blurb":"A dozen or more dogs wander loose through a set of lock-up wire cages and walkways — a shopping mall — while the Klangforum Wien plays Mozart from inside it. Platel put the animals there to stand for a time under threat, a society regulated by fear, and set that against the composer's apparent merriment; the title points at Wolfgang as much as at the dogs. One reviewer counted over fifty living creatures on stage, the dancers held to set pieces and the dogs improvising. — 2003"},"babelwords":{"blurb":"Made with Damien Jalet and the sculptor Antony Gormley, who supplied five steel space-frames that the thirteen performers push and reconfigure all evening into ziggurats which now unite the group and now split it. The languages the company happens to speak are the soundtrack. Against Babel’s confusion Cherkaoui sets the opposite nightmare: a performer at the controls of a gunship bellows ‘Shut the FUCK up!’, mows down the non-anglophones, then wonders why anyone would learn another language. — 2010"},"house":{"blurb":"Eyal made it with Gai Behar, who came to her out of Tel Aviv’s nightlife rather than a dance studio, and Ori Lichtik’s soundtrack is a magpie collage: Tindersticks, D12, Aphex Twin, Tuxedomoon, The Residents’ Kaw-Liga, and the koto standard Sakura, Sakura played by Kimio Eto. Twelve dancers, thirty minutes. The three of them later rebuilt the piece at nearly twice the length for the company they run together, which is the version that has toured. — 2011"},"somnole":{"blurb":"Charmatz whistles the whole score himself — sometimes tuneless, sometimes snatches of the Pink Panther or a spaghetti western, sometimes bird noises — so that the breath leaving his lips and the appearance of a movement are the same event. In a pleated, patterned skirt he pads, skips, runs, raises himself on his elbows into a back stand, all but dozes off, then throws himself into a frantic tap dance. Presenters called it the first proper solo he had made for himself in thirty years of work. — 2021"},"kontakthof":{"blurb":"A Kontakthof is a court of contact: to German hits of the 1930s, an ensemble in Rolf Borzik's hall displays itself and works through the rituals of meeting. Bausch later showed the piece belonged to every generation, restaging it with ladies and gentlemen over 65 in 2000 and with teenagers over 14 in 2008. — 1978"},"kutralakuravanji":{"blurb":"The first dance-drama the school produced, and the model for the sixty that followed. Rukmini Devi took a seventeenth-century Tamil kuravanji poem - a form that mixes classical and folk material, in which a hill woman reads the heroine's palm and foretells her union with the god - and set it for her students, with music composed for the occasion by V. Krishnamachari of Bangalore. It gave Bharata Natyam something it had not had: a group repertory of narrative works. — 1944"},"bodasdegloria":{"blurb":"A flamenco reading of Lorca's Blood Wedding staged by the Farruco family for Canal Plus - the legendary Gitano dynasty running the fundamental flamenco forms through a wedding, three generations of them on screen at once, with the child Farruquito and his younger brother among them. It won a prize at Montreux the year it was made. — 1996"},"jhanakjhanakpayalbaaje":{"blurb":"One of India's first Technicolor films and the one that put a classical dancer at the centre of a Hindi picture. A dance guru trains a young woman to partner his son for a competition; the two fall in love, neglect the training, and the guru moves to replace her. Gopi Krishna both starred and choreographed, and the film took the National Film Award for best Hindi feature and the Filmfare best film prize. — 1955"},"thelockerssoultrain":{"blurb":"Campbell invented the Campbellock in the cafeteria of Los Angeles Trade Technical College at the end of 1969, nearly two years before Soul Train first went out in national syndication. The appearances by his group - by then renamed The Lockers after a legal fight over the word - carried the step out of that cafeteria to a national Black television audience every week, which is how locking became a vocabulary rather than a local trick. — 1973"},"tablet":{"blurb":"Opus 24 in Taylor's own numbering: a duet to a commissioned score by David Hollister, with set and costumes by the painter Ellsworth Kelly, danced by Taylor with Akiko Kanda. Dance Magazine's reviewer described a constant flow of movement invention all tied to a quality of play — 'a touch of teasing in the game of boy and girl' — and called it one of his most accessible and ingratiating works. — 1960-07-01"},"jardinauxlilas":{"blurb":"1962-10-15"},"thelearningprocess":{"blurb":"Graham made it in Israel for the company she had just helped found, and drew it out of the house of Atreus: the cast list runs Cassandra, Helen, Paris, Young Clytemnestra, Aegisthus and Clytemnestra's Image. Halim El-Dabh wrote the score and Jean Rosenthal lit it, with Arnon Adar rebuilding the lighting locally. The archive records twenty performances and then nothing. — 1964-12-17"},"herodiadebatsheva":{"blurb":"Graham's duet for a woman and her attendant, restaged on the company she had just helped found: Hindemith's score, Isamu Noguchi's set, Edythe Gilfond's costumes, and Jean Rosenthal's lighting rebuilt in Israel by Arnon Adar. Rena Gluck and Laurie Freedman led it. It ran up forty-two performances and went to the United States on the 1972 tour, and the archive records a first showing in a hall in Herzliya two days before the Tel Aviv opening. — 1964-12-14"},"hoshifuruizumi":{"blurb":"1989-06-15"},"sodalake":{"blurb":"A solo in silence for Michael Clark, danced in a black sleeveless leotard around Nigel Hall's 1968 sculpture — a rod and a circle, both hung from above, the artist's response to the flat geometry of the dry Soda Lake in the Mojave. Alston had made it for a BBC programme that fell through, and Nigel Osborne was to write a score; on seeing the choreography Osborne said it was complete in itself and that music would detract rather than enhance. — 1981"},"hailthenewpuritan":{"blurb":"A day in Michael Clark's life shot as fake fly-on-the-wall: he wakes, rehearses, takes an interview about coming to London and about Scottish dance, and the company runs scenes from New Puritans while Leigh Bowery — who designed the film and appears in it — gets ready for the clubs. The Fall supply most of the music, and Mark E. Smith and Brix Smith turn up in a mock television interview. It closes before dawn with Clark stripping and dancing to Elvis's 'Are You Lonesome Tonight?' — 1986"},"pushcomestoshove":{"blurb":"Tharp built it for Mikhail Baryshnikov's arrival at a company that had never had a dancer quite like him, giving him an easy ragtime prelude to Joseph Lamb before dropping the whole cast into Haydn's Eighty-Second Symphony with Baryshnikov as master of ceremonies. No major ballet company had presented a work that mixed the popular and the classical this way. Twenty-nine dancers, twenty-two minutes, and companies from the Paris Opera to Miami City Ballet still take it on. — 1976-01-09"},"occasionpiece":{"blurb":"A duet made of two solos danced at the same time, one by Cunningham and one by Mikhail Baryshnikov — eight minutes, for a four-performance retrospective of Cunningham's career. Jasper Johns adapted the Duchamp-derived set he had built for Walkaround Time thirty-one years earlier, and the sound was Cage's Music for Marcel Duchamp, so the occasion assembled the old collaborators around a guest from ballet. — 1999-07-21"},"warhorse":{"blurb":"Three puppeteers to a horse, called the Head, the Heart and the Hind: the Heart is the rudder and the steering wheel, the Hind is the engine, and the Head, one of them said, is like the marketing department — ready to go at any moment and making the moves look as though they were his own idea. Small bicycle brakes work the ears. Sedgwick's own contribution to the storytelling was the idea that the foal could dissolve into the grown horse. — 2007-10-17"},"kaash":{"blurb":"'Hindu Gods, black holes, Indian time cycles, tablas, creation and destruction': Khan's first full-length ensemble work, bridging kathak and contemporary dance inside Anish Kapoor's set, to Nitin Sawhney's score. The first tour alone ran to 133 shows across 65 venues in 23 countries. — 2002-03-28"},"configurations":{"blurb":"Jeyasingh asked Michael Nyman for music and he wrote his Second String Quartet, first for her solo Miniatures and then for the group version. The dance keeps Bharata Natyam's rhythmic cycles and percussive footwork and drops its narrative and its devotional address, setting the geometry against a Western string quartet - a claim, in 1980s Britain, that a classical Indian form was contemporary material and not heritage. — 1989-02-17"},"demonmachine":{"blurb":"Four women move without a care in an intact world and are then pulled into the orbit of a female Demon until they become a machine, working faster and faster and all but throwing sparks. Bodenwieser danced the Demon herself with a lightning bolt fixed to her head, to hammering piano music by Lisa Maria Mayer. It is the second part of a four-part dance drama, Gewalten des Lebens, and the only part that survives — rebuilt from a notation and film by two of the original dancers, it runs barely five minutes. — 1924-02-04"},"howlongbrethren":{"blurb":"A WPA-era protest work set to songs of resistance from African American tradition, from Tamiris's decade of dances against racism and war; it won Dance Magazine's first award for group choreography. Its method has been argued over since — Susan Manning's critique, that Tamiris used white dancers to convey Black experience, is now part of its history. — 1937-05-06"},"lesnoces":{"blurb":"A Russian peasant wedding as ritual: Stravinsky's ballet-cantata for four pianos, percussion, chorus and four vocal soloists, its words drawn from wedding songs in Kireevsky's 1911 collection. Nijinska stripped the dancers of individual identity — a reading now called protofeminist — the bride set on 'a predetermined path, as marriage was regarded as the way to maintain and grow the community.' — 1923-06-13"},"riteofspring":{"blurb":"A girl dances herself to death at the end, and the dancers moved with what Cyril Beaumont called 'slow, uncouth movements' 'in complete opposition to the traditions of classical ballet.' The uproar on the first night was loud enough that they could not take their cues from the orchestra. Nijinsky's choreography got seven or eight performances and by 1920 nobody could remember it; what is danced now is Hodson and Archer's 1987 reconstruction from prompt books, sketches and Marie Rambert's memory. — 1913-05-29"},"atthehawkswell":{"blurb":"Yeats's Noh-inflected play gave Ito the Guardian of the Well: a hawk-woman who stands beside a dried-up well on a bare mountainside, falls into a trance, rises and dances in hawk-like motions, then leaves — and while the hero chases her, the water he has waited for rises and is missed. Masks by Edmund Dulac, a patterned screen for scenery, drum, gong and zither for accompaniment. Ito had trained in Dalcroze eurhythmics, not in Noh. — 1916-04-02"},"mutations":{"blurb":"Tetley made the stage choreography and van Manen the films: dancers appear clad and unclad at once, on screen and on stage, to Stockhausen's electronics, until figures in transparent suits sweep the action off and leave a naked couple alone as the lights fade. Alexander Bland, reviewing the London run, counted four men and one woman dancing naked in full spotlight, one of them 'enormously magnified and slow-motioned' on film, and called the nudity 'a stimulating but serious ingredient.' — 1970-07-03"},"bodasdesangre":{"blurb":"Lorca's play danced without a word of it: a bride runs from her wedding with her former lover, the groom hunts them, and the two men fight to the death. Gades stripped the theatre out and left the ballet to carry the story on flamenco alone, which is the step that let flamenco hold an evening-length narrative. Carlos Saura filmed his rehearsal-room version of it in 1981. — 1974"},"chickensoup":{"blurb":"A solo built out of Cummings's memories of her grandmother in the kitchen. She scrubs a floor on her hands and knees - exemplary realism - then breaks off, rears up and shakes in jagged convulsive bursts, as though caught in a strobe. She dances with a frying pan. The score is by Meredith Monk, Collin Walcott and Brian Eno. It became part of a six-part suite, Food for Thought, and in 2006 the National Endowment for the Arts named it an American Masterpiece. — 1981"},"vespers":{"blurb":"Six women and a row of straight-backed wooden chairs, the dancers propelling themselves between the rows at a speed that never relents, to Mikel Rouse's percussive score. Dove built the piece out of memories of his grandmother's place of worship, and what came out is stark and ferocious rather than pious — the Village Voice wrote of the women's streamlined power and how coldly they can burn. — 1986"},"thefalsedavid":{"blurb":"1997"},"twosuns":{"blurb":"The show Mukai raised the flag on his new group with, and he built it out of his own background: Hiroshima and the atomic bomb. It went up in a theatre in Kichijoji at the end of the year. Nothing describing what was on the stage turned up in this pass — only the subject, the room, and the fact that a company started here. — 2014"},"theriteofspring":{"blurb":"A Rite of Spring danced in total silence — the company's own note calls it soundless from end to end, and a problem piece. Four men danced it in a converted warehouse in Nippori, Mukai among them, a year after he had founded the group with a piece about Hiroshima. It was the second thing they made. — 2015"},"theforgotten":{"blurb":"A woman keeps watch at the bedside of an unconscious man; years pass, and she goes to fetch him back, a rescue that becomes an initiatory journey as reality is slowly contaminated by the fantastic. Boitel's first full-scale piece, built out of aerial work, contortion and dance made on household objects — a big fan, a sofa, a bed, a table, clothes — and, by her own account, on a set of visionary women: the Countess of Castiglione, Loïe Fuller, Unica Zürn, Camille Claudel. The company took its name from it. — 2014-07-01"},"5eshurlants":{"blurb":"Five circus artists of different nationalities, each interrogated about their relationship to one apparatus — wire, dance-acrobatics, juggling, straps, hoop — fly, slip, fall, get up and begin again. The company glosses the title with a proverb, seven times down and eight times up, and calls the piece a homage to circus and to perseverance rather than to virtuosity. Its rig, the Spider, was designed by Tristan Baudoin, who has lit and built every Boitel piece since 2011. — 2015"},"whenangelsfall":{"blurb":"A figure in a long grey coat drops out of the flies onto a horizontal bar and swings in a backlight broken into strobe; six more join it, wrapped in the same dark cloaks, angels who no longer remember what made them fall. Boitel started from Plato's cave. Lamps on articulated mechanical arms stalk the performers and rule the space into geometry, while the group huddles round a horn gramophone grinding out Harry Dacre's 1892 music-hall song Daisy Bell. — 2018-10-11"},"shadowscast":{"blurb":"It opens with an aerialist on a flying rope in a darkness pierced by one triangle of light, appearing and vanishing, laughing strangely, recounting a recurring dream that always ends in a cellar. The piece is a family drama about a thing not said, and it is where words entered Boitel's work for the first time; the father is played by Alain Anglaret, who had never been on a stage before. Made just as the pandemic closed the theatres, it went almost unperformed for its first years. — 2021"},"bromance":{"blurb":"Three folded chairs against the back curtain, a Cyr wheel spotlit in a black box, and an hour on what men are permitted to do with one another's bodies: it starts from a handshake and works outward through hugging, jealousy and a hand-to-hand duet thick with chalk dust. One of the three folds into a human springboard and launches the other two off his bent thighs. The Guardian thought the homoerotic undercurrent, though undeniable, went almost entirely unacknowledged. — 2014"},"kin":{"blurb":"Five men on plinths in laurel crowns competing for the attention of one impassive woman, each turn ending with a Britain's Got Talent buzzer. One writhes to Purple Rain and gets buzzed; another finishes on the Cyr wheel and is thrown a banana like a performing monkey. Late on she stands cloaked on their shoulders, eighteen feet up and monolithic — and then the cloak falls away and she is small, teetering, dependent on the men below to keep her from falling. — 2016-04-20"},"pelleasetmelisande":{"blurb":"Cherkaoui and Damien Jalet staged Debussy's only opera as something seen from inside an eye. 'The libretto is so much about vision that we thought to make the story unfold inside an eyeball,' Jalet said. Marina Abramović built the set — huge kinetic sculptures like crystals, and a seven-metre screen before a concave mirror carrying Marco Brambilla's video made from Hubble photographs — and eight dancers moved beside the singers as their interior lives. — 2018"},"yama":{"blurb":"Yama is Japanese for mountain, and Jalet took the piece from the yamabushi, the ascetics still practising in the sacred mountains of Tohoku — the region wrecked by the 2011 tsunami — who hold that the mountain is a womb you emerge from and return to at death. Eight dancers work on and around a geometric form by Jim Hodges that reads as an ant's nest, a volcanic crater or a medicine wheel, pierced by a single passage hole. — 2014"},"itsgoingtogetworse":{"blurb":"Gruwez dances a speech. Maarten Van Cauwenberghe's soundtrack is cut from the preaching of the American televangelist Jimmy Swaggart, and she conducts it: she begins backlit on grey carpet in grey trousers, white shirt and patent leather shoes, her gestures minimal, and as the sentences build the movement grows until she is shuddering and bouncing on the spot. A Belgian critic called it a threatening pas de deux between male voice and female performer. — 2012"},"shinkainotamashii":{"blurb":"2019-12-22"},"sasayaki":{"blurb":"2022-01-07"},"sixteendances":{"blurb":"The nine permanent emotions of Indian aesthetics — anger, humour, sorrow, heroism, disgust, wonder, fear, the erotic, tranquillity — with interludes set between them, and the order fixed by chance rather than by feeling. It was the first piece in which Cunningham used chance operations, the method that would organise the next half-century of his work. The costumes shifted from dark to light as the fifty-three minutes ran, against Cage's score for piano and small orchestra. — 1951-01-17"},"suitebychance":{"blurb":"Cunningham spent months building charts of movements, timings and directions, then let chance decide the order, the number of bodies onstage, and every entrance and exit. Christian Wolff's For Magnetic Tape gave it one of the earliest electronic scores made for dancing. Audiences found the result disturbing in its refusal of feeling; Cunningham argued the opposite — that the structure left each dancer's own quality naked, powerful and unashamed. — 1953-03-24"},"summerspace":{"blurb":"Bursts of speed and suspension, dancers zigzagging every which way like flying creatures, in leotards Robert Rauschenberg painted to go with his own speckled backdrop. Morton Feldman's Ixion — delicate, with occasional distant thunder — was written independently of the steps. It is the standard demonstration of Cunningham's method: sound, décor and dance made separately and meeting for the first time in performance. — 1958-08-17"},"anticmeet":{"blurb":"Ten numbers that overlap like vaudeville turns: Cunningham as a clown-like figure lost and finding his way among the others, in Rauschenberg's fur coats and parachute dresses over black leotards, to Cage's Concert for Piano and Orchestra. The comedy at the heart of the Cunningham–Cage–Rauschenberg collaboration of the 1950s. — 1958-08-14"},"crises":{"blurb":"Four women and a man banded together — literally, elastic bands at wrists, arms, waists and legs — in Rauschenberg leotards of clashing reds, to Nancarrow's player-piano studies. Cunningham called it 'an adventure in togetherness'; Cage, a dramatic though not narrative dance about decisive moments between a man and four women. — 1960-08-19"},"winterbranch":{"blurb":"Built on what Cunningham called facts in dancing — falling, and getting up. It opens with him crawling across the stage with a flashlight; then dancers in sweatsuits and sneakers, eyes blacked with makeup, drop at varying speeds before rising together. Rauschenberg lit it to feel like night with car headlights swinging into faces, and La Monte Young's sound was made from ashtrays scraped on a mirror and wood rubbed on a Chinese gong. — 1964-03-21"},"walkaroundtime":{"blurb":"Jasper Johns rebuilt Duchamp's Large Glass as transparent boxes set around the stage, so the audience looked through the décor at the dance and at the spectators on the far side of it. Cunningham took the shape of the piece from computing: you feed the machine information, he said, then you wait while it digests. Across seven sections the waiting is the material, to David Behrman's ...for nearly an hour... — 1968-03-10"},"rainforest":{"blurb":"Andy Warhol's helium-filled silver pillows drifted loose among the dancers, who wore flesh-coloured tights that Jasper Johns had roughened with a razor blade. Each of the six performed a role, left the stage and never returned; only Cunningham stayed throughout. David Tudor's score chirped and chattered like animals, and the piece traced back to Cunningham's memories of the Pacific Northwest he grew up in. — 1968-03-09"},"sounddance":{"blurb":"Ten dancers burst in through a slit in a hanging drape and are finally swept back into it as though sucked into a wind tunnel. Made after a residency at the Paris Opera and pitched deliberately against the rigidity Cunningham found in ballet, it is fast and vigorous, the footwork and torso articulation so densely packed that the company seems to be moving under a microscope. David Tudor's Toneburst supplied the noise. — 1975-03-08"},"torse":{"blurb":"The numbers one to sixty-four, taken from the I Ching, set both the floor plan — conceived as an eight-by-eight square — and the movement phrases. The dancing works through five positions of the spine: upright, arch, twist, tilt, curve, which is what the title names. Mark Lancaster dressed the women in black leotards over yellow tights and the men in the reverse, across fifty-five minutes for ten dancers. — 1976-01-15"},"roaratorio":{"blurb":"Cunningham set dancing inside Cage's Wake machine: field recordings made at the Irish places Joyce names, mesostic poems spun out of the letters of JAMESJOYCE, and traditional Irish players going at once. He said he took the feeling of dance already in the novel — jig and reel motifs, a hopping dance, promenades and strolls, which keep opening out into large communal circles for sixteen dancers. — 1983-10-26"},"pointsinspace":{"blurb":"The title comes from Einstein's remark that there are no fixed points in space. It was made for the camera first, shot in BBC studios in two parts — the dance itself and a documentary about making it — and Cage supplied Voiceless Essay, built from consonants pulled out of his own writings and analysed by computer. Cunningham danced in the video, then cut his own role when the seven sections moved to the stage the following year. — 1986"},"beachbirds":{"blurb":"Eleven dancers in white with black gloves phrase the movement individually — 'they can dance like a flock of birds, when they suddenly take off' — to Cage's FOUR3, while chance-determined light crosses a scrim like a shore passing from dawn to dusk. The sections breathe to different lengths each night; a film version, Beach Birds for Camera, followed. — 1991"},"ocean":{"blurb":"Cage's plan was a circle: the dancers in the middle, the audience ringed around them, the musicians ringed around the audience. He conceived it in 1991 and it stayed unbuilt until a commission three years later paid for the ring, with Andrew Culver's score over David Tudor's electronics. Nineteen sections came from the I Ching's sixty-four hexagrams doubled to a hundred and twenty-eight phrases; the last performances were danced at the bottom of a Minnesota quarry in 2008. — 1994-05-18"},"biped":{"blurb":"Motion-captured phrases return as animations projected on a front scrim while the dancers move behind it, choreography assembled with DanceForms software, to Gavin Bryars, over a floor of lit squares. Cunningham compared watching it to 'switching channels on the TV' — his late landmark at the meeting of live dance and digital technology. — 1999-04-23"},"splitsides":{"blurb":"Everything exists in two versions — two dances, two scores, two sets, two sets of costumes and lights — and chance decides at each performance which halves are paired, so the order is never twice the same. Radiohead and Sigur Rós wrote the music, both working without vocals or beats and layering processed electronics instead. It is Cunningham's method reduced to its bare proposition: make the parts separately, let a procedure assemble them. — 2003-10-14"},"theatrepieceno1cunningham":{"blurb":"1952"},"variationsv":{"blurb":"Antennas stood around the stage: come within range of one and a dancer triggered electronic sound, with photoelectric cells at their bases doing the same job — and the equipment often failed. Nam June Paik distorted the dancers on video while Stan VanDerBeek's film ran, and Cunningham handled objects wired with contact microphones so that ordinary activity made noise too. For once the movement drove the score instead of running beside it. — 1965"},"hexentanz1914":{"blurb":"The witch dance of Wigman's Munich debut, shown beside Lento and Ein Elfentanz, that grew into one of her most important works. The first version itself is gone; what endures is what it began — the expressionist solo she would remake twelve years later with mask and percussion, one of the earliest landmarks of German expressionist dance. — 1914-02-11"},"hexentanz1926":{"blurb":"The remake, masked and driven by percussion: a figure working from crouched and seated positions with clawing hands, an unsettling, supernatural creature photographed by Hugo Erfurth. Visual documentation survives, and that record is much of why the witch remains among her most frequently performed works — the expressionist solo that can still be reconstructed. — 1926"},"lesacreduprintemps":{"blurb":"Stravinsky danced on a stage covered entirely with earth, the dancers soiled and visibly exhausted as the ritual closes in, the Chosen One in a red dress dancing herself to death. It fixed Bausch's reputation for turning classical music into embodied, human movement, and half a century on it is still crossing the world's opera houses. — 1975-12-03"},"nelken":{"blurb":"Thousands of carnations carpet the floor and everything happens on top of them: two dogs barking at the edge, a guard demanding passports and humiliating whoever he stops, men dressed as little girls skipping through the flowers, four stunt specialists hurling themselves about. A performer signs Gershwin's 'The Man I Love' — a part that has gone to a man at every performance since the premiere. At the end the dancers tell the audience, one at a time, why they wanted to dance. — 1982-12-30"},"viktor":{"blurb":"Twenty-foot walls of mud on three sides, so the twenty-nine dancers look excavated — a mass grave, or an archaeological dig — while a man at the top shovels earth back down on them for the whole three and a half hours. It opens with two corpses being married, their bodies shoehorned into a limp embrace, and the same pair return as pimp and prostitute. Made in response to Rome, it was the first of the world-city pieces and, one London critic thought, the harshest of them. — 1986-05-14"},"palermopalermo":{"blurb":"Bausch built it the way she came to build her late work: the company travelled to Palermo, watched the city, met people there, and brought the material home to be assembled in the studio. Palermo's own Teatro Biondo was a co-producer, which put the city on both sides of the piece — as its subject and as its patron. — 1989-12-17"},"setandreset":{"blurb":"Brown's working rule for the piece was visibility and invisibility, and Rauschenberg dressed it to match — translucent costumes, a translucent set, bodies that keep half-disappearing into their own surround. The dancing turns seductively fluid one moment and unpredictably geometric the next, carried for twenty-three minutes by Laurie Anderson's Long Time No See. It is generally taken as the summary work of Brown's company at its height. — 1983-10-20"},"accumulation":{"blurb":"Dance built by arithmetic: a gesture stated, repeated, the next added, the sequence run again from the top. Brown's accumulations went anywhere a body could — public spaces of all kinds, even water, dancers floating on rafts as they worked methodically through the graduated gestures — and later versions added talking. — 1971"},"glacialdecoy":{"blurb":"Brown's first collaboration with Robert Rauschenberg and the work that carried her from alternative spaces toward the theater — the beginning of her larger stage productions, with visual elements bearing Rauschenberg's aesthetic, and the start of an artistic partnership with him that continued across her career. — 1979-06-20"},"watermotor":{"blurb":"Two and a half minutes, danced alone in a t-shirt and grey trousers to no sound but the room. Babette Mangolte filmed it twice inside one film — one take at speed, one in slow motion — and that film is how the solo has mostly been seen and studied since; Yvonne Rainer called it one of the best dance films ever made. Even the spelling comes in two versions: Water Motor on the film, Watermotor in the company's repertory. — 1978"},"waterstudy":{"blurb":"Danced in silence — the notation catalogue lists the music as '[Silence]'. The experiment, in the notator's words, was 'to discover the natural rhythms of the body independent of musical accompaniment', and the rhythms Humphrey chose 'created the effect of the ebb and flow of the sea'. Ten dancers, eight minutes, timed to breath rather than to a count. — 1928"},"theshakers":{"blurb":"The Shakers kept the sexes apart at worship and shook to be shaken clean of sin; Humphrey's dance is, in the notation score's phrase, 'a stark depiction of their struggle to attain purity', with the shaking readable as sexual repression as much as ecstasy. Thirteen dancers to a traditional Shaker tune. One of the last surviving Shakers saw it and, by the same account, found himself 'enthralled'. — 1930-11-12"},"newdance":{"blurb":"Absolute dance with no story: the individual set against the group and brought, by the end, into an ideal state of harmony, to a Wallingford Riegger score. John Martin called it 'comparable to a symphony'. The Borzoi Book of Modern Dance called it 'an affirmation of faith in a possible democratic society, in which the individual could develop his own capabilities in harmonious relationship with, and to the benefit of his fellow'. — 1935-08-03"},"missabrevis":{"blurb":"Limón had just been sent through Poznań, Wrocław, Katowice and Warsaw on a State Department tour: 'what I saw and felt as an artist and as a human being is probably the most complex and devastating experience in my life,' he wrote home. He answered with Kodály's Missa in tempore belli, written at the end of the war, danced before a backdrop recalling the bombed-out Budapest church where that score was first sung. Twenty-two dancers; the original cast included Steve Paxton and the future critic Deborah Jowitt. — 1958-04-11"},"thereisatime":{"blurb":"Verses from Ecclesiastes taken one at a time — a time to be born and to die, to kill and to heal, to plant, to mourn, to laugh and to dance. After each section the company re-forms its circle, which the Dictionary of Modern Ballet reads as the whole of the life of the community of which each variation is only a part. Forty minutes, and among the most poetic things Limón made. — 1956"},"thetraitor":{"blurb":"Judas, played as though the betrayal were happening now. 'The Traitor was Limón's response to the McCarthy hearings and the climate of betrayal that haunted the world of arts and entertainment during that time,' the Limón Journal records — set against a Gunther Schuller score of violence, passion and tenderness, and running eighteen minutes. — 1954-08-19"},"shango":{"blurb":"Built from a possession Dunham had watched in Trinidad, where a man ran to the altar with a queer hook step, hands locked behind him, and knelt in the blood of the sacrificed fowl. Onstage a priest offers a chicken, the congregation sways to the drums, and one man convulses, flicks his tongue and undulates along the floor as the loa Damballa takes him — a deity outside the Trinidadian Shango pantheon, since Dunham said she hesitated to do the strictly authentic in ritual, feeling a little superstitious. — 1945"},"choros":{"blurb":"Nine minutes of the early nineteenth-century Brazilian quadrille put on a concert stage: Vadico Gogliano's score keeps the French quadrille's form and fills it with Brazilian folk material, and John Pratt dressed it. Dunham made it for Tropical Revue. The Alvin Ailey company took it into repertory in 1972 and still dances it, and the Dance Notation Bureau holds a score, which is why a wartime revue number is still recoverable. — 1943"},"veracruzana":{"blurb":"A slight plot — a husband leaves for work and his wife takes up with a local cad — carrying an argument. Dunham learned the folk material from elderly men in Mexico City and set the bamba among ballet and modern dance steps, dressing it in elaborate costumes and sets so that no one could take it for less than a top theatrical production. By claiming the right to dance and sing the bamba, her largely black company reminded audiences of an African presence in Mexico that was generally ignored. — 1949"},"tropicalrevue":{"blurb":"Thirteen dances, eight of them already seen in New York, staged now by the impresario Sol Hurok with a full orchestra, singers and a Dixieland band added for entertainment value — and sold on Al Hirschfeld's caricature of Dunham's breasts and hips beside disembodied black hands on a drum, punctuated by the words primitive rites. The day before the opening Dunham told the Amsterdam News she had never gone into a direct social message, because a company working to the same standard as any other could reach people propaganda could not. — 1943-09-19"},"thenegrospeaksofrivers":{"blurb":"Langston Hughes's poem danced to a score by Sarah Malament, and one of the pieces Primus carried through her ten-month engagement at Cafe Society Downtown. Margaret Lloyd described undulating rhythms over deep-flowing currents that wind into whirlpool spins: Primus pivoting on one knee or circling the stationary bent leg with the free one, the body leaning in a long slant away from the travelling foot, the pale soles flashing. Lloyd called it one of Pearl's best. — 1943-02-14"},"hardtimeblues":{"blurb":"A protest against sharecropping set to Josh White's song and driven by the leaps that amazed audiences — 'the shape the body takes in the air,' Primus held, could carry 'the defiance, desperation, and anger of the sharecroppers' she had met on her field research through the Deep South. — 1943-02-14"},"fanga":{"blurb":"A Liberian dance of welcome, carried home from Primus's eighteen-month research journey through West Africa: the ritual dramatized and shortened for the stage, its foundational movement vocabulary kept intact. It entered her repertory as one of its icons, and in 1974 she staged it for Alvin Ailey's company. — 1949"},"salome":{"blurb":"Horton took the story from Wilde's one-act play at twenty-eight, and then went back to it again and again, in version after version and under more than one title, until the year he died — 'perhaps the most interesting, and certainly the most obsessive, involvement with the story' any choreographer has managed, in Richard Bizot's account of those nineteen years. It outlived him: companies were still staging it after 1953. — 1934"},"tojoseclementeorozco":{"blurb":"A duet about the Mexican muralist, made in the year Horton died, with Carmen de Lavallade in the original cast. Alvin Ailey remounted it and planned to take it to Southeast Asia in 1962, but the State Department refused the work because, as Ailey put it, Orozco 'was a little left of center'. Reviewing a later revival the New York Times called it 'an unabashed piece of social protest'. — 1953-07-22"},"mymother":{"blurb":"Ohno made it for his dead mother and Hijikata directed it — one of two works Hijikata directed for him after Admiring La Argentina, both danced with his son Yoshito. The dancer best known for appearing in women's dress appears here in a man's black suit, and that is the image other choreographers reach for when they quote him. Beyond the fact of the commemoration, the sources read here describe the piece only at second hand. — 1981"},"waterlilies":{"blurb":"Ohno built it on Monet's water lily canvases — and Monet, Fraleigh points out, had been working from Japanese woodblock prints, so the borrowing runs in a circle. What stops it being homage is the collision inside it: the father enters gliding, quiet and flowery, while the son, in a Western suit, dances a seething, blocky solo to Pink Floyd, clawing with his hands and crossing on a diagonal that throws out one leg in a blunt kick. — 1987"},"deadsea":{"blurb":"Ohno saw the Dead Sea for the first time on tour in Israel in 1983 and built the piece from one image he took away: mountainsides thick with animals, which he read as a mother's womb eaten from inside. Hijikata directed. Ohno enters in a pale yellow dress and girl's dress pumps, trips and poses on his toes, then crumples and rises in another dress and a heavy cape as an old woman, arms outstretched to fill it. Yoshito Ohno, off the stage since 1969, came back for it. — 1985"},"bigcity":{"blurb":"Jooss's dance of the modern city, to Alexandre Tansman's music, made in the same Essen year that produced The Green Table. In exile it traveled with the Ballets Jooss repertory on the international tours of 1934–40 — the city ballet kept alive on the road while the company had no country. — 1932-11-21"},"aballinoldvienna":{"blurb":"The light-hearted counterpart to The Green Table from the same year — a comedy of the ballroom to Joseph Lanner's Court Ball Dances, arranged by F. A. Cohen. A 1935 film survives of the Ballets Jooss dancing it on tour, with Jooss himself operating the camera. — 1932-11-21"},"pavaneonthedeathofaninfanta":{"blurb":"Five minutes at the Spanish court, where a young Infanta keeps trying to hold to the prescribed steps of the pavane and keeps breaking out into a dance of her own. The ceremonial has no room for it. She fails a last time and sinks to the floor, and the ladies and gentlemen of the court walk coldly over her. Jooss set it to Ravel's piano piece and had Sigurd Leeder design the costumes. — 1929"},"dance":{"blurb":"Dancers cross the stage in pairs, repeating the same balletic, geometric phrases to Philip Glass for nearly twenty minutes at a stretch, while Sol LeWitt's film of the choreography plays on a front scrim — the live cast visible behind it, in sync with its own filmed image, performers turned into the scenery itself. — 1979-10-18"},"availablelight":{"blurb":"Made for the raw shed of MOCA's Temporary Contemporary: Gehry built a split-level set out of industrial platforms and chain-link fencing and banked the audience above the dancers, so the piece is watched from overhead. Childs took a handful of ballet steps and repeated them into pattern; John Adams wrote an electronic score called Light over Water. Fifty-five minutes, and the only time the three of them worked together. — 1983-09-29"},"einsteinonthebeach1976":{"blurb":"Childs was in the opera as a performer from the start, and choreographed her own material inside it - the big ensemble dances were Andy de Groat's. Her solo, a figure crossing and recrossing a diagonal, is the thing people remember; it won her an Obie. Eight years later she would be asked back to re-choreograph the whole of it. — 1976-07-25"},"einsteinonthebeach1984":{"blurb":"For the first revival Wilson had Childs re-envision the movement, replacing Andy de Groat's original choreography with her own. Hers is the version that has been used ever since, in 1992 and again in 2012, so the dancing most people have seen in Einstein is eight years younger than the opera. The Washington Post singled out her vibratory solo on a diagonal as worth the price of admission on its own. — 1984-12-11"},"lamentation":{"blurb":"A dancer alone on a bench, sealed in a tube of purple jersey, straining against the fabric until the figure is 'neither human nor animal, neither male nor female: it is grief itself.' Graham told of a bereaved mother who said watching it let her yield to grief without shame. Set to Kodály, it is still danced — a landmark of modern dance. — 1930-01-08"},"chronicle":{"blurb":"War evoked without battle scenes — 'the fateful prelude to war' and 'the devastation of spirit which it leaves in its wake' — to Wallingford Riegger's five-section score, made the year Graham refused the Berlin Olympics: 'I would find it impossible to dance in Germany at the present time.' Three sections survive in performance, 'Steps in the Street' among them. — 1936-12-20"},"clytemnestra":{"blurb":"The murdered queen argues her case with Hades and relives it all — Iphigenia's sacrifice, Agamemnon's murder, her children's revenge — to Halim El-Dabh's score, on Noguchi sets that turn underworld into palace into bedchamber. The summit of Graham's Greek cycle, a work of 'giant stature' in John Martin's verdict; its original three acts are now danced as one. — 1958-04-01"},"aureole":{"blurb":"Dancers in white to Handel: the first time Taylor set his 'loping antelope style' to baroque music, and the lyric surprise from an avant-gardist became, in Clive Barnes's words, a work whose 'grace and individuality instantly spun into orbit throughout the world of dance.' His first major success, kept in the Taylor Collection of essential works. — 1962-08-04"},"esplanade":{"blurb":"No dance steps at all — standing, walking, running, sliding, falling — set to two Bach violin concertos, sparked by the sight of a girl running to catch a bus. Found movement raised to the canon: 'I'd seen a classic of American dance,' Arlene Croce wrote. 'It confers a mythic dimension on ordinary aspects of our daily life.' — 1975-03-01"},"3epitaphs":{"blurb":"A parade of faceless, gray-leotarded figures to early New Orleans jazz — funeral music — in costumes by Robert Rauschenberg, and only the fourth dance Taylor made. A critic's verdict has stuck to it ever since: 'one of the funniest dances anywhere. An essay on posture and gesture — and genius.' — 1956"},"thefugue":{"blurb":"A twenty-count theme put through twenty variations — reversed, inverted, retrograded, re-sequenced, re-timed — on the model of Bach's Musical Offering, with three dancers taking the bass, alto and soprano lines. There is no music: the score is the sound of their feet on an amplified floor. Tharp counted it as the first piece she made to keep rather than to learn from, and it became the earliest of her works licensed out to other companies. — 1970-08-16"},"eightjellyrolls":{"blurb":"Tharp's first work intended for a proscenium stage, set to Jelly Roll Morton and his Red Hot Peppers, in eight sections for six women — Tharp, Sara Rudner, Rose Marie Wright, Naomi Cohn, Mary Curry, Isabel Garcia-Lorca. Her own solo in it was nicknamed 'The Drunk', and the loose-limbed athleticism it made visible became the thing her name now means. Kermit Love made the costumes, Jennifer Tipton lit it. — 1971"},"deucecoupe":{"blurb":"Tharp's own dancers and the Joffrey's on one stage, seventeen in all, to the Beach Boys. Each section is labelled with a run of ballet vocabulary in alphabetical order — 'Ailes de pigeon through Attitude', 'Balancé through Ballon', on to 'Six through Suite' — and one dancer, Erika Goodman, appears in every one of them. United Graffiti Artists are credited as the scenic designers: they spray-painted the backdrop live while the dance was going on. — 1973"},"intheupperroom":{"blurb":"Tharp's athletic signature at full stretch, ballet technique run together with contemporary movement to Philip Glass, in Norma Kamali's costumes under Jennifer Tipton's original lighting. Two years after its festival debut it entered American Ballet Theatre's repertory, and it took the Laurence Olivier Award in 1991. — 1986"},"paradesandchanges":{"blurb":"Its most legendary seven minutes had the whole cast undress, dress and undress again, unhurried, holding the eye of someone in the audience — a task rather than a provocation, and one Halprin traced to a striptease she had once performed in anger at a Gestalt workshop. Dancers travelled imaginary parallel lanes, picking up or discarding whatever came within reach. In New York a warrant was issued for her arrest, and Clive Barnes, who came to sneer, found the paper-tearing section beautiful and somehow liberating. — 1965"},"apollo":{"blurb":"The young god led toward adulthood by three muses, to Stravinsky's Apollon Musagète — the collaboration Balanchine counted as his turning point: 'I could dare not to use everything, that I, too, could eliminate.' For decades he kept eliminating, shedding sets and ornate costumes down to practice clothes, and it survives as the oldest Balanchine ballet still danced. — 1928-06-12"},"serenade":{"blurb":"Balanchine made it on students as a lesson in how to be on a stage, and kept the accidents: a girl who fell, another who arrived late, both written into the choreography at the point where they happened. He reversed the order of Tchaikovsky's last two movements so that the ballet ends on the Elegy rather than the Russian dance — a farewell rather than a finish. It was the first original ballet he made in America. — 1934-12-06"},"agon":{"blurb":"Twelve dancers in practice clothes, Stravinsky's twelve-tone score, and steps modeled on a mid-seventeenth-century French dance manual — the apex of the Balanchine–Stravinsky collaborations. At its center is the pas de deux made on Arthur Mitchell and Diana Adams, an interracial partnership that read as a statement in its era. — 1957-12-01"},"darkelegies":{"blurb":"Mahler's Kindertotenlieder, sung live onstage, while a village community mourns its dead children after a disaster — the narrative left implicit, the sorrow carried in small gestures and spatial relations rather than display. In Nadia Benois's designs, it endures as one of Tudor's masterpieces of psychological restraint. — 1937-02-19"},"ferai":{"blurb":"Odin's literary adviser Christian Ludvigsen — the Danish translator of Ionesco and Beckett — proposed Euripides' Alcestis, the wife who dies in her husband's place, and Peter Seeberg wrote a text for the company. The title carries the whole idea in a pun: Pherai is Admetus's Thessalian capital, and Ferai the Latin name of the Faroes, kingdom of Frode Fredegod, so two mythologies lie on top of each other. It played the small towns of Jutland before it made the company's name in Paris. — 1969"},"uprising":{"blurb":"Seven men in cargo pants and shirts, wreathed in smoke and darkly lit, cross the stage in long knuckle-dragging lopes and shuffles on shoulders and elbows — meeting, closing, breaking apart. Shechter wrote the pounding drum score himself, and reviewers reached for the haka, a rugby scrum and a breakdance jam to describe what they were watching. The middle quietens to the sound of rain, the men leaning head to head or kneeling alone, before the finale racks the thunder up again. — 2006"},"inyourrooms":{"blurb":"Built in three stages for three London houses in turn, each version enlarged to fit the next room, and assembled from lightning-fast tableaux: squares of light flare open on a hunched, flinching duo or a cluster of combatants kicking and punching the air, then shut. Shechter composed the score as well and could be seen belting out the percussion track in a dimly lit box above the stage. The Observer's critic called it probably the most important new dance work created in Britain since the millennium. — 2007"},"grandfinale":{"blurb":"'A world at odds with itself, full of anarchic energy and violent comedy,' danced to a live band onstage in Tom Scutt's designs — end-times revelry one critic called 'the ultimate danse macabre.' Comic, bleak and beautiful at once, it was nominated for the Olivier and named Production of the Year by tanz magazine. — 2017-06-14"},"aattenentionon":{"blurb":"Three dancers, one to each level of an open-sided tower, each with a few square metres of floor and no sight of the others: no touching, no exchanged looks, no unison, no lifts, by Charmatz’s own listing of what the piece refuses. PJ Harvey plays at the start; then they strip to nothing but skimpy white T-shirts and dance in silence — loose kicks, arbitrary freezes, thumping falls. The title is the French ‘Attention!’ pulled apart, a syllable at a time. — 1996"},"enfant":{"blurb":"Twenty-six children aged six to twelve, nine adult dancers, and machines — a crane, a conveyor belt, a rising platform — that seize inert bodies and set them moving, as the choreographing machines had done in Charmatz’s earlier régi. The festival’s note traces the idea to his seeing a crane build the stage and tiers there at night. The dancers then catch the children’s bodies, raise them into the air and make them fly, hug them and make them glide, hard enough that the children answer back. — 2011"},"libertecathedrale":{"blurb":"Charmatz's first creation for the Wuppertal ensemble, made with dancers from his own company and set inside a brutalist concrete pilgrimage church. They rush the altar space as though their lives depended on it, throw themselves to the floor and sing — Beethoven's last piano sonata intoned between breaths, lying, rolling, dancing — the voices ringing clear under a dome thirty-four metres up, against the pipe organ at its fullest tone. — 2023-09-08"},"districtstoryville":{"blurb":"Louis Armstrong's boyhood, loosely told: a hyperkinetic aspiring young trumpet player called Little Lou hanging around the bordellos of the New Orleans red-light district, with the Countess - the stunningly elegant but no-nonsense madam - and an array of sporting-house girls and their patrons. Walter Terry, reviewing it: \"Crowning this wildly glorious event was the dancing.\" — 1962"},"tensileinvolvement":{"blurb":"Dancers hold the ends of long elastic tapes and run, stretching and reweaving a spider's web of white lines across the stage; the bodies become the knots in a moving lattice rather than the subject of it. One account calls the result a metaphorical projection of the body of the universe. It is the piece that opens Robert Altman's film The Company. — 1955-01-26"},"sanctum":{"blurb":"1964"},"noumenon":{"blurb":"Two dancers — later three — seated on a base and completely enclosed in sacks of elasticised cloth, so that every movement they make deforms the fabric into a new sculpture. The elasticity was imagined, one account has it, from the picture of a large drop of mercury. It was Nikolais's first move into pure abstraction, and some critics refused to call the result dance at all. — 1953"},"romeandjewels":{"blurb":"Harris had watched West Side Story wondering why there were no street dancers in it. The piece arrived when one of his dancers, Rodney Mason, walked into rehearsal and shouted \"Yo Rome, thou art a villain, so what's up?!\" - that night Harris started writing. The title is a joke at hip hop's expense: Rome as roam, Jewels as jewellery, roaming for jewellery. It became the longest-touring hip-hop dance theatre work in American history and took three Bessies. — 2000"},"jamonthegroove":{"blurb":"The b-boys of Rock Steady Crew and the Rhythm Technicians, working as GhettOriginal, put breaking on an off-Broadway stage for a commercial run. It tells no story: it is a set of vignettes built to frame the dancing, the first of which traces a line from African dance to hip hop. The New York Daily News called it the work of \"a dance company made up of aging hip hoppers\" - the dancers were in their late twenties and already preserving their own history. — 1995"},"camelamosnaquerar":{"blurb":"The title is Calo for \"we want to speak.\" Made by the Romani poet Jose Heredia Maya with the dancer Mario Maya, it sets recitations of the actual anti-Roma laws - from the Catholic monarchs' Pragmatic decrees of the 1490s down to Francoist statute - against flamenco scenes that answer them, with the barest staging. The written law appears as impersonal text while the cantaor and the dancers speak for themselves. It reached the stage months after Franco's death, and became a founding document of Roma emancipation in Spain. — 1976"},"oroviejo":{"blurb":"Old Gold: a young dancer's argument with the tradition she came out of, with the passing of time as its thread. Critics at the Seville Bienal reached for the comparison with Israel Galvan - what he had done for men's flamenco, she was doing for women's - and the following year it took the critics' prize of the Catedra de Flamencologia in Jerez. — 2008"},"eva":{"blurb":"The show Yerbabuena built her company around, made for the Seville Bienal in the year she founded it: seven dancers and eight musicians, with the guitarist Paco Jarana - her husband - as musical director. It is the piece that moved her from a dancer in other people's companies to a choreographer with her own. — 1998"},"unchartedseas":{"blurb":"A group kathak work about searching for something intangible - Mangaldas takes her frame from Krishnamurti (\"We look for fixed points, but there are none\") and hangs sections off lines from Meerabai, Kabir, Rumi and Josh Malihabadi: is the search outward, is it inward, is it a storm, is it revealed only when all searching stops. Shubha Mudgal and Aneesh Pradhan wrote the vocal music. Her own solo in it is built on speed and spin. — 2006"},"twentylooksorparis19":{"blurb":"One question, staged over and over for eight years: what would have happened in 1963 if someone from the voguing ball scene in Harlem had come downtown to perform alongside the early postmoderns at Judson Church? Harrell answered it not with a single piece but with a set of dances in garment sizes, extra small through extra large, of which the small one is a solo he performs himself. He has framed the underlying grievance plainly — Judson was admitted to the record as history and voguing was not. — 2009"},"kordian":{"blurb":"Grotowski moved the whole of Słowacki's Romantic drama into one of its own scenes, the madhouse: every event of Kordian's life replayed as the nightmare of a patient under a rational and diabolical Doctor. Jerzy Gurawski filled the theatre with beds, most of them bunks, and sat the audience on and among them, so that the Doctor addressed the spectators as patients too. The great monologue on Mont Blanc was delivered in a fit of fury while the hero was being bled — the romantic sacrifice reduced to a routine procedure. — 1962-02-14"},"doctorfaustus":{"blurb":"Grotowski cut Marlowe down to a single occasion: the farewell banquet Faustus gives on his last night, with the audience seated along the T-shaped tables that filled the auditorium as his invited students, and his whole life told to them as a confession. Mephistopheles was doubled and played at once by a man and a woman in black habits, the text's good and evil angels collapsed into one. The pact was sealed as a baptism, Faustus lowering himself into the gap between the tables to the sound of gurgling. — 1963-04-23"},"studiumohamlecie":{"blurb":"Hamlet set down in a Polish village sunk in torpor: peasants rouse themselves, build up a soundscape of prayer songs broken by the cawing of crows, and make an inept attempt at staging the play, while an elegantly dressed Jewish intellectual standing apart from them takes the title role seriously. Grotowski worked from Wyspiański's own Hamlet study as much as from Shakespeare. It was the first of his productions written on stage — the script he had prepared was torn up in collective improvisation. — 1964-03-17"},"ewangelie":{"blurb":"1967"},"timepiece":{"blurb":"A chance dance built from a chart with columns for quality, timing, duration, movement, space, space-time and facing — one facing option marked simply with a question mark. Its five movement choices included red bag, untying, and heron leg to floor. It opened with forty seconds of stillness, which appalled Emerson until she realised she was allowed to do that. The title puns on the stopwatch that Cage and Robert Dunn both carried. — 1962-07-06"},"shoulder":{"blurb":"Made by cutting up Labanotation scores and recombining the pieces. Emerson's score lists space-covering steps — walk, run, triplet, crawl, skip, hop — then floor patterns drawn as a yin-yang, a circle, rectangles, a triangle, a wavy line; then body parts, movement qualities, levels, and instructions as small as which way to focus and when to smile. The dance was whatever the recombination produced. — 1962-07-06"},"suitesummers":{"blurb":"Three sections named for musical forms — Galliard, Saraband and Twist — which reads either as a tribute to Louis Horst's pre-classic form assignments or as a joke at their expense. Summers asked the composer John Herbert McDowell to help her structure the music correctly, and the two went to the Peppermint Lounge to research the last section. McDowell built his Gigue by shuffling cards to order the eighteen Bach gigues in his house and making a row out of their first notes. — 1962"},"indexjudithdunn":{"blurb":"A duet for Dunn and Paxton assembled by chance procedures — the first piece Dunn choreographed and showed publicly after joining Cunningham's company, and the work that led her to leave it. Its phrases included Dunn handing Paxton five coins while he held his hands behind his back. Johnston described a wild precise allegro of feet and legs stopping dead as Paxton throttles his partner with a hand at the neck. — 1963-01-30"},"randombreakfast":{"blurb":"A six-part collage for Gordon and Setterfield with at least a dozen costume changes, opening with a strip tease in a borrowed blue velvet gown and closing with Gordon explaining to the audience what he might do if he were making an interesting dance, then illustrating each option. About eighty per cent was improvised around fixed subject matter. He meant it as a sardonic comment on what he called the Judson Church Dance Factory Gold Rush. — 1963-05-09"},"speedlimit":{"blurb":"Morris wheeled Dunn on in a cart; both in white coveralls, they worked on a gym mat through balancing, bracing and pulling that ran on into rolls and falls, bumping, rebounding and quiet grappling, including a double somersault taken with both bodies curved together. Dunn called it a gymnastic struggle. Johnston wrote that the dance held one horizontal level of tension — the speed limit is slow, and there seemed no reason to hurry. — 1963-05-09"},"arizona":{"blurb":"Four sections of near-nothing. Morris turned his torso from front to left so slowly that five minutes passed, while his taped voice described the animatedly laborious business of sorting cows; then he aimed and threw a javelin, then swung a small light on a cord as the room's lights faded until only the moving point was visible, then adjusted a T-shaped form. He had noticed at a roller rink that a large space swallowed movement, and that only the smallest movements kept their mass in it. — 1963-06-23"},"acapulco":{"blurb":"Unrelated activities set side by side, with a structure Johnston traced to the child's hand game of scissors, rocks and paper: a woman irons the dress she is wearing while leaning on a diagonal tilt, two women play cards on the floor as if in anybody's living room, a girl in a nightgown rises from her chair in slow motion until she falls off balance into a waiting man's arms. It was the only dance Dunn made that did not depend on conventional dance technique. — 1963-07-30"},"countryhouses":{"blurb":"A dance about entrances and exits. Performers came in from the audience, up through a trap door and down a ladder from the balcony, and a good deal of it happened in the air, with one woman held up by two men who kept changing positions so that she never touched the ground. Robert Dunn assembled the spoken text from Oscar Wilde's plays, avoiding the famous paradoxes, and drilled the dancers to deliver the lines in an exact flat tone. — 1963-08-01"},"twentyonethree":{"blurb":"Morris stood at a lectern and mouthed a prerecorded reading of the introduction to Erwin Panofsky's Studies in Iconology, matching his lips to a voice that was not his. Banes places it beside Site and Box with the Sound of Its Own Making as work in which a finished cultural product is set against the visible signs and traces of the labour that carries it. — 1964-02-10"},"naturalhistory":{"blurb":"A trio for Judith Dunn, Lucinda Childs and Tony Holder to Schumann, played live at the piano by Robert Dunn - Romantic piano music at a moment when Judson dances mostly went in silence, to tape, or to noise. It was one of the premieres on the group's Philadelphia programme and was danced again in New York. Banes records the cast and the score and nothing at all of how it looked. — 1964-04-24"},"silverpieces":{"blurb":"Gordon and Setterfield sat on chairs watching a television set, getting up now and then to perform ten sequences of action in indeterminate order. The set was switched between stations at whim and never adjusted, so screen and stage met at random: at one performance a commercial for a headache cure coincided with a sequence of head-clutching and belly-aching. Gordon had glued together bits of unfinished work to make it, and the same dance appeared elsewhere under the title Fragments. — 1964-04-24"},"duetharmonica":{"blurb":"Two dancers held a single harmonica between them, and as one blew out the other breathed the note in, so that their heads stayed stuck together while the rest of their bodies moved with as much energy as they could manage. The accompaniment was their own harmonica-ised breathing. Ross reads it as anticipating by several years the task-oriented work of the Judson choreographers; decades later Halprin said that if she retitled it she would call it Kiss. — 1958"},"apartment6":{"blurb":"Three performers spent two hours trying to be their offstage selves onstage, improvising in a set built to look like a real apartment while a sculptor built a sculpture beside them. The instruction was to explore rather than suppress what they actually felt: in one rehearsal a tense request to pass the salt sent Leath rummaging like an animal, flinging things and knocking Graham out of his chair, then back to his newspaper saying evenly that he didn't know where it was. Two of the three left her company soon afterwards. — 1965-03-19"},"atomicsuen":{"blurb":"A solo that took Ashikawa's instruction to search for the smallest unit of the body toward particle physics. The research continued as Atomic Event, staged inside the cyclotron room of the Svedberg Laboratory in Uppsala with a researcher joining the dancers, video artist and musician. It belongs to a working method in which choreography comes out of an encounter with an actual place and its materials rather than from a theme. — 1999"},"headless":{"blurb":"Bones and skin are the costume; pain and gravity are the choreography. SU-EN describes it as an intensely personal experience and story, made strong enough to become artistic material that reaches beyond the individual. The programme notes set it in the bardo, the Tibetan state between death and rebirth: a head looking for a body, a body looking for a head, and a place where biting into the flesh is an act of love. — 2001"},"kaiinnouma":{"blurb":"Maro's first major work after Amagatsu, Murobushi and the other founders had left and he had to take full control — the piece he calls the monumental one that defined the company's style. Across nine scenes he searches for a phantom horse and meets monsters: twenty white-painted dancers twitching with eyes wide open, then Maro hunchbacked like a blind man among a crowd in black carrying a lantern. Anna Kisselgoff called it visionary theatre that twists the epic and brings it down, frequently with street-wise humour. — 1980"},"mesukasan":{"blurb":"The first show by the all-women company that had just broken away from Dairakudakan — and it was made almost entirely by men: Bishop Yamada supplied the title, Dairakudakan's male dancers supplied the choreography, and Maro Akaji directed. The archive calls what they built a mad banquet of barren women. The split had come about because Maro doubted whether Ikeda, already established as a modern dancer, belonged in his company at all. — 1975"},"butohkaden":{"blurb":"Eighty-eight movements Hijikata choreographed in the 1970s, written down by one of his dancers and organised into seven thematic worlds that can be read in sequence or crossed as a web. Waguri was frank about the limits: Hijikata was too occupied with creating to systematise his choreographic words, and varied them by period, performer and performance, so what this offers is one perspective on his butoh. Its effect was that it is now widely accepted that Hijikata's butoh is, in fact, butoh-fu. — 1998"},"danceenergies":{"blurb":"1959"},"mrpuppet":{"blurb":"Fonaroff had walked out of the Graham company to run her own group when she was asked to make this for two classical stars, Alicia Markova and Anton Dolin — 'believed to be the first work to be created for classical dancers by a contemporary dance choreographer', in the Guardian's phrase. The obituaries do not agree on when she made it; the Independent dates the piece two years before the Markova and Dolin performances. — 1949"},"painsanasardo":{"blurb":"1970"},"betweentwo":{"blurb":"A duet Ron made for himself and Shelley Sheer, to music by Moshe Lustig, with costumes by Bertha Kwartz and lighting by Haim Tchelet. It was the last of the four pieces he made for the company he had spent his dancing life in, and it was given ten times. — 1980-03-10"},"takazashiki":{"blurb":"One of the last things Hijikata choreographed, made two years before he died, for Bishop Yamada and Yuki Yuko in the north; the title is glossed in English as Jumping Spider. For Takenouchi Atsushi, who had been in that company since he was eighteen, it was his last performance with them. What was actually on the stage the sources read here do not record. — 1984"},"suirenwaterlilies":{"blurb":"Danced by Ohno Kazuo, and the point after which he made his movements smaller still — not, he held, to accommodate an ageing body, but because movement too big or too fast broke the unity of body and soul he was after, the soul having to direct the motion. A Toronto reviewer wrote that the gestures were more implosive than explosive, and that it was remarkable such small gestures could communicate such monumental emotions. — 1987"},"nikkinoichipeji":{"blurb":"A solo, danced to a score by Yamada Kōsaku, on what Ishii described as passing beyond the world of yearning and arriving at the seat of ecstasy — he retitled it Hōetsu, Ecstasy, two years later. He had nothing to build it out of but the rudiments of ballet Giovanni Vittorio Rosi had drilled into him at the Imperial Theatre, the eurhythmics Yamada had brought home from Europe, and the Japanese dance to hand. The house was a record thin one and a magazine attacked it. — 1915"},"pollenrevolution":{"blurb":"Kasai opens as the maiden from the kabuki Musume Dōjōji — long red kimono, black obi, ornate wig, white face — set against a white scroll covering the back wall and the floor, until electronic tones cut through the wood blocks and flute and the figure comes apart, hissing. Attendants strip him; he returns in black, then in a white men's suit with pink around the eyes, while white particles fall and a rap track starts. Shapes surface and shatter as he goes: a Graham penché, Nijinsky's angled hands. — 2001-04"},"nagasukujira":{"blurb":"Considered the first Harupin-Ha performance, and choreographed not by the company but by Hijikata, in Tokyo. It is where he taught Koichi Tamano the hand position that turns up again in the second scene of Rebellion of the Body, and a string of its movements, traced by Kobayashi Saga to Francis Bacon paintings, was carried straight into Story of Smallpox later the same year. — 1972-09"},"stabatmatercohan":{"blurb":"An all-female cast, each dancer a facet of Mary grieving at the foot of the cross, to Vivaldi's setting. The costumes run from blues into maroons because Cohan took the colours from the dusk skies he watched from the house in the south of France he shared with his designer, Norberto Chiesa. — 1975-09-29"},"waterlessmethod":{"blurb":"Cohan built it out of a swimming manual: the dancers work through the instructions in a dry swimming pool, to a score by Bob Downes and a set by Ian Murray Clark. Among a repertory otherwise full of rituals and traps, his obituarist singles this one out as the witty one. — 1974-06-11"},"gemini":{"blurb":"Two couples and no plot: Henze's Third Symphony, designs by Nadine Baylis, twenty-seven minutes on pointe. Marilyn Rowe, Carolyn Rappel, John Meehan and Gary Norman danced the first cast, with the ballerina's role made on Rowe. American Ballet Theatre brought it to New York less than two years later with Cynthia Gregory and Martine van Hamel. — 1973-04-06"},"metallics":{"blurb":"A trio: one woman in black, a couple in white, and a set of hanging metal tubes between them. The dancers work in front of the tubes, behind them and through them, so that the soloist and the couple read as two separate worlds or emotional time zones. Alvin Ailey's company took it into repertory in 1968 and kept it — Dudley Williams, Sylvia Waters and Sara Yarborough danced it — which is why a Sanasardo work is still performed at all. — 1964"},"elegiesschenfeld":{"blurb":"For the women of the company, to Bach, in costumes Schenfeld made herself, and first shown at Ein Gev on the Sea of Galilee rather than in a city theatre. Haim Tchelet lit it, as he lit most of what Batsheva put on in those years. Twenty performances. — 1974-04-11"},"fattuesdayandallthatjazz":{"blurb":"A Mardi Gras dance musical made with Allan Jaffe and the musicians of Preservation Hall, with Dejan's Olympia Brass Band playing on stage. The cast list is the piece: King and Queen Zulu, a Grand Marshal danced by Hall himself, a line of Baby Dolls, Marie Laveaux the voodoo queen, the Queen of the Mardi Gras Indians, a gambler, a hustling woman, shoe-shine boys. WETA taped it for In Performance at Wolf Trap, which is why it can still be watched. — 1977-02-10"},"herenowjamison":{"blurb":"A portrait of the sprinter Florence Griffith Joyner, who died of a heart seizure at thirty-nine, commissioned by the Olympic Arts Festival and set to a Wynton Marsalis score. Jamison researched Flo-Jo but refused biography: \"I want to capture her spirit, her beauty... she was also a beautiful, glamorous individual and an inspiration to young athletes.\" The dancers carry the athlete's strength rather than reenact her races. — 2001"},"tolieinthesky":{"blurb":"The title comes from May Swenson's poem 'The Question' - \"How will it be / to lie in the sky / without roof or door / and wind for an eye\" - which is recited at the end. Seven dancers cross a stage under projections of streets and countryside filmed by videographers in nine countries; they settle into shapes and other dancers fit themselves into those shapes; then they hug themselves as if suddenly cold, coats fall out of the sky, they put them on and leave. An aerialist makes the last voyage upward alone. — 2012"},"skinnystructures":{"blurb":"A collaboration between the three star students of Cornish's modern dance programme - Fort, Merce Cunningham and Dorothy Herrmann - in which each played a silly character: Cunningham spoofed a bellhop from a cigarette advertisement, Fort did a take-off on 'The Good Ship Lollipop', Herrmann played a kind of streetwalker. It began as a site-specific piece occupying three narrow row houses on the campus, and was first given as a benefit for the Spanish Civil War cause. — 1938"},"thevillageiknew":{"blurb":"Seven episodes from Sholem Aleichem — village life, the Sabbath, and an exodus after a pogrom — made more than a decade before Jerome Robbins turned the same stories into Fiddler on the Roof. The programme note: 'In Czarist times Jewish communities were frequently uprooted by the authorities and the people driven from their homes... a series of scenes culminating in the despairing, hopeless flight of people once again made homeless.' Everyone in it is poor and everyone laughs, and underneath the laughing is sadness. — 1949"},"harmonicabreakdown":{"blurb":"A solo to Sonny Terry's blues about the exploitation of Black sharecroppers, carrying one line on the programme: 'Gotta Keep on Goin' or I'll Cry.' Dudley's own account of it — 'a dance of misery, and defiance rising out of it.' It has since been the standing example in an argument about what Susan Manning calls metaphorical minstrelsy, a white body carrying a Black subject; Mark Franko answers with Dudley's line, 'I was in defiance of the white man, even though I am white'. — 1942-03-10"},"theshiningdark":{"blurb":"Helen Keller, danced by Koner, with two other women as her teachers Annie Sullivan and Polly Thomson, to Leon Kirchner's First String Quartet. The subject startled the audience — a handicapped person was not what a dance concert was for. Koner has written that she looked for her gesture themes in real sources, but that in the dance they are not recognisable, and are not supposed to be. — 1956-08-16"},"spiritdance":{"blurb":"A woman is taken by a supernatural power and then released by it. Sitting on a bench she quivers and gasps as the spirit approaches; standing, she shakes, stamps and jumps ecstatically as it takes her over; then, as it withdraws, the movement weakens and drains out of her. King drew it from Native American ceremony and danced it to a traditional Kwakiutl Raven song and a Tlingit mourning song. It travels in the record under three different titles. — 1945"},"americanfolksuite":{"blurb":"1938"},"onceuponatimeshearer":{"blurb":"Not one dance but a suite of solos for characters with invented names — Genonsh, Ingria, Lillioni, Medmiga, Nollie, OJ, Quilla, Simollindell, Yanchi and Ziff, later joined by Relluckus and Rundunse — each set to a different composer, from Scarlatti and Brahms to Debussy, Prokofiev and Ravel. Shearer's own foundation dates ten of the twelve to one evening in Philadelphia, two years before the last two arrived. — 1949-11-30"},"lettheheavensopen":{"blurb":"1946-04-28"},"songofdeborah":{"blurb":"Deborah, prophet and judge, with one arm wound in a dark twist of fabric that reads as tefillin — the leather straps of male morning prayer, which Jewish women in America did not then wear. Lang put the sign of male piety on her heroine and left it there. The sources cannot agree whether it was a solo or a duet for two women, nor on which year she made it. — 1953"},"baodorilocusfocus":{"blurb":"2006"},"colddreamcolour":{"blurb":"A dance homage to Louis le Brocquy, Ireland's foremost twentieth-century painter, made by the company Oguri had co-founded the year before with Morleigh Steinberg. Steinberg directed it and shares the choreography with Oguri and the Dublin choreographer Liz Roche; the score is by The Edge of U2 with Paul Chavez. The presenters described it as turning the painter's imagery into music and dance — a Los Angeles butoh line and an Irish contemporary one on one stage. — 2012-05-16"},"boxofbirds":{"blurb":"An immersive late-night piece built on Anne Ferran's photographic trilogy of 1940s psychiatric patients, staged through the industrial passageways of a converted railway workshop so that the audience is led into its nooks and crannies. Body Weather dancers move among large-scale photographs and live music, with readings from Nietzsche threaded through. Ferran sculpted the costumes out of existing felt objects. — 2013-11"},"moondanceisotopic":{"blurb":"An hour-long solo built on Vsevolod Vlaskine's photographs of two lights — the light of the full moon and the light of the earth — with Samuel James's video and Vic McEwan's sound around it, danced in a heritage-listed church in inner Sydney. A reviewer described a piece that develops into an almost trancelike meditative state and demands intense control. — 2015-09-04"},"framedinnerlife":{"blurb":"Two women, one of them the Maori dancer Victoria Hunt, are literally framed — held inside a frame at the meeting point of visual art, theatre, dance, music and installation — and work through the eight emotional states of the Natyasastra, the foundation text of Indian classical performance. It runs forty minutes. The difference in the two performers' ages is part of the material, and the piece belongs to a series de Quincey began in 2003 out of a long exchange between Australian and Indian artists. — 2012-08-08"},"cinderellaabt":{"blurb":"Baryshnikov did not make this one alone: he shared the choreography with Peter Anastos, founder of the drag ballet company Les Ballets Trockadero de Monte Carlo, whom he said he first met by going to watch the Trockadero perform. The New York Times warned that anyone expecting a throwback to nineteenth-century story ballet was 'in for a surprise.' Santo Loquasto designed it; it did not survive in the repertory. — 1983"},"swanlakeabt":{"blurb":"Baryshnikov chose not to reinvent it: he kept Petipa and Ivanov and added his own choreography around them, and Martin Bernheimer wrote from the opening that 'Baryshnikov & Co. did not choose to follow any fashionable trends toward revisionism.' Susan Jaffe danced Odette-Odile opposite Andris Liepa as Siegfried, in designs by the Italian PierLuigi Samaritani. It lasted a little over a decade before Kevin McKenzie's own staging replaced it. — 1988"},"oiseaudefeu":{"blurb":"Bejart threw out Stravinsky's fairy tale and used the orchestral suite the composer himself preferred, leaving what he called pure music and two qualities to be danced: Stravinsky the Russian and Stravinsky the revolutionary. The Firebird accordingly stopped being a princess-bird and became a phoenix - and a male role, made on Michael Denard. 'The Poet, like the Revolutionary, is a Firebird,' Bejart wrote in his note. — 1970-10-31"},"petitemort":{"blurb":"Made for the bicentenary of Mozart's death out of two slow concerto movements cut away from their fast ones — 'leaving them as mutilated torsos,' Kylián wrote, 'without arms and legs, unable to walk or embrace.' Six men, six women and six fencing foils: the foils are the men's real partners, and turn out to be the more stubborn ones. Black baroque dresses, rigid shells on wheeled platforms, cross the stage, now moulded to a body, now plainly separate from it. — 1991-08-23"},"forgottenland":{"blurb":"Marcia Haydée brought Kylián back to the company where he had started out, and he set the piece to Britten's Sinfonia da Requiem — a score the Japanese government commissioned in 1939 for a dynastic anniversary, then refused over its Latin liturgical titles. Six couples dance in front of John Macfarlane's semi-abstract seascape; the three principal pairs wear white, red and black, after the three ages of the woman in Munch's The Dance of Life. Land taken by the sea is the governing image. — 1981-04-12"},"balletmecanique":{"blurb":"A machine ballet, first billed as The Iron Foundry after Mosolov's factory score and also called The Spirit of the Factory, with Mussorgsky's Night on Bald Mountain following the Mosolov, costumes by John Hambleton and lighting by Abe Feder. Elise Reiman and Robert Bell led the first cast. Bolm brought it into Ballet Theatre's founding season in 1940, by then titled Mechanical Ballet. — 1931-07-28"},"revenge":{"blurb":"Il Trovatore in four scenes without the singing: Leonora is blackmailed into marrying the Count to free Manrico, takes poison, and dies dancing at her own wedding; Azucena then reveals to the Count that Manrico is his brother, and the last image is the Count on the ground with her standing over him. Antoni Clavé designed the costumes and sets. Page's first version had been danced at the University of Chicago that January; this one was a slight revision. — 1951-01-27"},"camille":{"blurb":"La Traviata without the singing, made for Marjorie Tallchief: a courtesan's party where she dances until she faints, a picnic in the country broken up by Armand's father, a death in bed. The plea scene carries the argument — the father sits her down and has dancers act out what the affair will cost, his own daughter turned away by her groom at the altar, until Camille gives Armand up. Isaac Van Grove arranged Verdi's score. — 1959-01-13"},"thebells":{"blurb":"Poe's poem in five episodes: a skeleton chapel grows at the back of the stage while dancers costumed as bells attend a Bride and a Bridegroom, and the Bridegroom ends in the hands of the King of the Ghouls. Isamu Noguchi designed the scenery and costumes and Darius Milhaud wrote the score. Joellen Meglin singles out the Groom's duet with the Ghoul King — lifts, contact, partner work — as a male-male pas de deux unusual for its date. — 1946-04-26"},"billysunday":{"blurb":"The sermons of the baseball player turned revivalist, danced as he preaches them from a podium on a platform: an exotic dancer stretched out on the floor by three men in turbans, women in long dresses squaring off against women in short ones, hooded Klansmen who are unrobed and turn out to be ballplayers. Sunday changes costume on stage between episodes. Page wanted Kurt Weill for the score, considered John Cage, Virgil Thomson and Nicolas Nabokov, and settled on Remi Gassman. — 1948-03-02"},"anamericanpattern":{"blurb":"A woman turns from one false promise to another — sex, money, mysticism and the mob, each in the shape of a male power figure — and ends with nothing but 'dull routine, to which she succumbs,' in the words of an early program note. Page and Bentley Stone built it on a scenario written with Nicolas Remisoff, to a Jerome Moross score. Joellen Meglin argues it was very likely the first feminist ballet made in the United States, twenty-six years before The Feminine Mystique. — 1937-12-18"},"beautifulmessything":{"blurb":"2026"},"einsvorzweizurueck":{"blurb":"Classical ballet spends its effort going up, and takes the floor mostly to gather momentum for the next rise. Mandafounis reverses that: the steps fall, break off, invert. Four men, one a Semperoper principal, work through five ballets, Petipa's Sleeping Beauty at one end and MacMillan's Manon at the other, the old gestures spelled out as a guessing game — 'I', 'not', 'you', 'love'. In one duet to Tchaikovsky the taller dancer holds his line while the smaller keeps getting tangled in him. — 2026-02-06"},"alacarte":{"blurb":"Mandafounis's first piece as the company's director predetermines almost nothing: the choreography is composed live on stage, and the audience is asked what it would like. At the opening the dancers climbed into the house, collected wishes and called them down to the evening's compere — 'feeling', 'pink light', 'Swan Lake' — which produced a short flood of pink and green and nothing else. It went on, the critic noted, with feeling and without swans. — 2023"},"barbarians":{"blurb":"Three separately made pieces played as one evening: six dancers in white to Couperin cut with Shechter’s own electronica, five in gold, and last a duet in which one of the pair is inexplicably in lederhosen. A recorded woman’s voice runs through it — ‘I am you. You are me. You are not alone,’ which the dancers bark back like drilled recruits — then rounds on the choreographer: ‘Why did you do it, Hofesh?’ — 2015"},"lesaloncarrizo":{"blurb":"The middle room of that trilogy watches a once-wealthy family come apart: the aristocratic grandfather slowly loses control of his house, his bladder and, finally, his mind, while the children who now run the household treat him as shabbily as they treat each other. The company says the performers made it collectively, taking their cue from Satyajit Ray's The Music Room. It won the Montblanc Young Directors Award at the Salzburg Festival. — 2004-11-04"},"lesoussolcarrizo":{"blurb":"The family of the first two rooms is now dead and buried and carries on underground, in a place where the social codes have lapsed and the pecking order is ready to be turned over. The dead discuss their own deaths and audit the lives they led; the company names Dostoevsky's Bobok as the source. The cast included the butoh dancer Maria Otal, then eighty. — 2007-03-28"},"themissingdoor":{"blurb":"Carrizo's first piece for these dancers puts a dying man in a room of doors that will not open and plays out the last minutes of a life in the gap between living and dead, while everyday sounds decay into lost rhythms. She designed the set and the costumes herself. A Herald Angel at the Edinburgh International Festival in 2017 went to it alongside two Lightfoot and León pieces on the same bill, and Chartier later added two more panels to it. — 2013-12-06"},"thelostroom":{"blurb":"Chartier's answer to Carrizo's The Missing Door moves onto a ship, into a labyrinth of rooms and corridors where several stories run at once and the characters seem to exist in every time and place. The company calls the effect a melancholic nostalgia for the future — memory rebuilt wrongly, with the present and the future reaching back to alter it. It took the Dutch Swan award for most impressive dance production of 2016. — 2015-10-01"},"thehiddenfloor":{"blurb":"The third panel puts the characters in a public room — maybe a restaurant — that nature has taken back, and the water rises around them as they try to find a way out, physically and mentally. In the company's own later remount the stage stands an inch deep in water; a critic there objected that the women in it were either traumatised or hauled about. It closed an evening billed as Side B: Adrift. — 2017-10-05"},"lavisita":{"blurb":"The company's first site-specific piece, rebuilt for each place it visits — a gallery closing down, a hospital chapel, a museum of fine art. Characters from earlier Peeping Tom shows are rehoused there and mix with the people who actually work, live and study in the rooms. At its first outing it was played three times only, to a hundred people at a sitting, who followed the performers through the galleries. Its development had already won the FEDORA Van Cleef & Arpels Prize for Ballet. — 2022-11-29"},"s6258w6039":{"blurb":"The coordinates in the title fix a wrecked sailing boat at Deception Island, with a crew of survivors stuck in the ice and no explanation of how they got there. Then a performer turns and addresses the director, the shipwreck story falls apart, and what surfaces is a trauma the company says has fed Chartier's whole body of work — one the performers say they no longer want to play. The piece carries the dedication 'To the fathers I keep staging'. — 2023-09-20"},"thebrides":{"blurb":"Devised by Glass with Margherita Fusi and Silvia Bruni of the Sienese company Topi Dalmata, working with female performers in Siena, and described by Glass as an outrageous work on the feminine principle that took shape as the #MeToo movement unfolded. It had already been restaged in Singapore and China with local casts. What the London International Mime Festival hosted in 2022 was not the show but a six-day workshop run by the original creative team to prepare a UK production. — 2022"},"youandme":{"blurb":"Bernie Schürch left the stage in June 2012 after 5,700 performances, leaving Frassetto the last of the founding trio; she recruited four young movement artists and built the new sketches with Tina Kronis and Richard Alger. The result stages scenes from ordinary life with neither words nor music. About 130,000 people saw it in Switzerland before it went to Broadway, China, Taiwan, Italy, Spain, Egypt and Germany. — 2016-12-01"},"mummenschanznext":{"blurb":"Preparations began in 1998, the year a financial disaster brought in the Rhine valley industrialist Hans Jörg Tobler, who took over the running of the company, founded the Mummenschanz Foundation and housed the troupe in a factory hall in St Gallen. Raffaella Mattioli and Jakob Bentsen were brought in for it, making Schürch and Frassetto a quartet. The company's own history calls the New York run that followed in January 2001 the trial by fire. — 2000-03-18"},"ladansedelavilleetdeschamps":{"blurb":"The first show the Copiaus made entirely themselves — scenario by Michel Saint-Denis, music by Jean Villard — mixing dance, acrobatics, song, mime, masks and improvisation, and introducing Saint-Denis's stock figure Oscar Knie. François leaves the countryside and his sweetheart for the city, loses his money, and meets on the road home a ruined bourgeois travelling the other way. Copeau seemed delighted at the applause and demolished the staging the next day. — 1928-03-04"},"thelivingroom":{"blurb":"Richards's answer to the problem Action had posed — how to let witnesses in. The space is furnished as a living room, sofas and chairs old and new; the performers are hosts, offering the arriving audience coffee, tea and petits fours, and the songs and movement grow out of that sociability rather than interrupting it. A reviewer who saw it at Summerhall in 2016 counted about seven hosts, Richards among them, and noted it had been shown in private dwellings before it went to festivals. — 2009"},"kantan":{"blurb":"Bing made the first French translation of the nō, from Arthur Waley's 1921 English version, and directed the school's students in it — the first time a nō was staged in France. She refused any exacte japonisation: the students composed the music themselves, and she scored the piece on five staves, notating sung and chanted voice, feet on the floor, flute and drums. It never reached the public — Aman Maistre, who played Rosei, was laid up with fluid on the knee. — 1924"},"flut":{"blurb":"A solo in which Linke unravels a long length of cloth and wraps herself back into it. Her own word for the image was drowning — drowning in monotony, in despair, in materialism. It went out under the plain working title Solo mit Stoff, Solo with Cloth, and is danced to Fauré's Élégie in a rehearsal recording conducted by Pablo Casals. — 1981-06-16"},"imbadewannen":{"blurb":"A solo danced around and inside a cast-iron bathtub. Describing it in a lecture-demonstration, Linke called the tub a metaphor for a partner, for a uterus and for a grave. She set it to Satie — En habit de cheval and the Gymnopédies in Debussy's orchestration — and made the costume herself, as she did for most of her solos. — 1980-04-24"},"frauenballett":{"blurb":"Linke's subject was the daily grind of women's work, danced to Penderecki's Magnificat and Renaissance guitar music. It has outlasted almost everything else she made in those years and been restaged again and again — for Grupo Corpo in Belo Horizonte, for the Kibbutz Company in Israel, for a Folkwang company in Costa Rica, and repeatedly for the studio that first performed it, each time with a different soloist. — 1981-01-17"},"schritteverfolgen":{"blurb":"Linke's first full solo evening, made with the visual artist VA Wölfl, who did the set, the costumes and the light. Its subject was her own difficult childhood and how she became a dancer at all, cut to a sound collage that runs Berlioz, Chopin and Grieg up against Ginger Baker's Airforce and drum music from Rwanda and Togo. In 2007 she rebuilt it and handed it to a group of younger dancers, which is the only way a solo of this kind survives its maker. — 1985-09-18"},"ruhrort":{"blurb":"Six men, and the work of men in the steel mills and pits of the Ruhr: Linke turned physical exhaustion itself into the material of the dance. Frank Leimbach built the set with the sculptor Robert Schad advising. When it was rebuilt in 2014 she gave it to the dancers of Renegade, a street-dance company from Herne, so that the old industrial Ruhr was danced by the new one. — 1991-09-30"},"amreigenplatz":{"blurb":"Linke's first evening-long Tanztheater piece, after Euripides' Bacchae, with Dionysos, Pentheus and Teiresias each given to a soloist and the rest of the studio behind them. It has no score in the ordinary sense: the sound is animal noises. It went out in a theatre tent pitched in the Englischer Garten for the Munich theatre festival. — 1983-05-25"},"wirkonnennichtallenurschwanesein":{"blurb":"A group piece for nine dancers to parts of Tchaikovsky's Pathétique — the same score Linke had used two months earlier for her solo Es schwant... The title carries the argument: we cannot all just be swans. It went out first at the Holland Festival in The Hague rather than at home in Essen. — 1982-05-20"},"affekte":{"blurb":"A duet Linke made and danced with Urs Dietrich, taking the ground themes of Dore Hoyer's Afectos Humanos — vanity, desire, fear, love — which she had reconstructed the year before, and moving them out of the solo body and into a relationship between two people. The sound is Bach set against the Japanese drumming ensemble Ondekoza and recorded animal and nature noise. — 1988-12"},"dansefunebre":{"blurb":"Made for the Bagnolet competition outside Paris, with Linke herself dancing the solo part billed as The Soul of the Dead Girl and Reinhild Hoffmann among the four other dancers from the Folkwang master class. The music is Mahler — Der Abschied from Das Lied von der Erde and Ich bin der Welt abhanden gekommen. Pina Bausch took it into the Wuppertal company the following year. — 1975-03-15"},"puppe":{"blurb":"An early piece that took second prize at the Cologne choreography competition, danced to the Andante of Mozart's String Trio K. 563. The soloist was Vivienne Newport, on loan from Bausch's Wuppertal company, with Folkwang students behind her — Lutz Förster among them, years before he became the man who signs 'The Man I Love' in Nelken. — 1975-07"},"dienachstebitte":{"blurb":"Four soloists and a small group, danced to American hit songs of the nineteen-fifties. Linke made the costumes herself with the studio's seamstress, as she did for most of her early pieces. It was brought back in the autumn of the same year and again in 1984, when the studio took it on tour through South America. — 1978-05"},"lastatue":{"blurb":"Decroux made it in 1948 with his pupil Éliane Guyon, as an attempt to put the living marionette on a stage: a body handled and posed the way a statue is, moving as though someone else were moving it. It belongs to the family of pieces he filed under mobile statuary. Beyond that the record is thin — a filmed performance circulates, and the piece survives better than any account of it does. — 1948"},"thenautch":{"blurb":"The palace of a rajah entertaining a guest. St Denis sits on a divan as the favourite nautch girl, swathed in a gold veil, and dances a while, rests a while, dances a while — the alternation meant to convey the all-night tedium of the real thing. Her skirt eventually ran to a hundred and twenty-five yards of silk. She then toured America with Inayat Khan and a native orchestra of seven, which by Shawn's account was the first real Indian music heard there. — 1908"},"ascendance":{"blurb":"1944"},"diesirae":{"blurb":"Its full title is Dies Iræ: The Preposterous Theatrum Interioris Show. Biagini directed it, performed in it and built its montage, setting writings by T. S. Eliot and Kafka and anonymous ancient texts against songs running from Gregorian chant to traditional Chinese. It was the Workcenter's most deliberate step towards a work that takes a spectator's perceptions actively into account, and it was played for several years before being filmed in 2006. — 2004"},"colorharmony":{"blurb":"1928"},"inviewofgod":{"blurb":"A full-length work in three acts, made with Donya Feuer, in which eleven children stand as solemn witnesses to erratic adult behaviour. The young Pina Bausch, then a student at Juilliard, took over the role of the Mother. Of the children she said: 'There was nothing childish... There was nothing cute. It was very simple.' Walter Sorell found the whole thing frustrating but granted that the dancers set a morbid mood and reached some stunning images. — 1959"},"thisstoryislegend":{"blurb":"One of four dances on a programme called On American Themes that O'Donnell and José Limón toured up and down the west coast for three years, small towns and big cities, with Ray Green as their manager and composer. Limón shares the choreography. Another piece on the same bill, Three Inventories of Casey Jones, had Limón as the conductor and O'Donnell as the engine. — 1941"},"dieplaneten":{"blurb":"Kreutzberg danced Holst's suite with Yvonne Georgi, on a scale rarely attempted by two people: a monumental abstract set of dark cave-like entrances out of which spiralling ramps and towering slanted walls emerged. They were often far apart in the space and held together only by complementary movement. Karl Toepfer reads that as the flaw in the partnership — the pair never built dramatic tension between each other — and this was the last year they toured. — 1931"},"lejardincarrizo":{"blurb":"Peeping Tom's first work for a stage is a diptych: a film shot in an African nightclub in Brussels, then a live piece in which three characters from the film turn up again in a carefully tended garden. Chartier held the camera himself. The company's own site credits the creation to Carrizo, Chartier and Simon Versnel, and makes it the first room of a trilogy that ran on through Le Salon and Le Sous Sol. — 2002"},"thecachucha":{"blurb":"Guest read Fanny Elssler's solo out of Friedrich Albert Zorn's notation and word-description in his Grammar of the Art of Dancing and turned it into a Labanotation score, published in 1981 as Fanny Elssler's Cachucha. The reconstruction was then filmed: Margaret Barbieri of the Sadler's Wells Royal Ballet dancing it, traditional music arranged by John Lanchbery, the costume copied from Achille Devéria's lithograph of Elssler. — 1967"},"trioanthony":{"blurb":"A dance called Trio that survives as a solo. The Dance Notation Bureau's score is nine pages of excerpts for one woman, taken down by Ruth Mésavage in the year of the premiere, to Brahms's Intermezzo op. 116 no. 5; on that evidence only one of the three parts ever reached paper. The catalogue files it as a student project and a rough manuscript in fair condition, and refuses it for performance: 'Score is not suitable for staging'. — 1962-05"},"parisquiritparisquipleure":{"blurb":"Marceau played a Parisian street newspaper-seller dreaming of the national lottery jackpot; Joseph Kosma wrote the score and Jacques Noël the sets and costumes. Karl Toepfer notes that all of Marceau's mimodrames, this one included, conjured a nineteenth-century Paris despite the title's claim on the present. Because he never published a scenario, the staging survives chiefly in Étienne Bertrand Weill's photographs. — 1959-05-13"},"mortavantlaube":{"blurb":"Marceau's second mimodrame, and by Karl Toepfer's account a sombre one — he put Bip into it, which he mostly stopped doing afterwards, wanting pieces where Bip was at most one ensemble character among several. It won him the Prix Deburau at the Concours des Jeunes Compagnies, an award named for the Funambules Pierrot he was consciously succeeding. Its full title was Je suis mort avant l'aube, and its year is unsettled: sources date the same piece to 1947, 1948 and 1950. — 1948"},"shishiodori":{"blurb":"Eguchi watched the deer dance of the Tsuruhagoromo hamlet in Iwate for four years and never let himself be taught the steps or the drumming; he only looked. He kept the eight dancers, the drum at the chest and the sasara — eight feet of split bamboo on the dancer's back, struck against the floor by folding far forward — and invented the rest. Kōno Kunio rebuilt the local paulownia deer head in thin steel, and Eguchi liked that a great eye shines inside each erect ear. — 1951-11-17"},"purometenohi":{"blurb":"A Japan five years out of defeat, and Eguchi said the heaviness of it, and the faint dawn light sought inside it, were what made him: sixty-odd dancers, Io turned into a cow by a demon, Prometheus stealing fire from the sun and then chained on the Caucasus and pecked by eagles — one of them danced by Ohno Kazuo. Fire regulations killed it. Naked flame was banned on stage around 1960 and Eguchi stopped performing it; no film survives, and his pupils rebuilt the dance from memory. — 1950-12-11"},"kitsunekenbai":{"blurb":"The second of the Nihon no Taiko pieces, in four chapters like the deer dance, and the same project: taking a northern folk performance and making something an orchestra and a proscenium could hold. The score is credited to Ifukube Akira, who wrote the first — though a musicologist working through the surviving materials flags that attribution as possibly spurious. — 1960-10"},"shujutsushitsu":{"blurb":"Ohno Kazuo never saw it; he saw a photograph and the publicity blurb, and that settled his life. \"The human body is cut open with a knife,\" he said of it. \"It cannot be spoken — and if you say that because it cannot be spoken there is no content, well, for me he staked the whole of the human on grappling with the problem of the operating room. Probably the first time there was abstract dance in Japan.\" What survives is one photograph, Eguchi's comment and a short review. — 1933-10"},"tango1933":{"blurb":"Shown at the recital that summed up the months Eguchi and Miya had just spent at Wigman's school in Dresden. A Japanese account says it and Operating Room were the two pieces most praised there; a German reviewer of the same evening commended the effort to fold Japanese elements into Western rhythm but found none of the works comprehensible. They left Germany as Hitler rose, were home by the end of December, and had it on their first Tokyo bill by March. — 1933-10"},"hamon":{"blurb":"1935-11"},"mugitoheitai":{"blurb":"Made from Hino Ashihei's bestselling front-line reportage and given as the opening production of the Eguchi–Miya Dance Theatre — but the house was then a Shochiku first-run cinema, and the dance went on as the live attraction between screenings of Fritz Lang's M and a Japanese war film. From the next year the company carried parts of it to the war itself, four tours to 1942; by the count in Eguchi's notebooks a hundred thousand soldiers saw them. It was never performed again. — 1938-10"},"igozaida":{"blurb":"The title is the German word Ikosaeder: Laban's icosahedron, the twenty-sided scaffold he used to map the directions a body can reach. Eguchi and Miya and twelve dancers worked inside and around the apparatus in three parts, to a piano score by Ifukube Akira — an avant-garde thing for its moment, and the company's first new work after the war. — 1947-12"},"ayanotsuzumi":{"blurb":"The last of the Nihon no Taiko pieces, and the one Ifukube Akira did not write: the score is by his pupil Kosugi Tairō. Little else about it is settled. Two scholarly accounts write the title as Aya no Taiko rather than Aya no Tsuzumi, and one of them dates the premiere a year earlier than the chronologies do. — 1963-11"},"sukarazanomaritsukai":{"blurb":"A solo Eguchi built on Neue Tanz principles after Germany, danced to the first of Schubert's Two Scherzi D.593 — one of the small pieces he kept coming back to, revived forty-five times. It arrived into an art world that assumed a pupil's work was simply the master's art handed on, so his own dances were long received as Mary Wigman's, and it was a while before people accepted that she had taught fundamentals and each pupil had made a dance of their own out of them. — 1935-10-11"},"yamaonoboru":{"blurb":"A man and a woman climb a mountain and reach the top; that is all of it. Ishii took the walking of a sloping path as his one motif and repeated it, crossing the stage four times, so that the crossings carry the passing of the climb — a minimalist piece, danced to Grieg. It stayed in the repertoire more than fifty years. The year after he died, a stone went up in the grounds of Sensōji carved with him dancing it beside his sister Eiko, the inscription lettered by Tanizaki Jun'ichirō. — 1925"},"ningenshaka":{"blurb":"Three acts and something like two hours, fifty dancers, and Ishii himself as the Buddha — Siddhartha leaving the palace, the years in the forest, and the return home as the awakened one, told in dance and music with no words. He wanted the Buddha as a great man rather than as a religion, to give a wrecked postwar Japan something to hold. It ends with a mixed chorus singing in Pali while silver lotus petals fall. It was played more than two hundred times; only the sound of the first act survives. — 1953-11-06"},"chirinosho":{"blurb":"Not an Ishii subject but Takamura Kōtarō's poem \"The Book of Geography\", which the Imperial Rule Assistance Association had printed in its recitation anthology with a note explaining that under cover of describing the archipelago's landforms it sang the character, destiny and resolve of the Japanese people. Ishii set it for his thirtieth anniversary in dance: his brother Ishii Gorō wrote the music, Nanbu Kunihiko spoke the poem, the Tamagawa Gakuen choir sang, and ten dancers performed. — 1942-11-05"},"karasuando":{"blurb":"One act in three scenes, from a story by Nogami Akira, with the set devised by the painter Okamoto Tarō. Ohno Kazuo danced the Crow and Ando herself the Woman; Hijikata Tatsumi, then twenty-six and still signing himself Kunio, was in the ensemble. Ando put serious work and jazz numbers on the same bill, so the same evening had Hijikata playing a cucumber in a piece called Salad in L.P., accompanied by Hara Nobuo's big band. — 1954-09-13"},"dasseltsamehaus":{"blurb":"One of three premieres given on a single evening — Baby in der Bar and a Tanzsuite were the others, danced by Georgi, Cirul, Kreutzberg and the company. Georgi took her scores from living composers, Hindemith, Wilhelm Grosz and Egon Wellesz among them, and used the dances to get that music heard. Working on this and its companion pieces, she and Kreutzberg grew so close that, the Deutsches Tanzarchiv says, they were soon convinced they could not exist without one another. — 1928-04-13"},"gluecktodundtraum":{"blurb":"An idea of Georgi's that Gottfried von Einem set as a thirty-minute ballet in one scene, subtitled the Alpbach Dance Serenade: a woman speaking a text by Friedrich Rasche over oboe, clarinets, bassoons, horns and trumpet, with four men and three women dancing. Her Hannover ensemble danced it, but the first performance was in the Tyrol. Austrian television filmed it in Vienna seven years later. — 1954-08-23"},"revolte":{"blurb":"A solo shown at the dancers' congress Rudolf von Laban had called in Magdeburg — a meeting convened less about art than about the social plight of most dancers and the wish to organise them as a trade. Two months earlier Kreutzberg had been playing the minstrel in Reinhardt and Vollmoeller's Mirakel in Dortmund. — 1927"},"bizarrerie":{"blurb":"Kreutzberg's debut, a solo of his own inserted into somebody else's ballet evening. He was training as a fashion illustrator at the Dresden art school and had been causing a stir at its parties in costumes he made himself. The ballet mistress Ellen Petz seems not to have valued his modern way of moving, and he could not warm to classical form; that same year he enrolled in the amateur classes at Mary Wigman's school. — 1921-06-01"},"barabau":{"blurb":"Vittorio Rieti's dance comedy, which Diaghilev's company had premiered in London three weeks earlier in Balanchine's choreography. Georgi's was a New Year's Eve opening, and the burial scene — in which the dancers guyed the mourning — was too much for Gera: the house shouted \"Aufhören!\", and only the Reuss prince's presence at every performance kept the protest from breaking out. It pulled the Berlin critics down to Gera, and then went to the Leipzig Schauspielhaus and the Berlin Volksbühne. — 1925-12-31"},"arabischesuite":{"blurb":"The opening item of the evening with which Georgi introduced herself to Gera, where at twenty-one she was — after Vera Skoronel — the second-youngest ballet mistress in Germany. She danced it as a solo to music by the living composer Felix Petyrek. Milhaud's Saudades do Brazil, set for the group, came next, and Egon Wellesz's Persisches Ballett closed the evening. — 1925-10-01"},"pulcinella":{"blurb":"Georgi's Pulcinella exists twice in 1926: a Gera staging in which she danced Pimpinella herself — \"dragonfly-like\", a critic wrote — and the first chamber dance evening she gave in Hannover that December, where she paired it with Petrushka and brought Harald Kreutzberg in as a guest for the male lead. The Hannover evening was an extraordinary success. The choreography is lost. — 1926"},"tanzevorgott":{"blurb":"Not a new piece but a gathering: Kreutzberg pulled his religious solos into a single programme and for the first time gave an audience nothing but his dramatic work. A Vienna evening that December ran Halleluja, the Angel Lucifer to Vivaldi, the 1940 Star Song, and a Requiem for the Living called Vivas in Pace. The same year he opened a dance academy in Bern with Hilde Baumann. — 1955"},"dererstemensch":{"blurb":"A solo Kreutzberg was dancing by the autumn of 1939, when the Nazi leisure organisation Kraft durch Freude put on the Vienna evening that carried it. He worked straight through those years, and his partnership with his composer and accompanist Friedrich Wilckens was, Susan Manning writes, an open secret at a time when gay men were being sent to the camps. Who wrote this piece's music is unsettled: the 1939 programme credits Wilckens, a 1952 one Gluck, a press caption Bach. — 1939"},"deepend":{"blurb":"Anna Kisselgoff, who called DV8 the Sex Pistols of dance, noticed that unlike Bausch they did not simulate the violence: they really wrestled, went red in the face, panted from the strain. Newson jumps onto the much smaller Michelle Richecoeur's neck from behind, over and over, while she is blindfolded and totally submissive; Liz Ranken bellows aloud; Charnock ends the piece by throwing himself repeatedly against a wall. Audiences tended to laugh at the punishment. — 1987-02-19"},"elementhreesex":{"blurb":"A ménage à trois in half an hour, which DV8's own leaflet called a translation into violent physical terms of the habitual traps of male and female relationships; the rehearsal video was subtitled An endless comedy. The three performers devised it during a four-week residency at Yorkshire Dance Centre, to Andrew Poppy's Beating of Wings. It played three dates in a single week and was not revived, and the company never wrote a press release for it. — 1987-04-01"},"hellbent":{"blurb":"A confident transvestite cabaret singer by night, a Shirley Bassey act, and a sad, confused, lonely man by day — though the reviewer thought calling either of them a character would be wrong. Charnock threw himself off the metal staircase that dominated the set, stepped across broken crockery when frustration turned to violence, and abused the audience with both voice and body. In one passage the lonely man tries to seduce a dummy built to look like him. Songs by Nicholas Skilbeck. — 1995"},"frankcharnock":{"blurb":"An improvised solo named for his father and because it was to be nothing less than frank about love, death, sex and family — part extreme stand-up, part drama, part dance, the balance between set choreography and invention shifting nightly. In Yerevan in 2007 he danced on the Armenian and British flags and caused a national row; Armenia's culture minister said it was unacceptable that someone considered a national treasure in Britain should bring such low-quality art to Armenia. — 2003"},"fevercharnock":{"blurb":"A structured improvisation for Charnock and Michael Riessler's string quartet, hung on a handful of Shakespeare's love sonnets — \"like a miniature musical of Shakespeare's sonnets\", he said, though the programme would only promise \"possibly: No. 18, 34, 16…\". What happened between the six of them changed every night. In Venice he finished it standing in the open exit door in his boxer shorts with the rain coming down, shouting \"I love you!\". — 1998"},"haunteddauntedandflaunted":{"blurb":"Three solos made over three years and later run as one evening. Haunted opens on an empty stage — a chair, a jacket over it, a microphone — and a television voice announcing a real-sounding crime: \"What follows now is a reconstruction, using actors. We hope it will help you remember something, anything.\" Daunted is twenty minutes of stand-up dance: she stands at the microphone telling jokes that begin \"There was this pirouette who went into a bar…\" and animates them with small phrases of movement. — 1995-19"},"the48almostlovelyrics":{"blurb":"Houstoun as a live recycling bin for media debris: old movies, countdown numbers, a close-up of feet preparing to dance. She presents and is presented, changes identity, dances as instructed, pulls on a rubber mask, becomes a DJ destroying his own records and then a mediocre rock singer, while off-stage a film murder victim screams and screams. She crawls about picking up fragments of smashed vinyl, singing \"All this talk of getting old, it's getting me down\". — 2003"},"happyhour":{"blurb":"A barmaid on a bender, with additional text and direction from Tim Etchells: bar-stool philosophy sliding into bar-floor desperation across one shift. She tells terrible jokes she cannot finish, asks the house \"are these ones dead at the back? Are these your dregs at the front?\", and picks a fight with herself — \"I don't want to see my face around here again or there'll be trouble because I know where I live.\" At the end she says \"Drink up. I'm showing you the door,\" and walks out through it. — 1999"},"desertislanddances":{"blurb":"She walks on in ordinary clothes, chalks a big palm tree, switches on a boom box of sea, and describes the island — then keeps revising the description until it turns murderous. Her boat is a wooden crate on wheels she climbs into and shoves along with her body. The chalk was meant to glow under ultraviolet and does not, so she explains all the extraordinary things she would have done instead, and finishes by telling the audience about the show they were going to see: \"You'd have loved it.\" — 2006"},"fiftyacts":{"blurb":"Fifty acts, some no longer than a stage direction and none longer than a Chopin prelude, and it opens by announcing itself: \"This is Act 1. This is the bit where the lights go down and this is the bit where I turn around and walk to the back.\" She stands at a music stand with a hammer and a pile of vinyl and smashes a record on each accent of the prelude until both the music and the pile are finished. She puts a microphone to her heart and we hear a beat; to her head, and we hear an ambulance. — 2011-03-03"},"medea":{"blurb":"Her husband, the actor Anders Ek, had left her for another woman the year before, and she put the divorce on stage — at one point her own twins, Mats and Malin, appeared as the children, which is to say as the victims. Maurice Béjart danced Jason at the premiere. The score is thirteen Bartók piano pieces, among them the Allegro Barbaro and selections from Mikrokosmos, orchestrated for her by Herbert Sandberg. — 1950-10-31"},"moonreindeer":{"blurb":"A Sami girl is turned into a white reindeer by a sorcerer and lures young men to their deaths, until one of them breaks the spell and she is human again. Cullberg had just been refused a renewed contract in Stockholm; Copenhagen took her in, Knudåge Riisager wrote her a new score, and this became her second breakthrough and her first international one. The Royal Theatre played it a hundred and twenty-six times before 1975. — 1957-11-22"},"odysseus":{"blurb":"Anders Ek had come back to the family — they were living as neighbours in a collective house in Stockholm — and Cullberg made the returning husband into a ballet. Einar Englund wrote the score on a commission from the Finnish National Opera and Kerstin Hedeby designed it. The company was touring America when it first went on, so the premiere was danced to two pianos in Worcester, Massachusetts; Helsinki heard it with an orchestra five months later. — 1959-09-30"},"theladyfromthesea":{"blurb":"Ibsen's five acts became a few taut scenes. The curtain rises on Ellida with her back to the house, staring at a sea-green curtain; then the sailor appears and pulls her into a duet that had the two of them, Time thought, writhing like a couple of fighting fish. The high point is a dream in which the corps, got up as ocean creatures, comes undulating and swaying across the stage like sea plants caught in a riptide. — 1960-04-20"},"romeoandjuliet":{"blurb":"The middle of three. Cullberg had made a Romeo and Juliet in 1944 for her own first company at the Stockholm Concert Hall, remade it for the Opera in 1955 with costumes by Jan Brazda, and made it again in 1969 — that last one carrying a flag dance choreographed by her son Mats Ek. The Prokofiev was cut and rearranged for her. She read the play as a power struggle between two Renaissance houses that crushes the lovers between them. — 1944"},"drosselbart":{"blurb":"Kurt Jooss's scenario, out of a Grimm tale and set to Mozart, danced by a school that was five years old. Lola Botka took the Princess — the part Jooss had given her when he made his own Drosselbart and she was in his company — and Rudolf Pescht doubled the king and the beggar flute-player he disguises himself as. The reviewer thought the second of the six scenes, in the poor quarter, the strongest thing in it. It ran twelve times that season, to an enthusiasm he said was rarely seen. — 1946-11-13"},"laleyendadejose":{"blurb":"Two worlds that cannot hold together: Potiphar's sensual court, and the shepherd boy haloed in mystic light who finally brings it down. The reviewer singled out the entry of the oriental dancing girls, the wrestlers' scene and above all the Furies and keening women who close in around Potiphar's wife — Lola Botka's part. He was much less kind about the sets, ordered from the Teatro Colón's designer, which he thought came dangerously close to the cheap Egyptian prints run up for Aida. — 1947-11-26"},"czardasenlanoche":{"blurb":"No story links the scenes; they simply follow one another as a run of images from Hungarian folklore, the Hungarian motifs alternating with Gypsy ones as they do in the Kodály it is danced to. Lola Botka took the gypsy woman. It shared a subscription evening with Puccini's Gianni Schicchi, and the company's own later historian wrote it off as a colourful folk print of no consequence — set against the arguable but important Don Juan that followed it. — 1949-08-26"},"donjuan":{"blurb":"Uthoff wrote his own libretto and had Víctor Tevah re-orchestrate Gluck. It opens on a prelude that is simply the man's character — he passes from one woman to the next, and his confidant leads him off towards another. He runs the girl's father through and abducts her; at the ball she watches his passion for her burn out as fast as all the others and goes mad; the guests flee, the confidant gives up on him, and in the epilogue the phantoms of everyone he used finish him off. — 1950"},"tocrushtime":{"blurb":"Old fridges, bin bags and a giant hoarding for freshly squeezed orange juice, with one musician playing Bach against Sepultura. A Geneva reviewer counted a woman brandishing a revolver made of her own fist inside her flies, three men cutting the figures of a cleaner, a fifth-rate down-and-out and an apathetic musician, food gulped down or vomited up, and people sprawling on the floor howling. It was the first piece Augustijnen made himself, from inside Platel's company. — 1997-11-04"},"plagetattoo":{"blurb":"Two dancers and three musicians from the band Zita Swoon, on a man and a woman who live beside each other and drown in each other. Stef Kamil Carlens wanted the music cold, hard and almost robotic — an icy frame the dancers could fill with indifference. The shared sources were Of Mice and Men, Deliverance, and Elvis Presley's last concert, at which he could barely stay upright. De Morgen's best image: Carlens in women's underwear pitying an exhausted Augustijnen, a very beautiful pietà. — 1999-06-22"},"justanotherlandscape":{"blurb":"The party is over, the blue dawn is coming on, five people are left, and the jukebox coughs up one last favourite: one foot in, one foot out, one last dance, and it is never enough. The playlist runs from Purcell and Bach through the Bee Gees, Shirley Bassey, Cabaret, Kylie Minogue and the B-52's. The dramaturg published a note about the piece's debt to Kosinski's The Painted Bird, then admitted after the premiere that he had written it too early and that the thing needed no interpretation at all. — 2002-03-26"},"bache":{"blurb":"The title is the tarpaulin at the back of the stage, and it fits a piece about covering up. Six men in workaday clothes: Ted Stoffer wriggles out backwards from under the sheeting as if breech-born, and the others jab and swipe at him until his flinches turn into falls; he winds his sweatshirt round another man's head and hauls him about like a dog on a leash. Steve Dugardin sings Purcell over it, and as he sings of music as balm to the soul, one of the men jerks in torment. — 2004-03-02"},"importexport":{"blurb":"A stage stacked with crates like an industrial warehouse, its floor miked so that it thunders when the six dancers rock across it; against that, four women playing a string quartet up on the stacks and a countertenor singing French baroque threaded with electronics. The subject is powerlessness. A man swings from his crutches and then falls off them; another flops like a landed fish and is thrown about like so many pounds of flesh, while a woman watching trembles with pity. — 2006-11-14"},"altijdprijs":{"blurb":"Not a dance piece but Arne Sierens's play, with Augustijnen making the movement for two actors who are not dancers. Dino is stuck keeping his vanished brother's shady nightclub open and minding the asylum seekers his father exploits; Pierre turns up, a troubled man with a psychiatric past. They dangle from a trapeze, and the stage itself shudders back and forth with a mechanical puff, more and more often, until at the end it is shaking them almost continuously. — 2008-05"},"ashes":{"blurb":"Eight dancers, two singers and five musicians on a grey, colourless set of different levels, to Handel arranged for violin, cello, lute, accordion and marimba. Augustijnen picked dancers deliberately unlike one another so that none of them had the usual physical references to fall back on. There is a trampoline built into the balcony. Near the end a fictional burial gives each performer a few gestures alone in a spotlight, and it closes with the company rolling side to side on the floor like a tide. — 2009-02"},"verliebt":{"blurb":"Nine sheets of pencil at the Kunsthaus Zürich, dated in the exhibition catalogue only as around 1939 — the date of the notation, not necessarily of any dancing. Perrottet had watched Labanotation being invented and may have contributed to it, but wrote her own dances in a private language instead: schematic figures and free lines, hundreds of sheets of it. The Cabaret Voltaire put the score on public view in 2025, the first time her notations had been shown at any scale. — 1939"},"diestadtwartet":{"blurb":"Made from Gorky's sketch of a city animated by the tormenting wish to be lifted up towards the sun. The people's upstretched hands make the outlines of Gothic windows; there are no flourishes and no pirouettes, a few movements stand for the mechanical monotony of the working day and a few for the turn of a flywheel. In the best scene the rhythm of elegant couples in a dance hall reaches a blind beggar who has no part in it, the way an ocean swell reaches a distant shore. Kraus danced the boy herself. — 1933-03-05"},"ghettolieder":{"blurb":"A cycle for Kraus and her women dancers to music by Joseph Achron of the Society for Jewish Folk Music, danced at the international congress where Laban, Wigman and Ted Shawn were all present. It made her name outside Austria. The Jewish Women's Archive reads it as a kind of political statement in that time and place, and the Austrian music lexicon counts her among the very few exponents of Ausdruckstanz whose group works carried explicit political commitment. — 1930"},"desdichterstraum":{"blurb":"A group choreography to the first movement of Schubert's Unfinished, made — the German account says — as an interpolation inside somebody else's evening. It is the one work of Kraus's that the Austrian music lexicon records as having been reconstructed in recent decades. Its date and its house are unsettled: that lexicon puts it in 1945 at the Tel Aviv Folk Opera, where her dancers were the resident company through the war years. — 1940"},"eightcolumnline":{"blurb":"The title is newspaper jargon for a headline running across a whole page, and the piece is an anti-fascist ballet with a newsboy leaping across the stage to trumpet each ominous development. Krenek made the twelve-tone row do the politics: every form of the basic series belongs to the figure of peace and harmony, its inversion to the megalomaniac tyrant, retrograde inversions to the newsboy. Salvador Dalí and Gala were in the house one night. — 1939-05-19"},"statementofdissent":{"blurb":"Kloepper's Bennington fellowship produced this and two other pieces in one evening, and John Martin in the New York Times took her two solos as the high mark of it. Dance Observer found an elegance of phrase in it second to none in the dance theatre. It survives because it was filmed for the 1939 picture Young America Dances — which is what Ella Rosewood danced from when she restaged it, coached by Claudia Melrose, in a costume copied from the original. — 1938-08-04"},"elellayelotro":{"blurb":"Botka's own piece, shown at the Munich dance festival where Kurt Jooss saw her and took her into his company at Essen — and where Ernst Uthoff, one of Jooss's dancers, was in the audience; she married him, and the two of them later founded a national ballet in Chile. The date is disputed. The Chilean accounts say 1928 and eighteen years old; the Hungarian ones place her among Olga Szentpál's dancers at the third German Dancers' Congress in Munich in 1930, and there was no Munich congress in 1928. — 1930"},"levraymysteredelapassion":{"blurb":"The pupils worked it up themselves in the school Suzanne Bing had started in a room above the theatre; she taught it alone, Copeau took no part, and he came in March 1920 only to watch. It was an end-of-cycle showing rather than a production. The Société d'Histoire du Théâtre's dictionary of women directors lists it as a Bing staging, and credits her teaching with working out the mime, the rhythmic displacements and the gestural vocabulary of the emotions Copeau later deepened under his own name. — 1920-03"},"whitejade":{"blurb":"The Chinese goddess Kuan Yin as a statue coming to life, the body almost still and the arms doing nearly all of it. St Denis began it in China on the Denishawn Far East tour, and of everything she brought home from those fifteen months it is the one her own dancers thought realised her aims — none of the rest, Jane Sherman wrote, matched the solos she had made long before she ever visited the Orient. — 1926"},"rauschtanz":{"blurb":"Rausch is intoxication, or rapture; the sheet that records the dance carries no date at all. The estate at the SAPA Foundation in Lausanne lists Perrottet's notation for it simply as undated, which is the ordinary condition of that archive — her dance notations run from the 1920s to the 1960s, and the exhibition catalogue admits the context in which most of these scattered sheets were made is now difficult to determine."},"dansecafe":{"blurb":"The title is where the record starts and very nearly where it stops: SAPA in Lausanne holds an undated sheet of Perrottet's dance notation called Danse Café, and the Kunsthaus Zürich a second undated set of notes that simply begins with the words. Both belong to a life spent partly in cafés and cabarets — she was the pianist at the Zurich Dada soirées from July 1916 to April 1919, and named in the programmes — but nothing in the archive dates the dance itself."},"chinesischeballade":{"blurb":"The score survives; the dance had to be rebuilt out of it. Reading it again in 2017 took four researchers spread across three time zones and three thousand miles, a rare copy of Laban's 1926 Choreographie to help decode the signs, and a computer to animate the result — Bartenieff had written it down long before she founded the Laban/Bartenieff Institute of Movement Studies. What reached the HASTAC conference was not the dance but a film of the reconstruction, retitled Schrifttanz zwei. — 1927"},"kisobushi":{"blurb":"Not a piece for the company's own stage. On the front-line tours the Eguchi–Miya dancers put their repertory aside and played what the soldiers asked for, and this setting of the Kiso valley folk song was one of the numbers that took its place — danced round field hospitals and bivouacs in southern China and on Hainan, two or three shows a day, moving by train, truck and bus, with memorial rites for the dead in between. — 1939"},"bacharietanz":{"blurb":"1927"},"derjuedischeknabe":{"blurb":"A solo, shown in the year Kraus was also serving, with Gisa Geert, as one of Rudolf von Laban's chief assistants on his enormous Vienna pageant of the trades. Fred Hildenbrandt wrote of it in the Berliner Tageblatt — in the German Wikipedia's paraphrase, since the notice itself is not online — that it was precisely the ground of traditional Jewish culture that gave her dancing a depth and weight that choreographers more taken up with their own egos could not reach. — 1929"},"shulamit":{"blurb":"1946"},"nachtstueck":{"blurb":"Leeder wrote this one down in a notation of his own, worked out from Laban's Choreographie I, which had been published in Jena the same year. He was twenty-four. The manuscript is among the two thousand-odd pages of notation he left behind, and it is the only reason anything of the dance is left at all. — 1926"},"derglaeubigelandmann":{"blurb":"Leeder made 'the devout countryman' as a solo and danced it himself; a photograph of him in it, captioned there as a strongly expressive pose, hangs in the Swiss dance collection that now holds his estate. The dance outlived its decade by a long way — among the Leeder films the SAPA Foundation has digitised is a five-and-a-half-minute record of it made around 1972, some forty years on. — 1933"},"dansemacabre":{"blurb":"A group dance for eighteen people to Saint-Saëns, made during the summer course of 1935 at the Jooss-Leeder School, which Leeder had moved from Essen to a Devon country house the year before, taking a large body of students with him. Dartington's own film unit recorded it in the Barn Theatre. He also wrote it down, and published his own notated score of it from Herisau in 1980 — forty-five years later, a year before he died. — 1935"},"donnaclara":{"blurb":"1937"},"sailorsfancy":{"blurb":"Leeder worked alongside Jooss for twenty-three years, mostly as teacher, dancer and notator rather than as the company's choreographer; this is one of the few pieces of his own the troupe put on. It was danced in the years after the company had reformed in England, when The Green Table dominated everything else in the repertoire and was performed more than three thousand times before Jooss dissolved the company in 1947. — 1943"},"boleroleeder":{"blurb":"Leeder's school taught notation as a subject in its own right — every course, professional or holiday, included writing choreographies down — and his estate of twelve thousand items went to the Swiss archive SAPA rather than to New York, where the Dance Notation Bureau catalogues two of his dances and not this one. Bolero stayed in the repertory at least a quarter-century: SAPA's digitised Leeder films hold a quarter-hour of it from 1970 and sixteen minutes more from December 1974. — 1950"},"figuratragica":{"blurb":"Leeder made it for Ludmila Mlada, who danced the premiere, to music by the Dutch composer Bernard van Sigtenhorst Meyer. A rehearsal film survives from about 1967-71, just under six minutes, danced by Erika Ackermann — and a notation score of it turns up in Mlada's own archive at Surrey, which is to say that the dancer it was made on kept the writing-down of it. — 1952"},"diepforte":{"blurb":"A group dance for fourteen people to music by Charles-Valentin Alkan, and among the last things Leeder made. He wrote it down and published his own notated score of it the following year — one of a shelf of scores he issued himself, each with a cover he had designed. He had trained as a graphic artist in Hamburg before he ever danced, and was still editing and annotating his notations when he died. — 1977"},"inweitemschwung":{"blurb":"One of the early solos, remembered for its temper rather than its steps: Palucca was the cheerful, humorous counter-pole to Wigman's drama, and this and Tanzfreude are the two titles reached for whenever the point is made. The rest of the early list runs the same way — Verklingend, Plötzlicher Ausbruch, Fern, Stilles Lied, Treibender Rhythmus. No source found gives it a year, and the best dated work-list of hers has instead a Mit Schwung of 1925, to Portuguese folk music."},"tanzfreude":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"tanzderangst":{"blurb":"His first appearance before a Berlin audience: a solo out of Egon Wellesz's dance symphony Die Nächtlichen. He danced in a stage space whose walls closed oppressively in on him, platforms sinking and others rising, and in the middle of the moving room the figure collapsed. Max von Schillings, who ran the house, congratulated him afterwards — \"Kreutzberg, you have the marshal's baton in your knapsack!\" — and Oscar Bie called it the surprise and the high point of the evening. — 1924"},"apokalyptischerengel":{"blurb":"One of the angels. Kreutzberg came back to angel figures for forty years — the Annunciation in 1927, the Last Judgement two years later, Lucifer to Vivaldi in 1946 — and the Deutsches Tanzarchiv reads the series as a discipline: figures that forced him to get the most intense effect out of the least formal language. Friedrich Wilckens, his accompanist and companion of decades, wrote the music. Siegfried Enkelmann photographed him in it. — 1929"},"waffentanz":{"blurb":"The warrior's dance for sixty men in the fourth section of Olympic Youth, led by Kreutzberg and answered by the women's lament Wigman led. Janet Flanner, who made no secret of her scepticism, described pink and saffron Greek ballet warriors chieftained by Kreutzberg, and their mournful female followers in loops of varied gray. The New York Times reported the scene's moral as the pacifist one, that modern war destroys victor and vanquished alike. — 1936"},"persischesballett":{"blurb":"The Gera staging was Georgi's own, but the piece had reached the stage the year before at the Donaueschingen chamber-music festival, choreographed by Kurt Jooss, with Georgi dancing the female lead and Harald Kreutzberg — then the Berlin Staatsoper's star soloist — the male. Wellesz had written it for Ellen Tels, a Russian dancer who had fled to Vienna, and the Donaueschingen version cut the orchestra down to nine instruments and percussion. Its success made the Georgi–Kreutzberg partnership. — 1925"},"elektronischesballett":{"blurb":"The first of three ballets Georgi made to tape music by the Dutch composer Henk Badings, put together on the apparatus of the Philips physics laboratory at Eindhoven; the score is his Kaïn en Abel. Electronic music, she said, drove her \"to overstep the boundaries of the usual dance vocabulary\". Evolutionen followed in 1958 and Die Frau von Andros in 1960, and the Hannover city archive calls the three of them legendary. — 1957"},"skorpion":{"blurb":"Her last piece and her last big success, made shortly before her seventieth birthday: a solo for Heidrun Schwaarz to music by Morton Gould, which the Sächsische Biografie says showed the strong woman. The Hannoversche Allgemeine wrote that the ovations for this mixture of jazz, dance and classical ballet may have proved to Georgi how unshakeable her reputation had stayed. — 1973"},"carminaburana":{"blurb":"No pointes, no fouettés, no entrechats: the Jooss training shows in an invented movement that gets everything from plasticity and near-total simplicity, figures playing close to one another and never quite joining. Fortune turns her wheel and death carries off the child, the king and the old man like a great puppeteer, while between them adolescents come to love and lose it. Ángel Rama, seeing it in Montevideo, knew nothing else of Uthoff's to set beside it. — 1953-08-12"},"missjulie":{"blurb":"The two worlds of Strindberg's play get two techniques: Julie's aristocratic refinement is danced on full pointe, in sharp contrast to everyone else's earthbound weight. Cullberg danced the cook herself. The critic Anna Greta Ståhle wrote that erotica had never been shown so unconcealed by dancers before, which gave the ballet a certain pornographic sheen and did it no harm at the box office. The opera director came out to Västerås to see it and took the whole thing into the Royal Opera that autumn. — 1950-03-01"},"denelakadrottningen":{"blurb":"It was not Snow White that interested Cullberg but the queen — why was she so wicked? She made it straight for the camera and treated the screen as a framed picture: she drew every shot and decided in advance where the cuts would fall, so she came to the studio minutely prepared. Her producer said her signature was to use the one-eyed camera's lack of perspective to build depth between foreground and background. It won the Prix Italia, the first time Swedish television had taken the prize. — 1961"},"rottvinigronaglas":{"blurb":"A pas de deux for the poet Bellman and his imaginary Ulla Winbladh, danced by Niklas Ek and Mona Elgh into painted backgrounds by Watteau, Fragonard and Turner: the backgrounds were shot on one camera and the dancers on another, and chroma key put the dancers inside the pictures. Cullberg was the first to use the trick for art. A dancer from those years remembered the cost — you had to be in exactly the right spot in frame, and the dancing sometimes suffered while Birgit was happy with the picture. — 1970"},"vandenbranden32chartier":{"blurb":"Two rickety caravans stand on a snowfield under a wide-open sky, and the neighbours stranded there cannot get away from each other or from themselves. A Total Theatre reviewer describes a woman taking a tumble in the snow, being suckled one breast at a time by two young men, then walking off and returning with a shotgun and a mezzo-soprano voice. Imamura's The Ballad of Narayama was one source; the piece took the 2015 Olivier for best new dance production. — 2009-11-28"},"alouerchartier":{"blurb":"A large hall, probably a hotel's, hung with long red drapes over black-and-white tiles. An attendant sets the room; the tall lamp beside the piano switches on and off in time with him, and his legs start moving without the rest of him. A proprietor shows visitors round, an opera singer delivers an aria, and at the close the whole cast shifts the furniture while the butler at last sinks into the armchair a guest had announced he came for. — 2011-10-15"},"vader":{"blurb":"The visiting area of an old people's home, walled so high that it feels underground, with the father at its centre — by turns godlike and ridiculous, possibly hiding a secret, possibly just deluded. The Guardian's reviewer found a woman's head popping out of a saucepan of soup, a resident who cannot remember his daughter's name, and a lank-haired man at the piano crooning Feelings. Chartier directed it alone, the first time in fifteen years he and Carrizo had split up to work. — 2014-05-10"},"moeder":{"blurb":"A shifting museum of memories, with a mother dying in a coffin inside a glass booth at the back of the stage. Paintings bleed and start to eat the people looking at them, and are swapped one by one until every picture shows the father as a young man. One reviewer describes a man rescuing a woman drowning on a dry floor — no water is visible, but you hear her splashing. Carrizo directed alone, taking the mother while Chartier had taken the father. — 2016-09-29"},"kindchartier":{"blurb":"A forest of pines on one side, a rock cliff on the other, workers in white overalls hoisting a boulder above the stage, and a child played by the opera singer Eurudike De Beul, far too big for her little bicycle. The Guardian's reviewer catalogues a creature shaped like an ostrich with a deer's head and human legs in stilettos, a woman nursing a conifer that trails umbilical roots, and the child seizing a rifle to keep firing at a body already down. It is about childhood and is not for children. — 2019"},"triptychchartier":{"blurb":"The company remade its three short pieces for its own dancers and set them all on one ocean liner: a corridor of doors that will not open, a cabin, and a flooded restaurant. The sets are struck and rebuilt in full view, like live film editing. Chartier says they advertised for a new ensemble, got two thousand applications and auditioned six hundred people. The Guardian's writer summed the evening up as opening with a maid mopping blood and ending with the apocalypse. — 2020"},"apresmididunfaune":{"blurb":"Nijinsky devised a private notation, adapted from the Stepanov system he had learned at the Imperial school, and wrote his own ballet down in 1915. The Ballets Russes never used that score — the piece was passed on from memory instead — and Guest and Claudia Jeschke spent years deciphering it before publishing a Labanotation transcription. What came back is a Faune whose nymphs move in far more varied detail than the memory version had kept; oral transmission had smoothed them into uniformity."},"theearlyones":{"blurb":"Kramer crowdfunded it, choreographed it and danced in it herself to mark turning a hundred. She had come back to Sydney the year before at ninety-nine after decades away — she said she missed the kookaburras and the smell of the gum trees — and met the filmmaker Sue Healey soon after, which began the collaboration that carried the rest of her working life. — 2014"},"abuddhaswife":{"blurb":"A dance-drama Kramer drew out of her own travels in India, made at 102 and forming one part of a larger work assembled around her life. The Arts Health Institute paid for it. She had no company and no company's money; the late pieces were funded piece by piece like this. — 2017"},"lesfrereskaramazov":{"blurb":"The adaptation that turned Copeau from a critic into a practitioner: Dostoevsky cut down with his schoolfriend Jean Croué, begun on Jersey and finished at the end of 1910. Jacques Rouché directed it and reviewers singled out Charles Dullin's Smerdiakov; a second staging that October put Louis Jouvet on as Father Zossima. Copeau later revived it himself with Dullin as Smerdiakov again, Jouvet as Feodor and himself as Ivan. — 1911-04-06"},"lanuitdesrois":{"blurb":"Copeau commissioned the Bloomsbury painter Duncan Grant to design the costumes and scenery and played Shakespeare on a startlingly plain stage, with Suzanne Bing as Viola and Louis Jouvet as Sir Andrew Aguecheek. Critics said it asked the audience to imagine what the set did not supply, something no Paris stage had demanded since Paul Fort in the 1890s. The run closed on schedule because the troupe was already booked to tour Alsace; Parisians did not see it again until 1920. — 1914"},"lesfourberiesdescapin":{"blurb":"Copeau opened the New York company's first season with Molière, and set in the middle of the stage the bare platform he had called for in his Appel — a trestle the actors could loiter on until a scene needed them. John Corbin of the New York Times noticed what that freed: it made possible \"many combinations and groupings, all sorts of telling encounters\". Copeau played Scapin, Jouvet played Géronte, and Jean-Louis Gampert cut the costumes after Jacques Callot. — 1917"},"lamaisonnatale":{"blurb":"Copeau's own play, more than twenty years in gestation and one of the two things he counted as a high point of his last season. An autocratic father's two elder sons have already left; the youngest, André, stays on, and when the father dies must choose between running the family factory and going after his own life. He leaves, with his grandfather's blessing. Reviewers found it short on dramatic action and it failed, which Copeau took hard. — 1923"},"lillusion":{"blurb":"Copeau welded Rojas's La Celestina to Corneille's L'Illusion comique to make the first working example of what he called the Comédie Nouvelle — a French answer to commedia dell'arte, played by the Copiaus in masks and stock types of their own invention. Suzanne Bing played La Célestine, Michel Saint-Denis Oscar Knie, Jean Dasté Monsieur César. It was the centrepiece of the Burgundy company's repertoire. — 1926-10-03"},"lemysteredesantauliva":{"blurb":"A fifteenth-century Florentine sacra rappresentazione revived for the first edition of the Maggio Musicale Fiorentino, with incidental music Ildebrando Pizzetti wrote for the occasion and conducted himself. Copeau's promptbook survives and Maria Ines Aliverti published it in a critical edition, which makes this better documented than most of his late work. — 1933"},"lemiracledupaindore":{"blurb":"Copeau adapted a medieval mystery, the Miracle de Pierre le Changeur, with André Barsacq for the five-hundredth anniversary of the hospital's founding and staged it in the courtyard; Marie-Hélène Dasté made the costumes. It was the last production he directed. He died in the same building six years later. — 1943"},"cequemurmurelasumida":{"blurb":"The nō research Bing began at the school in 1923 and was never able to show finally reached a stage here: she translated the play from the English and Marie-Hélène Dasté directed it. Jean Dasté's programme note named her as the woman with whom he had experimented with spoken chorus, improvisation and the mask. Bing wrote to Copeau afterwards that Japanese spectators who had seen the real thing at home told the Dastés that, transposed to European terms as was right, they had found the true spirit of nō. — 1947"},"lemenuisier":{"blurb":"A carpenter works a plank of wood, and Decroux built the whole piece on the muscular effort of it — a continual oscillation between contraction and release, with the carpenter breathing before each push. It belongs to the second of his three ages of production, the artisanal life, and comes straight out of the jobs he had held himself: dishwasher, labourer in a wagon-repair works, nurse, plumber, mason, butcher, navvy. Of actors and directors who had done none of that he said he wondered how they managed to stage anything. — 1931"},"lalavandiere":{"blurb":"A washerwoman washes, wrings, irons and sews, and because the work is so familiar that it needs no thought she is free to drift off into a daydream. In one sequence she mimes sewing and the thread, instead of shortening, keeps getting longer, drawn out by an extending arm and an arching back: by Decroux's account it is not the thread but time that is being delayed by her dreaming. He set it to Ravel's Boléro, in keeping with his taste for music repetitive enough not to overtake the body. — 1931"},"lecombatantique":{"blurb":"Two performers almost naked but for a loincloth — the Big one stronger and slower, the Small one faster and weaker — fighting with imaginary spears and knives, David and Goliath as a study in spacing. Unlike the standing pieces of the repertoire it lives on the ground the two cover, closing and opening the distance. Decroux made the first version with his wife and then with Jean-Louis Barrault; later he played it with his son Maximilien, who said it was the one thing of theirs that never failed. — 1945"},"lusine":{"blurb":"A trio, and the last of Decroux's three ages of production — the modern one. The performers wear black costumes ruled with white lines and their faces are masked, so no individual is left, only instruments of mass production; one actor is by turns the worker, the foreman, the machine, the time clock and the clock. It is the only piece where the performer must learn the music by heart, and that music, an arrhythmic assembly of sounds, Decroux made out of the internal rhythm of the actions. — 1946"},"lesarbres":{"blurb":"Four performers of different heights stand one behind another, smallest at the front, and move slowly and softly — the opposite of the factory piece — to Beethoven's Moonlight Sonata. The image is of trees outlasting time, and Decroux filed it under mobile statuary: an inner journey shown as a bodily one. It leans on what he called annelated movement, one segment carrying the next until the whole body arrives at a precarious balance. — 1947"},"lameditation":{"blurb":"It grew out of Le Penseur, The Thinker, one of the standing improvisation subjects at Decroux's school, and hardened into a repertory piece. What is left of it is a set of photographs: the sequence Étienne Bertrand Weill shot of it is treated as the high point of the photographer's long collaboration with the mime, and is the material scholars have used to argue that a photograph of movement interrupts rather than petrifies it. — 1957"},"laboyeuseetlautomate":{"blurb":"Not a mime piece at all: Gabriel Cousin's tragédie-farce in two acts and ten tableaux, which Lecoq staged jointly with Roland Monod. The French national library's catalogue entry is most of what is left of it — the co-direction, the date of 19 October 1961, and the odd generic label the author gave his play. — 1961"},"etudesauxallures":{"blurb":"One of two short pieces Lecoq directed for Italian television in 1961, the other being Les Trois Soldats; both were produced by Luigi Squarzina. He had spent eight years working in Italy before opening his Paris school in 1956, and these are among the few records of him directing rather than teaching. — 1961"},"lestroissoldats":{"blurb":"The companion to Études aux allures: two pieces Lecoq directed for Italian television in the same year, produced by Luigi Squarzina. By then he had been running his own school in Paris for five years, and the French record of his directing is thin enough that an Italian broadcast list is where these turn up. — 1961"},"bip":{"blurb":"The figure in a striped pullover and a battered, be-flowered silk opera hat that symbolized life's fragility, drawn from Chaplin's approach to silent performance. Bip's misadventures 'with everything from butterflies to lions, from ships and trains to dance halls and restaurants, were limitless,' and he remained Marceau's alter ego — his Little Tramp — for the rest of his career. — 1947"},"lejoueurdeflute":{"blurb":"1947"},"lepierrotdemontmartre":{"blurb":"Marceau took the argument from Adolphe Willette's black Pierrot — the Montmartre cartoonist's own invention — and played Pierrot himself, with Gilles Segal as Harlequin and Jacques Fabbri as the patron; Joseph Kosma wrote the music and Jacques Noël the sets and costumes. Le Manteau, his Gogol piece, shared the programme. Marceau never published a scenario for any of his mimodrames, so what actually happened on stage survives mainly in Étienne Bertrand Weill's photographs. — 1952"},"donjuanmarceau":{"blurb":"A mimodrama in ten tableaux, devised by Marceau with André Laszlo out of Tirso de Molina's Burlador de Sevilla. The two said they saw in the Burlador a lone wolf. The New York Times, watching a performer whose act was built out of short solos take on a full-length narrative, judged the result more a transposition than an adaptation. — 1964"},"levagabonddesetoiles":{"blurb":"1972"},"lacage":{"blurb":"The mime finds himself shut inside an invisible cage and works his hands along walls that are not there. It is the piece of Marceau's that most people have actually seen — several filmed versions survive from the early sixties on — and where most of the world first met the invisible wall. No first performance is recorded for it."},"thanatospolski":{"blurb":"The last stage work the Laboratory Theatre company made, led by Ryszard Cieślak and built deliberately between the paratheatre experiments and an ordinary production. It began under the working title Po dostojewsku — In Dostoyevskian — and drew on readings of Dostoevsky, on the group's practice of building open relations between people, and on what was happening in Poland that year. Performances stopped when Antoni Jahołkowski, for whom it was a last role, died that September. — 1981"},"lesassiettes":{"blurb":"A two-hander clown act Gaulier made with Pierre Byland, in which the pair broke two hundred plates a night. Roger Blin, who had directed the first Waiting for Godot and Endgame, worked on the direction with them. Gaulier's account of the intention is unadorned: Paris theatre at the time was preoccupied with Brecht, and \"us, we wanted to do the best idiot show in the world.\" They played it through the 1970s at the Odéon and on tour. — 1972"},"bonjourmonsieurdecroux":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"lapomme":{"blurb":"Not a stage piece but a television production, made in the year the three founders broke off touring altogether — a year's holiday followed by a year on hiatus. Wikipedia's English and French articles both record that it took the Rose d'Or, the Golden Rose of Montreux; neither says what is in it, and no fuller account of its content was found. Bossard would live another twelve years. — 1980"},"thenewshow":{"blurb":"1981"},"gormenghast":{"blurb":"John Constable's adaptation of Mervyn Peake, staged behind a black wall of doors of different sizes that slid in and out of their slots, so that Flay's round of the castle — endless corridors, staircases, echoing slammed doors, John Eacott's score — read as though a Steadicam were following him. The cast of seven doubled as an ensemble of hooded, Kabuki-derived figures; Rae Smith designed it. It won Glass the TMA award for best director. — 1992-05-05"},"popeye":{"blurb":"Billed by the company as the first stage adaptation of Popeye. Seven performers, live music and effects; Gary Barber caught the Popeye voice down to the syllable, even through an Astaire ballad, with Mary Roscoe as Olive and Rex Doyle as Wimpy. Total Theatre's reviewer called it a masterpiece and then complained that the first half was a children's show inviting no participation, with a funny script written for the adults in the room. — 1993-06-08"},"lucky":{"blurb":"Glass played an autistic man, almost alone, with occasional interventions from Sarah Collins, who also wrote the music, the whole thing enclosed in a small dark scaffold. Total Theatre's reviewer began sceptical — transposing the physical experience of autism onto a tall, well-built frame is a hard thing to do decently — and came out calling it touching and harrowing: entrapment, claustrophobia, frustration, paranoia, and a self withdrawn from reality. — 1995-03-20"},"themosquitocoast":{"blurb":"An adaptation of Paul Theroux's novel played on a raised platform of mud, with capoeira and the movement of the coastal and jungle peoples of Central America advertised as its physical language. Total Theatre's reviewer thought neither ever appeared: two and a half hours of staging without real innovation, and a script that never got at the Fox family, so that the disaster waiting for their expedition drew no compassion at all. — 1995"},"disembodied":{"blurb":"Glass's return to performing after years spent directing. A hunched figure descends a staircase built from upturned shoes, climbs to a single red velour theatre seat and then talks the audience through a routine for removing the brain without surgery, vaudeville-style; the condensed brain is finally blown out of his nose into his hand. A tongue refuses to stay in the mouth, is cut off in desperation and climbs down the wall. Co-created with Tom Morris, and scratched at the BAC the year before. — 2004"},"redheart":{"blurb":"Lily's first solo piece after three years with Three Women Mime, and, as first solo pieces tend to be, autobiographical: her Australia, a continent whose population clings to the coastline while the centre stays wide, wild and ancient, set against the banality of suburban daily life. She was still billing her work as mime theatre at this point; the term physical theatre had not yet been coined. — 1983"},"wendydarling":{"blurb":"Lily had been dreaming about flying; Rex Doyle told her to look at Peter Pan, and on rereading it what caught her was the unrequited love. A woman arrives in an abandoned nursery and settles to spend the night, and the props unfold the story: leaves cascade out of a book, then a conch, feathers, a boat with a pirate flag, a hook; the fox fur comes alive and the crocodile handbag eats the alarm clock. It ends with a woman of thirty at the window, waiting for a boy who cannot come. — 1988"},"hiroshimamonamour":{"blurb":"No relation to the Resnais film, Lily insists. Where her other solo pieces of the period were movement-led, this one was a scripted character speaking directly to the audience — the form her director Rex Doyle pushed her towards. Time Out called it a show about the end of the world and the power of kitsch, and found it extremely funny. — 1991"},"begpetalily":{"blurb":"It began as a surreal scene between a woman and a man inexplicably wearing a dog mask, after Paula Rego's paintings, and in conversation with David Glass turned into Cronenberg-style body horror. Penelope, a surgeon so let down by men that she sets out to build a better one, cuts a man open, puts a dog inside him and sews him up. Rae Smith turned a hospital drip stand into a rose bush hung with white and red bags of fluid. Robert Golden disliked the show, then filmed it. — 1992"},"topless":{"blurb":"Written out of two very bad years — a marriage ending, her own illness and her father's, her mother's death, a brush with breast cancer — and made only because somebody offered her free nights at the Lion and Unicorn. Lily borrowed the stand-up theatre form from Claire Dowie and Colin Watkeys, whose two-hander she had just toured in: direct address, strong concept, almost no production. Her own billing: about life and death and love and hate and sex and sticking plaster and breasts. — 1999"},"midriff":{"blurb":"The middle panel of Lily's stand-up theatre trilogy: the years after Topless, spent asking whether to go home to Australia to look after her father and feeling like a coward and a villain for not wanting to. She leaned on Hamlet for it — the indecision, the constant self-critique — and billed it as being about choices and chakras, courage and cowardice, flesh and blood and bellies. It needed a large strong table, which made it awkward to tour. — 2002"},"invocation":{"blurb":"Lily had not performed for six years after MiDRiff; this is what brought her back, an account of having given up performing and of the journey, at once spiritual and commercial, that followed. She files it as the closing part of the trilogy that began with Topless, and sums it up as base chakra and existential crisis. Directed by Di Sherlock; first produced by Jimmy Jewell's ladida group, and taken up in 2014 by Giant Olive. — 2010"},"chastitybelt":{"blurb":"Lily calls it an anatomical contender for the third part of her autobiographical trilogy while conceding it is less directly autobiographical than the others and carries a great deal of rhythmic verse. It works by her usual method of retelling a story from the wrong side, rewriting Artemis and Aristophanes' Lysistrata as one-woman comic confession, set, in her billing, on the battleground between trust and lust. Di Sherlock directed. — 2012"},"mozartpreposteroso":{"blurb":"Rae plays Mozart's exploitative father first; all the audience sees of the child is a plume scribbling away inside a cot, from which scores shoot out to be seized on by the disbelieving father. Then a foot-high puppet Mozart pounds a piano that is only a big box, made real by sound effects, before the grown Mozart switches from puppet back to performer. Made with John Mowat, it charts the absurdities of the life from birth to death."},"exitnapoleonpursuedbyrabbits":{"blurb":"Rae comes in through the auditorium as an army cook, a Michelin-man bundle of clothes and blankets with cooking pots clattering off her and the first of many mangy rabbits pulled from her kit-bag. Finding Napoleon's tent empty, she steps into his boots; the audience becomes toy soldiers mimed into standing, sitting and applauding, and one spectator is promoted to Josephine, set on a pedestal and danced with. Chaplin's The Great Dictator is the acknowledged source."},"elizabethslaststand":{"blurb":"The piece with which Rae abandoned the sketch format she had worked in since 1975 and committed instead to a single wordless character sustained across a whole evening — a change her own circulated biography dates to exactly this show. Simon McBurney directed it. Two accounts describe it identically: an old woman alone, developing delusions of grandeur, rebuilding the court of Elizabeth I in her living room. Neither is sourced to a review, and no first-hand description of the staging was found. — 1990"},"andtheshipsailedon":{"blurb":"Two women share a cabin on an ocean liner. Rae enters as Caledonian primness — knife-pleat kilt, beret, polished shoes — and arranges her few belongings until the space is hers; Sally Owen follows as a Mediterranean widow in shawls and loose bra straps, and festoons it with a light-up Madonna, a salami and black underwear. Eighty minutes of border war without words, directed by the Argentinian Carlos Trafic, carried by object theatre, clowning and comic dance. — 1993"},"shakespearetheworks":{"blurb":"Made with John Mowat: Macbeth, Hamlet, King Lear and Romeo and Juliet in one wordless evening, four tragedies played as comedies, the Hamlet performed by two hands. Rae's biography says it toured for three years to twenty-six countries. Subverting tragedy became her trade as a director afterwards — Lorca's House of Bernarda Alba, Strindberg's Miss Julie, Ibsen's Wild Duck and Stoker's Dracula all went through it."},"homemadeshakespeare":{"blurb":"A two-hander made with the Swedish actor Lasse Åkerlund of Teater Allena — the exception in a repertory otherwise built on solo, wordless work. English Wikipedia says this is the piece in which Rae used her voice on stage for the first time, but cites nothing for it; Rae's own circulated biography lists it only as one of her duo shows. What the evening actually consisted of is not established in either."},"thenewshow1986":{"blurb":"Four years of work in the company's studio at Zurich's Rote Fabrik went into it; the new routines included a four-metre octopus and a pair of giant hands, and Simon Murray describes this second programme as one in which the disguises grew so large that the human frame inside could hardly be made out. It ran three months at the Helen Hayes while separate Mummenschanz troupes toured the United States at the same time — the peak, by the company's own account, of the Mumms' American success. — 1986"},"mummenschanzparade":{"blurb":"Andres Bossard died of Aids in March 1992; the two survivors stopped, doubted whether to go on at all, and decided to try again in his name. Parade is what came of that decision — a retrospective played by Schürch and Frassetto as a duo, reviving numbers from the earliest years and splicing them with material from The New Show. The American John Charles Murphy joined them, and it became the company's largest success in the United States. — 1992"},"mummenschanz3x11":{"blurb":"Not a new show but a retrospective: Frassetto described it as a kind of best-of drawn from the company's three creative periods, with much of the old material redesigned and pushed further. Since Mummenschanz made roughly one new programme a decade — Simon Murray counts three shows in thirty years — a compilation was less a stopgap than the house rhythm. It began its touring life with a season in Australia. — 2006"},"mummenschanz50years":{"blurb":"The half-century programme is a curated best-of: the clay masks and the toilet-paper faces the company has been reshaping on stage since the early 1970s, the air-filled giants, the pipe creature, with a few new shapes worked in among them. Frassetto, by then the last of the three founders, put it together during the lockdown that had stopped the touring. The company counts about 250 performances of it worldwide by 2025. — 2021-12-10"},"thelittledictator":{"blurb":"Seventeen actors and a satirical tyrant, worshipped one moment and ridiculed the next by a public with no memory, who loses himself in his own absurd games chasing a paradise he cannot reach. Wasson made it in 1985–86; the company says it was given the Label de la Révolution in 1989, and it played the Avignon Off in 1990. A second part, The Invaded Room, followed in 1990–91 with the tyrant old, blind and bloated, sheltering in his bed while the room turns into an arena for every battle he has ever fought."},"beyondthegarden":{"blurb":"A piece for three actors that Wasson and Soum made in 1987–88, described by the company as a visual poem of the haunted adult heart trying to keep hold of an innocence while the world outside falls into chaos — the wish, in everybody, to remain a child. It toured Paris, Tel Aviv, Jerusalem and Haifa, Winnipeg, Luxembourg, and a theatre festival held in the Carthusian monastery at Padula."},"crusade":{"blurb":"A duet Wasson and Soum made in 1985. An awkward, timid angel meets a young woman on the night of her birthday and pushes her towards the question \"who am I?\"; she is judged by eyes that turn out to be her own and struck by ghosts that turn out to be her own heartbeat. It became the company's touring workhorse — Amsterdam, Montreal, Winnipeg, Berlin, Dresden, London, Israel, Poland, Arkhangelsk, St Petersburg, Stockholm and New York."},"themanwhopreferredtostand":{"blurb":"The first attempt to put Decroux's own repertoire back on a stage: twelve of his major pieces, spanning his first productions of 1930 to his last of the 1980s, reconstructed by his last two assistants for twelve actors and funded by the French Ministry of Culture. The title quotes Decroux's retort to critics who asked him to explain himself — 'My art is an art standing up'. It ran two months in Paris in 1994 and was still being staged in Warsaw in 2007. — 1992"},"entangledlives":{"blurb":"Soum built it in 2000 around Jean Tardieu's poem cycle Monsieur Monsieur, for seven mime actors working in seven different languages — the musicality of the languages carrying as much weight as the movement. The subject is the duality inside a person, unconscious impressions surfacing through a present life. Wasson assisted. It went to the London International Mime Festival, the Edinburgh Fringe and the Vlaanderen mime festival in 2001."},"thegovernmentinspector":{"blurb":"Gogol for fifteen mime actors, made in 2000 with the University of Bologna's Dipartimento di Musica e Spettacolo and first played at the Arena del Sole. The company's own listing credits Steven Wasson as creator and director, with Soum assisting. A second version reached the Edinburgh Festival in 2002, where The Stage nominated it for Best Ensemble Work, and it then toured Israel on a British Council tour, Spain and Ireland."},"theorpheuscomplex":{"blurb":"Orpheus relocated to an asylum: an absurd, dysfunctional \"family\" under the spell of an eccentric alienist, through which twelve mime actors play out the descent to the underworld, the loss of Eurydice and Orpheus's fascination with the figure of Death. The company traces its sources from Ovid to Cocteau rather than to a single text, and credits Wasson as creator and director with Soum assisting. It toured Mainz, Périgueux, Bologna, London and São Paulo between 2004 and 2006."},"passageofmanonearth":{"blurb":"Not a new piece but a seven-actor enlargement of one of Decroux's own: Passage des hommes sur la terre, which he made in 1942, remade in 1962, and which his last assistants re-created in 1997. The company describes it as a symbolist account of mankind's journey through love and war. Wasson directed it, and it went to the London International Mime Festival and the Vlaanderen mime festival in 1998."},"resonance":{"blurb":"Made in 1997 for the centenary of Étienne Decroux's birth: a duet for Soum and Wasson built around La Méditation, his principal solo, spliced with several of his love duets, poetic texts and new material of their own. The company calls it a collage dialectique — an argument with the dead teacher in movement rather than a revival of him. It toured the London International Mime Festival, Brazil, Mexico, Belgium, Italy and Montreal."},"howtolive":{"blurb":"Charnock cut up Ibsen's late plays and scratch-mixed them: scenes interrupt each other and lie on top of one another, and it opens with the four actors building a sparse chamber setting while arguing over four contradictory sets of stage directions, each bidding to define what the hell is going on. The premise is that Ibsen's people are driven by their sexual dreams, so the texts, delivered in melodramatic parody, become pretexts. One of them counts: \"He touched me 576½ times today.\" — 1995"},"downstairsaction":{"blurb":"The lower-floor half of a two-group experiment: Richards and Biagini worked under Grotowski's eye on the ground floor while a second group upstairs, led first by James Slowiak and then by Maud Robart, built its own Upstairs Action. Neither was made for outsiders. Mercedes Gregory filmed the downstairs work in 1989, and that film, released as Art as Vehicle, is most of what survives of it. The group kept refining it until 1992, when it gave way to the piece called simply Action. — 1988"},"actionworkcenter":{"blurb":"Grotowski worked on it with Richards from 1994 until his death, and it was never offered as a spectacle: the montage is made in the doers, not the spectator. Paul Allain, who saw it in 2001, found it left out of the festival programme and shown to twelve invited guests in a brick farmhouse five miles outside town, Biagini introducing it as \"not a theatre event\" before returning with the group to do it. It runs on the resonance of Afro-Caribbean songs, with precise walks and dances between them. — 1994"},"onebreathleft":{"blurb":"A co-production with the Singaporean company Theatre Ox, in residence at the Workcenter — and, unlike Action, avowedly theatrical: texts, music, objects, lighting, costumes, five or so rows of seats and two long streams of cloth making a funnel of a stage. It draws on old Chinese songs and a story from Chinese philosophers, sung high, nasal and atonal instead of in the deep Afro-Caribbean resonance of the Workcenter's other work. It took a special Ubu prize in 1999. — 1998"},"thetwinsaction":{"blurb":"The title under which the action later called The Letter was shown while it was still being made — Richards's first structure of this kind after Grotowski's death. It travelled alongside Action, including to the t:me festival in Ghent, and was still being worked as \"an Action in creation\" at sessions in 2007 before it settled into The Letter. — 2003"},"thebridgedevelopingtheatrearts":{"blurb":"Not a single piece but a frame Biagini built: a bridge between theatre and \"art as vehicle\", so that a work could carry one arrow of intention aimed at the people watching and another at the people doing it. One Breath Left and Dies Iræ were both made inside it. Paul Allain, who saw the results, called it an experiment in directing the audience's attention at some moments and abandoning it at others, and reported that the experiment works. — 1999"},"iamamerica":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"electricpartysongs":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"nothistorysbones":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"keepdancing":{"blurb":"A typist gets waylaid by the act of preparing and finds herself in a loop she cannot get out of. She holds a duologue with a talking computer — wearing a headdress of red feathers, so plainly not just a girl in the typing pool — and the wordplay bats back and forth until the machine grinds down, clogged with repeated phrases and then with single repeated syllables. Behind her, archive film of 1950s bombshells water-skiing. — 2009"},"anatomylessons":{"blurb":"Not an evening of her own but one item on a bill of six. Clod Ensemble asked a handful of artists each to give an anatomy lesson — a performance, a lecture, a demonstration — as part of a season built on teaching medical students through the arts. The poets John Hegley and Malika Booker were on the same bill, and Lois Weaver, as her country-singer alter ego Tammy WhyNot, compered and asked the guests what Tammy needed to know about anatomy. — 2011"},"inhonorofthechild":{"blurb":"1979"},"axismundi":{"blurb":"Fleming begins lying inert in a shallow circle of water; for a long while the only motion is a rumble through the torso, then an arm snakes upward and a leg lifts in a sideways twist, the pool doubling every shape until it stops reading as human. She ends standing upright with bare branches held in both arms and her mouth open in a silent scream. Deborah Jowitt, watching an earlier showing, saw her fall in the water breathing convulsively, gashed and bleeding, a small branch stuck to her rib cage. — 1988"},"wateronthemoon":{"blurb":"Behind a scrim a steep flight of banisterless stairs rises; Fleming, draped in translucent white, unveils and lets her naked body twist and slip down the velvet-covered steps. She then stretches out in a shallow pool to Greek Orthodox chant and stands with branches spiralling up her wrists and a streak of red beneath one breast; at the end, to a Strauss waltz, she cups an egg dangling on a thread and crushes it against her mouth. The loose source is the myth of Psyche and Eros. — 1989"},"erosfleming":{"blurb":"Solos old and new, shared with Yoshito Ohno, Kazuo Ohno's son. Behind a scrim a pale naked woman swims head first on her back down a black velvet staircase while a distant soprano sings from Faure's Requiem, and near the bottom red petals spew from her mouth. Ohno, in trailing flesh-coloured trousers, steps into the shallow pool so that his reflection throws a dark void onto the backcloth, then pours water from a pail into it. Deborah Jowitt found Fleming's images \"beautiful, cruel\". — 1991"},"spherefleming":{"blurb":"Discrete short solos, some new, some old. It opens with Fleming nude and high in the air, leaning forward at an angle that looks impossible until her body almost closes a circle; between sections, circular films show her face dissolving under a wind-blown veil. She walks on in a conservative suit and tie, stands motionless on her head in it, spins a metal rod until it reads as a sphere, and falls headfirst down a long staircase so slowly that the fall becomes the dance. — 1993"},"ilparadiso":{"blurb":"1994"},"aftereros":{"blurb":"A suite of solos made into a full evening. The fall down a flight of stairs is stretched to nine minutes; message boards flank the stage with David Henry Hwang's text - \"Because I love Eros I labor\" - which jumps from the Psyche myth to Serbia, Bosnia and Palestine. The title points at the man on a bicycle who cut in front of her mother's car in Japan when Fleming was two, laughed and rode away; by her own account the spinal injury from that crash is where her slow twisting began. — 1996"},"dappi":{"blurb":"1998"},"ikiyoshioka":{"blurb":"Yoshioka spends the piece inside a transparent plastic installation by Joachim Manger - a bed, a couch and tubing, all see-through - pressing and rolling through its inflating channels with a pocketknife tucked away in case she suffocates. Sondra Fraleigh, watching a showing at Schloss Broellin, describes her entering in baggy white trousers and black braces, trying out the bed as it inflates around her, then feeling her way along a closed tube like a nearsighted person towards the last tent of air. — 1999"},"beforethedawn":{"blurb":"2000"},"testlaborz005":{"blurb":"2001"},"orpheuslabyrinth":{"blurb":"Not a stage work but a site-specific improvisation made with the Italian performer Annalisa Maggiani and played outdoors - one Berlin edition took place under the Koenigskolonnaden in the Heinrich-von-Kleist-Park. The two dance a journey into the shadow world against Mario Morleo's moving images and live music; the group's own description sets the Italian street-theatre tradition beside performance art and supplies butoh as the third element. Yoshioka's site still lists the project as ongoing. — 2006"},"ketsui":{"blurb":"The stage is a platform about three feet high, laid with artificial turf, studded with turf-covered balls and bored through with holes some two feet across; the dancers surface out of the holes limb by limb - a hand, an arm, feet, a pelvis - before hauling themselves clear. A giant padded golf club hangs over the middle and is swung at them like a pendulum. The title means the decision, and the club stands for the manipulation that sets a process running once a choice has been made. — 2008"},"unmei":{"blurb":"A solo Yoshioka made for herself, inside an installation and lighting by Joachim Manger, shown as the last of four pieces in the group's fifteenth-anniversary run. Its subject is the destiny a person shapes rather than receives, and money as the visible token of the winning and losing that goes with it; the company's own text promises a \"body-dance-desire-machine\" running an absurd game of greed, dominance, eroticism and control. No account of the staging itself has been located. — 2010"},"konton":{"blurb":"Eight dancers from eight countries inside an installation by the sculptor Joachim Manger, with a score by Zam Johnson and video art by Yoann Trellu. The fullest surviving account is the company's own: chaos as the gap that opens in an order already named, \"the mother of creativity\", and the performance as an attempt to use that gap as free space out of which a future can be generated. What the eight bodies actually did in it is not documented in any review located. — 2013"},"rokakunitsubasa":{"blurb":"The performance Waguri founded his company on, and Hijikata directed and choreographed it. Waguri walked out of Asbestos-kan straight afterwards and spent the next eight years as an Edo komon stencil dyer before coming back to dancing. He returned to the title three more times over the following twelve years, each time with a subtitle attached. — 1978"},"ningennomizu":{"blurb":"The fourth part of the Rokaku ni Tsubasa cycle Waguri had begun twelve years earlier with a piece Hijikata directed; this one adds the characters for human and water to the series title. He was still giving it nine years later at a municipal theatre in Shonandai. The chronology his archive publishes carries the sequence and the rooms but nothing of the stage. — 1990"},"aoihashira":{"blurb":"Waguri worked here with the membrane sculpture of Yoshie Shozo, in Studio 200 — the Seibu department-store space where Hijikata had staged the last two parts of his Tohoku Kabuki cycle six years before, with Waguri dancing in both. It is the first piece his archive bills under his own name as well as the company's. — 1991"},"bekkoame":{"blurb":"Waguri took his company back into Asbestos-kan, Hijikata's own studio, and billed the evening as a Hijikata Method Study Group performance — a show framed as research into what their teacher had left, five years after his death. Two of these were given in the space of seven months; this was the first. — 1991"},"natsukashinoshichigatsu":{"blurb":"The second of the two evenings Waguri's company gave at Asbestos-kan under the Hijikata Method Study Group banner, seven months after the first — and the last thing the chronology places in Hijikata's studio for a year. What was actually danced, no source read here records. — 1992"},"jitsugetsutan":{"blurb":"Waguri's company in a Shibuya hall, to a score by Yabuki Shiho and Muramatsu Yasumune, seven months after the second of the Hijikata method evenings at Asbestos-kan. His archive lists the composers and the room and describes nothing further; by that June the company was back in Hijikata's studio again. — 1993"},"nonokonrei":{"blurb":"Waguri's company with a score by YAS-KAZ, the percussionist and composer who also wrote for Sankai Juku. It opened the busiest stretch in the chronology his archive publishes: four separate company programmes inside nine months, each with a different collaborator credited. — 1994"},"gensonochishigaku":{"blurb":"Adapted from a text by the aesthetician Tanigawa Atsushi and carrying the subtitle 'the distance to the body'. It arrived in the same season as Butoh Kaden, the CD-ROM in which Waguri published Hijikata's butoh-fu — the notation of images the choreography was built from — for the first time. Six months later he took the piece to the Art Summit in Jakarta; a second part followed in 2002. — 1998"},"charila":{"blurb":"SU-EN's programme note takes the piece from an ancient Greek rite in which dolls called Charila were buried in the earth and turned into goddesses, then turns it on the audience: \"What do we bury in our soil?\" She calls it a ritual anticipating the outbreak of spring - \"bones clothed in blossom\". She danced it as a solo, in a costume and a stage space of her own making, to music by Rob Angus mixed with recorded nature; later versions added other dancers. — 1995"},"scrapbodies":{"blurb":"A solo premiered inside a building at a metal scrapyard, and the clearest case of Ashikawa's instruction to be near the real thing. Its programme notes call it a stillborn beauty germinating from twisted metal, mountains of unwanted car engines and barrels of magnesium spiral cut-offs — a graveyard of matter giving birth to a choreography of infinity. The collaboration with the yard outlived the piece, producing a dance video and live events performed with trucks and scrap-metal workers. — 1998"},"slice":{"blurb":"A solo in which the body is already part of death, developed in performance-art contexts with real fish guts on stage and a large shark hook dangling in the space; the final stage version added an art video of the body immersed in the guts. Its programme notes read like instructions — die whilst alive, dissection of the flesh, an incision, one more, the dance of the ribs, devoured to the bone, a visceral walk. — 2003"},"cracks":{"blurb":"An ensemble piece playing classical butoh against burlesque convention: five dancers as enigmas of the desert in a harsh space abundant with watermelons, with a solo, Scratch dance, ending in a cleansing ritual using saliva. A different chapter of the same book files it instead among SU-EN's site dances, bodies emerging from the snaggy harsh beauty of the Swedish landscape only to be showered in brightly coloured flowers. The two accounts are hard to read as the same piece. — 2008"},"soot":{"blurb":"A turn away from the lush ensemble works before it into a sparse, harsh reduction of line: a solo merging a more classical butoh style with a political view of the body as a vessel for activism, and aimed at the way human beings disrupt nature's balance. Its programme notes ask what remains when pain leaves, and when civilisation falls asleep. — 2013"},"togetherwithhijikata":{"blurb":"1992"},"yoshitodanceexperience":{"blurb":"Yoshito Ohno had danced the boy in Kinjiki at twenty, opposite Hijikata, in the duet usually counted as the first butoh, and spent the sixties inside Hijikata's company. This recital under his own name ended that: afterwards he stopped performing in public, took over a drug store in Yokohama, and later worked as personal assistant to the novelist Jiro Osaragi's wife. He did not appear on a stage again for fifteen years. — 1969"},"lastportraitdoriangray":{"blurb":"Built not from a dance idea but from a text — the script the kabuki critic and butoh scholar Gunji Masakatsu left behind. It was Yoshito Ohno's first billing as a soloist since the 1969 recital after which he had stopped performing altogether, and it came a year after his mother died. Fraleigh gives the title as The Last Picture of Dorian Gray; the studio's own chronology says Portrait. — 1998"},"flowerandbird":{"blurb":"Yoshito Ohno was seventy-five when he made this solo, three years after his father died. He went on touring it for the rest of the decade — Munich, Bologna, Barcelona, Poland, China — and the subtitle drifted as he went, from A Letter to My Future Self to Butoh, A Way of Life, which is also what he called his book. The studio's chronology lists no new work of his after it; he died in Yokohama in January 2020. — 2013"},"zarathoustraikeda":{"blurb":"Murobushi's representative choreography for the company: eight tableaux of oneiric and hallucinatory scenes turning on the ambiguity between dream, illusion and reality, madness and violence, reworking the myth of Ariadne, Dionysus and the Bacchantes. At the Tokyo premiere a gigantic iron Minotaur is decapitated and the female dancers emerge from behind the dead man-bull as Nijinsky's fauns. The dancers descend holding Ariadne's endless thread — for Murobushi an intercultural way of acquiring knowledge rather than an exotic one. — 1980"},"utt":{"blurb":"Ikeda's first solo, after seven years leading her company, subtitled 'Carlotta Ikeda's everything'. Ko Murobushi choreographed and produced it while hiding the fact — he listed himself on the flyer as a musician and wrote the programme texts as though she had written them — and commissioned Eikoh Hosoe, who had photographed Hijikata for Kamaitachi, to shoot it. A live band covered the costume changes; at the end the whole company came on to hand her flowers. — 1981"},"aiamour":{"blurb":"A duo with Ko Murobushi that opens with the two of them standing side by side, her head resting lightly on his shoulder, and stays there a long while before they begin to swing and tangle. The archive reads the title three ways at once: the French avoir, the Japanese ai for love, and itai, the word for pain. Murobushi bends a knee into a slovenly shape, then straightens as though he were an object of worship. — 1993"},"waitingikeda":{"blurb":"A solo built as a handful of short tableaux, made during a residency. Reviewing it in Paris two years later, Libération wrote that Ikeda seems to sculpt the void in it and to palpate desire; the Numeridanse notice says the piece teaches an audience to stop again, and gives back the taste for watching and the pleasure of waiting. — 1996-04-20"},"harunosaiten":{"blurb":"Murobushi and Ikeda choreographed it together for seven women: Ikeda, one other Japanese dancer, and one each from Germany, Italy, Spain, Lithuania and France. They refused every earlier Rite — not Nijinsky's choreography, and, Murobushi wrote, not one glance at the European variations from Wigman through Béjart to Bausch. What they kept was Stravinsky, cut up by computer into something more metallic and driven into the dancers' bodies. All ten performances sold out. — 1999"},"uchuucabaret":{"blurb":"Ikeda gave up butoh's darkness here and played monstrosity as farce: a cabaret of short numbers with nothing linking them, danced by an all-female cast made up, costumed, and at points harnessed three metres above the stage. One reviewer described a waking dream that keeps tipping into a Freaks-version nightmare, and complained that the systematic construction left the dancers no real room to move. — 2008"},"medeaikeda":{"blurb":"Pascal Quignard read his unpublished text aloud as a prologue, and one critic called what followed a solo for two. Ikeda comes out of the dark slow and hieratic, playing the water-sleeves of her costume against pliés of impossible slowness, beneath a suspended sun whose clock hands have stopped. Her Medea holds the rage entirely inside — a volcano of ice, he wrote — until at the end she strips the costume off like dead skin and stands straight as a torch. She was seventy. — 2011"},"seraphita":{"blurb":"The piece Kasai came back with. He had closed his Tokyo studio in 1979, spent years in Stuttgart studying Steiner's eurythmy, and on returning to Japan found he could not dance — too disconnected, he said, from the people around him to perform at all. He calls it his first socially active work, and rebuilt Tenshi-kan around it, running now on eurythmy as much as butoh. How long the silence lasted the sources dispute by about four years. — 1994-01"},"myownapocalypse":{"blurb":"Kasai's first appearance at the San Francisco Butoh Festival, and it went badly: another butoh artist heckled him from the audience in Japanese — reportedly 'You're not doing butoh, you need to stop' — and he answered, 'How dare you say that. This is my dance. This is my life.' That exchange is what the piece is remembered for. His fast, non-image-based approach has had his work denied the name butoh more than once. — 1994"},"workexusiai":{"blurb":"Kasai's first work made with Western dancers, and the piece Pollen Revolution grew out of. He gave them a proposition instead of steps — the organic forces in minerals and planets set against the inorganic energy of machines, the friction between them ending in apocalypse — then left them to work it out in group improvisations for months, handing over choreographic forms only shortly before the show. For Kasai choreography arrives from outside and shocks the dancer into moving, so it cannot come early. — 1998"},"butohamerica":{"blurb":"Kasai made it for himself and five dancers trained in American contemporary dance and ballet, and pointed it at present-day America — a Japanese choreographer dancing in America about America, which Fraleigh notes runs the anthropological traffic backwards. She describes him moving like a rock star among technically sharp bodies. The New York Times found the scenes 'failed to build a tangible portrait of \"Butoh America\" — whatever that is'. — 2007"},"hayasasurahime":{"blurb":"Maro Akaji and Kasai were both born in 1943 and both came out of the sixties butoh scene, and this was the first time they had shared a stage. They took Japan's foundation myth as material — the other gods throwing a party to lure the sun goddess Amaterasu out of her cave so the world can begin — and put butoh, modern dance and eurythmy on one stage together. Two years later it went to Mexico with an all-female cast except for the goddess of the title, danced by Kasai's son Mitsutake. — 2012"},"spielyugi":{"blurb":"A duet with the French choreographer Emmanuelle Huynh, played in a high black box with the audience on three sides. What Kasai wrote home about was not the dancing but the talking: Huynh wanted a long discussion about what the performance meant in the minutes before going on, which he said dancers in Japan essentially never do. He put the difference down to primal feelings — shame for the Japanese, anxiety for the French. — 2013"},"juninohikari":{"blurb":"Not one performance but twelve: through that year a different piece was given at the studio every month under this title, with Koichi Tamano among the guests. Ko Murobushi danced solos in it — one with his whole body gold-powdered, taken from Leni Riefenstahl's Nuba photographs, another painted black after the back of a Lou Reed sleeve. Partway through the run his apartment burned down while he slept; the one thing he got out was his copy of Kamaitachi. — 1980"},"kochuten":{"blurb":"Not one work but a company inside the company, named after the small Tokyo basement Dairakudakan uses as office, workshop, rehearsal room and performance venue. Emerging members choreograph and perform there under Maro's guidance in order to develop styles of their own — an extension of his one dancer, one company principle. The first piece was Mukai Kumotaro's Paradise in a Jar Odyssey 2001. Maro calls what happens in the series post-post-post-butoh dance. — 2001"},"subjectmintanaka":{"blurb":"Tanaka walked out of a modern-dance career and began dancing naked in streets, parks, subway stations and galleries — head and body shaved, nothing on but an elastic bandage over his genitals, moving at bisoku, extreme slowness. He kept it up for about seven years, three or four times a day, lowering himself into full contact with ground that people had walked and spat on. He was arrested often; the bandage and the shaved hair kept charges from sticking. — 1974"},"hyperdance1824":{"blurb":"Hyperdance was one of several names Tanaka gave the naked, site-specific practice he ran through the second half of the 1970s, and Fuller groups it with Drive and the MMD performances as exercises in pure movement that refused emotional, figurative or narrative expression. That December he danced on Yume-no-shima, a garbage-dump island in Tokyo Bay, among broken glass and metal and at times covered in flies. What the 1824 hours of the title counts, no source read here establishes. — 1977"},"emotionmintanaka":{"blurb":"A deliberate break: where Hyperdance, Drive and the MMD performances with Milford Graves and Derek Bailey had been exercises in pure movement that refused emotional, figurative or narrative expression, this was built on intense emotional expression, physical action and material taken from daily life. Fuller places the start of the series just before Tanaka's first contact with Hijikata, and a photograph in his chapter catches it at La MaMa in New York the following year. — 1982"},"genzainoodori":{"blurb":"1982"},"renaibutohha":{"blurb":"A solo Hijikata directed for Tanaka, part of the run of work Tanaka helped him produce at Plan B after five relatively inactive years. The collaboration astonished the butoh world, since Tanaka was widely seen — in his own words — as being against butoh, and has spent a career objecting to the idea that butoh is a genre at all. Two years later he declared himself the legitimate son of Hijikata. — 1984"},"riteofspringtanaka":{"blurb":"One of Tanaka's group works, and so more precisely composed and rehearsed than his solos, which are wholly improvised. It was restructured, often radically, each time it was performed in a different place — Tanaka holds that his conception of dance has no room for the finished piece, because for him a dance is never finished. This is the version featured in the film Butoh: Body on the Edge of Crisis. — 1990"},"swamptamano":{"blurb":"1979"},"fetusofnature":{"blurb":"1988"},"peaceonearthtamano":{"blurb":"Three nights, three years after Koichi and Hiroko Tamano moved the company from Tokyo to Berkeley. What survives is a set of photographs in a San Francisco archive, and the archive will not say who is in them: it lists seven dancers who may be in the frames and declines to match any to a body. Wikipedia's list of Tamano's work gives the title as Piece on Earth and dates it a year earlier. — 1990-03-02"},"wingsofacenturylife":{"blurb":"1990"},"wingsofacenturydeath":{"blurb":"1991"},"mandalove":{"blurb":"1992"},"shiokubi":{"blurb":"Salted heads — dead heads preserved with salt — a title Yamada chose for its somatic sensation, having found every dish too salty when he first arrived in Tsuruoka. His job was less to choreograph than to build a vessel for dancers who were mostly their own bosses: a large coffin, and big round holes edged with straw mats cut into a raised clay stage, through which performers rose and vanished. Shaved heads in thick kabuki makeup, eyes blazingly wide, floated up out of them in blue light. — 1975"},"gekkanoune":{"blurb":"Made in Otaru, where Yamada had moved his company in 1976 and kept a tavern-studio inside a red-brick building of 1906, with a small theatre nearby. It belongs to a run of pieces built out of the Hokkaido climate — a princess who smells of fish, a study of Atka mackerel — on his conviction that butoh's roots lay in the indigenous north. Soon after, he gave the experiment up, concluding butoh could not be expanded outside the cities, and went back to Tokyo. — 1982"},"ezomen":{"blurb":"Linear logic vanished; there was no plot, though plenty of mood, and energy and gesture were brutally economised. Shinichi Iova-Koga, who saw Yuki Yuko's Suzuran-toh dance it in San Francisco in 1991, remembered a man under a thick quilt seeming to sleep as he drifted across the stage centimetres above the ground on a thin mist, a woman taking a small eternity to rise from a squat to standing while loud driving music vibrated the walls, and two women in mock sumo. — 1989"},"kalpaoguri":{"blurb":"2012-01"},"radha":{"blurb":"A goddess wakes in her niche in the temple and takes the five senses one at a time — jewels for sight, bells for hearing, garlands for smell, a bowl of wine for taste, and for touch she kisses her own hands. Then a square figure of fourfold misery, and finally a lotus figure danced on the balls of the feet for the joy that follows renunciation. St Denis had never been to India: she took the material from a troupe of performers at Coney Island and a fortnight in the Astor Library, and set it to Delibes. — 1906-03-22"},"incense":{"blurb":"A high-caste woman in a smoke-grey sari comes through the curtains carrying a tray of incense, sets it on the flames, and then becomes the smoke herself — arms rippling higher and higher, then descending like trailing wisps — and goes back through the curtains as quietly as she came. Ted Shawn saw it in Denver in 1911, wept in a crowded theatre, and dated his own artistic birth from that night. — 1906-03-22"},"thecobras":{"blurb":"A bazaar near the Ganges. St Denis, in a ragged reddish-brown dress and a brown turban, sits cross-legged on a small platform; huge emerald rings on the index and little fingers turn her two hands into cobra heads, and the snakes coil, hiss, intertwine and strike while she plays the charmer holding them. Shawn wrote that the whole dance was also in her face, and that hardly a cartoonist in America or Europe failed to have a try at it. — 1906-03-22"},"egypta":{"blurb":"Five scenes — the Invocation to the Nile, the Palace Dance, the Veil of Isis, the Dance of Day and the Dance of Night — with movement falling constantly into the hieroglyphic attitudes of tomb painting. St Denis had the idea in 1904, before Radha, and shelved it because staging it would cost three or four thousand dollars; Shawn's verdict was that she could have afforded it and simply lacked the faith. When it finally went on she had six weeks to mount five scenes each as elaborate as an opera set. — 1910-12-12"},"soaring":{"blurb":"Five dancers and one large silk scarf, to Schumann's Aufschwung. The scarf is the point: it becomes a wave, a flower unfolding, a stormy sky, and, as the notation score puts it, 'has a life of its own to which the dancers must respond'. A music visualisation with no story. The young Doris Humphrey shares the choreographic credit and danced in the first cast. — 1920"},"liebestraum":{"blurb":"1922"},"deathofadonis":{"blurb":"Shawn stands on a plinth, nude but for a fig leaf, painted white head to foot and wearing a pale wig so that he reads as marble. The statue begins visibly to breathe, then moves in slow motion through thirty-two poses taken from antique sculpture — a hunt, and then the god's death by the boar — to Godard's Adagio pathétique. He had promised in print that audiences could take it: 'to see the naked body of one who is healthy, strong, symmetrical and of noble proportion is to experience a sense of divine revelation.' — 1923"},"cosmicdanceofsiva":{"blurb":"Shawn as Shiva Nataraja, working from temple sculpture and painting: slow controlled poses of the god's creation, preservation and destruction, danced on a platform inside a life-sized ring of fire. In the final section he lights incense so that plumes of smoke rise around him, invoking Shiva's favourite dancing ground — the burning ghats of the Ganges, where the dead are cremated. Lily Strickland, an American composer then living in India, wrote the score. — 1926"},"thedivineidiot":{"blurb":"Shawn's dance of Plato's allegory of the cave: he plays the philosopher who returns to tell the chained prisoners about a reality beyond their comprehension and is undone by the attempt. It is one of several works in which he took what he called his Credo character — the visionary punished for living on a more enlightened plane. Danced to Bach. — 1930"},"laborsymphony":{"blurb":"Four kinds of work — in the fields, the forests, the sea, and finally the factory. The last section is a machine dance in which the men move as pistons, cogs and gears, and at one point become a living telegraph: their lower bodies beat the machine's pulse in stiff sideways strides while their sleeved arms slide across bare chests to spew dots and dashes. Shawn had picked his Men Dancers out of the athletes he taught at Springfield College. — 1934"},"kineticmolpai":{"blurb":"Twelve sections for eight bare-chested men to a Jess Meeker score — Strife, Oppositions, Solvent, Dynamic Contrasts, Resilience, Successions, Unfolding, Folding, Dirge, Limbo, Surge, Apotheosis — with no story in them, only what Ailey's repertory note calls a ritual of patterns, energy and ensemble work stressing male prowess. Shawn later reused the whole thing as the third act of O, Libertad! Alvin Ailey revived it in 1972, staged by Barton Mumaw, one of the original Men Dancers. — 1935"},"olympiad":{"blurb":"A suite of sports dances: a banner-bearer entering the Olympic stadium with the five linked rings, three cheerleaders, a decathlon in which one man does all ten events, fencing without foils or masks, a boxer training and then winning a bout, and finally basketball. Most of it was made by the dancers themselves, each taking the sport he had excelled at in college. It ends with the whole company in Mobilization for Peace. — 1937"},"olibertad":{"blurb":"Three acts of American history taken from Whitman. The past runs from Moctezuma's last night through the Penitentes and the Forty-Niners; the present goes from a 1914 college campus into No Man's Land and out into a Jazz Decade danced by eight masked figures; the future is the whole of Kinetic Molpai. In the Depression solo Shawn appears as a masked hag in a cloak printed with a plunging stock graph — a hostile parody of Wigman's Witch Dance and Graham's Lamentation, and of two women who had criticised him. — 1937"},"danceoftheages":{"blurb":"Four movements, one for each element — fire, earth, water, air — built out of Edward Carpenter's Toward Democracy, with Shawn appearing in each as a leader coaxing his men towards what he called a life of greater dimension. The sections run from a primitive hunting dance and a shaman through vegetation and animal studies, a village craftsman and a political machine, to a whirlwind and a finale approaching a fifth element. — 1938-09-09"},"samayoerugunzo":{"blurb":"Ishii had been sacked by the house in his twenties for fighting back against Giovanni Vittorio Rosi, the Italian mime master it had brought over from London to drill its opera department. He came back to that stage with this large group work more than thirty years later, choreographed to a score by Ifukube Akira — the first of the late works with that composer, of which Ningen Shaka five years on is generally called his best. — 1948"},"dustbowlballads":{"blurb":"Two Guthrie songs, not the whole album: 'I Ain't Got No Home in This World Anymore' and 'Dusty Old Dust', danced as the songs of migratory workers, in costumes by Edythe Gilfond. Guthrie himself was billed on the New Dance Group concerts that carried it, and was by then courting a Graham dancer, Marjorie Mazia, whom he married in 1945. — 1941"},"folksay":{"blurb":"Carl Sandburg's The People, Yes spoken by Earl Robinson while Woody Guthrie sang the ballads live on stage, his guitar lettered against the war in Europe: 'This guitar Kills Fascists.' The dances run through 'Where're You From, Stranger', 'On Top of Old Smoky', 'Aw Nuts!', 'Sweet Betsy from Pike' and 'Hey You, Sun, Moon, Stars' to a finale. Edwin Denby said the audience took it 'as the reflection of a lovely summer day, and that is what it really is'. — 1942-03-10"},"champion":{"blurb":"1948"},"thedybbukmaslow":{"blurb":"Not a Maslow dance but a job inside someone else's work — the choreography for David Tamkin's opera of Ansky's Yiddish play, a score written in the 1930s that waited almost twenty years for a staging. Maslow made her own Dybbuk later, for a Hanukkah festival and then for the Harkness Ballet in 1965. This is the earlier, borrowed one. — 1951"},"poemmaslow":{"blurb":"Five dancers, one live poet and one live drummer. Maslow set it to Duke Ellington and Billy Strayhorn's Such Sweet Thunder with a Lawrence Ferlinghetti poem spoken over the top; the score's supplementary papers include the poet's text and the book it came from, These Are My Rivers. The notation was left incomplete and the catalogue rules it unstageable without a coach. — 1963-03-09"},"neitherrestnorharbor":{"blurb":"Batsheva took it into repertory in Israel in 1969, in the season Jane Dudley directed the company, with Rina Schenfeld leading, a score by Robert Starer and a set by Bernard Johnson. The archive's performance count for that staging is three. — 1964"},"prologuemaslow":{"blurb":"Seven dancers. The introduction to the notated score gives the entire subject in one sentence — 'Set in the early Renaissance period, this ballet is about the city of Florence, when they had plague and cholera' — and records neither a premiere date nor an original cast to go with it. — 1976"},"timeismoney":{"blurb":"Danced without music. Someone in the wings speaks Sol Funaroff's communist poem — 'Tick-tock. Skeleton crews in speed-up shifts clang their iron deaths and spin a wealth of misery' — and the dancer works against its rhythm rather than with it, held and shackled, repeating small assembly-line gestures, until at the end she turns and confronts the audience directly. Dudley built it to work in a union hall or on a concert stage. Restaging it in 1993 she cast a man, and decided it went better that way. — 1934"},"undertheswastika":{"blurb":"1937"},"shortstorydudley":{"blurb":"A trio for three women to a Paul Creston score, with costumes by Charlotte Trowbridge and an epigraph printed on the programme, from the poet David Wolff: 'But each gets punished for his open face / And turns back into himself again.' Nothing else about it has survived in the public record — no review, no film, no notation. — 1942"},"newworldacomin":{"blurb":"1945"},"thelonelyones":{"blurb":"Built on three drawings from William Steig's cartoon book of the same name, and made by Dudley with Maslow and Bales — the side of the Dudley-Maslow-Bales Trio that put its humour on stage rather than its politics. John Martin reviewed it as an effective piece. Which three cartoons, and what the score was, has not survived in the public record. — 1946"},"islanddudley":{"blurb":"1988"},"themeandvariations":{"blurb":"What the Dance Notation Bureau has is an excerpt one minute long: nine pages, rated elementary, taken down by Catharine Hees in 1955, fourteen years after the fact, for ten dancers to Ray Green's music, and marked a student project. The record does not agree with itself. The Bureau dates the dance 1941 and counts six women and four men; the Library of Congress chronology of O'Donnell puts it in 1945 and calls it a solo. — 1941"},"suspension":{"blurb":"Seven dancers move slowly around two boxes beneath a hanging mobile by the sculptor Claire Falkenstein, sometimes touching, mostly alone; the whole thing has the quality of the mobile itself, turning without going anywhere. O'Donnell said it came from standing on a Californian hilltop in wartime and watching a plane pass below her. She put Eliot in the programme note — 'At the still point of the turning world... there the dance is.' Ray Green, her husband, wrote the score. — 1943"},"thequeensobsession":{"blurb":"1952"},"dancesonata":{"blurb":"Sonata form taken literally: 'Dance movement as thematic idea is used throughout the work in much the same way that musical themes are used in a Beethoven or Mozart Sonata,' says the programme note. Six dancers, twelve minutes, to Ray Green's Dance Sonata for two pianos. The first of the two O'Donnell made under this title was dedicated to Gertrude Shurr, with whom she ran her studio. — 1952"},"danceconcerto":{"blurb":"1954"},"homagetoshiva":{"blurb":"1980"},"icaro":{"blurb":"King had danced in the Humphrey-Weidman company since its founding in 1928; this was the first large-scale work of her own. It takes Lauro de Bosis's poem on the fall of Icarus, with a score by David Diamond and the percussionist Franczeska Boas, and Jack Cole in the title role. — 1937"},"songaboutamerica":{"blurb":"1939"},"roadstohell":{"blurb":"Four deadly sins, four solos, all danced by King alone in a single sitting to a Genevieve Pitot score. Pride was Mussolini and grew steadily more flamboyant. Envy was political mud-slinging, punctuated with audible hisses. Sloth — a funny, lazy lump of a figure — was the United States refusing to join the Allies. Wrath opened with the hands making the Chinese ideogram for rage, then stamped fanatically. Each goes, in the words of a dancer King later coached, 'from a position of power into a disintegrating impotence'. — 1941"},"tenantofthestreet":{"blurb":"A woman on the street takes wary measure of a city that has already beaten her — guarded, shuffling, angular movement, and a stare doing most of the work. Gentry built it out of Käthe Kollwitz's drawings: 'I never made a dance without looking at this,' she told a visitor late in her life, showing her the Kollwitz book. She made it inside the New Dance Group, whose early slogan was 'The dance is a weapon in the class struggle'. — 1938"},"crynirenska":{"blurb":"It took first prize for choreography and second for performance at the international competition in Vienna, and launched the solo career that carried Nirenska across Europe — and she never danced it again. Half a century later, making a new solo called Shout, she told a friend that it had a feeling like Cry's, but that she no longer remembered a step of the original. — 1934"},"ascarecrowremembers":{"blurb":"1946"},"thedividedself":{"blurb":"1981"},"dirgenirenska":{"blurb":"1981"},"thetiredmagician":{"blurb":"1984"},"shoutnirenska":{"blurb":"1987"},"outofsorts":{"blurb":"1988"},"inmemoryofthoseiloved":{"blurb":"1990-07-28"},"kaleidoscope":{"blurb":"Four prop dances — Discs, Pole, Straps and Clothes — in which the object does as much of the dancing as the body. In Discs the dancers wear coloured aluminium discs on one foot, extending the base and range of balance and adding vertical lift, with shape, colour and material supplying both the visual and the aural material. Nikolais made the tape score himself, one of eleven he spliced together out of recorded noise before the synthesizers arrived. — 1956-05-25"},"imago":{"blurb":"Three spotlights set in the footlight trough threw panels of colour onto the cyclorama — shadows in one dance, moving colour designs in another, a strobe effect made by the dancers' own motion in a third. When the piece moved to Lincoln Center the new house could not supply those three instruments and had to borrow them from Henry Street. Nikolais built the score at the Columbia-Princeton lab with James Seawright, and it was backstage here that Robert Moog introduced himself. — 1963-02-24"},"vaudevilleoftheelements":{"blurb":"Commissioned by the Walker Art Center and designed for the thrust stage of the Guthrie, this was the evening in which Nikolais pushed blacklight furthest and built two dances lit entirely by lamps the performers carried and switched on themselves — hand-held dowels topped with coloured reflectors, and reflective tubes worn on arms and legs. Its third act, Tower, ends with flash pots, and is the section still revived on its own. — 1965-12-10"},"gallerynikolais":{"blurb":"The title means a shooting gallery at a fairground. The dancers duck behind a wall and vanish, then pop up as a row of green-lit heads with glowing red eyes — black unitards and hoods make the rest of them invisible — and when the music goes bang the picture changes. Sometimes they are not heads at all but green disks with red centres, or black-and-white pinwheels held up on cue. The audience is put in the position of the shooters; by the end the disks and the masks appear to have been shot through. — 1978-04-19"},"crucible":{"blurb":"A slanted mirror wall, nearly the width of the stage, hides the dancers' legs and doubles their upper bodies. Arms come over the top and acquire reflections joined at the armpit, so you watch two-handed worms, or legs with feet at both ends; later, buttocks surface like hippos in a pond. The performers wear only flesh-coloured jockstraps and no camera trick is involved — the whole illusion is the mirror and where a body puts itself behind it. — 1985-06-13"},"bachsuitelouis":{"blurb":"No story at all. Louis's instructions, recorded in the introduction to the notation score, are that it 'should be more done than danced', that the performer should 'be there', and that the dancing should go against the music: 'Don't fall into Bach's methodical beat, work against it.' It runs on drop and lift and swing, using the weight but always with bearing — and on one further quality he insisted on. 'It should be performed with lust in a very dignified way.' — 1956-11-11"},"calligraphformartyrs":{"blurb":"1961-04-29"},"junkdances":{"blurb":"1964-11-27"},"chimeralouis":{"blurb":"A twenty-minute solo, at a point when the length was itself the provocation. Ruth Grauert lists it among the things the critics' edicts said a choreographer must not do, alongside Louis's use of classical music and the four minutes of pure trembling he had put in Incredible Garden. She also takes the title as the key to his whole manner: a shape whose visible form changes while you look at it. — 1966-02-25"},"proximities":{"blurb":"Six dancers to Brahms's Serenade in A, op. 16 — a classical score at a moment when modern dance was expected to commission its own. The introduction to the notated score gives the whole premise in a line: a dance 'choreographed with the idea of proximity or nearness.' Grauert's summary of what Louis did with it is that, under the musical auspices of Brahms, he winked at the audience. — 1969-01-30"},"hoopla":{"blurb":"The furthest Louis took the clown figure that runs through his work from Little Man onward. Before the crowd, Grauert writes, the gregarious loner gives his galloping good fun to one and all; left alone, the same clown tenderly reveals dreams of starring beauty, and a duet then sets the two halves of a clown's ambition against each other. — 1972-01-26"},"porcelaindialogues":{"blurb":"1974-02-19"},"canarsievenus":{"blurb":"Louis made it for his own company and for Rudolf Nureyev, who danced in the first cast of ten; the music is Cole Porter, arranged by William Bolcom. Grauert calls it the last refinement of the Little Man figure he had been working since 1953 — pedestrian gesture and naive self-preening at the start, the wondering hurt of everyman at the end. — 1978-04-11"},"icarushoving":{"blurb":"A trio for two men and a woman, first danced by Hoving with Chase Robinson and Pat Christopher to a score by Shi-chi Matsushita. The introduction to the notated score states the argument and nothing else: Daedalus, imprisoned with his son on the isle of Minos, devised wings to escape, and 'Icarus flew too close to the sun and his wings were burnt, causing him to fall to his death.' Ray Cook notated it in 1977, which is the reason it can still be put on a stage. — 1963-04-05"},"shesleavinghome":{"blurb":"Hoving made it not on his own company but on students, setting nine dancers to a tape collage he assembled himself. The notation catalogue records the score, the auditorium and the arranged tape, and leaves the original cast list empty — which is most of what is now known about the piece. — 1969-02-27"},"assemblagehoving":{"blurb":"Sixteen dancers, a university-scale cast Hoving never commanded in his own company, moving to a collage of taped and live sounds he arranged with Eileen Cohen. Like She's Leaving Home the year before it was made on the Ohio State dance company, and it survives because it was written down there in Labanotation. — 1970-03-05"},"suiteforasummerday":{"blurb":"No year is recorded here because the notation catalogue records none either: the surviving score covers a single section, the Girl's Dance, for three dancers, with no premiere date, no venue and no cast. What it does name is the music — Peter Schickele's Suite for a Summer's Day, written under his P. D. Q. Bach alias."},"cassandrakoner":{"blurb":"A solo to Copland: the prophetess nobody believes. Koner made it in the year the congressional investigating committees were at their height — one of them called Jerome Robbins to testify — and she has said that Cassandra 'represents truth staring people in the face, and people still don't see it'. Eleven minutes, alone on the stage. — 1953"},"concertinokoner":{"blurb":"Its full title is Concertino in A Major: a trio for three women to Pergolesi, with no programme and no story, and yet not simply a visualisation of the score either. Margaret Lloyd thought the three could have been 'ladies from some remote court playing at being shepherdesses'. Koner danced it herself with Lucy Venable and Elizabeth Harris. — 1955-08-20"},"thefarewell":{"blurb":"1962"},"solitarysongs":{"blurb":"1975"},"theantagonists":{"blurb":"A duet for Currier and Betty Jones to Stravinsky, in costumes by Lavina Nielsen. Walter Terry called it 'a powerful slice of theater, employing movements of great physical strength almost shocking' in effect; the NYPL's survey of Stravinsky stagings recorded that the conflict was never defined but the emotional projection was unmistakable. — 1955-08-20"},"quartetcurrier":{"blurb":"It began as an exercise Currier set herself: to compose for four different dance qualities, as in a string quartet, and to make each of the four sections grow out of a physical action idea and its emotional implications. Four dancers, thirteen minutes, to a Durante concerto in G minor for strings — a piece about how a movement quality behaves rather than about anyone in particular. — 1957"},"brandenburgconcertofour":{"blurb":"Humphrey died with this one unfinished; Currier completed it, and the completion started her own career as a choreographer. Her account of the three movements, given in 2000: 'The first movement is a celebration of an idyllic enclosed space. The second gently, firmly slices through that safe containment and opens new vistas. The third is exuberant in release into open space.' The notation credits both women. — 1959-05-12"},"placescurrier":{"blurb":"Five dancers to Vivaldi, notated the same year it was made by Carl Wolz and Ray Cook under Lucy Venable's supervision — Venable danced in it, alongside Currier, Joan Miller, Jennifer Scanlon and Jim Payton. The catalogue is blunt about what came of that: the manuscript is partial, twelve pages and a set of floor plans, and 'not suitable for staging.' — 1961"},"towishtogetherfearsomely":{"blurb":"Ten dancers, and a piece Currier built from the inside out. The score's introduction says the movement 'arises from the intensity within the individual rather than from an imposed spatial design' and that the quality of the movement far outweighs its design in space — and for that reason the notes and images Currier used while composing were bound into the score itself, which notators almost never do. — 1965-02-17"},"salutationscurrier":{"blurb":"The catalogue records no description of this one, only its dimensions: a hundred and twenty-five pages of Labanotation, set down by Mary Jane Evans Warner against Alfredo Casella's Serenata for small orchestra. All that paper still did not finish the job. The Dance Notation Bureau files the score as a partial rough manuscript and refuses it for performance, with the flat note 'Score is not suitable for staging'. — 1970-03-05"},"thedesperateheart":{"blurb":"A bare, darkened stage and a woman dancing her anguish in sweeping kicks, spins, scuttling steps and yearning arms, while Brinnin's poem is spoken from the wings — the playwright Horton Foote read it at the first performances. Louis Horst called it the finest solo work in the modern dance repertory of the decade. Not everyone wanted the words: the Kenyon Review's critic thought it 'the best dance Valerie Bettis has ever done' and complete without the poem. — 1943-03-24"},"yermabettis":{"blurb":"Lorca's play in five sections danced without a pause — Marriage Ritual, Dance of a Woman With Child, Conflict of Juan and Yerma, Lamentation of Yerma, Conclusion — for three dancers, Bettis with Duncan Noble and one other. It does not try to stage the play. The sources disagree over who wrote the score, Leo Smit or Bernardo Segall. — 1946"},"virginiasampler":{"blurb":"A Virginia town after the Revolution, its pattern disturbed by the arrival of strangers — frontiersmen, soldiers, and a mysterious Woman on Horseback. It was the first work any American ballet company took from a modern dance choreographer, with Merce Cunningham's The Seasons following a few weeks later, and some of the Ballet Russe dancers regarded it with open hostility. The reviews were not much warmer: Theatre Arts called it a hodgepodge. — 1947"},"asilaydying":{"blurb":"Faulkner's novel with the spoken text cut into the movement, so that Addie Bundren walked onto a New York stage and danced. Horton Foote had secured Faulkner's permission. John Martin called it 'a completely authoritative work of art'; Doris Hering wrote that 'only an artist with the deepest feeling for movement and drama could have worked the wonders Miss Bettis did with the material at hand'. — 1948"},"dominofurioso":{"blurb":"Another of Bettis's attempts to marry movement to the spoken word, on a text by John Malcolm Brinnin, subtitled A Commedia in Four Acts and resembling — to Jack Anderson — a variation on Pirandello's Six Characters in Search of an Author. Contemporaries found it far too complicated to work. Doris Hering's verdict has outlived the piece: 'If Miss Bettis is not careful she will talk us all to death.' — 1949"},"streetcarnameddesire":{"blurb":"Frederic Franklin telephoned Bettis and asked her for it, then danced Stanley opposite Mia Slavenska's Blanche, to a ballet score Alex North wrote separately from his film music and Rayburn Wright orchestrated. Bettis took over Blanche herself when Ballet Theatre acquired the piece two years later; Nora Kaye had it after her. Critics noticed how rarely it completed a choreographic phrase, and how it kept sliding between fantasy and reality. — 1952"},"thegoldenround":{"blurb":"A solo for Lady Macbeth — the title is Macbeth's own phrase for the crown — showing the degeneration rather than the crime. Bettis later opened it out into a trio, adding Macbeth and a third figure. Dance Magazine, watching that version, judged the original to have been over-energised and over-choreographed. — 1955"},"echoesofspoonriver":{"blurb":"1976"},"onthebrinkoftime":{"blurb":"1969"},"kinaesonata":{"blurb":"1970"},"pietaslewitzky":{"blurb":"1971"},"spacesbetween":{"blurb":"Lewitzky's husband, the architect and set designer Newell Taylor Reynolds, hung transparent plexiglass sheets to make multi-levelled dance platforms in mid-air; the dancers work on them and between them, to a score by Cara Bradbury Marcus. Thirty years on, the Los Angeles Times was still describing the plexiglass hangings and platforms as the witty thing about it. — 1974"},"inscape":{"blurb":"1976"},"devilinmassachusetts":{"blurb":"The Salem witch trials, made three years after Marion Starkey's book of the same name. Louis Horst wrote that it 'objectively portrays a horrifying climax of the witch-hunting mania' of the seventeenth century. Anthony revised it at least once, and Dance Magazine's complaint was that she set herself meaty ideas and then would not give herself the time span to develop them. — 1952"},"threnody":{"blurb":"Synge's Riders to the Sea: a mother loses her sons to the water, in three movements that hold still like a series of tableaux, with props, a set and long dresses. Anthony came upon Britten's Sinfonia da Requiem by chance and built the piece on it. One image survives every telling — the mother kneeling with a bundle in her arms, then crossing the stage without appearing to move any part of herself. — 1956"},"songsanthony":{"blurb":"Three of Blake's Songs of Innocence set to Debussy's String Quartet in G minor, with Anthony herself among the seven dancers. Only the third part, To Each His Own, was ever taken down in notation, and it is the part that kept coming back — danced again at the New Dance Group's gala concert in 1994, nearly forty years on. — 1957-11-10"},"ladymacbethanthony":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"inthebeginninganthony":{"blurb":"1975"},"fatalbirds":{"blurb":"1966"},"excursionssanasardo":{"blurb":"1966"},"footnotes":{"blurb":"1970"},"thepathsanasardo":{"blurb":"1972"},"childreninthemist":{"blurb":"1985"},"pierrotlunaire":{"blurb":"Three dancers and a scaffold at centre stage: Pierrot on it, and Columbine and Brighella working him. Tetley made the movement a deliberate alloy — the long lean line of ballet welded to the gravitational pull, tensed angles and visceral gesticulation of Graham — and danced Pierrot himself opposite Linda Hodes and Robert Powell, with Rouben Ter-Arutunian designing. Sanjoy Roy calls it a pivot point in dance history and, on the Freudian iconography that drives it, creepy. — 1962-05-05"},"theanatomylesson":{"blurb":"Built on Rembrandt's Anatomy Lesson of Dr Nicolaes Tulp. The Oxford Dictionary of Dance describes what Tetley does with it: the piece 'contrasts the inner life and passions of the man laid out on the table with the coldly scientific context in which he has been placed after death.' He made it on Nederlands Dans Theater, the company he was then working with almost exclusively. — 1964-01-28"},"ricercare":{"blurb":"A duet of twenty-one minutes to music by Mordecai Seter, designed by Rouben Ter-Arutunian — Tetley making a two-hander for a large classical company four years after starting his own troupe. The Benesh score is held at the Royal Academy of Dance in London, which is the only reason it remains stageable. — 1966-01-25"},"embracetiger":{"blurb":"Movement drawn out of t'ai chi ch'uan and danced flat, without pointe shoes, by five women and five men to Morton Subotnick's Silver Apples of the Moon, with Nadine Baylis designing. Voices of British Ballet reads it as another step in Ballet Rambert's turn away from a classical repertory; Britannica records the plainer fact, that Tetley put an ancient Chinese exercise on a ballet stage. — 1968-11-21"},"fieldfigures":{"blurb":"Tetley's first piece for the Royal Ballet, and the signal that its newly renamed and re-formed New Group was being pointed at contemporary dance. Stockhausen from Aus den sieben Tagen, designs by Nadine Baylis, and a first cast of Deanne Bergsma, Desmond Kelly, Vergie Derman and Nicholas Johnson. British audiences already knew his work from Ballet Rambert; this was the first time the Royal commissioned it. — 1970"},"voluntaries":{"blurb":"Made in memory of John Cranko, who had invited Tetley to Stuttgart as resident choreographer shortly before he died and whose company Tetley then took over. Poulenc's Concerto for Organ, Strings and Percussion carries it, Rouben Ter-Arutunian designed it, and five principals and a corps of twelve dance thirty-three minutes on pointe. — 1973-12-22"},"sacretetley":{"blurb":"Stravinsky's score in a revised and reduced orchestration by Robert Rudolf, with scenery and costumes by Nadine Baylis. Tetley made it during his two years directing the Stuttgart Ballet, where it entered the repertory beside Voluntaries; American Ballet Theatre took it two years later with Baryshnikov, Martine van Hamel and Clark Tippet at the head of the cast, and revived it in 1992 in a staging by Bronwen Curry. — 1974-04-17"},"passinthrough":{"blurb":"A nine-minute solo to Pete Seeger's Goofing-Off Suite, and the comedy in it is anatomical. It is 'humorous in tone', wrote Amanda Smith in the Boston Phoenix, 'primarily achieved through exaggeration of body parts'. The Folkways recording it needs has long since gone out of circulation, which is one reason it is danced so rarely. — 1959-05-27"},"earthling":{"blurb":"1963"},"lhistoiredusoldat":{"blurb":"1966"},"patinaredlich":{"blurb":"1974"},"lakeoffire":{"blurb":"1975"},"tracesredlich":{"blurb":"1975"},"disposal":{"blurb":"1981"},"signalsalarms":{"blurb":"1984"},"radicalardent":{"blurb":"1999"},"takingtimetobevulnerable":{"blurb":"1999"},"tensuggestions":{"blurb":"A fourteen-minute solo to Tcherepnin's Bagatelles, op. 5, made in the first year Morris had a company and now danced by others. The Daily Gazette's description remains the most useful one going: 'An earthy solo — rather, a duo with a chair.' — 1981-02-13"},"gloriamorris":{"blurb":"Ten dancers, thirty minutes, Vivaldi's Gloria in D. The New York Times reads it as Morris borrowing the spiritual language of earlier modern dance 'while roughing up the piety that can stick in the throats of a skeptical contemporary audience.' He kept working on it — the lighting was redesigned twice — and at the company's twenty-fifth anniversary in 2006 he conducted the score himself for the first time. — 1981-12-12"},"drinktomeonly":{"blurb":"Made on American Ballet Theatre to Virgil Thomson's piano etudes, whose titles are finger exercises — 'Ragtime Bass', 'For the Weaker Fingers', 'Parallel Chords', 'Double Glissando' — with costumes by Santo Loquasto. An excerpt had already been shown at the Dancing for Life benefit the previous October. It has since gone into the repertories of Houston Ballet, San Francisco Ballet, English National Ballet and the Royal New Zealand Ballet. — 1988-05-31"},"lallegro":{"blurb":"Handel's oratorio on Milton's poems, danced entire, from Morris's Brussels years in Béjart's former post. Among the most celebrated of his works: it took the Laurence Olivier Award for Best New Dance Production in 1998, Morris published a volume of photographs and critical essays devoted to it in 2001, and San Francisco Ballet made it the heart of the all-Morris program marking fifteen years of his collaborations there. — 1988-11-23"},"didoandaeneasmorris":{"blurb":"Morris put Purcell's singers in the pit and kept the stage entirely for dancing, then cast himself as both Dido and the Sorceress who destroys her. Joan Acocella's reading is that the gender switch, plus the merging of heroine and villainess, 'shot the show into a mythic territory, beyond realism and beyond conventional morals.' He danced it until 2000; when the company remounted it in 2006 the two roles were split between two dancers, and later joined again. — 1989-03-11"},"thehardnut":{"blurb":"Hoffmann's The Nutcracker and the Mouse King moved into the world of the comics artist Charles Burns. Act I is a Christmas party danced as polka, hokey-pokey, hesitation, stroll, bump and waltz; G.I. Joes fight the rats; Marie kills the Rat King with her slipper. Twenty pounds of confetti come down each night in the Waltz of the Snowflakes. — 1991-01-12"},"grandduo":{"blurb":"Four movements — Prelude, Stampede, A Round, Polka — to Lou Harrison's Grand Duo. The Polka, which comes last, was choreographed first and given on its own a year before the rest existed. The Times called the result savage and orderly, comic and bleak; the Guardian, one of the masterpieces of the late twentieth century. — 1993-02-16"},"fallingdownstairs":{"blurb":"Fifteen dancers, a staircase and Bach's Third Cello Suite, made with Yo-Yo Ma playing it. Isaac Mizrahi dressed them in heavy velvet; the Boston Globe saw medieval figures in a cathedral making searching gestures as they moved on and off the central stair. The film Barbara Willis Sweete made of the collaboration won an Emmy. — 1997-03-06"},"divining":{"blurb":"Jamison's first piece for the Ailey company, made at Ailey's request five years before she took over as its director. It is built on dance idioms from across Africa over African polyrhythms by Kimati Dinizulu and Monti Ellison, and the title points at a search rather than a story. The New York Times praised her \"invention of movement shapes and movement quality that give it a fascinating originality.\" — 1984"},"forgottentime":{"blurb":"Twelve dancers to the sound of Le Mystere des Voix Bulgares, in what Jamison framed as ancient rituals of love and rites of passage - complex partnering, and long held stillnesses used as structure rather than as pause. It is her quietest large work, and the one that most depends on the audience being willing to wait. — 1990"},"hymnjamison":{"blurb":"Jamison's tribute to Ailey four years after his death, and the only one of her works to win an Emmy. Anna Deavere Smith took recollections from Jamison and from the dancers themselves and arranged them into a spoken libretto, so the company talks about Ailey while dancing about him, over a driving percussion score by Robert Ruggieri. Explosive full-company sections alternate with solos shaped to each dancer. — 1993"},"riverside":{"blurb":"Premiered as the novelty of the Ailey company's annual City Center gala, to a score Jamison commissioned from the percussionist Kimati Dinizulu, who had also written for her Divining a decade earlier. Beyond the gala notice and the commission the record found for this pass is thin; what the dance did is not described in it. — 1995"},"sweetrelease":{"blurb":"A love story told through a cast of characters based on Jamison's own friends - couples young and old, a minister, his congregation, and a tempter. The Lincoln Center Festival commissioned it and let her choose the composer; she asked Wynton Marsalis, who wrote the score. — 1996"},"doubleexposure":{"blurb":"Jamison's eighth ballet for the company, and the one where she wanted two things at once: a male pas de deux, and a way of showing two sides of a single person. \"We are all multi-layered and multi-level human beings,\" she said; the women entered the story as catalysts, \"because in my ballets I always have some strong, powerful female figures.\" Robert Ruggieri's jazz score came first, with the title, and animated electronic scenery ran behind the dancing. — 2000"},"lovestories":{"blurb":"Jamison made it with two other choreographers - the hip-hop maker Rennie Harris and Robert Battle, who would later run the company - and the three styles are stitched together rather than blended: Philly bop, Lindy hop, hip hop and modern in one evening, to Stevie Wonder. Its governing image is Sankofa, the Akan bird that flies forward while looking backward. It opened the company's first permanent home. — 2004"},"sahelacogny":{"blurb":"1987"},"yewaeausublime":{"blurb":"1994-09-06"},"zacogny":{"blurb":"1995"},"fagaala":{"blurb":"Fagaala is Wolof for genocide. Acogny read Boubacar Boris Diop's novel Murambi, the Book of Bones and made a piece about Rwanda with the Japanese choreographer Kota Yamazaki, so that butoh's slowness and collapse meet West African dance in the same bodies. It won her a Bessie and has been touring ever since. — 2004"},"waxtaan":{"blurb":"Waxtaan is Wolof for palaver, the long public argument. Acogny made it with her son Patrick, and the dancers spend it parodying heads of state, ministers and the men in power - calling them to account by dancing them. It is the funniest and most directly political thing in the Jant-Bi repertory. — 2007"},"lesecaillesdelamemoire":{"blurb":"The Scales of Memory: the first collaboration between Jawole Willa Jo Zollar's Urban Bush Women and Acogny's Compagnie Jant-Bi, seven American women and a company of Senegalese men working together from 2004 onwards before it reached a stage. Two vocabularies with the same ancestors and three hundred years of divergence between them, put in one room to see what still matched. — 2007"},"songookyaakaar":{"blurb":"Songook Yaakaar means facing up to hope. A solo, danced and spoken, in which Acogny finally says what she calls \"her Africas\" - the greatness and the faults together, without softening either. She was in her mid-sixties and had spent a career letting other people's material speak for her; this is the one where she speaks. — 2010"},"aunendroitdudebut":{"blurb":"Acogny in her seventies, alone on stage with her own family: photographs and film projected behind her, and a book written by her father Togoun Servais Acogny in her hands. She left Africa, lived in exile in Europe, came back, and the solo is the reckoning with that. Mikael Serre directed it. The New York Times review was headlined 'An African Dance Matriarch Brings Out the Knives'. — 2015"},"bacchanalefort":{"blurb":"For her senior recital Fort asked John Cage, then the dance department's accompanist, for something with an African inflection. Cage wanted a percussion ensemble; the hall had no room for one, only a piano. So he opened the lid and pushed screws, bolts and paper clips between the strings. This was the first prepared piano, and it exists because a Black student choreographer asked for a sound the room could not otherwise make. — 1940-04-28"},"yanvalou":{"blurb":"Named for the Haitian Vodou dance for Damballah, the serpent. Nothing of Fort's own staging is described in the sources found, but the piece outlived her: Glory Van Scott and Joan Peters put it back on stage at Symphony Space in 1993, and Jack Anderson wrote in the Times that what made it striking was the way the cast conveyed communal solidarity and awe in the presence of divine powers. — 1940"},"carnivalfort":{"blurb":"One of the Afro-Caribbean pieces Fort made while running her own school, and one of the few that outlived her: Glory Van Scott and Joan Peters put it back on stage at Symphony Space in 1992 and 1993 alongside Yanvalou, Rhumba and Danza. Nothing describing Fort's own staging turned up for this pass. — 1950"},"rhumbafort":{"blurb":"An Afro-Caribbean piece from the year Fort and Buddy Phillips opened their own studio one block north of the Dunham school. It survived her: Glory Van Scott produced it at Symphony Space in 1992 and 1993 with Yanvalou, Carnival and Danza. What it looked like is not recorded in the sources found for this pass. — 1954"},"danzafort":{"blurb":"Fort's Spanish piece, and the one her dancers remembered her own performance in. Glory Van Scott: \"She moved like a feather - light and flowing. When she danced her role in Danza, you'd be mesmerized. It was Spanish with a Moorish accent.\" Jennifer Dunning, reviewing the 1992 Symphony Space restaging, called it a delicately perfumed yet sturdy abstraction of the stylised Spanish school. — 1955"},"prodigalsonfort":{"blurb":"Fort choreographed Langston Hughes's Prodigal Son, directed by Vinnette Carroll, with Glory Van Scott in the female lead as Jezebel. Van Scott's whole verdict on it, decades later, was two words: \"That was hot choreography!\" Perron's account dates the choreography to 1958 but captions a production photograph 1965, so exactly which staging is meant is not settled there. — 1958"},"obatala":{"blurb":"Named for the Yoruba orisha who shapes human bodies. The Arthur Hall Collection's surviving film of it was shot at the bottom of the grand staircase of the Philadelphia Museum of Art - black and white, no sound, and the footage catches another crew in shot with a sound recordist and a cameraman measuring the light, which was low. What the choreography was is not described in the archive's notes. — 1958"},"africansketches":{"blurb":"Known now mainly as a television broadcast: WCAU carried it on its Repertoire Workshop programme shortly after Easter 1962, including a Temptation duet danced with Ione Nash. The Arthur Hall Collection has not located the film, and suspects it went with the station when Channel 10 was sold. — 1958"},"beclch":{"blurb":"Not a concert piece but a hire: the Theatre of the Living Arts engaged Hall's company for its production of the play Beclch, and the engagement was one of the first real openings for a troupe that had spent a decade rehearsing two nights a week in a recreation centre. Hall's own account, given to the Philadelphia Inquirer in 1968, is dry about it - the play, he noted, still roused an argument whenever anyone mentioned it. — 1967"},"mojuba":{"blurb":"Broadcast rather than staged: WCAU, then the CBS station in Philadelphia, carried it on its Repertoire Workshop programme, danced by Hall's ensemble in its guise as the company of the Theatre of the Living Arts, on a bill with Fanga, Hyena and Challenge. The Arthur Hall Collection has never found the film. When Channel 10 switched from CBS to NBC, the archivists' best guess is that the master went with the sale. — 1967"},"africaschildren":{"blurb":"A two-part television programme rather than an evening in a theatre: Philadelphia's Channel 12 carried it on 11 and 18 December, and WHYY put it out again in two parts the following January. Hall's ensemble was by then being sent into school assemblies as the city looked for a curriculum that answered to its Black population, and television was the other way out of the church halls and recreation centres. — 1968"},"orpheushall":{"blurb":"Hall's Orpheus was performed at Graterford Prison as part of the Model Cities Cultural Arts programme, co-produced with his own Ile Ife Black Humanitarian Center and the American Broadcasting Corporation. A DVD of it can be watched at Temple's Special Collections Research Center, but ABC claims the copyright and refuses to allow any reproduction - so a piece made by a community centre for the men inside a prison is now locked up by a broadcaster. — 1973"},"thegoldenstool":{"blurb":"1980"},"sensemaya":{"blurb":"Dance Theatre of Harlem gave the world premiere and Anna Kisselgoff reviewed it for the New York Times. The title is Nicolas Guillen's Afro-Cuban chant for the killing of a snake, best known through Silvestre Revueltas's tone poem on it. What de Lavallade made of that material is not described in the sources found for this pass. — 1979"},"sweetbitterlove":{"blurb":"De Lavallade first made this as a solo for herself after hearing Roberta Flack sing the Van McCoy song. Turning it into a duet for the Ailey company, she added the man so that both points of view were present, and told the dancers to think of Brief Encounter - two people who match too intensely and have found each other too late. Geoffrey Holder, her husband, dressed it. — 2000"},"comesunday":{"blurb":"De Lavallade's signature solo, made with Geoffrey Holder, to a spiritual sung by Odetta - the piece she kept dancing across decades and other companies took up. Jacob's Pillow holds film of Dance Theatre of Harlem performing it. No premiere date has been established for it here, which is why the atlas carries none."},"flak":{"blurb":"FLAK is short for Flieger Abwehr Kanonen, German for anti-aircraft gunfire. The dance lasts two minutes and nine seconds — 'the duration of many a WWII bomb run', reads the note on the filmed version, over Hodes's own name — and he had flown B-17s. It went on his first concert as a choreographer, one of four pieces; he later opened the solo out into a group dance. — 1951"},"noheaveninearth":{"blurb":"1951"},"aftertheteacups":{"blurb":"1963"},"abysshodes":{"blurb":"1965"},"primasera":{"blurb":"1968"},"boedromion":{"blurb":"1974"},"ashapeoflight":{"blurb":"1974"},"ithoughtyouweredead":{"blurb":"1996"},"iamthegate":{"blurb":"A nine-minute solo to the third movement of Hindemith's sonata for trumpet and piano — the Trauermusik, music for mourning. McGehee made it on herself and it survives unusually well for a solo of its date: a film of her dancing it from the year she made it survives, alongside a later recording of her talking through how it came about. — 1957"},"afterpossession":{"blurb":"1965"},"yarnmcgehee":{"blurb":"1965"},"suspendedpath":{"blurb":"1965"},"ceremoniesofremembrance":{"blurb":"1969"},"elretablodemaesepedro":{"blurb":"Master Peter's Puppet Show — the episode from Don Quixote in which the knight attacks a puppet theatre — danced to Falla's opera of the same name, with a cast of twenty. It is by a wide margin the largest work of McGehee's in the notation catalogue, and she staged the notated version herself. — 1971-10-14"},"theoresteiamcgehee":{"blurb":"1976"},"endolor":{"blurb":"A solo to Manuel de Falla's Pantomime: a woman mourning a personal loss and having to deal with her grief and her anger. It has been described, the notation catalogue records, as 'a Lament of memory'. Winter danced it herself, and no one wrote the movement down until 2006. — 1944"},"inavacuum":{"blurb":"A worker doing the same thing over and over, sealed off from everyone around, played somewhere between Chaplin and machinery. The archive that holds the film describes it as portraying the mechanisation and monotony of the individual 'engaged in repetitive tasks, isolated from all about them'. Shearer danced it to Mussorgsky — the Promenade and Pictures at an Exhibition — on the solo debut programme that made her name. — 1941-10-22"},"fablesandproverbs":{"blurb":"1961"},"thereflectioninthepuddle":{"blurb":"1963"},"flameshearer":{"blurb":"2005"},"legendlang":{"blurb":"The first version of the dance Lang kept coming back to: she rebuilt it and renamed it The Possessed for a 1975 premiere, and that became her best-known work. Both are built on Ansky's 1914 Yiddish play The Dybbuk. The Library of Congress catalogues Legend as a work in its own right, but nothing survives describing what it looked like before the rebuilding. — 1951"},"andjoyismywitness":{"blurb":"Twelve dancers to Bach's organ Toccata, Adagio and Fugue in C major, taken at the piano. The casting note in the notation score is short and telling — dancers must have endurance. Lang danced it herself, with Bruce Marks and Paul Sanasardo among the eleven around her. — 1955-04-01"},"shirah":{"blurb":"From the score itself: 'Shirah is conceived as a song with undertones of meaning suggested by a Chassidic parable. The title itself means song in Hebrew.' Nine dancers to Hovhaness's concerto for viola and strings. Only the first movement was ever written down — five minutes, nineteen pages, notated in 1963 by Jennifer Muller — and the Dance Notation Bureau marks that score a student project, incomplete, and 'not suitable for staging'. — 1960-08-19"},"apasionada":{"blurb":"1961"},"tonguesoffire":{"blurb":"1967"},"pieceforbrass":{"blurb":"1969"},"thepossessedlang":{"blurb":"1975"},"littletheodolina":{"blurb":"1942"},"oftragicgesture":{"blurb":"1946"},"borntoweep":{"blurb":"1946"},"thefeastfonaroff":{"blurb":"1946"},"cafechantant":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"lazarusfonaroff":{"blurb":"1952"},"winddrum":{"blurb":"1962"},"thecryyuriko":{"blurb":"1963"},"eventsyuriko":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"taleofseizure":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"cooliedance":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"sketchesyuriko":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"totemross":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"vanyathreepastels":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"noahross":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"raymondduncan":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"cellcohan":{"blurb":"Six hand-picked dancers shut into a situation the piece never names, in three parts, to Ronald Lloyd's angry musique concrete. Cohan made it during the Vietnam war and the nuclear scare, and it was the work that moved him off abstraction and convinced Robin Howard he had found the right director. It still closed the company's final programme twenty-five years later, and Bob Lockyer filmed it for the BBC in 1982. — 1969"},"stagescohan":{"blurb":"A full-evening multimedia account of a hero's journey to self-awareness, designed by Norberto Chiesa and Peter Farmer, scored by Arne Nordheim and Bob Downes, with film by Anthony McCall. It also solved a booking problem: theatre managers found a single-work programme far easier to sell than a mixed bill, and Cohan went on making them. — 1971"},"classcohan":{"blurb":"Every step in it comes from the company's daily technique class. Cohan's own note says as much — the movements' 'primary use is to strengthen, coordinate, and shape a dancer's body' — and adds that the material 'demonstrates several years evolution from the Martha Graham technique that we started with, to our own present style.' Fourteen dancers in plain black lycra. The Labanotation score is completely restricted and may not be used for any purpose at all. — 1975-10-01"},"nympheas":{"blurb":"Monet's water lilies, with enlarged details of the paintings carried on the dancers' body tights, danced to Debussy; his obituarist calls the result fluid and impressionistic. Norberto Chiesa designed it and John B. Read lit it — the pair who recur through Cohan's chronology more often than any other collaborators. — 1976"},"forestcohan":{"blurb":"Cohan's own chronology credits Brian Hodgson with sound rather than music: the piece runs on a soundtrack, not a score, with Norberto Chiesa designing and Cohan lighting it himself. Batsheva took it up four years later in Haifa and danced it eleven times, and the Martha Graham company brought the duet out again for the concert marking Cohan's ninetieth birthday. — 1977"},"communioncohan":{"blurb":"Cohan was ninety-four and going back to Mass, the ritual piece he had made for London Contemporary Dance Theatre in the 1970s. Ten celebrants begin with an inexorable pacing, to music by Nils Frahm; the subject, as so often with him, is the individual whose loneliness a group activity makes worse rather than better. It was his seventh work for Yorke Dance Project. — 2019"},"ettspelomforemal":{"blurb":"1967"},"themagicflutefeuer":{"blurb":"1975"},"thenijinskyfilms":{"blurb":"1975"},"marthagrahamat100":{"blurb":"1994"},"jephthahsdaughter":{"blurb":"A duet for Schenfeld and Moshe Efrati on the Jephthah story from the book of Judges. Mordecai Seter wrote the score and David Sharir the set; Schenfeld made her own costume. It was her first choreography for the company she had helped found the year before, and it was given ten times. — 1965-07-27"},"blindmansbuff":{"blurb":"Ten dancers to Elliott Carter, with costumes by Galia Gat, who also danced in it. It was Schenfeld's second commission from the company, and the archive holds dozens of production photographs of it, most of them by Jacob Agor — a far fuller visual record than survives for the rest of her work there. — 1967-03-06"},"curtainsschenfeld":{"blurb":"Three named figures: The Figure, The Clownlike Figure and The Woman, taken by Schenfeld, Moshe Efrati and Linda Hodes. Noam Sheriff wrote the score, Dani Karavan built the set and Hodes designed the costumes. It became the company's most-travelled piece of the period — sixty-four performances, and tours to Denmark, Germany and Sweden within weeks of the premiere, then to North America and France. — 1969-08-13"},"cornersschenfeld":{"blurb":"A trio, and the credits are the striking part: set and costumes by the painter Lea Nikel, lighting by John Benn, and no composer named at all. Yair Vardi, Esther Nadler and Derek Linton danced it twenty-eight times, including on tour in Germany and Portugal that summer. — 1973-04-02"},"threadsofsolo":{"blurb":"1978"},"tinsandhair":{"blurb":"1980"},"dancemetotheendoflove":{"blurb":"2008-08-06"},"sinliethatthedoor":{"blurb":"Cain, Abel and a Demon — Moshe Efrati, Ehud Ben-David and Rina Schenfeld — with the title lifted from the warning God gives Cain in Genesis. Noam Sheriff wrote the music, Dani Karavan the set, Linda Hodes the costumes. It was Efrati's first choreography, it entered the repertory on the same night as Schenfeld's Curtains, and it was danced sixty-four times, exactly as often as hers. — 1969-08-13"},"eindor":{"blurb":"Saul goes to the witch of Endor. Efrati danced the king himself, Tselila Goldstein the seeress, and four more men appeared as the Many Faces of Saul. Zvi Avni wrote the score and Dani Karavan designed it; the piece went straight onto the company's North American tour that autumn, which is how a work first shown at a kibbutz in the Negev came to be given twenty-four times. — 1970-07-16"},"paceandsteptogether":{"blurb":"Ten dancers, scores by Zvi Avni and Carlos Surinach, costumes by Ilana Ben Shaul. The archive's performance count for it is one — commissioned, made, shown, never repeated. It sits between Efrati's two Batsheva successes and the company of deaf dancers he founded later the same year. — 1971-04-20"},"amanbeginshisday":{"blurb":"1975"},"untilthatiarose":{"blurb":"1976"},"andthesunrises":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"reminiscencesefrati":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"aspirationsefrati":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"byorderofcreon":{"blurb":"Antigone against Creon, with Haemon between them and three soldiers to enforce the order. Linda Hodes danced Antigone and designed both set and costumes; Ben-David played Creon and lit his own piece; Daniel Shalit wrote the score. It opened at a kibbutz in the north and was given six times. — 1966-12-27"},"fantasiaron":{"blurb":"Ron's first piece for the company he had spent a decade dancing in: eight dancers, music by John Frank Williams, costumes he designed himself. Eleven performances and then it left the repertory. He made three more works for Batsheva over the following three years. — 1977-02-09"},"daughtersofshiloh":{"blurb":"Ten dancers to Ravel, with set and costumes by Moshe Ben Shaul. The title comes from the end of the book of Judges, where the men of Benjamin carry off the daughters of Shiloh as they come out to dance in the vineyards. Ron made it in the Sanasardo years, and it went almost straight onto a tour of France and Spain. — 1979-02-18"},"stravinskydancecircus":{"blurb":"1979"},"machiniyoseruannui":{"blurb":"Ando's own recital piece, and one of a run of works she made about the street — a companion, two years later, was Rain in the Back Alleys. Ohno Kazuo appeared in it as a guest, in the year he opened his own studio and gave his first recital of his own; five years on she would put him and Hijikata Tatsumi on the same stage in The Crow. — 1949"},"recueil":{"blurb":"1961"},"strukturcebron":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"lecygne":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"dangerousworld":{"blurb":"1958"},"concertobarocco":{"blurb":"Bach's Double Violin Concerto made visible, two ballerinas shadowing the two solo violins through twenty plotless minutes, danced in practice clothes without scenery. Made for a South American tour, it later opened New York City Ballet's first night in 1948 and stands for what Clive Barnes named the hallmarks of American classical dance: 'poetry, athleticism, and musicality.' — 1941-06-27"},"fourtemperaments":{"blurb":"Hindemith's theme and four variations — commissioned by Balanchine as music to play at home with friends at his evening musicales — danced as the four medieval humors: melancholic, sanguinic, phlegmatic, choleric. Kurt Seligmann's sets and costumes were stripped away by 1951, leaving the angular modernism in practice clothes that anchors the repertory. — 1946-11-20"},"symphonyinc":{"blurb":"Bizet wrote the symphony at seventeen as a Conservatory student and it then vanished, unplayed, until the manuscript surfaced in 1933. Balanchine choreographed it in a fortnight, giving each of the four movements its own principals, and the finale brings every group back so that more than fifty dancers end together. He renamed it a year later for the opening programme of his new American company, and it has not left that repertory since. — 1947-07-28"},"jewels":{"blurb":"Conceived after a visit to Van Cleef & Arpels: Emeralds at Fauré's mesmerizing pace, Rubies racing through Stravinsky's jazz-inflected capriccio, Diamonds venerating the classical inheritance to Tchaikovsky, all in Karinska's costumes. Widely counted the first full-length plotless ballet — about facets of classical dancing, not gemstones. — 1967-04-13"},"nutcracker":{"blurb":"The first evening-length work the company ever mounted, and by the Balanchine Foundation's account the success that 'helped assure the permanence of the Company.' Balanchine cast children from the School of American Ballet — he had danced the Nutcracker and the Mouse King himself as a boy at the Maryinsky — and that set a precedent the company kept. Jerome Robbins choreographed the battle with the Mouse King; Maria Tallchief was the first Sugar Plum Fairy. — 1954-02-02"},"symphoniepourunhommeseul":{"blurb":"Bejart danced it himself with Michele Seigneuret against a tape of footsteps, voices and noise - Schaeffer and Henry's musique concrete, whose scenario he took along with the sound. Schaeffer's note set the terms: let the modern dancer, 'without costume or set,' be 'without drum or trumpet.' French sources call it the first musique-concrete ballet, though Merce Cunningham had set the same score at Brandeis in 1952. It ran all summer and made Bejart's name. — 1955-07-26"},"sacreduprintemps":{"blurb":"Béjart stripped the Russian folklore out of Stravinsky's rite and made it a hymn to sexuality and creation instead: spring as an immense primitive force, the union of man and woman in the flesh and of sky with earth. Massed dancers replace the storybook tribe, in sets and costumes by the painter Pierre Caille. It has been back in his company's repertory since 2007. — 1959-12-08"},"bolero":{"blurb":"A soloist dances on a table, ringed by seated men drawn one by one into Ravel's crescendo, 'culminating in a climactic union of the dancers atop the table.' Made on Duška Sifnios; Jorge Donn later became the first man to take the role, and his filmed performance in Lelouch's Les Uns et les Autres carried the ballet far beyond dance audiences — 'probably his best known and most popular dance,' by The New York Times's account. — 1961-01-10"},"ninthsymphony":{"blurb":"Bejart got the idea in Havana watching Cuban dancers, and gave each of Beethoven's four movements an element: earth for the struggle toward an ideal, fire for the joy of dancing, water for the Adagio's love, air for freedom at the end. Orchestra, chorus and dancers shared one stage in the round, with Nietzsche read over bongo percussion. It went on to St Mark's Square, the Kremlin and the 1968 Mexico City Olympics, and was recovered decades later from a pirated video shot at La Scala. — 1964"},"messepourletempspresent":{"blurb":"A ceremony in nine episodes rather than a ballet: the spoken texts ran from the Satipatthana Sutta to the Song of Songs and Nietzsche's Zarathustra, and one episode, Mein Kampf, was danced to a montage of military marches. It ended with the cast sitting motionless under rotating headlamps, the audience free to stay or go. Pierre Henry and Michel Colombier's score outlived the staging — its Psyche Rock became a pop standard. — 1967-07-31"},"chantducompagnonerrant":{"blurb":"Bejart set Mahler's Lieder eines fahrenden Gesellen - the one cycle whose words Mahler wrote himself - as a duet for two men, casting Rudolf Nureyev opposite Paolo Bortoluzzi: a wandering journeyman and the destiny that walks behind him. Bejart's note names the wound Mahler described, 'this knife in the chest,' as self-combat and solitude. Jorge Donn took over the Nureyev role in 1978, and the duet has stayed in repertory ever since. — 1971-03-11"},"nijinskyclowndedieu":{"blurb":"Béjart's ballet on the life of Nijinsky, drawn from the Diary, in which each character appears twice — the man and his double, the clown. Jorge Donn danced Nijinsky, perched like a wild animal. Pierre Henry wrote the score; Roger Bernard and Joëlle Roustan designed the production. It is where Béjart first worked out the métissage, the deliberate mixing, that runs through the later work. — 1971-10-08"},"pineapplepoll":{"blurb":"Charles Mackerras waited for Sullivan's copyright to lapse at the end of 1950, then stitched a score out of eleven Gilbert and Sullivan operas plus Cox and Box. Cranko took the plot from Gilbert's Bab Ballad 'The Bumboat Woman's Story' and gave Poll a suitor so it could end happily. The joke is staged rather than mimed: the town girls disguise themselves as sailors to board H.M.S. Hot Cross Bun, and at drill the captain never notices that one of his crew keeps rising onto pointe. — 1951-03-13"},"jeudecartes":{"blurb":"Cranko danced Stravinsky's 1936 poker score straight - three deals, the shuffle heard as a ritornello, five cards walking on to form each hand - and, by American Ballet Theatre's account, without reference to Balanchine's original choreography for the same music. The Joker rules it: thrown into the first hand he ejects the Queen of Hearts, is refused by the second, and in the third turns up as the Queen of Spades to complete a royal flush. Egon Madsen created him. — 1965"},"onegin":{"blurb":"Cranko built a full-evening ballet on Pushkin while refusing Tchaikovsky's opera: the arranger Kurt-Heinz Stolze wrote that the score 'does not contain one measure' of it, and assembled it instead from the piano pieces, The Seasons, the opera Cherevichki and Francesca da Rimini. The argument runs through three encounters staged as pas de deux, and the ending rhymes with the middle - having torn up Tatiana's letter in the second act, Onegin has to watch her tear up his. — 1965-04-13"},"opus1":{"blurb":"Eleven minutes of Webern's Passacaglia, and Cranko's own company describes what is inside it as an entire life - birth, love, despair and death, with a figure left helpless and alone inside the cycle. It was the plotless side of a choreographer whose reputation rested on three-act stories, and it outlived him fast: after his death Nederlands Dans Theater took it into repertory as an in memoriam. — 1965-11-07"},"tamingoftheshrew":{"blurb":"A full-length ballet comedy, of which the twentieth century produced very few: Kurt-Heinz Stolze orchestrated Scarlatti for it, and Cranko wrote the two leads onto the bodies of Marcia Haydee and Richard Cragun, whose Katherina and Petruchio argue their way through three pas de deux by turns hilarious and tender. The comic ballast is Bianca's three suitors - the fop Gremio, the snob Hortensio, the student Lucentio - and at the first performance Hortensio was danced by the young John Neumeier. — 1969-03-16"},"initialsrbme":{"blurb":"The title is four initials: Richard Cragun, Birgit Keil, Marcia Haydee and Egon Madsen, in the order they walk on. Each takes one movement of Brahms's Second Piano Concerto, and each keeps reappearing inside the others' movements - as John Percival described it, 'not necessarily to play any very active part, but just to be there.' A plotless ballet whose actual subject is who was in the room. Cranko died the following year. — 1972-01-18"},"sinfonietta":{"blurb":"Kylián had known Janáček's score since his conservatory years and took the commission against the advice of the company's administrative director, who thought the production time impossibly short; they rehearsed it on tour in Israel. Folk-dance material recast in classical technique is danced against Walter Nobbe's backdrop of the Bohemian countryside. By Kylián's account the audience never heard the end of the music: they were already standing on their chairs, throwing programme books. — 1978"},"symphonyofpsalms":{"blurb":"For the backdrop Kylián hung oriental carpets, whose patterns he took to carry religious meaning as much as decoration, and kept all sixteen dancers — from a company of twenty-four — on stage from the first note to the last. Sharing one space for the whole piece, he wrote, gave them a sense of belonging; the strict lines and curves break open at intervals so that individuals can make statements only they could make. He counted it the work that unified the company he had just taken charge of. — 1978-11-24"},"sechstanze":{"blurb":"Dancers in powdered wigs and eighteenth-century undress curtsey and pout while black baroque dresses on little wheels glide about like galleons in full sail, steered badly by the men and provoking duels. Kylián wrote that two centuries of wars and revolutions stood between him and Mozart's German Dances, so he could not simply make comic numbers: he set 'six seemingly nonsensical acts, which obviously ignore their surroundings' and are dwarfed by the troubled world. — 1986-10-24"},"fallingangels":{"blurb":"Eight women in black leotards to drumming rooted in Ghanaian ceremonial ritual music, dancing through 'ambition, seduction, pregnancy, birth, death, motherhood and self-awareness.' It belongs to Kylián's 'Black and White' series of ballets, the pared-down works most identified with his middle period. — 1989-11-23"},"bellafigura":{"blurb":"Black curtains rise, drop and slide sideways throughout, squaring off playing areas, wrapping a dancer like a sheet, falling in front of a row of dancers into their waiting arms; men and women dance in voluminous red skirts with bare torsos. Kylián's question was where a performance begins and ends — at the curtain, at birth, at the make-up table — and he used the Italian phrase in its second sense: putting on a brave face, whatever is happening offstage. — 1995-10-12"},"wingsofwax":{"blurb":"A bare tree hangs crown downwards above the stage with its roots in the air, and a low-slung spotlight traces a huge circle around it; dancers in tight dark costumes come out of the black background and are absorbed back into it. Kylián took the title from Icarus and argued that a dancer's life is its own kind of confinement — captive inside the body they exhibit as a work of art, in an art defined for centuries by the wish to defy gravity. — 1997-01-23"},"kyr":{"blurb":"Naharin's first piece after taking charge of the company, made with the rock band The Tractor's Revenge, who played it live while he sang. Its most durable section sets the cumulative Passover song 'Echad Mi Yodea' to rock: dancers in black suits shouting the verses and shedding a garment each round. Eight years later that section was to open the state's fiftieth-anniversary gala; ordered to put the dancers in long underwear, Naharin resigned instead and the company refused to perform. — 1990-06-08"},"anaphaza":{"blurb":"Ninety minutes grown out of the opening ceremony Naharin had made for the Israel Festival in Jerusalem earlier the same year, staged for the company's thirtieth anniversary, with a drummer and a bass guitarist playing live. It enlarged the chair sequence from Kyr into its centrepiece and reached into the house, the performers making contact with spectators as later Naharin works pointedly would not. A Jerusalem Post critic recalled it as 'glamorous, bursting with fashion and color'. — 1993-12-29"},"minus16":{"blurb":"A remix: Naharin cut sections out of three of his earlier pieces and set them to a score running from Dean Martin and mambo to techno. At the centre is 'Echad Mi Yodea' — black suits and white shirts, a semicircle of chairs, the thirteen verses driven through in unison, dancers standing on the seats, hurling themselves into backbends and finally tearing off their clothes into a heap centre stage. In the finale they fetch spectators out of the house to dance, so no two performances end alike. — 1999"},"decadance":{"blurb":"'Not a new work,' Naharin says of it: 'It is more about reconstruction' — sections of his existing pieces cut loose and reordered 'to look at it from a new angle,' beginnings, middles and endings of many stories reassembled until 'the result become as coherent as the original if not more.' A running self-portrait of a repertory, and each reworking, he says, teaches him something new about his own composition. — 2000"},"naharinsvirus":{"blurb":"Naharin set Peter Handke's Offending the Audience against dance. A performer in a man's suit sits atop a stage-wide wall that doubles as a blackboard and recites the play at the house; partway through he steps out of the suit, which stays standing where it was, empty. Dancers go up and draw on the wall all evening — 'you' scrawled on one side, 'atem', the Hebrew plural, on the other, and near the end a blood-red asterisk that two of them etch until it glows. — 2001-03-19"},"mamootot":{"blurb":"Naharin traded the proscenium for a square of studio floor with the audience on all four sides, close enough to hear the dancers breathe. Phrases are repeated facing each side in turn so every quarter of the room takes a turn at being the front, and the performers sit out among the spectators in reserved seats to watch. At the end they walk the perimeter and take viewers by the hand one at a time, holding the look. The title is Hebrew for mammoths. — 2003-03-23"},"max":{"blurb":"Five men and five women stand with their backs to the house as the curtain goes up, the women in deep plié, lit pink on one side and green on the other. There is no composer to look up: the score is credited to 'Maxim Waratt', a pseudonym Naharin uses for his own soundtracks, which is why sound and movement fit so exactly. A recorded voice recites what could be an alphabet, then counts to ten in an invented language while the dancers accumulate a gesture per number. — 2007-03-10"},"sadeh21":{"blurb":"Twenty-one numbered sections — sadeh is Hebrew for field — with the numbers projected on the back wall as they arrive, and gaps long enough between them to unsettle your sense of how long you have been watching. The pale panels at the back turn out to be a wall: in the last section the dancers climb it and drop off backwards, in back flips, cannonball jumps and swan dives, then climb and drop again while the credits roll across the same wall. — 2011"},"emergence":{"blurb":"Pite asked whether the hierarchy of a ballet company had a parallel in nature, looked at the beehive, and found from the swarm research that it did not: decisions are the group's and the queen does not govern. She put the dancers en pointe — something she rarely does — for the creature effect, and Owen Belton's score runs the drone of bees under the sound of marching. What you watch is a scurrying, insect-like crowd in a subterranean half-light, on a stage treated as a nest. — 2009-03-04"},"tempestreplica":{"blurb":"The Tempest is told twice. First as a storyboard: faceless figures in chalk white deliver the plot through gesture and posture inside a set built like a maquette of the island. Then the same story returns through dancers playing the characters as people, so the audience watches the diagram fill up with feeling. Pite reads the island as a mind — a place of mystery, spirit and ego — and takes Prospero's choice of virtue over revenge as the real event of the play. — 2011"},"betroffenheit":{"blurb":"Dance-theatre built with the writer-performer Jonathon Young out of his own experience of tragedy, addiction and loss, made with Electric Company Theatre: the title names the shock 'in the wake of a disaster... a timeless, liminal space where you return again and again.' Across its two acts trauma and crisis-management turn into dance, and it took the 2017 Olivier for Best New Dance Production among a sweep of honours. — 2015-07-23"},"thestatement":{"blurb":"Four dancers in unremarkable suits work around one long table in what could be a windowless basement, their bodies keyed to a recorded conversation played over them. Two come from the department that is to carry the blame for a conflict spiralling out of control; two are there to extract a statement saying it acted alone. Jonathon Young's script keeps the power offstage — it is only ever 'Upstairs' — and hands the dancers its language: the two sides were to 'cancel each other out'. — 2016-02-04"},"flightpattern":{"blurb":"Górecki's Symphony of Sorrowful Songs sung over thirty-six dancers — a corps become a crowd, Pite's response to the refugee crisis. 'A sombre and deeply affecting work' that 'cuts to the heart,' Luke Jennings wrote; it won Pite the 2018 Olivier Award for Best New Dance Production. — 2017-03-16"},"revisor":{"blurb":"Eight dancers move to a recorded soundtrack of actors speaking, embodying the dialogue so exactly that the words appear to be coming out of their bodies. Pite and the playwright Jonathon Young took an archetypal comic plot of political imposture as the frame for what is really a study of how much language a body can carry. It won the Olivier Award for best new dance production and a National Dance Award for best modern choreography. — 2019-02-20"},"bodyandsoul":{"blurb":"A recorded voice describes a movement sequence between two figures while the dancers carry it out; it ends with one handling the other's head — on the chest, in the hands, on the ground — and that handling becomes the motif the whole piece turns over. The first act is danced in near-darkness in long black overcoats against a row of standing lights, the second breaks into Chopin preludes, and then the stage turns bronze and the company returns as clawed insects in black latex. — 2019"},"lightofpassage":{"blurb":"Pite kept her one-act Flight Pattern of 2017 as the first part and used the rest of Górecki's Symphony of Sorrowful Songs for two more. The first is a sea of heavy grey overcoats that sways and falls like water on a shore. The second, built on a children's version of the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child, sets six ballet-school pupils aged nine to eleven in white among eighteen adults in black who build them corridors, hills and roads to travel. — 2022-10-18"},"politicalmother":{"blurb":"It opens in silence on a figure in full battle dress who draws his sword and plunges it through his belly. Then the score erupts: drummers in military tunics, faces invisible in the half-dark, a rock band whose riffs seem born of the same percussion, a demagogue ranting from on high in words no one can make out, ten dancers swinging between scraps of folk dance and abject capering. One reviewer read the message as unambiguous — state, party and advertiser all twist familial love into political loyalty. — 2010-05-20"},"sunshechter":{"blurb":"A brightly lit pastoral: dancers in eighteenth-century dress, a placid flock of cardboard sheep, and Shechter’s recorded voice promising this scene is a flash-forward to the ending and it is all going to be fine. It is not. A cardboard wolf slides in among the sheep, electronic rock shatters the music, and the dancing buckles into beatings and goose-stepping while a desperate showman keeps smiling at us. The closing tableau arrives as promised, overshadowed by a hanging figure. — 2013-10-12"},"contemporarydance20":{"blurb":"A rebuild for eight dancers aged eighteen to twenty-five of a piece Shechter first made in Gothenburg, and a joke at his own form's expense: the performers hold up hand-scrawled placards announcing each section. 'Part I, Pop' opens with music-video swagger and, ten minutes later, half the cast is flogging the other half. Part 4, labelled 'contemporary dance', swaps the electronic beats for Bach and the front-facing unison for private trajectories — but many of the steps are the same. — 2022-03-23"},"babygang":{"blurb":"Brazen adolescence, danced by a gang of nine on a stage fenced in by dark panels that throw out beams of light. In ivory shirts and anthracite trousers of Godani's own design, they break the rules of classical, modern and contemporary technique in search of an instinctive, almost animal source for movement — break-dance spins, knotted figures, off-axis positions borrowed from Forsythe. Two of the nine are women, cropped and androgynous enough that a reviewer could not pick them out of the pack. — 2004"},"rawmodels":{"blurb":"Godani designed the lighting and the costumes as well as the steps, and used darkness as a choreographic device: the dance comes in bursts between blackouts, dancers crossing on and off in stockinged feet so that four become seven with nobody seen to arrive. Movement starts in the torso and the limbs follow it, flicking and flopping, inside dark androgynous costumes one reviewer read as an alien exoskeleton — dance as science fiction, she wrote, dance as gothic horror. — 2011"},"soloatdusk":{"blurb":"Made in lockdown and danced on a purple carpet in the company's downtown parking lot, with the audience watching from inside their cars. Despite the title it is a piece for seven, every dancer in a three-dimensional floral mask — not printed flowers but face-covering bouquets, an effect one critic called Magritte-like until the dancers started shouting to each other. Smith called the result a fossil of the moment: the distance, the uncertainty, the search to be understood. — 2020"},"quartetforfive":{"blurb":"Five people held to the formal outline of a four-part Philip Glass score, which is the joke in the title and also the problem the piece sets itself. A white set, three black chairs and a black table pushed to one side, costumes in black, white and grey; dancers cross on hard diagonals, entering and leaving one at a time, until a soloist is left standing. Late on, against Glass's frenetic strings, the whole cast rushes to the centre in a bedlam of who wants whom, and nothing is settled. — 2022"},"missingmountain":{"blurb":"Smith and Schraiber built a room — red carpet wall to wall, a pale blue couch, an upright piano, a long table, a ladder against the wall — and left six people in it, never settling who they are to one another. One, in a tuxedo, steps to an old radio microphone and gives a manic weather report in accented English that keeps slipping into Japanese; another, playing director, asks a dancer whether she can become a mountain, and she does. Near the end a curtain is drawn back on a large painted mountain. — 2023"},"phantomlayer":{"blurb":"The title is a term from graphic design: stack images on top of one another and traces of the earlier ones stay visible. Sanchis built the piece on that, for three dancers who had made the movement material themselves and who performed it against fiction lifted from existing literature — text used to generate musicality as much as story. A video artist filmed from the first day of rehearsals, so a fictional context entered the work while it was still being made. — 2013"},"islands":{"blurb":"Not one piece but nine, numbered, each lasting between four and twelve minutes and each made inside a short residency that ended with a showing. Solos, duets and trios, with dancers from the earlier ones reappearing in the later; Sanchis described the format as what short stories are to literature or songs are to music, autonomous units with no overarching hidden structure to unravel. Full evenings were then to be assembled from different combinations, each potentially unlike the last. — 2014"},"radicallight":{"blurb":"Five dancers on an orange carpet, an hour long, the whole thing built on one shared pulse at 120 beats per minute. Sanchis set the loose dancing people do at home or in a club against formal composed dance and let the two feed each other rather than argue; it opens looking like a warm-up and thickens towards trance, with the dancers who are not dancing waiting in the half-dark at the edge, still moving. Discodesafinado's minimal techno is not accompaniment but the other party in the exchange. — 2016"},"alovesupreme":{"blurb":"Sanchis and De Keersmaeker rewrote their 2005 quartet for four young men, one dancer standing in for each player on Coltrane's record. It opens in silence under a single downcast light and stays there a long while — four solos building into a chaotic square, then an abrupt unison stop — and the album itself does not arrive until well in. There are no wings, so whoever is not dancing waits against the wall in full view, shedding a jacket, wiping the sweat off. — 2017-02-23"},"nutcrackerabt":{"blurb":"Baryshnikov cut the Sugar Plum Fairy and made the cast adult, so Clara herself dances the second-act grand pas de deux with her Prince; he also reordered it, putting the variations and coda before the adagio. The adagio then turns into a pas de trois - Drosselmeyer arrives to take Clara back out of the dream, and she is passed between godfather and prince, torn. Of the older choreography he kept Vasily Vainonen's Waltz of the Snowflakes. — 1976"},"donquixoteabt":{"blurb":"Baryshnikov kept the Petipa and Gorsky steps and rebuilt everything around them, breaking up the corps so that most of the fifty-odd dancers got a solo, a small ensemble or a scrap of business - Time's reviewer compared it to what Robbins had done to the Broadway chorus line and called the flavour 'distinctly American.' Basil, the first full-length role Baryshnikov had danced in Leningrad, keeps his mock-suicide scene, played hollow-eyed as a male Giselle. — 1978"},"drasticclassicism":{"blurb":"Ballet technique put in a room with a live punk band and not allowed to leave: Rhys Chatham's score, which he described as a very dissonant piece, played at a volume that becomes a physical condition of the dancing. It is the work in which Armitage began her systematic critique of classicism, repeatedly threatening the verticality that ballet exists to hold. Sally Banes treats it as the piece that brought new wave music into postmodern dance. — 1981"},"watteauduets":{"blurb":"The title comes from Watteau's fete galante paintings, and the piece turns the baroque idyll into open war between a man and a woman: pointe shoes used not for ethereality but as weapons, over forty minutes of duet with the musicians onstage. David Linton wrote the score; Charles Atlas made the costumes. Armitage and Joseph Lennon danced it, and the company still revives it. — 1985"},"elizabethanphrasing":{"blurb":"The title yokes Elizabethan verse to the free-jazz saxophonist Albert Ayler, and the piece is as spliced as that suggests: David Salle made the set and costumes, and Armitage used two monologues by the Beat-era comedian Lord Buckley as accompaniment for parts of it. This is the middle of her long collaboration with Salle, when a painter was effectively co-authoring her stages. — 1986"},"gogoballerina":{"blurb":"Armitage put ballet in a Lower East Side rock club, with a set and costumes by David Salle and Jeff Koons and Jimi Hendrix on the sound system. The New York Times headline was 'When Go-Go Girls Don Toe Shoes'. Jeffrey Deitch, who remembers it as the most memorable of her performances, saw the point of the venue: it put the uptown art crowd and the downtown one in the same room. — 1988"},"timeistheecho":{"blurb":"Dancers enter and leave through a shimmering beaded curtain made by the painter David Salle, who had been designing Armitage's stages since the early 1980s - by then the collaboration was two decades old and the set was doing as much of the work as the choreography. The New York Times review was headlined 'Under Copper Stars, Ghosts in Leotards'. — 2004"},"ligetiessays":{"blurb":"Armitage set a spread of Ligeti's vocal and instrumental pieces, taking the short ones as her unit: \"In these haiku-like compositions, Ligeti expresses the full gamut of our complex and contradictory natures - from the humorous to the tragic.\" It is the work in which she pushed hardest at the idea that a ballet body could carry that kind of compression. — 2007"},"itutu":{"blurb":"Itutu is Yoruba for cool, in the specific sense of composure under pressure. Armitage built the piece around Burkina Electric, the Burkinabe electronic band Lukas Ligeti co-leads, who played live on stage, and set it against painted backdrops by Philip Taaffe. It is her attempt to make a ballet vocabulary sit inside West African polyrhythm rather than borrow from it. — 2009"},"threetheories":{"blurb":"Three sections - Relativity, Quantum, String - drawn from Brian Greene's The Elegant Universe, which Armitage read as poetry rather than as physics. The scores are as disjunct as the theories: Rhys Chatham's Two Gongs, a Hindustani raga played by Sangeeta Shankar, and John Luther Adams. The piece takes the twentieth century's unreconciled pillars and refuses to reconcile them on stage either. — 2010"},"frankieandjohnny":{"blurb":"A street with a saloon at one end and a brothel at the other, a prostitute in the window: the 1899 ballad staged as ballet, with Frankie climbing a ladder to shoot Johnny and the piece ending with her rocking on his coffin while the mourners drink. It ran an unprecedented six weeks, and was later banned in Boston for 'extremes' in the choreography; when Page took it to Paris in 1950, Le Corbusier called it 'Homeric' and 'Rabelaisian.' — 1938-06-19"},"themerrywidow":{"blurb":"Lehár's operetta with the singing removed: Danilo refuses to court the rich widow to order, falls for her anyway, throws her scarf down at her feet in a jealous rage, and meets her again in a dream as the wood spirit Vilia — which had been the ballet's title. Georges Wakhévitch designed it; Isaac Van Grove and Hans May arranged the score. It kept going for thirty years, with Markova as the widow in 1955, Nureyev as Danilo in 1962, and Page narrating a television version in 1983. — 1953-04-30"},"birthdayoftheinfanta":{"blurb":"Wilde's story of the dwarf brought in to amuse a Spanish princess, to John Alden Carpenter's score, in costumes Robert Edmond Jones worked up from Velázquez. Bolm danced the Dwarf to Ruth Page's Infanta; a surviving photograph catches him on one knee looking up at her while she draws back. The daily papers and the music journals liked it, but it was revived only once and has not been in any company's repertory since. — 1919-12-23"},"krazykat":{"blurb":"Herriman wrote the scenario and made the scenery and costumes himself, so Coconino County reached the stage looking much as it did in the newspapers, and Carpenter — among the first American composers to put jazz rhythms into orchestral writing — subtitled the score a jazz pantomime. Bolm choreographed it and led the dancers. He was after a production that would be wholly American: a Mariinsky-trained ballet master making a ballet out of a comic strip. — 1922"},"gargoylesofnotredame":{"blurb":"1922"},"apollonmusagete":{"blurb":"Coolidge wanted dance at the centre of that year's festival and had the Music Division approach Bolm first; Stravinsky was commissioned only after de Falla and Respighi declined, and to her terms — half an hour, six dancers at most, an orchestra small enough for the hall. Bolm danced Apollo in Nicolas Remisoff's designs, with Ruth Page, Elise Reiman and Berenice Holmes as the Muses. It was given once; Stravinsky, who had reserved the European rights for Diaghilev, barely mentions it in his memoirs. — 1928-04-27"},"mephisto":{"blurb":"Bolm's last piece, made to Liszt's Mephisto Waltzes, with Peter Nelson as the Poet and Jocelyn Vollmar as the Beloved. He had left the stage four years earlier, after a final appearance as the Moor in Petrushka. It was revived the following year; after that the record thins to a handful of rehearsal photographs in the New York Public Library. — 1947"},"theunderscore":{"blurb":"Not a dance but a score for dancing that Stark Smith found rather than made. After fifteen years of teaching contact improvisation she kept prolonging the open, unstructured stretches of class, and her students told her they could feel a shape in them; she named the phases she and they were already going through. Twenty-odd phases, each with a name and a glyph, run three or four hours from small private internal activity to high-energy contact, and nobody calls the cues aloud. \"The Underscore is a hologram, not a railroad track.\" — 1990"},"tuningscores":{"blurb":"A practice, a performance and a research tool at once. Dancers move; anyone watching may interrupt with a spoken call - and the calls are the instrument, letting a watcher stop, replace, rewind or repeat what is happening. Everybody present is performer, director and spectator by turns, so the composition is edited out loud in real time by the whole room. Nelson built it out of asking what the senses are actually doing when we watch movement. — 1973"},"part":{"blurb":"An open improvisation with a fixed spine: Nelson and Steve Paxton danced whatever arose, but always inside the same recorded soundtrack - Robert Ashley's opera Private Parts, which is where the title's spacing comes from. They kept performing it for twenty-four years, until 2002, which makes it less a piece than a twenty-four-year conversation with the same fixed partner in the tape machine. — 1978"},"nightstand":{"blurb":"The second of the two long-running improvised duets Nelson and Steve Paxton kept in performance for decades; Danspace dates its performing life as running for close to a decade, ending in 2013. Beyond that span the sources found for this pass say nothing about what the duet consisted of. — 2004"},"doublesfor4":{"blurb":"Four dancers who are out of the dance walk on with a card table and four folding chairs, set them up, sit down and deal themselves shuffled cards printed with instructions for simple clapping patterns; the patterns play against one another and grow complicated. At the end of the assigned number of deals they put the cards away, fold up the table and chairs and leave, and the dance they had been sitting inside continues as before. Dunn's own description of the piece. — 1959"},"raft":{"blurb":"1980"},"war":{"blurb":"Morris and the painter Robert Huot, in armour collaged from found objects — one shield carried a picture of Eisenhower — ran at each other yelling and beat away with wooden weapons until they splintered, then released doves from inside their costumes. La Monte Young had spent the previous five minutes making a gong vibrate in darkness. Rainer thought it incredible, somewhere between medieval and pop art; Paxton, who wanted self-reflexive titles and pedestrian movement, thought it preposterous. — 1963-01-30"},"site":{"blurb":"Morris, in white with work gloves and a skin-tight flesh-coloured mask, stands before a white box playing a tape of a pneumatic drill recorded from his studio window. He lifts away three large plywood boards; pulling the last one aside reveals Carolee Schneemann reclining, dusted white, in the pose of Manet's Olympia. She stays transfixed while he bends, balances and twirls the remaining board around her. Work gestures surrounding a cultural icon, and thereby stripping it of its fetish. — 1964"},"watermanswitch":{"blurb":"Morris and Yvonne Rainer performed it naked, positioned in a single horizontal line with only their bare backs visible to the audience, on a set of wooden tracks Morris had built as a sculpture and never exhibited as one. The New York Times headed its notice 'Dance Program Offers 2 Nudes'. The cost fell on the building: after the Rainer-Morris concert of new works, Judson Memorial Church was nearly ousted from the American Baptist Convention. — 1965"},"column":{"blurb":"A grey rectangular column, eight feet by two by two, stands upright for three and a half minutes, falls - Morris pulled it over with a string from offstage - and lies on its side for another three and a half. His first performance contained no visible human being. Given seven minutes of stage time, he simply bisected them; his role as a performer, he said, was to facilitate the fall. — 1960"},"mannequindance":{"blurb":"A nine-minute solo of turning very slowly from stage right to upstage left and lowering to the floor, sung throughout — Second Hand Rose and Get Married Shirley — in a bloody biology lab coat. Gordon made it standing in a bathtub waiting for a crab lotion to take effect, which is about as much territory as a bathtub offers. The title survives a plan he never carried out: to rent department-store mannequins and reposition them between ten performances in one evening. — 1962"},"helensdance":{"blurb":"A class assignment set to Satie, which Gordon answered by ignoring the music — an option the assignment had not offered. Having no studio, he made a long narrow piece that ran through the three rooms of his railroad flat, then performed it in about twelve feet: twenty-odd activities in a straight line, one of them miming the planting of a flower. It was named for a friend who had just died of cancer, and he stopped performing it when he caught himself doing it sentimentally. — 1962"},"thematter":{"blurb":"Gordon's return to making dances after the five-year silence, and the piece he kept reopening for the rest of his life. It began from Muybridge's motion photographs - bodies broken into held instants - and included a solo for Valda Setterfield in which she moved nude through a sequence of such positions. Excerpts kept resurfacing in later works, and in 2012 he rebuilt it as The Matter/2012: Art and Archive. — 1972"},"notnecessarilyrecognizable":{"blurb":"1978"},"fieldchairandmountain":{"blurb":"Gordon's first commission from an American ballet company, and he took the assignment literally: the title names the score (a piano concerto by the Irish composer John Field), the props (folding chairs), and an image. Critics read it as an argument with Twyla Tharp's crossover ballets. Its immediate ancestor was Field Dances, made for London's Extemporary Dance Theatre. — 1985"},"instantchance":{"blurb":"Dancers threw huge numbered styrofoam blocks into the air like dice and read their instructions off whichever faces landed up — the shape fixing the kind or place of movement, the colour the speed, the number the rhythm. Summers made the method visible on purpose: most chance dances settled their order in advance, leaving an audience unable to tell chance from choice. Here not even the choreographer could predict what the dance would look like from one performance to the next. — 1962-07-06"},"thedailywake":{"blurb":"Summers laid the front page of the Daily News on the floor and read it as a score, using its words to structure the dance and treating the progression of its photographs across the page as a map. She taught the five performers a series of poses copied from those photographs, and wrote out movement qualities for each of the three sections. The title is the argument: what a daily paper reports is already dead and finished, so it has a wake-like quality. — 1962-07-06"},"danceforcarola":{"blurb":"A solo without music, structured as one thing: moving from standing to crouching and back to standing, evenly, in slow motion, for eight or ten minutes. It was the first dance to come out of the body work Summers had been doing for a decade, and the beginning of the discipline she later called kinetic awareness; she dedicated it to her teacher Carola Speads. She was frightened to do it, and said the luxury of taking however long a thing needed was what the group gave her. — 1963-06-23"},"fantasticgardens":{"blurb":"Summers called it the first full-evening intermedia concert in New York. Films were splashed over the ceiling, floor, walls and the audience, who sat around the edge of the sanctuary and were handed small mirrors to catch further images and to light the dancers moving inside large junk sculptures. A film of Sally Stackhouse ran while Stackhouse danced in front of it, not always in step. It was made, Summers said, on the bitter acknowledgement that you cannot see behind your own head. — 1964-02-17"},"energychanges":{"blurb":"1971"},"judsonfragments":{"blurb":"Summers had been shooting film around the Judson circle for years; here she cut the offcuts together and played with the medium itself - running speeds, negative, superimposition. Stan VanDerBeek and Ka Kwong Hui shot alongside her, and Malcolm Goldstein and John Herbert McDowell supplied the music. It is one of the few moving records of that room, made by someone who was in it. — 1964"},"narrative":{"blurb":"Each dancer held a score of walking patterns, focus and tempo, with cues to be taken from what the others did — the instructions abstract rather than psychological, one of them directing Paxton to take great care never to focus on Rainer or direct his movement at her. Emerson wanted a drama produced by nothing but people changing their spatial and temporal relations to one another, and judged afterwards that she had not brought it off. — 1962-07-06"},"cerebrisandtwo":{"blurb":"A dance built on a spatial grid: three women worked in designated areas, cueing off one another while throwing dice against a chart of movement possibilities, so that they had to think constantly. The and 2 of the title was a duet, whose available material included tongue to nose, yawn, slap ass, scratch, fluff hair, laugh, talk and cough. Rainer surprised Emerson by asking whether making faces was allowed. — 1963-06-23"},"sense":{"blurb":"Emerson used the large pipe trapezoid standing in the gym as apparatus: walking round its edges, then leaping up to grasp the horizontal pipe on top, swinging into a handstand on it and travelling the length of the structure. In a leotard she had sewn herself, legs bare, she looked more like a sports gymnast than a dancer. Others in the group found the structure heavy and a bit frightening. — 1963"},"motorcycle":{"blurb":"Dunn wanted the raw energy of the Village street rather than Cunningham's refinement, though nothing in the dance looked like a motorcycle and Larry Poons declined to ride his into the church. She held the yoga lion — squatting, eyes and mouth wide, tongue out — for twenty seconds, then fell over into a dance that stayed close to the floor. Four women backed her on the first night, stepping in stiff unison and chanting; on the second night there were two, and Johnston preferred it. — 1963-12-06"},"witnessii":{"blurb":"One long phrase travelling in four directions, which Dunn described as a mandala and danced across two evenings in three versions — solo, duet and trio — using it as a fluid link between the other pieces. For the solo Paxton designed her a costume: leotard and tights in different shades of brown, one leg missing and the other painted blue, twigs pushed through the fabric, and a shoe rigged so that grass seemed to be growing up between her toes. — 1963-12-06"},"lastpoint":{"blurb":"A collaboration with the filmmaker Gene Friedman and the composer Robert Dunn. Banes pairs it with Summers's Fantastic Gardens as the multimedia strand of Judson work — the line that ran furthest into the later 1960s — and dates the end of Judson Dance Theater as a workshop and a cooperative to the autumn in which it ran. Beyond a New York Times notice, her account preserves almost nothing of what the piece itself was. — 1964-10-19"},"musicircus":{"blurb":"Cage's instruction was close to the whole piece: invite anyone who will come to perform at the same time in the same place. The first one filled a hall built for judging cattle on the University of Illinois campus with simultaneous, unsynchronised performers and a crowd free to wander between them. To help people find the building, Cage promised to moor weather balloons outside it. — 1967-11-17"},"hpschd":{"blurb":"Seven harpsichord solos and fifty-two computer-generated tapes, running five hours in front of six thousand people in a basketball arena, with slide projections from NASA. Cage built the harpsichord parts by chance-processing Mozart, Beethoven, Chopin, Schumann, Gottschalk, Busoni, Schoenberg and himself; Lejaren Hiller wrote a program modelled on the dice game of Mozart's Musikalisches Wurfelspiel and the sixty-four hexagrams of the I Ching. The commission came from a Swiss harpsichordist, Antoinette Vischer. — 1969-05-16"},"waterwalk":{"blurb":"Cage performed it on Italian television, on the quiz show Lascia o raddoppia?, where he was appearing week after week as a contestant on the subject of mushrooms. A composition for solo television performer with a large number of properties: he moves among them on a timed score, and the studio audience laughs. It is the clearest surviving film of Cage treating a stopwatch as an instrument and a mass audience as a room. — 1959-02-05"},"walksanddigressions":{"blurb":"The solo that stopped him. \"The audience booed, hissed, clapped, stamped their feet, and walked out across the performance space while I was working,\" Gordon wrote, and the review was devastating. He did not make another dance for five years. Looking back he blamed himself as much as them: he had been working for the approval of his peers, and \"when the audience and my peers turned on me, I picked up my marbles and went home.\" He kept dancing - for Rainer, then in the Grand Union. — 1966"},"onelifeatatime":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"mickeyvideo":{"blurb":"Basil directed, produced and choreographed it herself, on no budget: \"They didn't put anything in the budget, I did everything myself. And this is the actual cheer sweater from Las Vegas High.\" The squad backing her were championship cheerleaders from Carson High School in California. A trained dancer in her late thirties in her own school uniform, chanting a cheer at a camera, became one of the defining images of early MTV. — 1982"},"onceinalifetimevideo":{"blurb":"Basil and David Byrne studied archive footage of religious ecstasy - Christian evangelists including Ernest Angley, Japanese sects, people in trances - and Byrne built a spastic dance out of what he saw. \"David kind of choreographed himself,\" Basil said. \"I set up the camera, put him in front of it, and asked him to absorb those ideas. Then I left the room... I just helped to stylize his moves a little.\" She shot it with an old-fashioned zoom lens to sharpen the jerks - about as low-tech, she said, as you could get and still be broadcastable. — 1981"},"sowhathappensnow":{"blurb":"Nine dancers telling their own lives and visions at Performance Space 122 - by the New York Times's reckoning probably the first hip-hop production to reach a mainstream New York dance stage. Mr. Wiggles brought the idea to Crazy Legs; he and Fabel had wanted to make a hip-hop musical, and this was the first attempt at one. — 1991"},"concretejungle":{"blurb":"City kids gather to show off, and the police victimise them. The Rhythm Technicians and Rock Steady Crew introduced it at a summer festival three years before their off-Broadway show - the point at which breaking started being used to argue about something rather than only to compete. — 1992"},"rockinit":{"blurb":"Ken Swift's evening of rock dance - uprocking and toprocking, the confrontational forms that predate the floorwork most people mean by breaking - performed by the Rock Division of his VII Gems crew. He describes it as the first rock dance theatre piece ever made. Breakin' Convention and Sadler's Wells sent it round eight British cities that May. — 2009"},"whosthatgirltour":{"blurb":"Shabba Doo - Adolfo Quinones, founding Locker, and Ozone in the Breakin' films - choreographed Madonna's second world tour. It is the point at which the vocabulary invented in a Los Angeles college cafeteria fifteen years earlier became the default grammar of the stadium pop concert, and it put a street dancer in charge of a stadium show. — 1987"},"sitaradevicarnegiehall":{"blurb":"Sitara Devi brought kathak to one of the great American concert halls at fifty-five, nine years after the Royal Albert Hall. The New York Times headline was 'Sitara Dazzles in Rich Kathak Dances', and the paper still reached for the evening a decade later as its benchmark: she \"dazzled a Carnegie Hall audience with her virtuosity and stage presence.\" At the time American audiences knew Bharata Natyam from the south far better than they knew the northern school. — 1976"},"orpheushellerau":{"blurb":"Staged in a theatre Adolphe Appia designed as one continuous space — no proscenium, no raised stage, no orchestra pit — with movable steps echoing the bleacher seating and thousands of lamps behind translucent linen, so that, as the chief technician put it, instead of a lighted space there was a light-producing space. Gluck's ending was cut: rather than Amor restoring Eurydice, the mourners returned and reprised the opening dirge, leaving the chorus in possession of the stage. Nearly all the performers were students of the institute. — 1913"},"bani":{"blurb":"Not a dance so much as the book that records several. Written in exile at Matiya Burj in the 1870s by the deposed last king of Awadh, Bani sets out thirty-six kinds of rahas — dance-dramas in Kathak style, grown out of the Krishna raas lila — with exhaustive notes on costume, jewellery and stagecraft. It is a text rather than a choreography, and it is catalogued as one: the Lucknow Digital Library, which holds a digitised copy, files it under music."},"radhakanhaiyakaqissa":{"blurb":"Wajid Ali Shah staged it in 1843, while still heir apparent to Awadh, and it is generally counted the first of his rahas - dance dramas on the Radha-Krishna theme built out of kathak, in which the prince himself danced. Two of his wives are said to have taken the parts of milkmaids. The form he invented in Lucknow's palaces is one of the lines along which kathak passed from the courts into the twentieth century."},"raicesflamencas":{"blurb":"Farruquito was fifteen. His grandfather Farruco had just died and the family line had fallen to him, so he made a show out of what he had inherited and called it Flamenco Roots. His mother, La Farruca, danced in it. It began in London and then toured, which is an odd first outing for a piece about a Seville family's private tradition. — 1997"},"sphinx":{"blurb":"Cocteau's La Machine Infernale cut down to one woman and two men, danced to Martinu's Double Concerto for two string orchestras, piano and timpani. Rouben Ter-Arutunian built the set, Willa Kim made the costumes, Jennifer Tipton lit it; Martine van Hamel, Clark Tippet and Kirk Peterson were the first cast. Twenty-seven and a half minutes, on pointe. — 1977-12-09"},"alicetetley":{"blurb":"1986"},"cyclometry":{"blurb":"1971"},"aconsortfordancers":{"blurb":"1975"},"bellssanasardo":{"blurb":"Title and maker are all the record holds for this one."},"tower":{"blurb":"The third act of Vaudeville of the Elements, and the part that outlived the rest of it. Dancers chatter at each other and at the audience while swinging on individual aluminium fences, which serve as doors, windows, vehicles and weapons before they are built into a corral with gates that decide who is in and who is out — and then into their own Tower of Babel. A crash, a blaze of light, a burst of smoke, darkness. The notated score calls it 'more performance oriented than dance… mostly improvisational movement with ad lib timing.' — 1965-12-10"},"fourbrubeckpieces":{"blurb":"1984-02-09"},"earthsaga":{"blurb":"A group work for nine dancers that Kloepper built out of her impressions of Pare Lorentz's documentary The River, about the people living along the Mississippi. Esther Williamson wrote an original piano score and played it with Harvey Pollins, and Arch Lauterer lit it. The verdict quoted in the standard history of the Bennington years is blunt: Earth Saga \"was definitely of beginner's stuff, derivative\". — 1938"},"romanticstudy":{"blurb":"Every source that names it calls it Romantic Theme rather than Romantic Study. Harvey Pollins wrote the music. It was the weaker of the two solos Kloepper showed that night — Dance Observer called it \"less definite\" beside Statement of Dissent — though John Martin, writing in the New York Times, took the pair of them together as the high mark of the evening. — 1938"},"eileenfilm":{"blurb":"Kramer dances the whole of it seated in a chair, at 107, to an instrumental called Eileen by the clarinettist David Orlowsky and the lutenist David Bergmüller. It went out as a classical music video in May 2022 and won the video-clip prize at the following year's Opus Klassik awards — a German record-industry award, which is not where a dancer trained in 1930s Sydney by a Viennese émigré is usually found. — 2022"},"afterworld":{"blurb":"Kramer's last performance, made with the filmmaker Sue Healey and the composer Laurence Pike from Pike's album The Undreamt-of-Centre — music he wrote in the pandemic out of Greek myth, Rilke's Sonnets to Orpheus and his own grief. Five younger dancers appear with her. She died in November 2024, a week after her hundred and tenth birthday, so the film met its audience two months after she had gone. — 2025"},"vivandierepasdesix":{"blurb":"Arthur Saint-Léon notated this Pas de Six in his own system, La Sténochorégraphie, and printed it as a worked example in his manual — which is why the fragment outlived the ballet it came from. Guest read the notation back into movement in 1975 and restored Pugni's original score with it; Pierre Lacotte staged the result for the Kirov in 1978 and the company has kept it in repertory since, along with many others under its Russian name, the Markitenka Pas de Six."},"folkwangschule":{"blurb":"The name came out of the Edda — Freya's hall, taken up by a museum founder who wanted a meeting place for townspeople of every class — and in 1927 an opera director and Kurt Jooss put music, dance and speech under one roof in Essen. Jooss ran the dance department and made The Green Table there. In 1933 he took his company out of Germany rather than dismiss its Jewish members. — 1927"},"marywigmanschuledresden":{"blurb":"Wigman meant to stay in Zurich; the Swiss refused her a residence permit, and a general strike then stranded her in Dresden, where she danced night after night and students began seeking her out. Berthe Trumpy bought a house whose living room served as the studio. By 1926 it held 360 students on a three-year course ending in a certification exam — men could enroll, but no man ever joined her dance group. — 1920–1942"},"joossleederschool":{"blurb":"Twenty-two students left Germany with Kurt Jooss and Sigurd Leeder, and the school they opened at Dartington Hall became the first professional activity of the estate's new arts department. It was the fount of personnel for Ballets Jooss. In 1940 enemy aliens were ordered away from England's southern coasts; the staff had to leave Dartington, and Jooss was interned at Huyton, Leeder at a camp on the Isle of Man. — 1934–1940"},"monteverita":{"blurb":"A hill above Ascona settled in 1900 as a commune for people who wanted out of bourgeois life, and later the year-round home of Laban's school, whose curriculum ran under the rubric dance-sound-word. Wigman came in 1913 for a summer course with a Berlin teaching post waiting; partway through, the contract arrived and she sent it back unsigned. By her account a short course turned into a life's direction. — 1913–1919"},"denishawnschool":{"blurb":"St Denis and Shawn ran the school as a touring enterprise, carrying repertories of exotica amply adorned with colourful sets and picturesque paraphernalia, and taught amid talk of religion, eugenics, sex philosophy, Greek elitism and Delsarte. Elizabeth Kendall's history argues it undercut its own training with the idea that a dance is finally something you make up yourself, and so handed its graduates to the German credos instead. — 1915–1931"},"jacobspillow":{"blurb":"Ted Shawn bought the Berkshire farm in 1930. Jacob's Ladder was the highest mountain in the neighbourhood, so the large sloping rock on his property became Jacob's Pillow. In 1933 he invited six young men to join him there as Ted Shawn and His Men Dancers, and for the seven years to 1940 the place ran as what one historian calls a male idyll. — 1933"},"marthagrahamschool":{"blurb":"Graham founded company and school together in 1926 and lived and worked out of a small Carnegie Hall studio. The teaching started from the most elemental movement she could isolate, contraction and release, and built a vocabulary outward from it. The school has run continuously ever since, which no other dance school in the United States can say. — 1926"},"humphreyweidmanschool":{"blurb":"Doris Humphrey and Charles Weidman took a studio-theatre at 108 West Sixteenth Street where they could teach and rehearse during the week and put performances on in the same room — a company with its own small stage, which few in New York had. Black modern dancers gave concerts there too, including a solo programme in April 1944. — 1928–1945"},"benningtonschoolofthedance":{"blurb":"A Vermont women's college summer session that gathered Graham, Humphrey, Weidman and Holm in one place from 1934, and in doing so trained a generation of college dance instructors. It fixed the career path American modern dance ran on for decades: study in a university physical education department, then finish in a New York studio, then teach in a university department. — 1934–1942"},"americandancefestival":{"blurb":"The successor to the Bennington summers, housed at Connecticut College in New London from 1948 and at Duke after 1978. A summer or two here became a standard step in an American dancer's training, the hinge between a college dance department and a professional studio in New York. — 1948"},"hanyaholmstudio":{"blurb":"It opened in 1931 as the New York branch of the Mary Wigman School, teaching the Dresden curriculum down to the choric class for lay and professional students together. When leftist dancers boycotted it over Wigman's accommodation with the Nazis, stones came through the window; Holm wrote to Dresden asking to drop the name, Wigman agreed, and by 1936 it was the Hanya Holm Studio. — 1931–1968"},"limoncompany":{"blurb":"Limon founded the company in 1946 with Doris Humphrey, his own teacher, and the post of artistic director began with her — an arrangement that reversed the usual order, the older choreographer directing the younger one's troupe. Six people have held that post since, the company having outlasted both of them by decades. — 1946"},"dunhamschool":{"blurb":"Opposed when she tried to open a school in a white neighbourhood, Dunham took the Broadway rehearsal room where George White's Scandals had drilled its chorus and opened on West 43rd Street in September 1945. GI Bill money brought veterans; Brando and James Dean came for drumming and dance class. By 1946 the timetable also carried anthropology, philosophy and playwriting. — 1944–1954"},"dancetheater":{"blurb":"Lester Horton formed the company with Bella Lewitzky in 1946, and he and a small group of co-workers built the theatre themselves; from 1948 to 1960 it stood between a repair shop and a bar, its bright yellow sign against rich brown walls. It was the first space in Los Angeles given over to modern dance, it housed school and stage under one roof, and it ran on an insistence on racial integration. — 1946–1960"},"newdancegroup":{"blurb":"Founded in 1932 as the Workers Dance League, it taught dance as a way of making audiences think about class struggle. By the time Pearl Primus arrived in 1941 it had grown less overtly political and more professional, but it remained one of the few New York schools with an interracial staff and student body. — 1932–2009"},"judimar":{"blurb":"Marion Cuyjet opened Judimar in 1948, its name made from her own and her daughter Judy's, to provide opportunities for brown-skinned Black girls in a city whose ballet schools would not take them. She kept it going for twenty-three years. Judith Jamison, Joan Myers Brown and Arthur Hall all trained there. — 1948–1971"},"soundsinmotion":{"blurb":"For two or three years in the late 1970s McIntyre's studio ran inside the Studio Museum in Harlem's first home, at Fifth Avenue and 125th Street. There were no mirrors — 'Our mirrors were the paintings on the wall' — and dancers going across the floor caught each other to keep clear of the art. Talley Beatty had standing permission to rehearse there and used it instead to stand at the window and watch how people walked. — 1972–1988"},"aaadt":{"blurb":"On 30 March 1958 Ailey and a group of young Black modern dancers gave their first performance at the 92nd Street YM-YWHA, on a shared concert bill rather than in a theatre of their own. Revelations premiered on the same stage on 31 January 1960 and won the audience immediately; the company has carried it ever since. — 1958"},"aileyschool":{"blurb":"Ailey opened it in Brooklyn in 1969 with 125 students; a year later school and company shared a renovated church on East 59th Street with Pearl Lang, who co-directed it with him. The certificate course is still counted in hours — 3,060 of them, across Horton, Graham, ballet, Dunham, West African, tap and hip hop — and no student may take a fourth year. Each December twenty-six of them audition into the section of Ailey's Memoria he built for company members and students, and dance it with the company at City Center. — 1969"},"dancersworkshop":{"blurb":"The studio was an outdoor plank deck in the woods below Mount Tamalpais, built to meander around the tree trunks — one step from wilderness, as A. A. Leath put it. Anna Halprin's two-week summer workshops there were for improvising to find out what bodies could do rather than learning somebody else's technique. Simone Forti once refused to refine a crawl and won the argument; Rainer and Trisha Brown passed through as summer students. — 1955"},"cunninghamstudio":{"blurb":"A loft at Fourteenth Street and Sixth Avenue, lent free of charge after John Cage asked Robert Dunn, an accompanist at the studio, to teach choreography in the autumn of 1960. Dunn charged twelve to fifteen dollars a course from each solvent student, returning students paid nothing, and one assignment read: make a five-minute dance in half an hour. Most of the dances on the first Judson programme were shown here first."},"judsonchurch":{"blurb":"Stanford White designed the church on Washington Square whose arts minister, after ten minutes of audition one hot evening, told the dancers there was no problem. Their first concert, 6 July 1962, ran from eight until midnight in ninety-degree heat: twenty-three dances by fourteen choreographers to a crush of spectators. The workshop that followed met Tuesday nights in the basement gymnasium under one rule — never 'I don't like it', only 'this was my intention, was it there?' The room did not stop after 1964: Movement Research has run its Monday night series in that sanctuary since 1991, moving to Dance Theater Workshop from 2004 while the church was renovated and returning in 2006 to a sprung wood floor. It is still weekly, and still filmed. — 1890"},"johnwebergallery":{"blurb":"Paxton spent a two-thousand-dollar grant on two weeks here in June 1972, trading room and board for the work of fifteen-odd students plus Barbara Dilley and Mary Fulkerson. The performances simply continued the rehearsals: five hours a day on a wrestling mat, no lighting, costumes, music or sets, the audience staying as long as it pleased. He called the dancing contact improvisation because the name described what they were doing and nothing else."},"oberlincollege":{"blurb":"In residence with the Grand Union in January 1972, Steve Paxton taught a class of male students the structure of a solo he had built for himself; eleven of them performed Magnesium with him on wrestling mats in a gymnasium, lurching falls giving way to minutes of near-stillness. A video artist who had wandered in by accident taped it. Contact improvisers date the form from that gym, and two Oberlin students carried it to New York that June."},"asbestokan":{"blurb":"Hijikata's house in Meguro, where apprentices did not visit but moved in. Some ten of them slept on the floor of a room of about fourteen square metres, trained from one in the morning until five, and danced in cabarets whose fees went to Hijikata's wife, each keeping 500 yen of them. Parents telephoned to demand their daughters back. — 1952–2003"},"hakutobo":{"blurb":"The company Hijikata built around a single dancer, Ashikawa Yoko, whose body he used to work out the word-scores that made his choreography transmissible. After his death in 1986 Ashikawa carried it on with the female dancers, moving out of Asbestos-kan to Kokubunji and later Nishi-Ogikubo, the members living communally on shared money earned in burlesque. — 1974"},"kazuoohnodancestudio":{"blurb":"A small studio beside Ohno's house in Kamihoshikawa where he taught several times a week and refused to teach either technique or phrases. Students improvised; by one longtime student's account some classes passed in silence all evening, others to records running from Bach to the Beatles while he talked about death and daily life. He often cooked a meal to close the class. — 1961"},"bodyweatherfarm":{"blurb":"Tanaka moved his company out of Tokyo to a village four hours away and had them learn farming from the neighbours. For eleven years the dancers lived as farmers while training, rehearsing and touring, on the argument that field labour exposes a body to more than a studio does — and that growing the food removed the need for outside funding. — 1985–2011"},"tenshikan":{"blurb":"Kasai Akira, who had suggested the word butoh to Hijikata, named his own studio after the Castel Sant'Angelo in Rome, a building that has held paintings, prisoners and the dead. From 1971 he ran it as a centre for butoh and esoteric study, for what one account calls non-hierarchical, anarchic bodily experimentation. He closed it in 1979 to study eurythmy in Stuttgart, and reopened the same room in 1991 as a eurythmy school. — 1971–1979"},"dairakudakan":{"blurb":"Maro Akaji, an actor, named his style tenputenshiki — being born in this world is a great talent in itself — because Hijikata had already taken the word butoh. The company worked in crowds when most butoh was solo, and once it grew Maro told the founding members to leave and start companies of their own, a rule he called one person, one school. — 1972"},"hijikataarchive":{"blurb":"The Art Center took the collection in April 1998 as its first research archive, and received it already sorted: Hijikata's widow Motofuji Akiko had spent her own money assembling it at Asbestos-kan in Meguro after his death. At its core are the butoh-fu, scrapbooks in which he pasted paintings cut out of art books and wrote beside them the names of the movements they were to yield. Copyright kept the filmed late dances effectively unwatchable until 2009. — 1998"},"gnome":{"blurb":"A student performing group that formed around Ashikawa's teaching in 1988, with a Hakutobo dancer always named as its official director. Members learned by sewing costumes, building props, rigging lights and cleaning floors, and took night work in club burlesque. SU-EN, arrived from Sweden, was one of its founding members. — 1988"},"mutekisha":{"blurb":"Natsu Nakajima was one of the first women to study under the two leaders of the butoh movement, and in 1969 she started her own company here — a second-generation butoh group, and one led by a woman. Where her teachers largely stayed in Japan, she toured abroad steadily, which made this one of the routes by which butoh left the country. — 1969"},"sankaijuku":{"blurb":"Maro Akaji gave Amagatsu both his stage name and the company's when he left Dairakudakan in November 1975: mountain and ocean, the paired characters of an old Chinese compendium of mythology. From 1982 Sankai Juku co-produced a new piece every other year with the Théâtre de la Ville in Paris, and has played more than seven hundred cities. In Seattle in 1985 a rope snapped and Takada Yoshiyuki, hanging by the ankles from a six-storey building, fell to his death in front of a lunchtime crowd. — 1975"},"ecoleduvieuxcolombier":{"blurb":"Copeau built the school on the conviction that the industrial body had gone dull and that an actor is trained by shedding rather than adding. He threw out Dalcroze's eurhythmics as narcissistic and by 1921 had taken up Georges Hebert's natural gymnastics — pull, push, climb, run, jump, carry, attack, defend, swim — as the route back to a child's instinct for play. Etienne Decroux came through it. — 1920–1924"},"ecolejacqueslecoq":{"blurb":"Lecoq opened the school in 1956 and in 1976 moved it into Le Central, a boxing gymnasium in the tenth arrondissement that had held famous fights and inspired a Marcel Carne film. His wife remembered taking it over as near a ruin, without electricity, changing rooms or lavatories. Only a small plaque on a steel door shows it to the street; inside, a wooden balcony still rings the old hall. — 1956"},"ecolephilippegaulier":{"blurb":"Gaulier opened the school in Paris in 1980 on money found for him by the child psychoanalyst Francoise Dolto and by Madeleine Milhaud. The Arts Council then invited him to England, and it spent eleven years moving around London — Highbury, Kentish Town, Cricklewood — before returning to France, to Montreuil, then Sceaux, and since 2012 Etampes. — 1980"},"workcenterofjerzygrotowski":{"blurb":"Grotowski left theatre-making for a rented flat and a workroom at Vallicelle, eight and a half kilometres outside Pontedera, found for him in 1986 by Roberto Bacci's theatre research centre. Seclusion was the method rather than a circumstance of it, and what came out of the room he called art as vehicle. Thomas Richards and Mario Biagini carried the work on after his death. — 1986–2022"},"teatrlaboratorium":{"blurb":"The word 'laboratory' was added to the Theatre of 13 Rows on 1 March 1962, after Kordian, to mark that the company had stopped making productions and started researching what an actor does. It ended as deliberately as it had been named: Grotowski wrote from abroad in December 1982 asking that it be closed, and the self-dissolution took effect on 31 August 1984. — 1959–1984"},"odinteatret":{"blurb":"Barba started it in Oslo in 1964 and moved it to Holstebro, a small Danish town, in 1966 — a company that made itself provincial on purpose and then drew the world's theatre people to it. In 1984 he built an institution around it, the Nordisk Teaterlaboratorium. — 1964"},"theatredusoleil":{"blurb":"The troupe took a derelict cartridge works in the Bois de Vincennes in August 1970 — a building put up after mutineers blew up the city's powder store in 1871 rather than surrender, later a detention centre during the Algerian war — and did the rebuilding themselves. Mnouchkine called it a wasteland as well hidden as Angkor was in the Cambodian jungle. The company, set up in 1964 as a co-operative, pays everyone the same wage and settles casting only after the actors have tried several parts. — 1964"},"ecoleinternationaledemimodrame":{"blurb":"The city of Paris founded it in 1978 at Marceau's initiative, in the old melodrama quarter the school itself calls the Boulevard du crime, and it taught Decroux's technique and Marceau's side by side — two years, five subjects, students aged eighteen to twenty-six from any country. In 2004 the city decided not to keep funding it as it stood, and no first year was auditioned that September. Marceau said he could have set up in America but wanted to defend French artistic heritage. — 1978–2005"},"dv8physicaltheatre":{"blurb":"Lloyd Newson's company made work from 1986 until it stopped — not by losing its funding or splitting up, but because he retired and the company was treated as inseparable from him. The announcement on its own site says so plainly. What survives is the films and an archive of the work, deposited at the University of Bristol. — 1986–2022"},"complicite":{"blurb":"Four people started it in 1983 — Annabel Arden, Fiona Gordon, Marcello Magni and Simon McBurney — as Theatre de Complicite, a name that shed its accents and its French over the next twenty years as the company became a British institution. What it kept was Lecoq's three words: disponibilite, le jeu, complicite. Openness, play, and being in it together. — 1983"},"mummenschanz":{"blurb":"Two Swiss actors met at Lecoq's school in Paris in 1970 and worked the streets as Gody & Pas; an Italian-trained actress saw them play in Rome and followed them back to a Paris café-théâtre as their assistant. The three then moved to Switzerland and spent three months turning their spoken pieces into masked ones — that was Mummenschanz, in 1972, its name joining the old word for masking to a pun on chance. From 1977 they held a Broadway house for three years without speaking a word. — 1972"},"theatredelangefou":{"blurb":"Corinne Soum and Steven Wasson were Decroux's last teaching assistants. They began performing corporeal mime together in 1981 and then ran a company of their own, whose repertory included a piece built directly on his work. What they were carrying was a technique that existed almost entirely in the bodies of the people he had trained. — 1984"},"gecko":{"blurb":"2001"},"franticassembly":{"blurb":"Scott Graham and two fellow students set the company up in 1994, having been struck by a physical theatre company resident in Swansea that specialised in provocative work. What they made was neither dance nor straight theatre: dance moves and thumping music carrying a playwright's text. By the end of the decade the press was calling them the future of British theatre. — 1994"},"parts":{"blurb":"De Keersmaeker started the school in 1995, three years into a residency at the Brussels opera house, on the view that Europe had nowhere to school a contemporary dancer properly. It shares a campus and studios with her company and the Ictus ensemble: three years of training for forty-odd students, then two more years of research for ten or fifteen of them. — 1995"},"ballettfrankfurt":{"blurb":"Forsythe took the municipal company in 1984, renamed it, and turned the dancers away from the audience to face the wings or the back wall; the subscription public expressed its displeasure loudly for some three years. By 2002 the city's politicians said they were tired of his aesthetic and one party called dropping him a liberating blow, while he pointed out that his ballet covered more of its own costs than any other institution in Frankfurt. It was wound up in 2004 over 516,000 euros. — 1984–2004"},"theforsythecompany":{"blurb":"Forsythe rebuilt his ensemble in 2005 out of the dancers of the dissolved Frankfurt company, on money pooled by two federal states, two cities and private sponsors, and gave it two homes: the Bockenheimer Depot in Frankfurt and the Festspielhaus Hellerau in Dresden. Hellerau had been built in 1911 for Jaques-Dalcroze's rhythm school, its hall designed with no fixed stage and no curtain, and had since served as a Nazi police school and a Soviet barracks. Jacopo Godani took the company over in 2015. — 2005–2015"},"batshevadancecompany":{"blurb":"Baroness Batsheva de Rothschild founded the company in 1964 and appointed Martha Graham as its artistic adviser, which made it the only company besides Graham's own permitted to dance her work. Its first generation included Moshe Efrati and Rina Gluck. Ohad Naharin directed it from 1990 to 2018 and developed Gaga in its Tel Aviv studios. — 1964"},"rosas":{"blurb":"The company took its name from the piece being made when it began in 1983, Rosas danst Rosas. From 1992 to 2007 it was in residence at the Brussels opera house, and the school De Keersmaeker built out of that residency, P.A.R.T.S., grew into a separate institution that still shares her campus and studios. — 1983"},"lesballetscdelab":{"blurb":"A remedial educationalist who had never trained as a director started it in 1984 with friends and relatives, working collectively, and gave it a mock-grand name: Les Ballets Contemporains de la Belgique. It kept putting professional dancers and amateurs on the same stage, and it became a platform rather than one man's company — Sidi Larbi Cherkaoui, Koen Augustijnen, Christine De Smedt and Hans Van den Broeck all made work under it. It closed on the last day of 2022. — 1984–2022"},"ultimavez":{"blurb":"The company's 1987 debut, What the Body Does Not Remember, threw stones across the stage and put its dancers in real danger; in New York it won a Bessie for what the citation called the dangerous, combative landscape of the piece. Vandekeybus says he was sick of aesthetic things and of pretending, wanted raw emotion and guts, and that decades later they are still throwing stones in the revival. The house is in Sint-Jans-Molenbeek, Brussels. — 1986"},"cullbergballet":{"blurb":"It was founded in 1967 inside Riksteatern, the Swedish national touring theatre, which meant a company with no house of its own and a duty to play the whole country. Birgit Cullberg ran it until 1981 and handed it to her son, Mats Ek, who rewrote the classics from the inside: his Giselle makes the princes medical staff, the heroine mentally ill and the royal court a care facility. The word Ballet was dropped from the name during the 2010s. — 1967"},"sashawaltzandguests":{"blurb":"Waltz and Jochen Sandig named the company in 1993 for its method: the Guests are architects, film-makers, designers, writers and musicians from more than sixty countries, drawn into over a hundred productions. The company also went into the business of making rooms for itself in Berlin, helping open the Sophiensaele in 1996, a church in 2004 and the Radialsystem in 2006. — 1993"},"levcompany":{"blurb":"Lev is Hebrew for heart. Sharon Eyal built the company in 2013 with Gai Behar, who was not a dancer at all but a Tel Aviv party producer and curator of multidisciplinary events; they had met at one of his electronic music nights. The dancing rests on the Gaga vocabulary she took from more than twenty years at Batsheva, over live rhythms by Ori Lichtik, one of the first Israeli techno DJs. It has been called S-E-D since 2022. — 2013"},"eastman":{"blurb":"Cherkaoui set it up in January 2010 in the port city he was born in, resident at deSingel, as a frame for a way of working rather than a fixed ensemble: he learns somebody else's dance — a flamenco dancer's, a tango dancer's, a ballerina's, Shaolin monks' — step by step and patiently, and builds the piece out of what the two languages turn out to share. He had reached that method through les ballets C de la B, which took him in after he won a prize Alain Platel had invented for Belgian dance solos. — 2010"},"studiowaynemcgregor":{"blurb":"McGregor's company dates from 1992, but the studio itself was made in 2017: three rooms in the Broadcast Centre at Here East, in the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park, two of them among the largest dance studios in London. What it does with them is give them away. Under FreeSpace an artist gets the space for nothing and may be asked to repay it in hours run in local schools rather than in money; a hundred artists have taken 4,500 hours on those terms. — 2017"},"tanztheaterwuppertal":{"blurb":"A municipal opera-house ballet in an industrial town, handed to Pina Bausch in 1973 by the theatre's director Arno Wustenhofer. She renamed it Tanztheater Wuppertal, the name itself an announcement that this would not be a ballet company and that the dancers would be freed from its conventions. She stayed with it, in Wuppertal, until her death in 2009. — 1973"},"mudra":{"blurb":"Béjart put his school in a disused locomotive depot at 103 rue Bara in 1970 and taught, on top of classical and modern dance, singing, percussion, yoga, flamenco and drama: he was after what he called the total actor, as in Greek tragedy or Japanese theatre. Entry was by competitive examination and the course ran three years. It closed in 1988 after he left Belgium, and on the night of 4 May 1992 a fire in the rue Bara destroyed its archives — what one writer called the second death of Mudra. — 1970–1988"},"nederlandsdanstheater":{"blurb":"Seventeen dancers walked out of the Nederlands Ballet in 1959 and started this company without subsidy. Its odd achievement was to build two more companies out of the age problem at both ends: a springboard group in 1978 for dancers not yet ready for a company, and in 1991 NDT 3, made up only of dancers who had grown too old to be cast. Kylián said they were not to be seen as indulging in what had passed but as exploring the richness of experience accumulated over the years. — 1959"},"kiddpivot":{"blurb":"For three years a Vancouver company carried a German name: from January 2010 a residency with Frankfurt's Kuenstlerhaus Mousonturm and the Kulturfonds Frankfurt RheinMain paid the bills, and in return it billed itself Kidd Pivot Frankfurt RM, making The You Show and The Tempest Replica there before the arrangement ended in 2013. Since 2015 it has weighed the carbon of its own touring and offset over a thousand tonnes of it, paid for by everyone in the company giving up a day's salary a year, matched by the company. — 2002"},"nationalnohtheatre":{"blurb":"Japan built a state theatre for noh at Sendagaya in 1983, at a cost of 4.6 billion yen. It holds 591 seats, has display space attached, and is used almost exclusively for nohgaku. The theatre's own account of the form traces its roots back to the eighth century. — 1983"},"kabukiza":{"blurb":"A journalist, Fukuchi Genichiro, opened it in Kobikicho in November 1889 as an argument for theatre reform: a Western facade with a Japanese cypress interior. It has been rebuilt four times since — after an electrical fire in 1921, the 1923 earthquake, the air raids of May 1945, and again in 2013 — each version keeping the tiled roof and undulating gable of the one before. — 1889"},"kathakkendra":{"blurb":"Bahawalpur House on Bhagwandas Road in New Delhi holds the Kathak Kendra, which a 1999 survey of the form called the leading school of kathak in India. It carries full-time students on its rolls, and Birju Maharaj was listed among the gurus teaching there. — 1964"},"kalakshetra":{"blurb":"Rukmini Devi took up a dance then carrying enough social stigma that a Brahmin woman performing it was a scandal, learned it from Pandanallur Meenakshisundaram Pillai, and in 1935 danced at the Theosophical Society's jubilee in the open-air theatre at Adyar. She moved the musicians to the side of the stage and dressed the dancers after temple sculpture. The school followed the next year. — 1936"},"corraldelamoreria":{"blurb":"A tablao opened in 1956 at Moreria 17 by Manuel del Rey, whose family ran Madrid restaurants, and still run by his widow and sons. It calls itself the oldest flamenco tablao in the world. Paco de Lucia presented Entre dos aguas on its floor and Isabel Pantoja made her professional debut there — a supper room that functioned as a proving stage. — 1956"},"juilliardschool":{"blurb":"William Schuman hired Martha Hill in 1951 to build a dance division on a proposition nobody had tried in a conservatory: ballet and modern dance taught in equal measure to the same students. Her first faculty put Graham, Limon, Tudor, Craske and Corvino in one building. She ran it until 1985; Paul Taylor and Pina Bausch came through as students. — 1951"},"metropolitanoperahouse":{"blurb":"In 1950 the house formed its own ballet company and school in cooperation with Ballet Theatre and put Antony Tudor in charge; he held the directorship on and off until 1963. He choreographed the operas, Alceste above all, then gave that work up to concentrate on teaching. Pina Bausch, her Juilliard scholarship year over and short of money, took a dancing job here. — 1883"},"paulsanasardodancestudio":{"blurb":"At Antony Tudor's urging Sanasardo asked Pina Bausch, then in New York on a grant to attend Juilliard, to work with him and Donya Feuer; she made Phases of Madness with them in 1960 — 'We just beat each other up onstage,' he said — and performed with them at the 92nd Street Y while still a student. The studio also kept a children's company that was not a recital: eleven of them carried In View of God in 1959. Bausch remembered them as 'adults, only very young ones. There was nothing cute.' — 1958–1981"},"eguchimiyadancestudio":{"blurb":"Eguchi and Miya returned from two years at Wigman's Dresden school and gave a first recital in 1934 whose reviews and photographs reached Ohno Kazuo, then a school physical education teacher; he began studying with them in 1936. What they taught was analytical — Wigman's method for eliciting movement rather than her dances — which set them against Ishii Baku's more theatrical line. — 1934"},"ishidancestudio":{"blurb":"Ishii moved his dance institute out to a Meguro hillside in 1928 because he liked the name of the liberal school opening next door: Jiyugaoka, freedom hill. When the railway meant to call the new station Fusuma the following year, he and other residents pressed for Jiyugaoka instead, and the district has been called that ever since. Ohno Kazuo spent a year in the studio from 1933, preparing himself to teach dance at a girls' school. Ishii called himself the dancing fool. — 1920–1962"},"tsudamoderndancestudio":{"blurb":"Hijikata was billed as a dancer of this studio when he made Kinjiki in 1959. Mishima Yukio came to see it, stayed through rehearsals, and was asked to supply titles for the improvisation exercises; he offered 'Dali's Melting Clock' and 'The Marquis de Sade', and regretted the second, since of the ten-odd dancers there only some had read Sade. — 1952–1962"},"schoolofamericanballet":{"blurb":"Balanchine would not start a company before a school, and the doors opened on 2 January 1934. The first ballet he made in America he made on whoever turned up: seventeen students came to the first Serenade rehearsal, nine to the second, six to the third, so he set passages for seventeen, nine and six, kept in one girl's fall and another's late entrance, and wrote no soloist roles because nobody in the room could dance one. Those students gave it its first performance that June, on a lawn in White Plains. — 1934"},"jacksonvilleuniversity":{"blurb":"William Forsythe, who spent a career taking ballet's vocabulary apart, did not begin classical training until 1967, when he arrived here at eighteen. His teachers were Nolan Dingman and Christa Long, the only two names any account of his training gives, and two years later the Joffrey Ballet School gave him a scholarship. What was taught in the room, and on whose syllabus, does not seem to be recorded anywhere. — 1934"},"stuttgartballet":{"blurb":"A balletomane named Fritz Höver founded the Noverre Society here in 1958, when ballet was, as the company itself puts it, the small and unloved step-sister of the all-powerful opera. When Cranko arrived in 1961 the two of them turned it into a matinee of four world premieres made by the company's own dancers, with Cranko talking to the audience in between. Neumeier, Kylián and Forsythe all made their first pieces on that stage before becoming resident choreographers of the house. — 1961"},"balletrambert":{"blurb":"The company was made in a Sunday school: 2 Ladbroke Road, built in 1851 for the Congregational chapel next door and converted by Marie Rambert's husband Ashley Dukes into the 150-seat Mercury Theatre, where the Ballet Club gave its Sunday performances. From 1956 the building was mainly the Rambert school's, and it closed in 1987. By then the company's own studios were at 94 Chiswick High Road, opened on 29 March 1971 and closed in 2013 — the room Richard Alston and Michael Clark actually worked in. — 1926"},"armitagedancecenter":{"blurb":"The day started with company class taught by former New York City Ballet dancers and then ran six hours of rehearsal, every day; only by working all day, every day, the company's own account argues, does a company build a culture together. It paid a weekly salary — above the industry standard, it says — to a roster of 65 dancers across forty years, funded in large part by a Circle of Artists, Armitage's peers in the visual arts, who gave work instead of money. It closed in spring 2022, blaming an eroded ecosystem. — 2004–2022"},"vaganovaacademy":{"blurb":"It began in 1738 with twelve girls and boys learning what the school still calls the foreign steps on an upper floor of the Winter Palace, by decree of the Empress Anna and on the initiative of a French dancing master. Agrippina Vaganova, a graduate of 1897, started teaching there in 1921 and wrote out a codified syllabus; the school took her name in 1957, six years after she died. Of the thousands who apply each year some seventy are accepted and about thirty finish. — 1738"},"whiteoakdanceproject":{"blurb":"Howard Gilman ran his Florida estate as a breeding centre for imperilled species from 1982, and turned part of it into studio space for Mikhail Baryshnikov. The touring company that took the property's name was built there, at a distance from New York and its critics, on ground otherwise given over to endangered animals. — 1990–2002"},"hostesabbatinimimeschool":{"blurb":"Hoste was a painter and a puppeteer before he was a mime, and in 1952 he turned his painting studio — a room under the sacristy of St Peter's church in Ghent — into a theatre with barely forty seats. The guide to his archive calls it the smallest theatre in Europe, and from it he brought Étienne Decroux, Marcel Marceau, Ladislav Fialka and Samy Molcho to the city. The mime academy he ran alongside it took Alain Platel in as a schoolboy in 1969. — 1952–1977"},"trivenidancecentre":{"blurb":"Akram Khan was seven when he was taken to Pratap Pawar, a celebrated kathak teacher then working in London, and he has gone on publicly acknowledging the debt. The Bhavan kept a resident kathak teacher on its staff, so a child in London could take the form up seriously without leaving Britain for Delhi. — 1981"},"academyofindiandance":{"blurb":"Tara Rajkumar founded it in 1979 at the Commonwealth Institute, and in 1984 it staged The Adventures of Mowgli — by its own account the first major Indian ballet to receive Arts Council funding — with the young Akram Khan as Mowgli. In 1987 it moved into The Place. In 1996 it stopped teaching evening classes altogether in order to research syllabi for kathak and bharata natyam and audit what South Asian dance provision the country actually had; the name Akademi came in 1998. — 1979"},"mudraafrique":{"blurb":"At the opening in Dakar Béjart told a journalist that he was not the director, Germaine Acogny was, and that she would realise his dreams but would do it differently. She had to hold that ground: European staff claimed to know African dance better than she did and tried to take over her share of the curriculum. The school lasted five years and closed in 1982 for want of money, taking with it Béjart's plan to make a Rite of Spring on African dancers with Acogny as the Chosen One. — 1977–1982"},"ecoledessables":{"blurb":"Germaine Acogny founded the school at Toubab Dialaw, a fishing village some sixty kilometres down the coast from Dakar, after an earlier school of hers had closed. Building it there gave contemporary African dance a teaching base on the continent itself, and her company Jant-Bi grew from the same ground. — 1998"},"speranzevadancestudio":{"blurb":"A Russian emigre who had played Moscow's Kamerny Theatre and the Chauve-Souris cabaret, and trained briefly with Wigman, taught in Chicago under the name Luda Paetz. With her student Katherine Dunham she started the Negro Dance Group there in 1932. Dunham later credited her with 'that explosive feeling, and also a freedom of sexual presentation'. — 1928–1940"},"habitationsauvage":{"blurb":"Twenty wooded acres of villas, pools and sculpture outside Port-au-Prince, reputed to have housed Pauline Bonaparte and later the French general who tortured Haitians at the close of the revolution. Dunham bought it for $25,000 while she and her New York school were deep in debt, and could do so only because the American occupation had rewritten Haiti's constitution to permit foreign ownership. She said she felt its evil the moment she set foot on it, and bought it anyway. — c.1950"},"daforadancestudio":{"blurb":"1934–1950"},"bodenwieserdancestudio":{"blurb":"She landed in Sydney on 23 August 1939 and was met at the ship by her own dancers, just off an Australian tour. Her husband was taken by the Gestapo and died at Auschwitz; unable to establish his fate, she was refused naturalisation until 1950, when a Vienna court declared him dead. She worked out of a Pitt Street studio, teaching small children and professional actors. The Bodenwieser Dance Centre on City Road came only after her death in 1959, opened by two of her dancers, Keith Bain and Margaret Chapple. — 1964–1996"},"balletnacionalchileno":{"blurb":"The Ballets Jooss had to dissolve at Caracas in 1941, in the middle of a tour, and the University of Chile engaged three of its stranded members — Ernst Uthoff and Lola Botka among them. The national ballet grew out of that hiring, with Uthoff its first director from 1945. A German company's collapse abroad became Chile's state dance institution. — 1941"},"hartforddancecenter":{"blurb":"The conservatory was founded in 1890 as one of the oldest performing arts schools in the country, and modern dance entered it in 1934, when Truda Kaschmann began teaching there and brought the German method with her. Alwin Nikolais took his first modern dance lessons from her; in 1939 the two of them shared a commission that produced the first piece he made. — 1934"},"viennaacademyofmusic":{"blurb":"It began in 1817 as a singing school planned to run four years, two teachers with twelve pupils each, in a house called the Red Apple on the Singerstrasse. The state took it over in 1909 and made it an academy. Artistic dance was offered as a course in 1920 and became a principal subject the year after, under Gertrud Bodenwieser. — 1817"},"cornishschool":{"blurb":"Nellie Cornish opened a music school in Seattle in 1914 and kept adding to it; a sign on the Booth Building in the 1920s advertised Expression, Art, Language, Dancing. Its teaching drew on Calvin Brainerd Cady, who had worked with John Dewey. A 1938 photograph in the school's own history catches Merce Cunningham mid-performance — he was a student here, and here he met John Cage. — 1914"},"92ndstreety":{"blurb":"A Jewish community house on Lexington Avenue whose concert hall became the room where American modern dance was auditioned in public. Ailey's company gave its first performance here on 30 March 1958, and Revelations had its premiere on the same stage in January 1960 — a debut venue for choreographers with no theatre and no money. — 1874"},"sarahlawrence":{"blurb":"Bessie Schonberg taught composition at this Bronxville college and Judith Dunn taught there too, and their students kept surfacing downtown: Lucinda Childs and Meredith Monk joined Cunningham's first composition course while they were still Dunn's students. A solo Childs made at school — one leg working in and out of a stretchy blue jersey bag, partly a satire on Nikolais — resurfaced at Judson as a section of Pastime. — 1926"},"universityofwisconsin":{"blurb":"Blanche Trilling sent Margaret H'Doubler — a biology graduate then employed to teach basketball and swimming — to New York in 1916 with an instruction: 'try to find some dance worthy of a college woman's time.' What she came back and built was approved on 8 June 1926, the first dance major in the country; her own first response to the idea had been 'What? And give up basketball?' Anna Halprin, who arrived in 1938 and could not get housing as a Jewish student, found in H'Doubler's classes 'a space for being accepted.' — 1848"},"kadamb":{"blurb":"Lakhia had been a solo dancer, and what she did at Kadamb from 1964 was to train a group and then compose for it — kathak, an art of the single dancer telling stories, turned into a group form and stripped of the stories. She started choreographing in 1973 with Dhabkar, Pulse. Of the work she said that each day the empty space stares her in the face, and that instead of emptiness she sees a space charged with dormant energy waiting to take form. — 1964"},"balletsrusses":{"blurb":"Diaghilev launched the first season in Paris on 19 May 1909; the triumph lost 76,000 francs. In 1911 he turned the enterprise from a seasonal one into a year-round touring company with no home theatre and, after that first Paris season, no formal ties to the city either. It was called Russian and it never once performed in Russia; by 1918 its impresario and its composer were stateless exiles. Diaghilev lived mostly in hotel rooms and left few personal possessions. — 1909–1929"},"grunewaldschool":{"blurb":"The prospectus promised a school that was democratic and international, where no distinction would be made by nation or class and where fatherless and motherless children and children of unknown origin were welcome; they boarded free at Trabener Strasse 16 from February 1905. Elizabeth Duncan lived in the house and taught while Isadora toured to pay for it, and the children called her Tante Miss. Isadora wrote to Gordon Craig: how Elizabeth goes along in this Inferno I don't know. — 1904–1908"},"moscowschool":{"blurb":"She told Lunacharsky she was prepared to live on black bread and salt, and asked for a thousand girls and boys from the poorest families. What she was given, by the autumn of 1921, was the mansion at Prechistenka 20 that had belonged to the ballerina Alexandra Balashova, and about a hundred and fifty children. On 11 November 1922 the school was struck off the state budget; Lunacharsky said he kept meeting blunt resistance from people to whom it seemed a luxury. — 1921–1949"},"beggarbar":{"blurb":"A cellar at Morton Street, corner of Bleecker, and the objections of the Greenwich Village police inspector who had to clear it for opening in December 1941: a barrel is not a table, one does not sit on crates that used to carry seltzer bottles, and the couch does not belong here. Gert changed the programme every evening, sang to piano and accordion, and taught dance and voice free to anyone promising. Tennessee Williams gave her the idea for the place and then waited tables in it. — 1941–1945"},"henrystreetplayhouse":{"blurb":"Alwin Nikolais became director of this settlement playhouse in 1948 and built a company out of its classes. Murray Louis, arriving from Anna Halprin's improvisation classes, ran the children's programme on improvising with an arm or a foot and all the things an arm or foot can do. Masks, Props and Mobiles (1953) is credited with inventing modern multimedia theatre: scores through seven speakers around the room, dancers lit from every direction. — 1915"},"blackmountaincollege":{"blurb":"A faculty-owned college in North Carolina where everyone took turns at farm work, construction and kitchen duty. One evening in 1952 John Cage staged a piece with no stage: he and Charles Olson read from ladders, David Tudor played prepared piano, Rauschenberg played Piaf on a horn recorder, and Cunningham danced among the audience — a claimant to being the first happening. Cunningham used a teaching residency here the next summer to form his company. — 1933–1957"},"theplace":{"blurb":"A drill hall, built in 1888-89 for the 20th Middlesex (Artists') Volunteer Rifle Corps, from which the Artists Rifles mobilised in August 1914 for the Western Front. It stood empty after the 21st SAS moved out in the late 1960s; locals had long called it the Artists Place, and when the Contemporary Ballet Trust took it in 1969 the name shortened to The Place. London Contemporary Dance School and Theatre were both formed in it, and Richard Alston's company was resident from 1994 until 2020. — 1969"},"sndo":{"blurb":"Pauline de Groot had spent seven years in American studios — Graham's and Cunningham's, working with Erick Hawkins and José Limón — before she started the school in 1975 as an attempt to find directions for dance beside the forms then dominating the field. What grew out of it is a dance school that does not award a dance degree: four years full-time, and the diploma is a bachelor's in choreography. The curriculum sets the conditions for a student's own making rather than teaching a technique. — 1975"},"lines":{"blurb":"King's company gave its first performance in 1982 in a university theatre at San Francisco State, and he calls the works 'thought structures'. It has made pieces with Baka artists from the Central African Republic and with Shaolin monks. Its studios on the fifth floor of 26 Seventh Street run the company's own advanced class as an open one: anyone over sixteen can walk in and take it beside the dancers, five days a week. — 1982"},"nationalcentreforcircusarts":{"blurb":"Circus Space started in a timber yard off North Road and in 1994 moved into the Shoreditch Electric Light Station, an 1896 refuse destructor that burned the borough's rubbish for steam and heated the public baths next door with what was left; the motto reads E PULVERE LUX ET VIS, out of the dust, light and power. Until 2014 it was the only place in Britain where you could take a degree in circus. Beren D'Amico and Charlie Wheeller came out of it and founded Barely Methodical Troupe. — 1989"},"lido":{"blurb":"1988"},"amordedios":{"blurb":"Flamenco in Madrid had been taught in the separate rooms of individual maestros — Estampio, La Quica, Antonio Marin — and from 1953 this centre gathered that scattered teaching into one building. Since then the great figures of successive flamenco generations have passed through its studios, which is the point of the place: not a school with a method but an address where the teachers are. — 1953"},"kalashram":{"blurb":"1998"},"roedingpark":{"blurb":"1903"},"tulareprojects":{"blurb":"No founding or closing date is recorded here — the people below are what places it."},"grandunion":{"blurb":"An improvisation collective rather than an address, formed in 1970 out of Yvonne Rainer's Continuous Project — Altered Daily, a work that had folded its own rehearsal process into performance. For six years the group improvised evenings in studios and colleges, sliding from surreal dramatic scenes to movement games to conversation, each performer granted extreme freedom. Paxton thought the anarchy opened up every possibility and then isolated everyone in it. — 1970–1976"},"erniepyletheatre":{"blurb":"The Takarazuka theatre in Yurakucho had spent the war as a balloon-bomb factory; the occupation requisitioned it on Christmas Eve 1945 and reopened it as the Ernie Pyle, reserved for the Eighth Army, with Japanese people forbidden to enter as audience. The man put in charge of it was Michio Ito, whom the United States had held in four internment camps and then deported in a prisoner exchange. He brought his brother Kisaku in to design, and in 1948 was allowed one production for a Japanese public. — 1945–1955"}}}});
